ot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="scope"><h2>Zero net force allows steady motion</h2></div><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-NLM-FIRST-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Laws of Motion"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Laws of Motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION" data-g9-meta-value="Zero net force allows steady motion"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Zero net force allows steady motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-orientation" data-blueprint-slot="orientation" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="hard_transition"><h4>Hard transition</h4><p>Zero net force does not mean zero motion; it means velocity does not change, so rest and steady motion are both compatible with the same condition.</p></div><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-CART-3KG-GLIDE" data-g9-stage="TEACHING" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-ANCHOR-CART-3KG-GLIDE" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="3" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-ANC-GLIDE-V0 VIS-NLM-ANC-GLIDE-V1 VIS-NLM-ANC-GLIDE-V2" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 840 300" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-CART-3KG-GLIDE-1--rep-nlm-anc-glide-title g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-CART-3KG-GLIDE-1--rep-nlm-anc-glide-desc">\n  <title id="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-CART-3KG-GLIDE-1--rep-nlm-anc-glide-title">A 3 kg cart gliding at 2 m/s on a frictionless track</title>\n  <desc id="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-CART-3KG-GLIDE-1--rep-nlm-anc-glide-desc">Stage 1: a 3 kg cart glides at 2 m/s on a frictionless track with no horizontal force on it. Stage 2: F_net = 0, so a = 0/3 = 0. Stage 3: 5 s later the cart still moves at 2 m/s: zero net force keeps the motion unchanged.</desc>\n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-CART-3KG-GLIDE-1--rep-nlm-anc-glide-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-CART-3KG-GLIDE-1--rep-nlm-anc-glide-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-ANC-GLIDE-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">A 3 kg cart gliding at 2 m/s on a frictionless track</text>\n    <line x1="40" y1="200" x2="800" y2="200" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="50" y1="200" x2="42" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="68" y1="200" x2="60" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="86" y1="200" x2="78" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="104" y1="200" x2="96" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="122" y1="200" x2="114" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="140" y1="200" x2="132" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="158" y1="200" x2="150" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="176" y1="200" x2="168" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="194" y1="200" x2="186" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="212" y1="200" x2="204" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="230" y1="200" x2="222" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="248" y1="200" x2="240" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="266" y1="200" x2="258" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="284" y1="200" x2="276" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="302" y1="200" x2="294" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="320" y1="200" x2="312" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="338" y1="200" x2="330" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="356" y1="200" x2="348" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="374" y1="200" x2="366" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="392" y1="200" x2="384" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="410" y1="200" x2="402" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="428" y1="200" x2="420" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="446" y1="200" x2="438" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="464" y1="200" x2="456" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="482" y1="200" x2="474" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="500" y1="200" x2="492" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="518" y1="200" x2="510" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="536" y1="200" x2="528" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="554" y1="200" x2="546" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="572" y1="200" x2="564" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="590" y1="200" x2="582" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="608" y1="200" x2="600" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="626" y1="200" x2="618" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="644" y1="200" x2="636" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="662" y1="200" x2="654" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="680" y1="200" x2="672" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="698" y1="200" x2="690" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="716" y1="200" x2="708" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="734" y1="200" x2="726" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="752" y1="200" x2="744" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="770" y1="200" x2="762" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="788" y1="200" x2="780" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <rect x="80" y="130" width="120" height="60" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="98" cy="195" r="5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="182" cy="195" r="5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="140.0" y="165.0" text-anchor="middle">cart 3 kg</text>\n    <line x1="200" y1="155" x2="280" y2="155" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-CART-3KG-GLIDE-1--rep-nlm-anc-glide-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="210" y="138">v = 2 m/s</text>\n    <text x="40" y="230">frictionless track; no horizontal force on the cart</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-ANC-GLIDE-V1" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="40" y="262" font-weight="600">F_net = 0, so a = 0/3 = 0</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-ANC-GLIDE-V2" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <rect x="560" y="130" width="120" height="60" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="578" cy="195" r="5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="662" cy="195" r="5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="620.0" y="165.0" text-anchor="middle">cart 3 kg</text>\n    <line x1="680" y1="155" x2="760" y2="155" stroke="#1f6fb2" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-CART-3KG-GLIDE-1--rep-nlm-anc-glide-arrow-acc)"/>\n    <text x="560" y="110" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">5 s later: still v = 2 m/s</text>\n    <text x="400" y="262" fill="#1f6fb2">zero net force keeps the motion unchanged</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n<div class="g9-stage-controls"><div class="g9-stage-chips" role="group" aria-label="Stages"><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="0" data-g9-stage-desc="Show the cart, its velocity and the frictionless track.">1: The gliding cart</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="1" data-g9-stage-desc="Sum the horizontal forces.">2: Net force</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="2" data-g9-stage-desc="Read the velocity 5 s later.">3: 5 s later</button></div><p class="g9-stage-desc" data-g9-stage-desc-text></p><div class="g9-stage-stepper"><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="prev">Previous stage</button><span data-g9-stage-label></span><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="next">Next stage</button></div></div><figcaption data-g9-block="representation_bridge" data-g9-caption="purpose">Show the 3 kg cart gliding at 2 m/s with no horizontal force, and its velocity 5 s later.</figcaption></figure><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="governing_relation"><h4>Governing relation</h4><div class="g9-relation"><div class="g9-math" data-g9-math="mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mtext>net</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow></math></div><p>The vector net external force on a chosen body equals its mass times its acceleration.</p><ul><li>m &gt; 0.</li><li>F_net and a refer to the same chosen body.</li><li>All signed components use one declared axis.</li></ul></div></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="compact_anchor"><h4>Compact anchor</h4><p>A 3 kg cart glides at 2 m/s on a frictionless track with no horizontal force on it. What is its velocity 5 s later?</p><p>F_net = 0, so a = 0/3 = 0 and the velocity is still 2 m/s: zero net force keeps the motion unchanged, it does not stop it.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_prompt"><h4>Before you go on</h4><p>One cart is at rest and another moves steadily in a straight line. For each, decide whether zero net force is compatible with the motion and explain the common rule.</p></div></section></article><article id="g9-CORE1--MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1" data-g9-search-text="Physics Laws of Motion Nonzero forces can cancel Medium concept Nonzero forces can cancel"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="scope"><h2>Nonzero forces can cancel</h2></div><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-NLM-FIRST-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Laws of Motion"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Laws of Motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO" data-g9-meta-value="Nonzero forces can cancel"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Nonzero forces can cancel</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-orientation" data-blueprint-slot="orientation" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="hard_transition"><h4>Hard transition</h4><p>Several nonzero forces can act on one body while their directed sum is zero; the motion responds to the net result rather than to the mere presence of forces.</p></div><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-BOX-2KG-5N-5N" data-g9-stage="TEACHING" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-ANCHOR-BOX-2KG-5N-5N" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="3" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-ANC-BOX55-V0 VIS-NLM-ANC-BOX55-V1 VIS-NLM-ANC-BOX55-V2" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 840 280" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-BOX-2KG-5N-5N-1--rep-nlm-anc-box55-title g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-BOX-2KG-5N-5N-1--rep-nlm-anc-box55-desc">\n  <title id="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-BOX-2KG-5N-5N-1--rep-nlm-anc-box55-title">A 2 kg box pushed with 5 N to the left and 5 N to the right</title>\n  <desc id="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-BOX-2KG-5N-5N-1--rep-nlm-anc-box55-desc">Stage 1: a 2 kg box is pushed with 5 N to the left and 5 N to the right at the same time. Stage 2: F_net = 5 − 5 = 0 N. Stage 3: a = 0/2 = 0: two real, non-zero forces act, and the velocity does not change.</desc>\n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-BOX-2KG-5N-5N-1--rep-nlm-anc-box55-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-BOX-2KG-5N-5N-1--rep-nlm-anc-box55-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-ANC-BOX55-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">A 2 kg box pushed with 5 N to the left and 5 N to the right</text>\n    <line x1="160" y1="200" x2="680" y2="200" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="170" y1="200" x2="162" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="188" y1="200" x2="180" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="206" y1="200" x2="198" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="224" y1="200" x2="216" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="242" y1="200" x2="234" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="260" y1="200" x2="252" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="278" y1="200" x2="270" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="296" y1="200" x2="288" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="314" y1="200" x2="306" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="332" y1="200" x2="324" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="350" y1="200" x2="342" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="368" y1="200" x2="360" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="386" y1="200" x2="378" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="404" y1="200" x2="396" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="422" y1="200" x2="414" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="440" y1="200" x2="432" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="458" y1="200" x2="450" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="476" y1="200" x2="468" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="494" y1="200" x2="486" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="512" y1="200" x2="504" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="530" y1="200" x2="522" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="548" y1="200" x2="540" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="566" y1="200" x2="558" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="584" y1="200" x2="576" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="602" y1="200" x2="594" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="620" y1="200" x2="612" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="638" y1="200" x2="630" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="656" y1="200" x2="648" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="674" y1="200" x2="666" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <rect x="360" y="130" width="120" height="70" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="420" y="170" text-anchor="middle">box 2 kg</text>\n    <line x1="360" y1="165" x2="260" y2="165" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-BOX-2KG-5N-5N-1--rep-nlm-anc-box55-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="250" y="158" text-anchor="end">5 N to the left</text>\n    <line x1="480" y1="165" x2="580" y2="165" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-BOX-2KG-5N-5N-1--rep-nlm-anc-box55-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="590" y="158">5 N to the right</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-ANC-BOX55-V1" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="40" y="240" font-weight="600">F_net = 5 − 5 = 0 N</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-ANC-BOX55-V2" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="300" y="240" fill="#1f6fb2">a = 0/2 = 0: two real forces act, and the velocity does not change</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n<div class="g9-stage-controls"><div class="g9-stage-chips" role="group" aria-label="Stages"><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="0" data-g9-stage-desc="Show both pushes on the box.">1: The box and two pushes</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="1" data-g9-stage-desc="Add the two pushes with their signs.">2: Net force</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="2" data-g9-stage-desc="Divide the net force by the mass.">3: Acceleration</button></div><p class="g9-stage-desc" data-g9-stage-desc-text></p><div class="g9-stage-stepper"><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="prev">Previous stage</button><span data-g9-stage-label></span><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="next">Next stage</button></div></div><figcaption data-g9-block="representation_bridge" data-g9-caption="purpose">Show the 2 kg box with its two opposite 5 N pushes, then their sum and the acceleration.</figcaption></figure><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="governing_relation"><h4>Governing relation</h4><div class="g9-relation"><div class="g9-math" data-g9-math="mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mtext>net</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow></math></div><p>The vector net external force on a chosen body equals its mass times its acceleration.</p><ul><li>m &gt; 0.</li><li>F_net and a refer to the same chosen body.</li><li>All signed components use one declared axis.</li></ul></div></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="compact_anchor"><h4>Compact anchor</h4><p>A 2 kg box is pushed with 5 N to the left and 5 N to the right at the same time. What is its acceleration?</p><p>F_net = 5 − 5 = 0 N, so a = 0/2 = 0: two real, non-zero forces act, and the velocity does not change.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_prompt"><h4>Before you go on</h4><p>An object has two equal opposite forces acting on it. Explain what is zero and what is not, then state what motion change the net result predicts.</p></div></section></article><article id="g9-CORE1--MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1" data-g9-search-text="Physics Laws of Motion A free-body diagram belongs to one chosen body Hard concept A free-body diagram belongs to one chosen body"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="scope"><h2>A free-body diagram belongs to one chosen body</h2></div><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-NLM-FIRST-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Laws of Motion"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Laws of Motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP" data-g9-meta-value="A free-body diagram belongs to one chosen body"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>A free-body diagram belongs to one chosen body</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP" data-g9-meta-value="HARD"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Hard concept</span></span></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-orientation" data-blueprint-slot="orientation" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="hard_transition"><h4>Hard transition</h4><p>A free-body diagram contains forces acting on one chosen body only; the equal-and-opposite partner of an interaction acts on the other body and belongs on that other body&#x27;s diagram.</p></div><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-HORSE-CART-600N" data-g9-stage="TEACHING" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-ANCHOR-HORSE-CART-600N" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="3" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-ANC-HORSECART-V0 VIS-NLM-ANC-HORSECART-V1 VIS-NLM-ANC-HORSECART-V2" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 840 360" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-HORSE-CART-600N-1--rep-nlm-anc-horsecart-title g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-HORSE-CART-600N-1--rep-nlm-anc-horsecart-desc">\n  <title id="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-HORSE-CART-600N-1--rep-nlm-anc-horsecart-title">A horse pulling a 200 kg cart along a road</title>\n  <desc id="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-HORSE-CART-600N-1--rep-nlm-anc-horsecart-desc">Stage 1: a horse pulls a 200 kg cart with 600 N, and road friction on the cart is 200 N. Stage 2: the cart\'s horizontal diagram has the horse\'s 600 N pull forward and the road\'s 200 N friction backward. Stage 3: F_net = 600 − 200 = 400 N, so a = 400/200 = 2 m/s²; the cart\'s pull on the horse acts on the horse and belongs on the horse\'s diagram, not in this sum.</desc>\n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-HORSE-CART-600N-1--rep-nlm-anc-horsecart-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-HORSE-CART-600N-1--rep-nlm-anc-horsecart-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-ANC-HORSECART-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">A horse pulling a 200 kg cart along a road</text>\n    <line x1="40" y1="200" x2="800" y2="200" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="50" y1="200" x2="42" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="68" y1="200" x2="60" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="86" y1="200" x2="78" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="104" y1="200" x2="96" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="122" y1="200" x2="114" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="140" y1="200" x2="132" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="158" y1="200" x2="150" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="176" y1="200" x2="168" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="194" y1="200" x2="186" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="212" y1="200" x2="204" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="230" y1="200" x2="222" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="248" y1="200" x2="240" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="266" y1="200" x2="258" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="284" y1="200" x2="276" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="302" y1="200" x2="294" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="320" y1="200" x2="312" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="338" y1="200" x2="330" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="356" y1="200" x2="348" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="374" y1="200" x2="366" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="392" y1="200" x2="384" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="410" y1="200" x2="402" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="428" y1="200" x2="420" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="446" y1="200" x2="438" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="464" y1="200" x2="456" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="482" y1="200" x2="474" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="500" y1="200" x2="492" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="518" y1="200" x2="510" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="536" y1="200" x2="528" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="554" y1="200" x2="546" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="572" y1="200" x2="564" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="590" y1="200" x2="582" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="608" y1="200" x2="600" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="626" y1="200" x2="618" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="644" y1="200" x2="636" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="662" y1="200" x2="654" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="680" y1="200" x2="672" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="698" y1="200" x2="690" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="716" y1="200" x2="708" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="734" y1="200" x2="726" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="752" y1="200" x2="744" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="770" y1="200" x2="762" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="788" y1="200" x2="780" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <rect x="80" y="120" width="140" height="70" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="98" cy="195" r="5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="202" cy="195" r="5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="150.0" y="160.0" text-anchor="middle">cart 200 kg</text>\n    <line x1="220" y1="160" x2="300" y2="160" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <rect x="300" y="110" width="110" height="60" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="355" y="145" text-anchor="middle">horse</text>\n    <line x1="320" y1="170" x2="320" y2="200" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="390" y1="170" x2="390" y2="200" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="40" y="230">horse\'s pull on the cart 600 N; road friction on the cart 200 N</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-ANC-HORSECART-V1" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="470" y="60" font-weight="600">The cart\'s diagram (horizontal)</text>\n    <rect x="580" y="90" width="90" height="50" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="625" y="120" text-anchor="middle">cart</text>\n    <line x1="670" y1="115" x2="780" y2="115" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-HORSE-CART-600N-1--rep-nlm-anc-horsecart-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="676" y="158">horse on cart 600 N</text>\n    <line x1="580" y1="115" x2="500" y2="115" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-HORSE-CART-600N-1--rep-nlm-anc-horsecart-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="574" y="158" text-anchor="end">road on cart 200 N</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-ANC-HORSECART-V2" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="40" y="268" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">F_net = 600 − 200 = 400 N, so a = 400/200 = 2 m/s²</text>\n    <text x="40" y="294">The cart\'s pull on the horse acts on the horse:</text>\n    <text x="40" y="316">it belongs on the horse\'s diagram, not in this sum.</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n<div class="g9-stage-controls"><div class="g9-stage-chips" role="group" aria-label="Stages"><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="0" data-g9-stage-desc="Show the horse, the cart and the two given forces.">1: Horse and cart</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="1" data-g9-stage-desc="Draw only the forces on the cart.">2: The cart&#x27;s diagram</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="2" data-g9-stage-desc="Sum the cart&#x27;s forces and place the partner force.">3: Net force and the partner</button></div><p class="g9-stage-desc" data-g9-stage-desc-text></p><div class="g9-stage-stepper"><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="prev">Previous stage</button><span data-g9-stage-label></span><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="next">Next stage</button></div></div><figcaption data-g9-block="representation_bridge" data-g9-caption="purpose">Show the horse and the 200 kg cart, then the cart&#x27;s own diagram with 600 N and 200 N and its acceleration.</figcaption></figure><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="governing_relation"><h4>Governing relation</h4><div class="g9-relation"><div class="g9-math" data-g9-math="mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mtext>net</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow></math></div><p>The vector net external force on a chosen body equals its mass times its acceleration.</p><ul><li>m &gt; 0.</li><li>F_net and a refer to the same chosen body.</li><li>All signed components use one declared axis.</li></ul></div></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="compact_anchor"><h4>Compact anchor</h4><p>A horse pulls a 200 kg cart with 600 N and road friction on the cart is 200 N. What is the cart&#x27;s acceleration, and where does the cart&#x27;s pull on the horse belong?</p><p>On the cart&#x27;s diagram only: F_net = 600 − 200 = 400 N, so a = 400/200 = 2 m/s². The cart-on-horse partner belongs on the horse&#x27;s diagram, not in this sum.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_prompt"><h4>Before you go on</h4><p>A person pushes a box. Describe the two contact-force partner arrows and state which one belongs on the box&#x27;s free-body diagram.</p></div></section></article><article id="g9-CORE1--MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1" data-g9-search-text="Physics Laws of Motion An accelerating observer needs a declared pseudo-force convention Hard concept An accelerating observer needs a declared pseudo-force convention"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="scope"><h2>An accelerating observer needs a declared pseudo-force convention</h2></div><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-NLM-FIRST-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Laws of Motion"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Laws of Motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE" data-g9-meta-value="An accelerating observer needs a declared pseudo-force convention"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>An accelerating observer needs a declared pseudo-force convention</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE" data-g9-meta-value="HARD"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Hard concept</span></span></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-orientation" data-blueprint-slot="orientation" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="hard_transition"><h4>Hard transition</h4><p>A force-based account depends on the observer: a non-accelerating observer uses the physical interaction forces, while an accelerating observer must add the corresponding pseudo-force convention to describe the same observed motion consistently.</p></div><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-VAN-BAG-2KG" data-g9-stage="TEACHING" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-ANCHOR-VAN-BAG-2KG" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="3" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-ANC-VANBAG-V0 VIS-NLM-ANC-VANBAG-V1 VIS-NLM-ANC-VANBAG-V2" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 840 320" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-VAN-BAG-2KG-1--rep-nlm-anc-vanbag-title g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-VAN-BAG-2KG-1--rep-nlm-anc-vanbag-desc">\n  <title id="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-VAN-BAG-2KG-1--rep-nlm-anc-vanbag-title">A 2 kg bag on the floor of a van accelerating forward at 3 m/s²</title>\n  <desc id="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-VAN-BAG-2KG-1--rep-nlm-anc-vanbag-desc">Stage 1: a 2 kg bag sits on the floor of a van that accelerates forward at 3 m/s². Stage 2: an observer riding in the van assigns the bag F_pseudo = −m a_frame = −2 × 3 = −6 N, that is 6 N backward. Stage 3: the pseudo-force is used only in the van\'s description and is never paired with a physical force.</desc>\n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-VAN-BAG-2KG-1--rep-nlm-anc-vanbag-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-VAN-BAG-2KG-1--rep-nlm-anc-vanbag-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-ANC-VANBAG-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">A 2 kg bag on the floor of a van accelerating forward at 3 m/s²</text>\n    <line x1="40" y1="240" x2="800" y2="240" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="50" y1="240" x2="42" y2="248" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="68" y1="240" x2="60" y2="248" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="86" y1="240" x2="78" y2="248" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="104" y1="240" x2="96" y2="248" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="122" y1="240" x2="114" y2="248" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="140" y1="240" x2="132" y2="248" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="158" y1="240" x2="150" y2="248" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="176" y1="240" x2="168" y2="248" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="194" y1="240" x2="186" y2="248" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="212" y1="240" x2="204" y2="248" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="230" y1="240" x2="222" y2="248" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="248" y1="240" x2="240" y2="248" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="266" y1="240" x2="258" y2="248" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="284" y1="240" x2="276" y2="248" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="302" y1="240" x2="294" y2="248" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="320" y1="240" x2="312" y2="248" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="338" y1="240" x2="330" y2="248" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="356" y1="240" x2="348" y2="248" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="374" y1="240" x2="366" y2="248" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="392" y1="240" x2="384" y2="248" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="410" y1="240" x2="402" y2="248" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="428" y1="240" x2="420" y2="248" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="446" y1="240" x2="438" y2="248" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="464" y1="240" x2="456" y2="248" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="482" y1="240" x2="474" y2="248" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="500" y1="240" x2="492" y2="248" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="518" y1="240" x2="510" y2="248" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="536" y1="240" x2="528" y2="248" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="554" y1="240" x2="546" y2="248" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="572" y1="240" x2="564" y2="248" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="590" y1="240" x2="582" y2="248" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="608" y1="240" x2="600" y2="248" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="626" y1="240" x2="618" y2="248" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="644" y1="240" x2="636" y2="248" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="662" y1="240" x2="654" y2="248" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="680" y1="240" x2="672" y2="248" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="698" y1="240" x2="690" y2="248" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="716" y1="240" x2="708" y2="248" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="734" y1="240" x2="726" y2="248" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="752" y1="240" x2="744" y2="248" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="770" y1="240" x2="762" y2="248" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="788" y1="240" x2="780" y2="248" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <rect x="120" y="100" width="340" height="120" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="460" y1="140" x2="540" y2="170" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="540" y1="170" x2="540" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="460" y1="220" x2="540" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="180" cy="228" r="12" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="470" cy="228" r="12" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <rect x="240" y="180" width="90" height="40" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="285" y="206" text-anchor="middle">bag 2 kg</text>\n    <line x1="560" y1="80" x2="660" y2="80" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-VAN-BAG-2KG-1--rep-nlm-anc-vanbag-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="560" y="70">a_frame = 3 m/s² forward</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-ANC-VANBAG-V1" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <line x1="240" y1="200" x2="160" y2="200" stroke="#1f6fb2" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-VAN-BAG-2KG-1--rep-nlm-anc-vanbag-arrow-acc)"/>\n    <text x="135" y="172" fill="#1f6fb2">F_pseudo = 6 N backward</text>\n    <text x="580" y="130" font-weight="600">In the van\'s frame:</text>\n    <text x="580" y="152">F_pseudo = −m a_frame</text>\n    <text x="580" y="174">= −2 × 3 = −6 N</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-ANC-VANBAG-V2" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="40" y="282" fill="#1f6fb2">Used only in the van\'s description; it is never paired with a physical force.</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n<div class="g9-stage-controls"><div class="g9-stage-chips" role="group" aria-label="Stages"><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="0" data-g9-stage-desc="Show the van, the bag and the van&#x27;s acceleration.">1: Bag in the van</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="1" data-g9-stage-desc="Add −m a_frame to the bag in the van&#x27;s frame.">2: The van observer&#x27;s pseudo-force</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="2" data-g9-stage-desc="Mark that the pseudo-force has no partner.">3: Only in the van&#x27;s frame</button></div><p class="g9-stage-desc" data-g9-stage-desc-text></p><div class="g9-stage-stepper"><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="prev">Previous stage</button><span data-g9-stage-label></span><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="next">Next stage</button></div></div><figcaption data-g9-block="representation_bridge" data-g9-caption="purpose">Show the 2 kg bag in the van accelerating at 3 m/s², then the 6 N backward pseudo-force the van observer assigns.</figcaption></figure><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="governing_relation"><h4>Governing relation</h4><div class="g9-relation"><div class="g9-math" data-g9-math="mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></math></div><p>For a person that remains in contact with a horizontal scale in an inertial ground frame, with upward positive and g the positive magnitude of gravitational acceleration, the signed vertical force balance is N - m g = m a_y.</p><ul><li>The ground frame is treated as inertial and the positive vertical direction is upward.</li><li>The person remains in contact with the scale, so N &gt;= 0.</li><li>The person and scale share the stated vertical acceleration while contact persists.</li><li>g is the positive magnitude of the downward gravitational acceleration.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-relation"><div class="g9-math" data-g9-math="mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mtext>pseudo</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mtext>frame</mtext></msub></mrow></math></div><p>In a one-axis frame accelerating with signed acceleration a_frame relative to an inertial frame, the pseudo-force assigned to a mass m is opposite the frame acceleration and has magnitude m|a_frame|.</p><ul><li>The selected observer frame has nonzero translational acceleration relative to the inertial frame.</li><li>F_pseudo and a_frame use the same declared axis and sign convention.</li><li>The mass is positive and treated as constant.</li><li>The pseudo-force is a frame-dependent modelling term, not a physical interaction and not a Newton-third-law partner.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-relation"><div class="g9-math" data-g9-math="mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">tan</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mtext>frame</mtext></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow><mi>g</mi></mfrac></mrow></math></div><p>For a bob stationary relative to a horizontally accelerating vehicle, with a massless taut string and no other horizontal interaction, the equilibrium string tilt magnitude theta from vertical satisfies tan(theta)=|a_frame|/g in the vehicle frame.</p><ul><li>The bob is stationary relative to the vehicle after transients, so its relative acceleration is zero.</li><li>The vehicle has constant horizontal acceleration over the represented state.</li><li>The string is massless and taut, and air resistance is neglected.</li><li>theta is measured from the vertical; the bob tilts opposite the vehicle acceleration.</li></ul></div></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="compact_anchor"><h4>Compact anchor</h4><p>A 2 kg bag sits on the floor of a van that accelerates forward at 3 m/s². What pseudo-force does an observer riding in the van assign to the bag?</p><p>F_pseudo = −m a_frame = −2 × 3 = −6 N: 6 N backward, used only in the van&#x27;s description and never paired with a physical force.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_prompt"><h4>Before you go on</h4><p>A bus accelerates forward and a loose object appears to move backward relative to the bus. Compare the roadside and bus-observer force descriptions without calculating any magnitude.</p></div></section></article><article id="g9-CORE1--MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1" data-g9-search-text="Physics Laws of Motion Net force, not any one force, sets acceleration Medium concept Net force, not any one force, sets acceleration"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="scope"><h2>Net force, not any one force, sets acceleration</h2></div><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-NLM-FIRST-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Laws of Motion"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Laws of Motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Net force, not any one force, sets acceleration"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Net force, not any one force, sets acceleration</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-orientation" data-blueprint-slot="orientation" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="hard_transition"><h4>Hard transition</h4><p>For a chosen body of positive mass in the Grade 9 inertial-frame model, acceleration points with the signed net external force and satisfies F_net = m a.</p></div><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-TROLLEY-5KG-20N-5N" data-g9-stage="TEACHING" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-ANCHOR-TROLLEY-5KG-20N-5N" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="3" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-ANC-TROLLEY5-V0 VIS-NLM-ANC-TROLLEY5-V1 VIS-NLM-ANC-TROLLEY5-V2" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 840 300" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-TROLLEY-5KG-20N-5N-1--rep-nlm-anc-trolley5-title g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-TROLLEY-5KG-20N-5N-1--rep-nlm-anc-trolley5-desc">\n  <title id="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-TROLLEY-5KG-20N-5N-1--rep-nlm-anc-trolley5-title">A 5 kg trolley with 20 N forward and 5 N backward on it</title>\n  <desc id="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-TROLLEY-5KG-20N-5N-1--rep-nlm-anc-trolley5-desc">Stage 1: a 5 kg trolley has 20 N forward and 5 N backward acting on it. Stage 2: F_net = 20 − 5 = 15 N forward. Stage 3: a = 15/5 = 3 m/s² forward, in the direction of the net force.</desc>\n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-TROLLEY-5KG-20N-5N-1--rep-nlm-anc-trolley5-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-TROLLEY-5KG-20N-5N-1--rep-nlm-anc-trolley5-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-ANC-TROLLEY5-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">A 5 kg trolley with 20 N forward and 5 N backward on it</text>\n    <line x1="160" y1="200" x2="680" y2="200" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="170" y1="200" x2="162" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="188" y1="200" x2="180" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="206" y1="200" x2="198" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="224" y1="200" x2="216" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="242" y1="200" x2="234" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="260" y1="200" x2="252" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="278" y1="200" x2="270" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="296" y1="200" x2="288" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="314" y1="200" x2="306" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="332" y1="200" x2="324" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="350" y1="200" x2="342" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="368" y1="200" x2="360" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="386" y1="200" x2="378" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="404" y1="200" x2="396" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="422" y1="200" x2="414" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="440" y1="200" x2="432" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="458" y1="200" x2="450" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="476" y1="200" x2="468" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="494" y1="200" x2="486" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="512" y1="200" x2="504" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="530" y1="200" x2="522" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="548" y1="200" x2="540" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="566" y1="200" x2="558" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="584" y1="200" x2="576" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="602" y1="200" x2="594" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="620" y1="200" x2="612" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="638" y1="200" x2="630" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="656" y1="200" x2="648" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="674" y1="200" x2="666" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <rect x="360" y="130" width="120" height="60" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="378" cy="195" r="5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="462" cy="195" r="5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="420.0" y="165.0" text-anchor="middle">trolley 5 kg</text>\n    <line x1="480" y1="160" x2="620" y2="160" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-TROLLEY-5KG-20N-5N-1--rep-nlm-anc-trolley5-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="630" y="165">20 N forward</text>\n    <line x1="360" y1="160" x2="300" y2="160" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-TROLLEY-5KG-20N-5N-1--rep-nlm-anc-trolley5-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="290" y="165" text-anchor="end">5 N backward</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-ANC-TROLLEY5-V1" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="40" y="240" font-weight="600">F_net = 20 − 5 = 15 N forward</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-ANC-TROLLEY5-V2" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <line x1="390" y1="80" x2="470" y2="80" stroke="#1f6fb2" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-TROLLEY-5KG-20N-5N-1--rep-nlm-anc-trolley5-arrow-acc)"/>\n    <text x="480" y="85" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">a = 15/5 = 3 m/s² forward</text>\n    <text x="40" y="270" fill="#1f6fb2">the acceleration points along the net force</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n<div class="g9-stage-controls"><div class="g9-stage-chips" role="group" aria-label="Stages"><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="0" data-g9-stage-desc="Show both forces on the trolley.">1: The trolley and its forces</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="1" data-g9-stage-desc="Add the forces with their directions.">2: Net force</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="2" data-g9-stage-desc="Divide by the mass.">3: Acceleration</button></div><p class="g9-stage-desc" data-g9-stage-desc-text></p><div class="g9-stage-stepper"><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="prev">Previous stage</button><span data-g9-stage-label></span><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="next">Next stage</button></div></div><figcaption data-g9-block="representation_bridge" data-g9-caption="purpose">Show the 5 kg trolley with 20 N forward and 5 N backward, then its net force and acceleration.</figcaption></figure><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="governing_relation"><h4>Governing relation</h4><div class="g9-relation"><div class="g9-math" data-g9-math="mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mtext>net</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow></math></div><p>The vector net external force on a chosen body equals its mass times its acceleration.</p><ul><li>m &gt; 0.</li><li>F_net and a refer to the same chosen body.</li><li>All signed components use one declared axis.</li></ul></div></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="compact_anchor"><h4>Compact anchor</h4><p>A 5 kg trolley has 20 N forward and 5 N backward acting on it. What is its acceleration?</p><p>F_net = 20 − 5 = 15 N forward, so a = 15/5 = 3 m/s² forward, in the direction of the net force.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_prompt"><h4>Before you go on</h4><p>A 4 kg body has a net external force of +12 N along a declared axis. Find its acceleration.</p></div></section></article><article id="g9-CORE1--MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1" data-g9-search-text="Physics Laws of Motion Friction opposes relative sliding at a contact Medium concept Friction opposes relative sliding at a contact"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="scope"><h2>Friction opposes relative sliding at a contact</h2></div><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-NLM-FIRST-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Laws of Motion"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Laws of Motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION" data-g9-meta-value="Friction opposes relative sliding at a contact"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Friction opposes relative sliding at a contact</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-orientation" data-blueprint-slot="orientation" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="hard_transition"><h4>Hard transition</h4><p>Friction is a contact force along the surface that opposes relative sliding, or the tendency for relative sliding, between the contacting surfaces; it is not defined by the object&#x27;s overall velocity alone.</p></div><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-BOX-2KG-PUSH-6N" data-g9-stage="TEACHING" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-ANCHOR-BOX-2KG-PUSH-6N" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="3" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-ANC-BOX2PUSH6-V0 VIS-NLM-ANC-BOX2PUSH6-V1 VIS-NLM-ANC-BOX2PUSH6-V2" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 840 300" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-BOX-2KG-PUSH-6N-1--rep-nlm-anc-box2push6-title g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-BOX-2KG-PUSH-6N-1--rep-nlm-anc-box2push6-desc">\n  <title id="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-BOX-2KG-PUSH-6N-1--rep-nlm-anc-box2push6-title">A 2 kg box on a rough floor pushed sideways with 6 N</title>\n  <desc id="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-BOX-2KG-PUSH-6N-1--rep-nlm-anc-box2push6-desc">Stage 1: a 2 kg box on a rough floor with μ_s = 0.5 and g = 10 m/s² is pushed sideways with 6 N and does not move. Stage 2: N = 20 N, so static friction can give at most μ_s N = 0.5 × 20 = 10 N. Stage 3: rest needs friction to balance the push, so friction is 6 N opposite the push, within the 10 N limit.</desc>\n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-BOX-2KG-PUSH-6N-1--rep-nlm-anc-box2push6-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-BOX-2KG-PUSH-6N-1--rep-nlm-anc-box2push6-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-ANC-BOX2PUSH6-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">A 2 kg box on a rough floor pushed sideways with 6 N</text>\n    <line x1="160" y1="200" x2="680" y2="200" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="170" y1="200" x2="162" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="188" y1="200" x2="180" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="206" y1="200" x2="198" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="224" y1="200" x2="216" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="242" y1="200" x2="234" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="260" y1="200" x2="252" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="278" y1="200" x2="270" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="296" y1="200" x2="288" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="314" y1="200" x2="306" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="332" y1="200" x2="324" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="350" y1="200" x2="342" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="368" y1="200" x2="360" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="386" y1="200" x2="378" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="404" y1="200" x2="396" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="422" y1="200" x2="414" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="440" y1="200" x2="432" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="458" y1="200" x2="450" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="476" y1="200" x2="468" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="494" y1="200" x2="486" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="512" y1="200" x2="504" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="530" y1="200" x2="522" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="548" y1="200" x2="540" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="566" y1="200" x2="558" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="584" y1="200" x2="576" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="602" y1="200" x2="594" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="620" y1="200" x2="612" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="638" y1="200" x2="630" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="656" y1="200" x2="648" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="674" y1="200" x2="666" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <rect x="360" y="130" width="120" height="70" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="420" y="170" text-anchor="middle">box 2 kg</text>\n    <line x1="250" y1="165" x2="358" y2="165" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-BOX-2KG-PUSH-6N-1--rep-nlm-anc-box2push6-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="250" y="155">push 6 N</text>\n    <text x="40" y="230">μ_s = 0.5, g = 10 m/s²; the box does not move</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-ANC-BOX2PUSH6-V1" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="560" y="70" font-weight="600">N = 20 N</text>\n    <text x="560" y="92">limit μ_s N = 0.5 × 20 = 10 N</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-ANC-BOX2PUSH6-V2" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <line x1="360" y1="196" x2="280" y2="196" stroke="#1f6fb2" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-BOX-2KG-PUSH-6N-1--rep-nlm-anc-box2push6-arrow-acc)"/>\n    <text x="270" y="190" text-anchor="end" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">friction 6 N</text>\n    <text x="40" y="262" fill="#1f6fb2">Rest needs friction to balance the push: 6 N, within the 10 N limit.</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n<div class="g9-stage-controls"><div class="g9-stage-chips" role="group" aria-label="Stages"><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="0" data-g9-stage-desc="Show the box, the push and the floor&#x27;s μ_s.">1: The pushed box</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="1" data-g9-stage-desc="Find N and the most static friction can give.">2: The limit</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="2" data-g9-stage-desc="Balance the push.">3: Friction rest needs</button></div><p class="g9-stage-desc" data-g9-stage-desc-text></p><div class="g9-stage-stepper"><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="prev">Previous stage</button><span data-g9-stage-label></span><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="next">Next stage</button></div></div><figcaption data-g9-block="representation_bridge" data-g9-caption="purpose">Show the 2 kg box pushed with 6 N at rest, then the 10 N limit and the 6 N friction rest needs.</figcaption></figure><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="governing_relation"><h4>Governing relation</h4><div class="g9-relation"><div class="g9-math" data-g9-math="mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>|</mo><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></div><p>For a contact that remains static, the static-friction magnitude adjusts to the value required by the tangential dynamics up to the limiting value mu_s N; equality applies only at impending slip.</p><ul><li>The surfaces are in contact and have no relative sliding during the state being tested.</li><li>N is obtained from the actual free-body dynamics, not assumed to equal mg.</li><li>Static friction opposes the impending relative slip direction.</li><li>Equality |f_s| = mu_s N is used only at impending slip.</li></ul></div></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="compact_anchor"><h4>Compact anchor</h4><p>A 2 kg box on a rough floor (μ_s = 0.5, g = 10 m/s²) is pushed sideways with 6 N and does not move. What friction acts on it?</p><p>6 N, opposite the push: rest needs friction to balance the push, and 6 N is within the limit μ_s N = 0.5 × 20 = 10 N.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_prompt"><h4>Before you go on</h4><p>A box is placed on a conveyor belt moving to the right faster than the box. Before the box matches the belt speed, what direction is the friction force on the box?</p></div></section></article><article id="g9-CORE1--MIC-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1" data-g9-search-text="Physics Laws of Motion Third-law partner forces act on different bodies Medium concept Third-law partner forces act on different bodies"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="scope"><h2>Third-law partner forces act on different bodies</h2></div><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-NLM-FIRST-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Laws of Motion"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Laws of Motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Third-law partner forces act on different bodies"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Third-law partner forces act on different bodies</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-orientation" data-blueprint-slot="orientation" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="hard_transition"><h4>Hard transition</h4><p>One interaction produces two partner forces of equal magnitude and opposite direction, but they act on different bodies and therefore do not cancel in a single body&#x27;s net-force sum.</p></div><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="MIC-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-SKATERS-60KG-30KG" data-g9-stage="TEACHING" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-ANCHOR-SKATERS-60KG-30KG" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="3" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-ANC-SKATERS-V0 VIS-NLM-ANC-SKATERS-V1 VIS-NLM-ANC-SKATERS-V2" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 840 350" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-SKATERS-60KG-30KG-1--rep-nlm-anc-skaters-title g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-SKATERS-60KG-30KG-1--rep-nlm-anc-skaters-desc">\n  <title id="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-SKATERS-60KG-30KG-1--rep-nlm-anc-skaters-title">On smooth ice, a 60 kg skater pushes a 30 kg skater with 90 N</title>\n  <desc id="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-SKATERS-60KG-30KG-1--rep-nlm-anc-skaters-desc">Stage 1: on smooth ice a 60 kg skater pushes a 30 kg skater with 90 N. Stage 2: each skater gets 90 N, on different bodies: forward on the 30 kg skater, backward on the 60 kg skater. Stage 3: the pushed skater has a = 90/30 = 3 m/s² forward and the pusher a = 90/60 = 1.5 m/s² backward; the pair never cancels, because each force is in a different body\'s net force.</desc>\n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-SKATERS-60KG-30KG-1--rep-nlm-anc-skaters-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-SKATERS-60KG-30KG-1--rep-nlm-anc-skaters-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-ANC-SKATERS-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">On smooth ice, a 60 kg skater pushes a 30 kg skater with 90 N</text>\n    <line x1="80" y1="212" x2="760" y2="212" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="90" y1="212" x2="82" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="108" y1="212" x2="100" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="126" y1="212" x2="118" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="144" y1="212" x2="136" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="162" y1="212" x2="154" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="180" y1="212" x2="172" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="198" y1="212" x2="190" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="216" y1="212" x2="208" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="234" y1="212" x2="226" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="252" y1="212" x2="244" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="270" y1="212" x2="262" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="288" y1="212" x2="280" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="306" y1="212" x2="298" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="324" y1="212" x2="316" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="342" y1="212" x2="334" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="360" y1="212" x2="352" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="378" y1="212" x2="370" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="396" y1="212" x2="388" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="414" y1="212" x2="406" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="432" y1="212" x2="424" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="450" y1="212" x2="442" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="468" y1="212" x2="460" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="486" y1="212" x2="478" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="504" y1="212" x2="496" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="522" y1="212" x2="514" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="540" y1="212" x2="532" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="558" y1="212" x2="550" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="576" y1="212" x2="568" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="594" y1="212" x2="586" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="612" y1="212" x2="604" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="630" y1="212" x2="622" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="648" y1="212" x2="640" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="666" y1="212" x2="658" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="684" y1="212" x2="676" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="702" y1="212" x2="694" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="720" y1="212" x2="712" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="738" y1="212" x2="730" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="756" y1="212" x2="748" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <circle cx="300" cy="110" r="10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="120" x2="300" y2="170" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="170" x2="288" y2="210" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="170" x2="312" y2="210" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="300" y="240" text-anchor="middle">skater 60 kg</text>\n    <circle cx="480" cy="110" r="10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="480" y1="120" x2="480" y2="170" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="480" y1="170" x2="468" y2="210" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="480" y1="170" x2="492" y2="210" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="480" y="240" text-anchor="middle">skater 30 kg</text>\n    <line x1="300" y1="140" x2="470" y2="140" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="390" y="132" text-anchor="middle">push 90 N</text>\n    <text x="80" y="268">smooth ice</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-ANC-SKATERS-V1" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <line x1="490" y1="150" x2="580" y2="150" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-SKATERS-60KG-30KG-1--rep-nlm-anc-skaters-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="590" y="155">90 N on the 30 kg skater</text>\n    <line x1="290" y1="150" x2="200" y2="150" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-SKATERS-60KG-30KG-1--rep-nlm-anc-skaters-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="190" y="155" text-anchor="end">90 N on the 60 kg skater</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-ANC-SKATERS-V2" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="40" y="290" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">30 kg skater: a = 90/30 = 3 m/s² forward</text>\n    <text x="40" y="312" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">60 kg skater: a = 90/60 = 1.5 m/s² backward</text>\n    <text x="40" y="334" fill="#1f6fb2">The pair never cancels: each force is in a different body\'s F_net.</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n<div class="g9-stage-controls"><div class="g9-stage-chips" role="group" aria-label="Stages"><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="0" data-g9-stage-desc="Show the skaters, their masses and the push.">1: The two skaters</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="1" data-g9-stage-desc="Place each force on its own body.">2: The pair</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="2" data-g9-stage-desc="Divide each force by its own body&#x27;s mass.">3: Each acceleration</button></div><p class="g9-stage-desc" data-g9-stage-desc-text></p><div class="g9-stage-stepper"><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="prev">Previous stage</button><span data-g9-stage-label></span><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="next">Next stage</button></div></div><figcaption data-g9-block="representation_bridge" data-g9-caption="purpose">Show the two skaters and the 90 N pair, one force on each body, then each skater&#x27;s acceleration.</figcaption></figure><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="governing_relation"><h4>Governing relation</h4><div class="g9-relation"><div class="g9-math" data-g9-math="mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mtext>net</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow></math></div><p>The vector net external force on a chosen body equals its mass times its acceleration.</p><ul><li>m &gt; 0.</li><li>F_net and a refer to the same chosen body.</li><li>All signed components use one declared axis.</li></ul></div></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="compact_anchor"><h4>Compact anchor</h4><p>On smooth ice a 60 kg skater pushes a 30 kg skater with 90 N. What are their accelerations?</p><p>Each gets 90 N, on different bodies: the pushed skater has a = 90/30 = 3 m/s² forward, the pusher a = 90/60 = 1.5 m/s² backward. The pair never cancels, because each force is in a different body&#x27;s F_net.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_prompt"><h4>Before you go on</h4><p>A swimmer pushes water backward. Identify the third-law partner force and state which body each force acts on.</p></div></section></article><article id="g9-CORE1--MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1" data-g9-search-text="Physics Laws of Motion Quantitative static and kinetic friction conditions Hard concept Quantitative static and kinetic friction conditions"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="scope"><h2>Quantitative static and kinetic friction conditions</h2></div><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-NLM-FIRST-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Laws of Motion"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Laws of Motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT" data-g9-meta-value="Quantitative static and kinetic friction conditions"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Quantitative static and kinetic friction conditions</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT" data-g9-meta-value="HARD"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Hard concept</span></span></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-orientation" data-blueprint-slot="orientation" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="hard_transition"><h4>Hard transition</h4><p>Static friction is a responsive contact force: solve for the friction required to prevent relative slip and compare it with the limiting value mu_s N. Equality is a threshold condition, while the kinetic model is used only after sliding is established.</p></div><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-BLOCK-2KG-PULL-14N" data-g9-stage="TEACHING" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-ANCHOR-BLOCK-2KG-PULL-14N" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="3" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-ANC-BLOCK2PULL14-V0 VIS-NLM-ANC-BLOCK2PULL14-V1 VIS-NLM-ANC-BLOCK2PULL14-V2" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 840 330" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-BLOCK-2KG-PULL-14N-1--rep-nlm-anc-block2pull14-title g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-BLOCK-2KG-PULL-14N-1--rep-nlm-anc-block2pull14-desc">\n  <title id="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-BLOCK-2KG-PULL-14N-1--rep-nlm-anc-block2pull14-title">A 2 kg block on a floor pulled horizontally with 14 N</title>\n  <desc id="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-BLOCK-2KG-PULL-14N-1--rep-nlm-anc-block2pull14-desc">Stage 1: a 2 kg block on a floor with μ_s = 0.5 and μ_k = 0.3 is pulled horizontally with 14 N; g = 10 m/s². Stage 2: N = 20 N, so static friction can give at most 10 N; 14 N is more than 10 N, so the block slides. Stage 3: kinetic friction f_k = 0.3 × 20 = 6 N acts against the motion, and a = (14 − 6)/2 = 4 m/s².</desc>\n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-BLOCK-2KG-PULL-14N-1--rep-nlm-anc-block2pull14-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-BLOCK-2KG-PULL-14N-1--rep-nlm-anc-block2pull14-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-ANC-BLOCK2PULL14-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">A 2 kg block on a floor pulled horizontally with 14 N</text>\n    <line x1="160" y1="200" x2="680" y2="200" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="170" y1="200" x2="162" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="188" y1="200" x2="180" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="206" y1="200" x2="198" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="224" y1="200" x2="216" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="242" y1="200" x2="234" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="260" y1="200" x2="252" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="278" y1="200" x2="270" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="296" y1="200" x2="288" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="314" y1="200" x2="306" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="332" y1="200" x2="324" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="350" y1="200" x2="342" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="368" y1="200" x2="360" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="386" y1="200" x2="378" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="404" y1="200" x2="396" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="422" y1="200" x2="414" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="440" y1="200" x2="432" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="458" y1="200" x2="450" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="476" y1="200" x2="468" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="494" y1="200" x2="486" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="512" y1="200" x2="504" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="530" y1="200" x2="522" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="548" y1="200" x2="540" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="566" y1="200" x2="558" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="584" y1="200" x2="576" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="602" y1="200" x2="594" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="620" y1="200" x2="612" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="638" y1="200" x2="630" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="656" y1="200" x2="648" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="674" y1="200" x2="666" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <rect x="320" y="130" width="120" height="70" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="380" y="170" text-anchor="middle">block 2 kg</text>\n    <line x1="440" y1="165" x2="560" y2="165" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-BLOCK-2KG-PULL-14N-1--rep-nlm-anc-block2pull14-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="570" y="170">pull 14 N</text>\n    <text x="40" y="230">μ_s = 0.5, μ_k = 0.3, g = 10 m/s²</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-ANC-BLOCK2PULL14-V1" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="40" y="262" font-weight="600">N = 20 N: static friction can give at most 10 N; 14 N &gt; 10 N, so it slides</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-ANC-BLOCK2PULL14-V2" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <line x1="320" y1="196" x2="240" y2="196" stroke="#1f6fb2" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-BLOCK-2KG-PULL-14N-1--rep-nlm-anc-block2pull14-arrow-acc)"/>\n    <text x="230" y="190" text-anchor="end" fill="#1f6fb2">f_k = 0.3 × 20 = 6 N</text>\n    <text x="40" y="294" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">a = (14 − 6)/2 = 4 m/s²</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n<div class="g9-stage-controls"><div class="g9-stage-chips" role="group" aria-label="Stages"><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="0" data-g9-stage-desc="Show the block, the pull and both coefficients.">1: The pulled block</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="1" data-g9-stage-desc="Compare the pull with the most static friction can give.">2: Static limit</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="2" data-g9-stage-desc="Use kinetic friction for the acceleration.">3: Sliding</button></div><p class="g9-stage-desc" data-g9-stage-desc-text></p><div class="g9-stage-stepper"><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="prev">Previous stage</button><span data-g9-stage-label></span><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="next">Next stage</button></div></div><figcaption data-g9-block="representation_bridge" data-g9-caption="purpose">Show the 2 kg block pulled with 14 N against a 10 N static limit, then the 6 N kinetic friction and its acceleration.</figcaption></figure><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="governing_relation"><h4>Governing relation</h4><div class="g9-relation"><div class="g9-math" data-g9-math="mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>|</mo><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></div><p>For a contact that remains static, the static-friction magnitude adjusts to the value required by the tangential dynamics up to the limiting value mu_s N; equality applies only at impending slip.</p><ul><li>The surfaces are in contact and have no relative sliding during the state being tested.</li><li>N is obtained from the actual free-body dynamics, not assumed to equal mg.</li><li>Static friction opposes the impending relative slip direction.</li><li>Equality |f_s| = mu_s N is used only at impending slip.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-relation"><div class="g9-math" data-g9-math="mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>|</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></div><p>Once relative sliding at the contact is established, the bounded dry-sliding model uses kinetic-friction magnitude mu_k N, directed opposite the relative sliding.</p><ul><li>Relative sliding between the two surfaces is established.</li><li>N is obtained from the actual free-body dynamics.</li><li>The friction direction opposes the relative sliding at the contact.</li><li>The simple dry-sliding coefficient model is being used.</li></ul></div></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="compact_anchor"><h4>Compact anchor</h4><p>A 2 kg block on a floor with μ_s = 0.5 and μ_k = 0.3 is pulled horizontally with 14 N (g = 10 m/s²). Does it stay put, and what is its acceleration?</p><p>N = 20 N, so static friction can give at most 10 N; 14 N &gt; 10 N, so it slides. Then f_k = 0.3 × 20 = 6 N and a = (14 − 6)/2 = 4 m/s².</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_prompt"><h4>Before you go on</h4><p>A contact has known normal reaction N and coefficient mu_s. The no-slip force equation requires tangential friction f_required. State the test for whether static contact is possible, when equality with mu_s N is justified, and what changes once sliding is established.</p></div></section></article><article id="g9-CORE1--MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1" data-g9-search-text="Physics Laws of Motion Connected bodies and a justified common acceleration Hard concept Connected bodies and a justified common acceleration"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="scope"><h2>Connected bodies and a justified common acceleration</h2></div><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-NLM-FIRST-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Laws of Motion"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Laws of Motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL" data-g9-meta-value="Connected bodies and a justified common acceleration"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Connected bodies and a justified common acceleration</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL" data-g9-meta-value="HARD"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Hard concept</span></span></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-orientation" data-blueprint-slot="orientation" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="hard_transition"><h4>Hard transition</h4><p>Bodies share an acceleration component only when a physical constraint keeps their relative separation fixed along that coordinate; the shared acceleration can link separate Newton-II equations, while internal partner forces cancel only if the bodies are deliberately treated as one system.</p></div><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-BLOCKS-4KG-2KG-30N" data-g9-stage="TEACHING" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-ANCHOR-BLOCKS-4KG-2KG-30N" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="3" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-ANC-BLOCKS42-V0 VIS-NLM-ANC-BLOCKS42-V1 VIS-NLM-ANC-BLOCKS42-V2" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 840 280" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-BLOCKS-4KG-2KG-30N-1--rep-nlm-anc-blocks42-title g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-BLOCKS-4KG-2KG-30N-1--rep-nlm-anc-blocks42-desc">\n  <title id="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-BLOCKS-4KG-2KG-30N-1--rep-nlm-anc-blocks42-title">A 30 N push on a 4 kg block pressing on a 2 kg block, on a smooth floor</title>\n  <desc id="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-BLOCKS-4KG-2KG-30N-1--rep-nlm-anc-blocks42-desc">Stage 1: a 30 N push moves a 4 kg block and a 2 kg block together across a smooth floor, the first pressing on the second. Stage 2: the maintained contact fixes their separation, so both blocks share one acceleration. Stage 3: a = 30/(4 + 2) = 5 m/s² for both blocks.</desc>\n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-BLOCKS-4KG-2KG-30N-1--rep-nlm-anc-blocks42-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-BLOCKS-4KG-2KG-30N-1--rep-nlm-anc-blocks42-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-ANC-BLOCKS42-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">A 30 N push on a 4 kg block pressing on a 2 kg block, on a smooth floor</text>\n    <line x1="120" y1="200" x2="700" y2="200" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="130" y1="200" x2="122" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="148" y1="200" x2="140" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="166" y1="200" x2="158" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="184" y1="200" x2="176" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="202" y1="200" x2="194" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="220" y1="200" x2="212" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="238" y1="200" x2="230" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="256" y1="200" x2="248" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="274" y1="200" x2="266" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="292" y1="200" x2="284" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="310" y1="200" x2="302" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="328" y1="200" x2="320" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="346" y1="200" x2="338" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="364" y1="200" x2="356" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="382" y1="200" x2="374" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="400" y1="200" x2="392" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="418" y1="200" x2="410" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="436" y1="200" x2="428" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="454" y1="200" x2="446" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="472" y1="200" x2="464" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="490" y1="200" x2="482" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="508" y1="200" x2="500" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="526" y1="200" x2="518" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="544" y1="200" x2="536" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="562" y1="200" x2="554" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="580" y1="200" x2="572" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="598" y1="200" x2="590" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="616" y1="200" x2="608" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="634" y1="200" x2="626" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="652" y1="200" x2="644" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="670" y1="200" x2="662" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="688" y1="200" x2="680" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <rect x="300" y="110" width="120" height="90" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="360" y="160" text-anchor="middle">4 kg</text>\n    <rect x="420" y="140" width="80" height="60" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="460" y="175" text-anchor="middle">2 kg</text>\n    <line x1="200" y1="155" x2="298" y2="155" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-BLOCKS-4KG-2KG-30N-1--rep-nlm-anc-blocks42-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="200" y="145">push 30 N</text>\n    <text x="120" y="230">smooth floor</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-ANC-BLOCKS42-V1" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="540" y="80" font-weight="600">contact maintained:</text>\n    <text x="540" y="102">one shared a for both blocks</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-ANC-BLOCKS42-V2" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <line x1="330" y1="90" x2="430" y2="90" stroke="#1f6fb2" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-BLOCKS-4KG-2KG-30N-1--rep-nlm-anc-blocks42-arrow-acc)"/>\n    <text x="438" y="95" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">a</text>\n    <text x="40" y="262" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">a = 30/(4 + 2) = 5 m/s² for both blocks</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n<div class="g9-stage-controls"><div class="g9-stage-chips" role="group" aria-label="Stages"><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="0" data-g9-stage-desc="Show the two blocks in contact and the push.">1: The pushed blocks</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="1" data-g9-stage-desc="State the constraint from the maintained contact.">2: Contact kept</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="2" data-g9-stage-desc="Apply the push to the combined mass.">3: Shared acceleration</button></div><p class="g9-stage-desc" data-g9-stage-desc-text></p><div class="g9-stage-stepper"><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="prev">Previous stage</button><span data-g9-stage-label></span><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="next">Next stage</button></div></div><figcaption data-g9-block="representation_bridge" data-g9-caption="purpose">Show the 4 kg and 2 kg blocks pushed together with 30 N, then their one shared acceleration.</figcaption></figure><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="governing_relation"><h4>Governing relation</h4><div class="g9-relation"><div class="g9-math" data-g9-math="mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow></math></div><p>When an active connection keeps the relative separation of bodies A and B fixed along one shared coordinate and both use the same positive sense, their acceleration components along that coordinate are equal.</p><ul><li>The connection/contact actively keeps the relevant relative separation fixed.</li><li>Both acceleration components use the same coordinate direction and sign convention.</li><li>The constraint has not gone slack, separated, or otherwise ceased to apply.</li></ul></div></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="compact_anchor"><h4>Compact anchor</h4><p>A 30 N push moves a 4 kg block and a 2 kg block together across a smooth floor, the first pressing on the second. What is their shared acceleration?</p><p>The maintained contact fixes their separation, so a_A = a_B = 30/(4 + 2) = 5 m/s².</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_prompt"><h4>Before you go on</h4><p>Two bodies are constrained to keep a fixed separation along one straight coordinate. State the acceleration relation, write the form of the two body equations, and explain when their interaction force can disappear from an equation.</p></div></section></article><article id="g9-CORE1--MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1" data-g9-search-text="Physics Laws of Motion Ideal-string tension belongs to each body-specific FBD Hard concept Ideal-string tension belongs to each body-specific FBD"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="scope"><h2>Ideal-string tension belongs to each body-specific FBD</h2></div><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-NLM-FIRST-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Laws of Motion"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Laws of Motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION" data-g9-meta-value="Ideal-string tension belongs to each body-specific FBD"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Ideal-string tension belongs to each body-specific FBD</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION" data-g9-meta-value="HARD"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Hard concept</span></span></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-orientation" data-blueprint-slot="orientation" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="hard_transition"><h4>Hard transition</h4><p>For one taut massless ideal string, with any redirection treated as frictionless and massless, the tension magnitude can be represented by one T throughout that same string; each body still receives its own tension force along the local string direction.</p></div><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-PULLEY-STRING-8N" data-g9-stage="TEACHING" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-ANCHOR-PULLEY-STRING-8N" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="3" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-ANC-STRING8-V0 VIS-NLM-ANC-STRING8-V1 VIS-NLM-ANC-STRING8-V2" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 840 340" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-PULLEY-STRING-8N-1--rep-nlm-anc-string8-title g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-PULLEY-STRING-8N-1--rep-nlm-anc-string8-desc">\n  <title id="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-PULLEY-STRING-8N-1--rep-nlm-anc-string8-title">A light string over a frictionless, light pulley joins two bodies</title>\n  <desc id="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-PULLEY-STRING-8N-1--rep-nlm-anc-string8-desc">Stage 1: a light string over a frictionless, light pulley joins two bodies, A and B; the string pulls A with 8 N. Stage 2: under the ideal model T_A = T_B = T. Stage 3: the string pulls B with 8 N too; each pull acts on its own body along its own string segment.</desc>\n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-PULLEY-STRING-8N-1--rep-nlm-anc-string8-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-PULLEY-STRING-8N-1--rep-nlm-anc-string8-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-ANC-STRING8-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">A light string over a frictionless, light pulley joins two bodies</text>\n    <line x1="200" y1="50" x2="440" y2="50" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="3"/>\n    <line x1="320" y1="50" x2="320" y2="80" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="320" cy="120" r="40" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="280" y1="120" x2="280" y2="230" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="360" y1="120" x2="360" y2="190" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <rect x="250" y="230" width="60" height="50" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="280" y="260" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="600">A</text>\n    <rect x="330" y="190" width="60" height="50" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="360" y="220" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="600">B</text>\n    <line x1="280" y1="228" x2="280" y2="176" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-PULLEY-STRING-8N-1--rep-nlm-anc-string8-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="272" y="196" text-anchor="end">T_A = 8 N</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-ANC-STRING8-V1" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="480" y="90" font-weight="600">ideal model:</text>\n    <text x="480" y="112">T_A = T_B = T</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-ANC-STRING8-V2" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <line x1="360" y1="188" x2="360" y2="164" stroke="#1f6fb2" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-PULLEY-STRING-8N-1--rep-nlm-anc-string8-arrow-acc)"/>\n    <text x="368" y="176" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">T_B = 8 N</text>\n    <text x="40" y="316" fill="#1f6fb2">Each pull acts on its own body, along its own string segment.</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n<div class="g9-stage-controls"><div class="g9-stage-chips" role="group" aria-label="Stages"><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="0" data-g9-stage-desc="Show the pulley, the string, the bodies and the 8 N pull on one.">1: Two bodies on one string</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="1" data-g9-stage-desc="State the equal-tension model.">2: Ideal model</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="2" data-g9-stage-desc="Place the equal pull on the other body along its own segment.">3: Pull on the other body</button></div><p class="g9-stage-desc" data-g9-stage-desc-text></p><div class="g9-stage-stepper"><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="prev">Previous stage</button><span data-g9-stage-label></span><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="next">Next stage</button></div></div><figcaption data-g9-block="representation_bridge" data-g9-caption="purpose">Show two bodies joined over an ideal pulley with the string pulling one with 8 N, then the same 8 N on the other.</figcaption></figure><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="governing_relation"><h4>Governing relation</h4><div class="g9-relation"><div class="g9-math" data-g9-math="mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></math></div><p>For one taut massless ideal string, with any direction-changing pulley/support treated as frictionless and massless in this bounded model, the tension magnitude is uniform throughout that same string.</p><ul><li>The same taut string connects the stated segments.</li><li>The string is massless and inextensible in the ideal model.</li><li>Any pulley/redirection used here is frictionless and massless and only changes string direction.</li><li>No nonideal feature is introduced that would require different tension magnitudes.</li></ul></div></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="compact_anchor"><h4>Compact anchor</h4><p>A light string over a frictionless, light pulley joins two bodies. The string pulls one of them with 8 N. How hard does it pull the other?</p><p>Also 8 N: under the ideal model T_A = T_B = T, and each pull acts on its own body along its own string segment.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_prompt"><h4>Before you go on</h4><p>Two bodies are attached to the same taut ideal string, redirected only by a frictionless massless fixed pulley. State the tension relation and explain where the two tension forces appear.</p></div></section></article><article id="g9-CORE1--MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1" data-g9-search-text="Physics Laws of Motion One fixed-pulley string gives one length constraint Hard concept One fixed-pulley string gives one length constraint"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="scope"><h2>One fixed-pulley string gives one length constraint</h2></div><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-NLM-FIRST-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Laws of Motion"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Laws of Motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT" data-g9-meta-value="One fixed-pulley string gives one length constraint"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>One fixed-pulley string gives one length constraint</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT" data-g9-meta-value="HARD"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Hard concept</span></span></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-orientation" data-blueprint-slot="orientation" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="hard_transition"><h4>Hard transition</h4><p>For one taut inextensible string over one fixed ideal pulley, the two changing straight segment lengths add to a constant; with coordinates measured away from the pulley this gives y_A + y_B = constant and therefore a_A + a_B = 0.</p></div><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-PULLEY-YA-YB-3" data-g9-stage="TEACHING" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-ANCHOR-PULLEY-YA-YB-3" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="3" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-ANC-YAYB3-V0 VIS-NLM-ANC-YAYB3-V1 VIS-NLM-ANC-YAYB3-V2" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 840 350" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-PULLEY-YA-YB-3-1--rep-nlm-anc-yayb3-title g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-PULLEY-YA-YB-3-1--rep-nlm-anc-yayb3-desc">\n  <title id="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-PULLEY-YA-YB-3-1--rep-nlm-anc-yayb3-title">Two bodies on a string over a fixed pulley, y_A and y_B measured from the pulley</title>\n  <desc id="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-PULLEY-YA-YB-3-1--rep-nlm-anc-yayb3-desc">Stage 1: over a fixed pulley, y_A and y_B are measured away from the pulley along the string; A accelerates away from the pulley at 3 m/s². Stage 2: y_A + y_B stays constant, so a_A + a_B = 0. Stage 3: a_B = −3 m/s², so B accelerates toward the pulley at 3 m/s².</desc>\n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-PULLEY-YA-YB-3-1--rep-nlm-anc-yayb3-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-PULLEY-YA-YB-3-1--rep-nlm-anc-yayb3-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-ANC-YAYB3-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">Two bodies on a string over a fixed pulley, y_A and y_B measured from the pulley</text>\n    <line x1="200" y1="50" x2="440" y2="50" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="3"/>\n    <line x1="320" y1="50" x2="320" y2="80" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="320" cy="120" r="40" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="280" y1="120" x2="280" y2="250" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="360" y1="120" x2="360" y2="190" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <rect x="250" y="250" width="60" height="50" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="280" y="280" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="600">A</text>\n    <rect x="330" y="190" width="60" height="50" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="360" y="220" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="600">B</text>\n    <line x1="230" y1="120" x2="230" y2="248" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-PULLEY-YA-YB-3-1--rep-nlm-anc-yayb3-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="222" y="190" text-anchor="end">y_A</text>\n    <line x1="410" y1="120" x2="410" y2="188" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-PULLEY-YA-YB-3-1--rep-nlm-anc-yayb3-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="418" y="160">y_B</text>\n    <line x1="200" y1="250" x2="200" y2="300" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-PULLEY-YA-YB-3-1--rep-nlm-anc-yayb3-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="192" y="285" text-anchor="end">a_A = 3 m/s² away</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-ANC-YAYB3-V1" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="480" y="80" font-weight="600">y_A + y_B stays constant,</text>\n    <text x="480" y="102">so a_A + a_B = 0</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-ANC-YAYB3-V2" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <line x1="450" y1="240" x2="450" y2="190" stroke="#1f6fb2" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1-MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-REP-NLM-ANCHOR-PULLEY-YA-YB-3-1--rep-nlm-anc-yayb3-arrow-acc)"/>\n    <text x="462" y="220" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">a_B = −3 m/s²</text>\n    <text x="40" y="334" fill="#1f6fb2">B accelerates toward the pulley at 3 m/s².</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n<div class="g9-stage-controls"><div class="g9-stage-chips" role="group" aria-label="Stages"><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="0" data-g9-stage-desc="Show the pulley, both coordinates and A&#x27;s acceleration.">1: The pulley and coordinates</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="1" data-g9-stage-desc="Write the constant-length constraint.">2: String length</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="2" data-g9-stage-desc="Solve for a_B and read its direction.">3: B&#x27;s acceleration</button></div><p class="g9-stage-desc" data-g9-stage-desc-text></p><div class="g9-stage-stepper"><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="prev">Previous stage</button><span data-g9-stage-label></span><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="next">Next stage</button></div></div><figcaption data-g9-block="representation_bridge" data-g9-caption="purpose">Show y_A and y_B measured from the fixed pulley with a_A = 3 m/s², then the string-length constraint and a_B.</figcaption></figure><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="governing_relation"><h4>Governing relation</h4><div class="g9-relation"><div class="g9-math" data-g9-math="mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>free</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mtext>constant</mtext><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></div><p>For one taut inextensible string over one fixed ideal pulley, with both coordinates measured away from the pulley along the two straight segments, the variable segment lengths sum to a constant and the signed accelerations sum to zero.</p><ul><li>There is one fixed pulley and one taut inextensible string.</li><li>Only the two declared straight segments change length; the wrapped/fixed portions are constant.</li><li>Both coordinates are measured away from the pulley along their local string segments.</li><li>The pulley itself does not translate.</li></ul></div></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="compact_anchor"><h4>Compact anchor</h4><p>Over a fixed pulley, y_A and y_B are measured away from the pulley along the string. A accelerates away from the pulley at 3 m/s². What is a_B?</p><p>y_A + y_B stays constant, so a_A + a_B = 0 and a_B = −3 m/s²: B accelerates toward the pulley at 3 m/s².</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_prompt"><h4>Before you go on</h4><p>One taut inextensible string passes over one fixed ideal pulley, with y_A and y_B measured away from the pulley along the two hanging segments. Write the length relation and its acceleration consequence.</p></div></section></article></section><section id="g9-role-CORE1A" data-g9-role-section="CORE1A"><h1>Force and laws of motion: Construction</h1><section class="g9-core1a-book" data-g9-bucket-orientation data-g9-bucket-ref="BUCKET-PHY-NLM-FIRST-LAW"><p class="g9-prov">Concept Book</p><h2>Newton&#x27;s first law and free-body diagrams</h2><div class="g9-bucket-orientation-grid"><div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="learning_promise"><h4>Learning promise</h4><p>Grade-9 Force and Laws of Motion plus the owner-approved bounded Pinnacle preparation slice: balanced/unbalanced force reasoning, body-specific FBD ownership, friction direction, static-friction limits and kinetic-friction model, F_net = m a, Newton-third-law interaction pairs, common-acceleration connected bodies, ideal-string tension, and one fixed-pulley single-string length constraint. Observer-frame/pseudo-force reasoning remains an explicit-demand extension.</p></div><nav class="g9-block" data-g9-block="concept_route" data-g9-concept-route aria-label="Concept Book route"><h4>Concept route</h4><ol><li data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION"><a href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION">Zero net force allows steady motion</a><ol><li><a href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-1">Separate &#x27;is moving&#x27; from &#x27;is changing its velocity&#x27;, and link zero net force only to the second.</a></li></ol></li><li data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO"><a href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO">Nonzero forces can cancel</a><ol><li><a href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-1">List every force on the body as its own contribution, then combine them with signs; a zero sum never means that no forces act.</a></li></ol></li><li data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP"><a href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP">A free-body diagram belongs to one chosen body</a><ol><li><a href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-1">Pick one body and admit only forces that answer &#x27;who acts on this body?&#x27;; a partner force, or the components of a force already listed, never joins that list as an extra force.</a></li></ol></li><li data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE"><a href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE">An accelerating observer needs a declared pseudo-force convention</a><ol><li><a href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-1">Say whether the observer is accelerating; keep the physical forces identical in both descriptions and add −m a_frame only for the accelerating observer.</a></li></ol></li><li data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW"><a href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW">Net force, not any one force, sets acceleration</a><ol><li><a href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-1">Choose one body and a signed axis, add every external force on that body, and divide the net force by the mass; on a fixed incline, choose axes along and across the contact.</a></li></ol></li><li data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION"><a href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION">Friction opposes relative sliding at a contact</a><ol><li><a href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-1">Before giving friction a direction, imagine the contact made perfectly slippery and ask which way the surfaces would slide relative to each other; friction opposes that, and is zero if there is no such tendency.</a></li></ol></li><li data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW"><a href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW">Third-law partner forces act on different bodies</a><ol><li><a href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-1">Name each force of an interaction as &#x27;A on B&#x27; or &#x27;B on A&#x27;, and put it on the diagram of the body it acts on, so the pair never meets in one sum.</a></li></ol></li><li data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT"><a href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT">Quantitative static and kinetic friction conditions</a><ol><li><a href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-1">Find the actual normal force and the friction that rest would need, compare that need with μ_s N, and use μ N as the friction value only at impending slip or once sliding.</a></li></ol></li><li data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL"><a href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL">Connected bodies and a justified common acceleration</a><ol><li><a href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-1">Name the physical link that fixes the separation, give both bodies one sign convention, and write a separate Newton-II equation for each body with the interaction force kept in it.</a></li><li><a href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-2">Add the body equations into one system equation only where the internal pair should drop out, then isolate the body that makes the requested interaction force external.</a></li></ol></li><li data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION"><a href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION">Ideal-string tension belongs to each body-specific FBD</a><ol><li><a href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-1">Declare the ideal string before writing a single T, then draw that tension on each attached body, pulling away from the body along its own segment.</a></li><li><a href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-2">Check that nothing breaks the ideal model, then take the tension from a chosen body&#x27;s signed Newton-II equation instead of setting it equal to a weight or an applied force.</a></li></ol></li><li data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT"><a href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT">One fixed-pulley string gives one length constraint</a><ol><li><a href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-1">Measure both coordinates away from the fixed pulley, write the changing length as y_A + y_B = constant, and differentiate that one relation.</a></li><li><a href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-2">Read the signed result back as motion, and keep the length argument for the accelerations separate from the ideal-pulley argument for equal tension.</a></li></ol></li></ol></nav></div><aside><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="foundation_route"><h4>Foundation route</h4><ul><li data-g9-foundation-ref="BUCKET-PHY-KIN-1D-MOTION" data-g9-availability="unlinked">One-dimensional motion <span class="g9-availability-note">— direct route unavailable in this product</span></li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="model_contract"><h4>Model contract</h4><ul><li>Choose the body before listing forces; each listed force must name an external agent acting on that body.</li><li>State whether the observer is accelerating before deciding whether a pseudo-force is part of the chosen description.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="scope_boundary"><h4>Outside this book</h4><ul><li>Momentum-conservation and collision algebra unless a verified syllabus or real question explicitly demands it.</li><li>Routine non-inertial-frame or pseudo-force treatment as ordinary Grade-9 Standard syllabus content.</li><li>Pulley-count formula catalogues, movable/compound pulley taxonomies, massive or elastic-rope models, and wedge-on-wedge systems.</li><li>Variable-mass rocket dynamics, torque and rotational dynamics.</li></ul></div></aside></div></section><article id="g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1A" data-g9-search-text="Physics Laws of Motion Zero net force allows steady motion Medium concept Zero net force allows steady motion" class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-concept-header" data-g9-component="CONCEPT_HEADER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="CONCEPT_HEADER"><h2>Zero net force allows steady motion</h2><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1A" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-NLM-FIRST-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Laws of Motion"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Laws of Motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION" data-g9-meta-value="Zero net force allows steady motion"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Zero net force allows steady motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span></div></div></section><div class="g9-split"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-construction" data-blueprint-slot="construction" data-required="true"><section id="g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-1" class="g9-cu" data-g9-cu="CU-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-1"><div class="g9-component g9-c-unit-header" data-g9-component="UNIT_HEADER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="UNIT_HEADER" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-1"><div class="g9-unit-head"><span class="g9-unit-no" aria-hidden="true">1</span><h3>Separate &#x27;is moving&#x27; from &#x27;is changing its velocity&#x27;, and link zero net force only to the second.</h3><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-concept">Medium concept</span></div><nav class="g9-cu-nav" data-g9-unit-navigation aria-label="Concept Book section navigation"><span data-g9-route-position>Section 1 of 14</span><span class="g9-cu-nav-links"><a data-g9-next-section href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-1" aria-label="Next section: List every force on the body as its own contribution, then combine them with signs; a zero sum never means that no forces act.">Next →</a></span></nav></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-banner" data-g9-component="KEY_STEP" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="BANNER"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="inferential_jump"><h4>The key step</h4><p>Zero net force does not mean zero motion; it means velocity does not change, so rest and steady motion are both compatible with the same condition.</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-tile" data-g9-component="MODEL_CONTRACT" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_TILE"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-prerequisites"><summary>What you need before this</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="entry_assumptions"><h4>Prerequisites</h4><ul><li>Can tell whether an object&#x27;s velocity is changing.</li></ul></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-step-cards" data-g9-component="CONSTRUCTION_STEPS" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEP_CARDS" data-g9-component-items="3" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-1"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="construction"><ol><li data-g9-step="NLM1-1"><strong>Separate the statement &#x27;is moving&#x27; from the statement &#x27;is changing its velocity.&#x27;</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> Motion and change of motion are different observations, and only the second identifies acceleration.<br><em>Result:</em> state A: moving steadily.  state B: speeding up, slowing down, or turning.</li><li data-g9-step="NLM1-2"><strong>Imagine a low-friction cart after the hand has stopped touching it and compare successive equal-time snapshots.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> Once the hand is gone, a continuing steady velocity can be observed without a continuing forward push.<br><em>Result:</em> successive equal-time snapshots -&gt; equal forward position advances; velocity unchanged.</li><li data-g9-step="NLM1-3"><strong>Compare the released moving cart with a cart that starts at rest under the same zero-net-force condition.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> If both keep their initial velocities, the rule is about unchanged velocity rather than about rest.<br><em>Result:</em> moving cart: stays steady.  resting cart: stays at rest.  both show no velocity change.</li></ol></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-equation-card" data-g9-component="EQUATIONS" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_EQUATION_CARD" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-1"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-equations"><summary>Formula reference · meaning and conditions</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="equation_matrix"><h4>Equations and validity</h4><div class="g9-table-scroll" data-g9-equation-matrix><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Equation</th><th scope="col">What it tells you</th><th scope="col">When you can use it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr data-g9-relation-ref="REL-NEWTON-SECOND-LAW"><td><div class="g9-math" data-g9-math="mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mtext>net</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow></math></div></td><td><p>The vector net external force on a chosen body equals its mass times its acceleration.</p></td><td><ul><li>m &gt; 0.</li><li>F_net and a refer to the same chosen body.</li><li>All signed components use one declared axis.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-predict-reveal-worked-card" data-g9-component="WORKED_EXAMPLE" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="PREDICT_REVEAL_WORKED_CARD" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-1"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_anchor"><h4>Watch one · predict before reveal</h4><p>An ice-hockey puck of mass 0.2 kg slides across smooth ice at 4 m/s after the stick loses contact. Friction and air resistance are negligible. What horizontal forces act on it, what is its velocity 3 s later, and would a puck at rest on the same ice start moving?</p><p class="g9-worked-instruction">Before opening each step, say what you would do next.</p><ol class="g9-watch-steps"><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 1, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>Once the stick loses contact, nothing touches the puck horizontally, so the horizontal net force is zero.</p></div></details></li><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 2, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>F_net = m a gives a = 0/0.2 = 0 m/s², so the velocity does not change: after 3 s it is still 4 m/s.</p></div></details></li><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 3, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>Equal times give equal advances: 4 m in each second, 12 m in 3 s.</p></div></details></li><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 4, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>A puck at rest under the same zero net force keeps its zero velocity, so it stays at rest.</p></div></details></li></ol><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="worked-result"><summary>Result and independent check</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_result"><h4>Result</h4><p>No horizontal force acts. The puck still moves at 4 m/s, having covered 12 m, and a puck at rest stays at rest.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_check"><h4>Check</h4><p>With even a small friction force added, the puck would slow down: the force changes the velocity rather than keeping it up.</p></div></div></details></div></div></section></section></div><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-representation" data-blueprint-slot="representation" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-staged-visual" data-g9-component="STAGED_VISUAL" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="STAGED_VISUAL" data-g9-component-items="3" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-1"><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="CU-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-1-REP-NLM-PUCK-ZERO-NET" data-g9-stage="TEACHING" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-PUCK-ZERO-NET" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="3" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-PUCK-V0 VIS-NLM-PUCK-V1 VIS-NLM-PUCK-V2" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 760 290" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-1-REP-NLM-PUCK-ZERO-NET-1--rep-nlm-puck-title g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-1-REP-NLM-PUCK-ZERO-NET-1--rep-nlm-puck-desc">\n  <title id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-1-REP-NLM-PUCK-ZERO-NET-1--rep-nlm-puck-title">Puck sliding on smooth ice: zero net force, unchanged velocity</title>\n  <desc id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-1-REP-NLM-PUCK-ZERO-NET-1--rep-nlm-puck-desc">Stage 1: A 0.2 kg puck slides across smooth horizontal ice at 4 m/s after the stick loses contact; friction and air resistance are negligible. Stage 2: No horizontal force acts, so the net horizontal force is zero; snapshots at 0, 1, 2 and 3 s are equally spaced. Stage 3: The velocity does not change: still 4 m/s at 3 s, having covered 12 m; a puck at rest stays at rest. Zero net force goes with no change of velocity, not with no motion.</desc>\n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-1-REP-NLM-PUCK-ZERO-NET-1--rep-nlm-puck-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-1-REP-NLM-PUCK-ZERO-NET-1--rep-nlm-puck-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-PUCK-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">A 0.2 kg puck slides on smooth ice at 4 m/s</text>\n    <line x1="30" y1="160" x2="430" y2="160" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="40" y1="160" x2="32" y2="168" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="58" y1="160" x2="50" y2="168" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="76" y1="160" x2="68" y2="168" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="94" y1="160" x2="86" y2="168" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="112" y1="160" x2="104" y2="168" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="130" y1="160" x2="122" y2="168" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="148" y1="160" x2="140" y2="168" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="166" y1="160" x2="158" y2="168" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="184" y1="160" x2="176" y2="168" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="202" y1="160" x2="194" y2="168" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="220" y1="160" x2="212" y2="168" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="238" y1="160" x2="230" y2="168" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="256" y1="160" x2="248" y2="168" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="274" y1="160" x2="266" y2="168" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="292" y1="160" x2="284" y2="168" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="310" y1="160" x2="302" y2="168" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="328" y1="160" x2="320" y2="168" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="346" y1="160" x2="338" y2="168" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="364" y1="160" x2="356" y2="168" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="382" y1="160" x2="374" y2="168" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="400" y1="160" x2="392" y2="168" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="418" y1="160" x2="410" y2="168" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <ellipse cx="60" cy="150" rx="22" ry="9" fill="currentColor"/>\n    <line x1="86" y1="150" x2="136" y2="150" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-1-REP-NLM-PUCK-ZERO-NET-1--rep-nlm-puck-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="88" y="138">4 m/s</text>\n    <text x="20" y="200">stick has lost contact;</text>\n    <text x="20" y="222">friction and air resistance negligible</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-PUCK-V1" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <ellipse cx="170" cy="150" rx="22" ry="9" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="3 3"/>\n    <ellipse cx="280" cy="150" rx="22" ry="9" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="3 3"/>\n    <ellipse cx="390" cy="150" rx="22" ry="9" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="3 3"/>\n    <text x="60" y="130" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14">0 s</text>\n    <text x="170" y="130" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14">1 s</text>\n    <text x="280" y="130" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14">2 s</text>\n    <text x="390" y="130" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14">3 s</text>\n    <text x="450" y="60" font-weight="600">horizontal forces on the puck:</text>\n    <text x="450" y="82">none</text>\n    <text x="450" y="104">net horizontal force = 0</text>\n    <text x="450" y="126">equal-time snapshots, equal gaps</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-PUCK-V2" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="450" y="170" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">velocity does not change:</text>\n    <text x="450" y="192" fill="#1f6fb2">still 4 m/s at 3 s, 12 m covered</text>\n    <text x="450" y="214">a puck at rest stays at rest</text>\n    <text x="450" y="236">zero net force ↔ no velocity change,</text>\n    <text x="450" y="258">not ↔ no motion</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n<div class="g9-stage-controls"><div class="g9-stage-chips" role="group" aria-label="Stages"><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="0" data-g9-stage-desc="Show the puck after the stick loses contact.">1: The sliding puck</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="1" data-g9-stage-desc="List the horizontal forces and show equal-time snapshots.">2: Forces and snapshots</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="2" data-g9-stage-desc="State the unchanged velocity and the resting case.">3: What zero net force predicts</button></div><p class="g9-stage-desc" data-g9-stage-desc-text></p><div class="g9-stage-stepper"><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="prev">Previous stage</button><span data-g9-stage-label></span><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="next">Next stage</button></div></div><figcaption data-g9-block="representation_bridge" data-g9-caption="purpose">Show the sliding puck with no horizontal force so &#x27;moving&#x27; is separated from &#x27;changing velocity&#x27;.</figcaption></figure></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-repair_closure" data-blueprint-slot="repair_closure" data-required="true"><section class="g9-cu-support" data-g9-support-for="CU-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-1"><h3>Separate &#x27;is moving&#x27; from &#x27;is changing its velocity&#x27;, and link zero net force only to the second.</h3><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-trap-card" data-g9-component="TRAP_REPAIR" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_TRAP_CARD" data-g9-component-items="1" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-1"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-trap-repair"><summary>Mistake clinic · diagnose and repair</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="wrong_path"><h4>A tempting wrong path</h4><ul><li>A force must keep acting in the direction of motion or the object must stop.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="diagnose"><h4>Diagnose</h4><ul><li>A low-friction cart is pushed and then released. After the hand loses contact, what horizontal force is required merely to keep it moving steadily?</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="repair"><h4>Repair</h4><ul><li>Separate velocity from change of velocity: a net force is required for acceleration, not for the existence of motion.</li></ul></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-triad" data-g9-component="QUICK_CHECK" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="TRIAD" data-g9-component-items="2" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-1"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="independent_check"><h4>1-2-3 quick check</h4><ol class="g9-triad"><li data-g9-triad-role=""><span class="g9-triad-head">1 · Check</span><span class="g9-triad-text">Put back a small friction force: the object must now slow down, which shows that a force changes the velocity rather than maintaining it.</span></li><li data-g9-triad-role=""><span class="g9-triad-head">2 · Check</span><span class="g9-triad-text">a = F_net/m gives 0 N / m = 0 m/s², and distance equals speed × time in metres for equal time intervals.</span></li></ol></div></div></section></section></div></div><div class="g9-split g9-split-support-only"><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-repair_closure" data-blueprint-slot="repair_closure" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-recall-card" data-g9-component="EXIT_RECALL" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="RECALL_CARD"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_task"><h4>Now you do one</h4><p>One cart is at rest and another moves steadily in a straight line. For each, decide whether zero net force is compatible with the motion and explain the common rule.</p></div><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your answer<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1A-MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-exit"><summary>Model answer</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1A-MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-exit"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_answer"><p>Zero net force is compatible with both. Each cart can keep its current velocity unchanged.</p><ol><li>Rest is a zero velocity that stays zero.</li><li>Steady straight motion is a nonzero velocity that stays unchanged.</li><li>The shared condition is no change of velocity, not no motion.</li></ol></div></template></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-chips" data-g9-component="LEARNING_TRANSITIONS" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="CHIPS"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="learning_transitions"><h4>Next steps</h4><div class="g9-transitions-row" data-g9-transitions><a class="g9-transition-chip g9-chip-practice" data-g9-transition="PRACTICE" href="core2.html">Practice this concept &rarr;</a><a class="g9-transition-chip g9-chip-qb" data-g9-transition="QUESTION_BANK" href="../../../public/question-bank/index.html?capability=MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION">All questions in Question Bank &rarr;</a></div></div></div></section></div></div></article><article id="g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1A" data-g9-search-text="Physics Laws of Motion Nonzero forces can cancel Medium concept Nonzero forces can cancel" class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-concept-header" data-g9-component="CONCEPT_HEADER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="CONCEPT_HEADER"><h2>Nonzero forces can cancel</h2><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1A" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-NLM-FIRST-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Laws of Motion"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Laws of Motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO" data-g9-meta-value="Nonzero forces can cancel"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Nonzero forces can cancel</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span></div></div></section><div class="g9-split"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-construction" data-blueprint-slot="construction" data-required="true"><section id="g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-1" class="g9-cu" data-g9-cu="CU-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-1"><div class="g9-component g9-c-unit-header" data-g9-component="UNIT_HEADER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="UNIT_HEADER" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-1"><div class="g9-unit-head"><span class="g9-unit-no" aria-hidden="true">1</span><h3>List every force on the body as its own contribution, then combine them with signs; a zero sum never means that no forces act.</h3><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-concept">Medium concept</span></div><nav class="g9-cu-nav" data-g9-unit-navigation aria-label="Concept Book section navigation"><span data-g9-route-position>Section 2 of 14</span><span class="g9-cu-nav-links"><a data-g9-prev-section href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-1" aria-label="Previous section: Separate &#x27;is moving&#x27; from &#x27;is changing its velocity&#x27;, and link zero net force only to the second.">← Previous</a><a data-g9-next-section href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-1" aria-label="Next section: Pick one body and admit only forces that answer &#x27;who acts on this body?&#x27;; a partner force, or the components of a force already listed, never joins that list as an extra force.">Next →</a></span></nav></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-banner" data-g9-component="KEY_STEP" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="BANNER"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="inferential_jump"><h4>The key step</h4><p>Several nonzero forces can act on one body while their directed sum is zero; the motion responds to the net result rather than to the mere presence of forces.</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-tile" data-g9-component="MODEL_CONTRACT" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_TILE"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-prerequisites"><summary>What you need before this</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="entry_assumptions"><h4>Prerequisites</h4><ul><li>Can interpret zero net force as unchanged velocity.</li></ul><ul><li data-g9-prereq="CAP-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION" data-bridged="true"><a href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION">Relate zero net force to unchanged velocity.</a></li></ul></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-step-cards" data-g9-component="CONSTRUCTION_STEPS" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEP_CARDS" data-g9-component-items="3" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-1"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="construction"><ol><li data-g9-step="NLM2-1"><strong>List two opposite pushes on the same object as separate contributions before combining them.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> Keeping the contributions visible prevents a zero total from being mistaken for no forces.<br><em>Result:</em> leftward push: present.  rightward push: present.</li><li data-g9-step="NLM2-2"><strong>Make the two opposite pushes equal in size and combine their directional effects.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> Equal opposite contributions cancel in the net result while each contribution still exists.<br><em>Result:</em> two nonzero opposite pushes -&gt; net force: zero.</li><li data-g9-step="NLM2-3"><strong>Increase both opposite pushes together while keeping them equal.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> The individual forces change while the zero net result persists, directly separating the parts from their sum.<br><em>Result:</em> larger opposite pushes: still net force zero; individual forces remain nonzero.</li></ol></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-equation-card" data-g9-component="EQUATIONS" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_EQUATION_CARD" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-1"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-equations"><summary>Formula reference · meaning and conditions</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="equation_matrix"><h4>Equations and validity</h4><div class="g9-table-scroll" data-g9-equation-matrix><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Equation</th><th scope="col">What it tells you</th><th scope="col">When you can use it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr data-g9-relation-ref="REL-NEWTON-SECOND-LAW"><td><div class="g9-math" data-g9-math="mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mtext>net</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow></math></div></td><td><p>The vector net external force on a chosen body equals its mass times its acceleration.</p></td><td><ul><li>m &gt; 0.</li><li>F_net and a refer to the same chosen body.</li><li>All signed components use one declared axis.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-predict-reveal-worked-card" data-g9-component="WORKED_EXAMPLE" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="PREDICT_REVEAL_WORKED_CARD" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-1"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_anchor"><h4>Watch one · predict before reveal</h4><p>A 1.5 kg lamp hangs at rest from a ceiling cord. Take g = 10 m/s² and up as positive. List each force on the lamp with its size, find the net force, and say what would happen if the cord were cut.</p><p class="g9-worked-instruction">Before opening each step, say what you would do next.</p><ol class="g9-watch-steps"><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 1, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>Two agents act on the lamp: the Earth, through its weight, and the cord, through its tension.</p></div></details></li><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 2, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>Weight: 1.5 × 10 = 15 N, so −15 N on the declared axis.</p></div></details></li><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 3, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>The lamp is at rest, so F_net = 0: T − 15 = 0 and T = +15 N. Both forces are real and non-zero.</p></div></details></li><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 4, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>Cut the cord and only the weight remains: F_net = −15 N and a = −15/1.5 = −10 m/s².</p></div></details></li></ol><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="worked-result"><summary>Result and independent check</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_result"><h4>Result</h4><p>The cord pulls up with 15 N and gravity pulls down with 15 N; the net force is zero. If the cord were cut, the lamp would accelerate downward at 10 m/s².</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_check"><h4>Check</h4><p>The cut-cord case shows that the 15 N weight was acting all along; the tension had been cancelling it, not removing it.</p></div></div></details></div></div></section></section></div><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-representation" data-blueprint-slot="representation" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-staged-visual" data-g9-component="STAGED_VISUAL" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="STAGED_VISUAL" data-g9-component-items="3" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-1"><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="CU-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-1-REP-NLM-LAMP-CANCEL" data-g9-stage="TEACHING" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-LAMP-CANCEL" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="3" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-LAMP-V0 VIS-NLM-LAMP-V1 VIS-NLM-LAMP-V2" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 760 270" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-1-REP-NLM-LAMP-CANCEL-1--rep-nlm-lamp-title g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-1-REP-NLM-LAMP-CANCEL-1--rep-nlm-lamp-desc">\n  <title id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-1-REP-NLM-LAMP-CANCEL-1--rep-nlm-lamp-title">Lamp at rest on a cord: two nonzero forces that sum to zero</title>\n  <desc id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-1-REP-NLM-LAMP-CANCEL-1--rep-nlm-lamp-desc">Stage 1: A 1.5 kg lamp hangs at rest from a light vertical ceiling cord; g = 10 m/s², up positive. Stage 2: Two forces act on the lamp: the cord pulls up with 15 N and the Earth pulls down with 15 N. Stage 3: The net force is 15 − 15 = 0, so the lamp stays at rest; if the cord were cut only the −15 N weight remains and a = −10 m/s².</desc>\n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-1-REP-NLM-LAMP-CANCEL-1--rep-nlm-lamp-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-1-REP-NLM-LAMP-CANCEL-1--rep-nlm-lamp-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-LAMP-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">A 1.5 kg lamp hangs at rest from a cord</text>\n    <line x1="80" y1="50" x2="240" y2="50" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="90" y1="50" x2="82" y2="42" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="108" y1="50" x2="100" y2="42" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="126" y1="50" x2="118" y2="42" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="144" y1="50" x2="136" y2="42" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="162" y1="50" x2="154" y2="42" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="180" y1="50" x2="172" y2="42" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="198" y1="50" x2="190" y2="42" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="216" y1="50" x2="208" y2="42" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="234" y1="50" x2="226" y2="42" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="160" y1="50" x2="160" y2="140" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <path d="M130 170 L145 140 H175 L190 170 Z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="210" y="165">lamp</text>\n    <text x="20" y="250">g = 10 m/s², up positive</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-LAMP-V1" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <line x1="160" y1="136" x2="160" y2="76" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-1-REP-NLM-LAMP-CANCEL-1--rep-nlm-lamp-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="170" y="96">cord on lamp: 15 N</text>\n    <line x1="160" y1="176" x2="160" y2="236" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-1-REP-NLM-LAMP-CANCEL-1--rep-nlm-lamp-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="170" y="236">Earth on lamp: 15 N</text>\n    <text x="420" y="60" font-weight="600">forces on the lamp:</text>\n    <text x="420" y="82">+15 N (cord, up)</text>\n    <text x="420" y="104">−15 N (Earth, down)</text>\n    <text x="420" y="126">two forces act, both nonzero</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-LAMP-V2" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="420" y="160" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">net force = 15 − 15 = 0</text>\n    <text x="420" y="182" fill="#1f6fb2">a = 0: the lamp stays at rest</text>\n    <text x="420" y="204">cord cut: only −15 N remains,</text>\n    <text x="420" y="226">a = −10 m/s² (down)</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n<div class="g9-stage-controls"><div class="g9-stage-chips" role="group" aria-label="Stages"><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="0" data-g9-stage-desc="Show the lamp at rest on its cord.">1: The hanging lamp</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="1" data-g9-stage-desc="List each force on the lamp as its own signed contribution.">2: Each force</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="2" data-g9-stage-desc="Add the forces with signs and predict the cut-cord case.">3: Signed sum</button></div><p class="g9-stage-desc" data-g9-stage-desc-text></p><div class="g9-stage-stepper"><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="prev">Previous stage</button><span data-g9-stage-label></span><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="next">Next stage</button></div></div><figcaption data-g9-block="representation_bridge" data-g9-caption="purpose">Show the lamp&#x27;s two nonzero forces as separate contributions whose signed sum is zero.</figcaption></figure></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-repair_closure" data-blueprint-slot="repair_closure" data-required="true"><section class="g9-cu-support" data-g9-support-for="CU-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-1"><h3>List every force on the body as its own contribution, then combine them with signs; a zero sum never means that no forces act.</h3><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-trap-card" data-g9-component="TRAP_REPAIR" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_TRAP_CARD" data-g9-component-items="1" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-1"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-trap-repair"><summary>Mistake clinic · diagnose and repair</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="wrong_path"><h4>A tempting wrong path</h4><ul><li>If the net force is zero, no forces are acting.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="diagnose"><h4>Diagnose</h4><ul><li>A book rests on a table. Are there no forces on the book, or can nonzero forces add to zero?</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="repair"><h4>Repair</h4><ul><li>List every interaction force on the body, then combine their directions; cancellation belongs to the net result, not to the individual forces.</li></ul></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-triad" data-g9-component="QUICK_CHECK" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="TRIAD" data-g9-component-items="2" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-1"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="independent_check"><h4>1-2-3 quick check</h4><ol class="g9-triad"><li data-g9-triad-role=""><span class="g9-triad-head">1 · Check</span><span class="g9-triad-text">Remove one of the balancing forces: the other must now produce an acceleration, which proves it was acting all along.</span></li><li data-g9-triad-role=""><span class="g9-triad-head">2 · Check</span><span class="g9-triad-text">With one axis declared, the balancing forces must carry opposite signs and equal sizes; two forces of the same sign can never sum to zero.</span></li></ol></div></div></section></section></div></div><div class="g9-split g9-split-support-only"><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-repair_closure" data-blueprint-slot="repair_closure" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-recall-card" data-g9-component="EXIT_RECALL" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="RECALL_CARD"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_task"><h4>Now you do one</h4><p>An object has two equal opposite forces acting on it. Explain what is zero and what is not, then state what motion change the net result predicts.</p></div><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your answer<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1A-MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-exit"><summary>Model answer</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1A-MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-exit"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_answer"><p>The net force is zero, but both individual forces are nonzero. The zero net result predicts no change of velocity.</p><ol><li>Each force remains an actual interaction on the body.</li><li>Opposite equal directional effects cancel only when combined.</li><li>A zero net force is consistent with unchanged velocity.</li></ol></div></template></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-chips" data-g9-component="PRACTICE_LINKS" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="CHIPS"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="practice_navigation"><h4>Practice this concept in Core2</h4><ul><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2009-P1-Q49" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2009-P1-Q49">IIT-JEE · 2009 · Paper I · Q49</a></li></ul></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-chips" data-g9-component="LEARNING_TRANSITIONS" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="CHIPS"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="learning_transitions"><h4>Next steps</h4><div class="g9-transitions-row" data-g9-transitions><a class="g9-transition-chip g9-chip-practice" data-g9-transition="PRACTICE" href="core2.html">Practice this concept &rarr;</a><a class="g9-transition-chip g9-chip-qb" data-g9-transition="QUESTION_BANK" href="../../../public/question-bank/index.html?capability=MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO">All questions in Question Bank &rarr;</a></div></div></div></section></div></div></article><article id="g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1A" data-g9-search-text="Physics Laws of Motion A free-body diagram belongs to one chosen body Hard concept A free-body diagram belongs to one chosen body" class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-concept-header" data-g9-component="CONCEPT_HEADER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="CONCEPT_HEADER"><h2>A free-body diagram belongs to one chosen body</h2><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1A" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-NLM-FIRST-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Laws of Motion"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Laws of Motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP" data-g9-meta-value="A free-body diagram belongs to one chosen body"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>A free-body diagram belongs to one chosen body</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP" data-g9-meta-value="HARD"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Hard concept</span></span></div></div></section><div class="g9-split"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-construction" data-blueprint-slot="construction" data-required="true"><section id="g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-1" class="g9-cu" data-g9-cu="CU-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-1"><div class="g9-component g9-c-unit-header" data-g9-component="UNIT_HEADER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="UNIT_HEADER" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-1"><div class="g9-unit-head"><span class="g9-unit-no" aria-hidden="true">1</span><h3>Pick one body and admit only forces that answer &#x27;who acts on this body?&#x27;; a partner force, or the components of a force already listed, never joins that list as an extra force.</h3><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-concept">Hard concept</span></div><nav class="g9-cu-nav" data-g9-unit-navigation aria-label="Concept Book section navigation"><span data-g9-route-position>Section 3 of 14</span><span class="g9-cu-nav-links"><a data-g9-prev-section href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-1" aria-label="Previous section: List every force on the body as its own contribution, then combine them with signs; a zero sum never means that no forces act.">← Previous</a><a data-g9-next-section href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-1" aria-label="Next section: Say whether the observer is accelerating; keep the physical forces identical in both descriptions and add −m a_frame only for the accelerating observer.">Next →</a></span></nav></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-banner" data-g9-component="KEY_STEP" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="BANNER"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="inferential_jump"><h4>The key step</h4><p>A free-body diagram contains forces acting on one chosen body only; the equal-and-opposite partner of an interaction acts on the other body and belongs on that other body&#x27;s diagram.</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-tile" data-g9-component="MODEL_CONTRACT" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_TILE"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-prerequisites"><summary>What you need before this</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="entry_assumptions"><h4>Prerequisites</h4><ul><li>Can keep several forces visible even when their net effect is zero.</li></ul></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-step-cards" data-g9-component="CONSTRUCTION_STEPS" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEP_CARDS" data-g9-component-items="4" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-1"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="construction"><ol><li data-g9-step="NLM3-1"><strong>Choose the cart as the body to describe and require every arrow to answer &#x27;who acts on the cart?&#x27;</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> The ownership question gives one mechanical test for whether a force belongs on the chosen body&#x27;s diagram.<br><em>Result:</em> chosen body: cart.  every listed arrow must be an agent -&gt; cart force.</li><li data-g9-step="NLM3-2"><strong>For the horse-cart contact, place the horse-on-cart force on the cart diagram and place the cart-on-horse partner on a separate horse diagram.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> The two forces belong to one interaction but act on different bodies, so combining them on one body&#x27;s diagram changes the question being asked.<br><em>Result:</em> cart diagram: horse -&gt; cart.  horse diagram: cart -&gt; horse.</li><li data-g9-step="NLM3-3"><strong>Switch the chosen body from cart to horse and rebuild the inventory from the ownership question.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> A correct diagram changes when the chosen body changes even though the physical interaction is the same.<br><em>Result:</em> switch body -&gt; switch which contact-force arrow belongs in the diagram; partner arrows never cancel on one diagram.</li><li data-g9-step="NLM3-4"><strong>When a physical force is resolved into components for chosen axes, keep the body-force inventory unchanged: use the components as a representation of that one force in the equations, not as additional interaction forces alongside the original vector.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> Resolving a vector changes its coordinate representation, not the number of physical interactions acting on the body. Counting both the original force and its components double-counts the same interaction.<br><em>Result:</em> one physical force -&gt; either original vector representation or its signed components in the chosen equations, never both as separate forces</li></ol></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-equation-card" data-g9-component="EQUATIONS" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_EQUATION_CARD" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-1"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-equations"><summary>Formula reference · meaning and conditions</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="equation_matrix"><h4>Equations and validity</h4><div class="g9-table-scroll" data-g9-equation-matrix><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Equation</th><th scope="col">What it tells you</th><th scope="col">When you can use it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr data-g9-relation-ref="REL-NEWTON-SECOND-LAW"><td><div class="g9-math" data-g9-math="mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mtext>net</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow></math></div></td><td><p>The vector net external force on a chosen body equals its mass times its acceleration.</p></td><td><ul><li>m &gt; 0.</li><li>F_net and a refer to the same chosen body.</li><li>All signed components use one declared axis.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-predict-reveal-worked-card" data-g9-component="WORKED_EXAMPLE" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="PREDICT_REVEAL_WORKED_CARD" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-1"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_anchor"><h4>Watch one · predict before reveal</h4><p>A 50 kg child stands on a 20 kg box that rests on the floor. Take g = 10 m/s². Build the free-body inventory of the box: name each force, its agent, its direction and its size.</p><p class="g9-worked-instruction">Before opening each step, say what you would do next.</p><ol class="g9-watch-steps"><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 1, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>Chosen body: the box. Every arrow must answer &#x27;who acts on the box?&#x27;.</p></div></details></li><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 2, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>Earth on box: weight 20 × 10 = 200 N down. Child on box: a contact push down; the child is at rest, so it equals the child&#x27;s weight, 500 N.</p></div></details></li><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 3, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>Floor on box: the box is at rest, so the floor pushes up with 200 + 500 = 700 N.</p></div></details></li><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 4, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>The child&#x27;s 500 N weight acts on the child, and the box&#x27;s 500 N upward push acts on the child too; both belong on the child&#x27;s diagram.</p></div></details></li></ol><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="worked-result"><summary>Result and independent check</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_result"><h4>Result</h4><p>On the box: its weight 200 N down (Earth), the child&#x27;s push 500 N down (child), and the floor&#x27;s push 700 N up (floor). The child&#x27;s own weight is not on the box&#x27;s diagram.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_check"><h4>Check</h4><p>Taking child and box together, the floor supports a total weight of 700 N, the same floor force the box&#x27;s own diagram gives.</p></div></div></details></div></div></section></section></div><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-representation" data-blueprint-slot="representation" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-staged-visual" data-g9-component="STAGED_VISUAL" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="STAGED_VISUAL" data-g9-component-items="4" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-1"><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="CU-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-1-REP-NLM-CHILD-ON-BOX" data-g9-stage="TEACHING" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-CHILD-ON-BOX" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="4" data-g9-stages-total="4" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-CHILDBOX-V0 VIS-NLM-CHILDBOX-V1 VIS-NLM-CHILDBOX-V2 VIS-NLM-CHILDBOX-V3" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 840 360" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-1-REP-NLM-CHILD-ON-BOX-1--rep-nlm-childbox-title g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-1-REP-NLM-CHILD-ON-BOX-1--rep-nlm-childbox-desc">\n  <title id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-1-REP-NLM-CHILD-ON-BOX-1--rep-nlm-childbox-title">A 50 kg child standing on a 20 kg box that rests on the floor</title>\n  <desc id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-1-REP-NLM-CHILD-ON-BOX-1--rep-nlm-childbox-desc">Stage 1: a 50 kg child stands on a 20 kg box resting on the floor; everything is at rest and g = 10 m/s². Stage 2: the box is the chosen body; its agents are Earth, the child and the floor. Stage 3: on the box act its weight 200 N down (Earth), the child\'s push 500 N down (child) and the floor\'s push 700 N up (floor). Stage 4: the child\'s own 500 N weight acts on the child and does not belong on the box\'s diagram.</desc>\n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-1-REP-NLM-CHILD-ON-BOX-1--rep-nlm-childbox-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-1-REP-NLM-CHILD-ON-BOX-1--rep-nlm-childbox-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-CHILDBOX-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">A child standing on a box that rests on the floor</text>\n    <line x1="20" y1="290" x2="420" y2="290" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="30" y1="290" x2="22" y2="298" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="48" y1="290" x2="40" y2="298" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="66" y1="290" x2="58" y2="298" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="84" y1="290" x2="76" y2="298" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="102" y1="290" x2="94" y2="298" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="120" y1="290" x2="112" y2="298" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="138" y1="290" x2="130" y2="298" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="156" y1="290" x2="148" y2="298" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="174" y1="290" x2="166" y2="298" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="192" y1="290" x2="184" y2="298" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="210" y1="290" x2="202" y2="298" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="228" y1="290" x2="220" y2="298" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="246" y1="290" x2="238" y2="298" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="264" y1="290" x2="256" y2="298" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="282" y1="290" x2="274" y2="298" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="290" x2="292" y2="298" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="318" y1="290" x2="310" y2="298" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="336" y1="290" x2="328" y2="298" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="354" y1="290" x2="346" y2="298" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="372" y1="290" x2="364" y2="298" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="390" y1="290" x2="382" y2="298" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="408" y1="290" x2="400" y2="298" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <rect x="140" y="200" width="140" height="90" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="210" y="250" text-anchor="middle">box 20 kg</text>\n    <circle cx="210" cy="119" r="9" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="210" y1="128" x2="210" y2="172" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="210" y1="172" x2="200" y2="200" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="210" y1="172" x2="220" y2="200" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="196" y1="142" x2="224" y2="142" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="235" y="130">child 50 kg</text>\n    <text x="20" y="330">Everything is at rest; g = 10 m/s².</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-CHILDBOX-V1" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <rect x="130" y="190" width="160" height="110" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="6 4"/>\n    <text x="470" y="40" font-weight="600">chosen body: the box</text>\n    <text x="470" y="62">every arrow answers \'who acts on the box?\'</text>\n    <text x="470" y="84">agents: Earth, the child, the floor</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-CHILDBOX-V2" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <rect x="580" y="160" width="120" height="60" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="640" y="196" text-anchor="middle">box</text>\n    <line x1="600" y1="160" x2="600" y2="100" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-1-REP-NLM-CHILD-ON-BOX-1--rep-nlm-childbox-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="592" y="120" text-anchor="end">floor on box 700 N</text>\n    <line x1="680" y1="110" x2="680" y2="158" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-1-REP-NLM-CHILD-ON-BOX-1--rep-nlm-childbox-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="690" y="124">child on box</text>\n    <text x="690" y="144">500 N</text>\n    <line x1="640" y1="220" x2="640" y2="280" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-1-REP-NLM-CHILD-ON-BOX-1--rep-nlm-childbox-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="650" y="276">Earth on box 200 N</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-CHILDBOX-V3" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="440" y="320" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">The child\'s 500 N weight acts on the child,</text>\n    <text x="440" y="342" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">not on the box.</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n<div class="g9-stage-controls"><div class="g9-stage-chips" role="group" aria-label="Stages"><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="0" data-g9-stage-desc="Show the child standing on the box at rest.">1: The child and the box</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="1" data-g9-stage-desc="Isolate the box and name the agents acting on it.">2: Choose the box</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="2" data-g9-stage-desc="Draw each force on the box with its agent and size.">3: Forces on the box</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="3" data-g9-stage-desc="Keep the child&#x27;s weight off the box&#x27;s diagram.">4: Not on the box</button></div><p class="g9-stage-desc" data-g9-stage-desc-text></p><div class="g9-stage-stepper"><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="prev">Previous stage</button><span data-g9-stage-label></span><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="next">Next stage</button></div></div><figcaption data-g9-block="representation_bridge" data-g9-caption="purpose">Show the 50 kg child on the 20 kg box with the box chosen as the one body whose forces are listed.</figcaption></figure></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-repair_closure" data-blueprint-slot="repair_closure" data-required="true"><section class="g9-cu-support" data-g9-support-for="CU-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-1"><h3>Pick one body and admit only forces that answer &#x27;who acts on this body?&#x27;; a partner force, or the components of a force already listed, never joins that list as an extra force.</h3><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-trap-card" data-g9-component="TRAP_REPAIR" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_TRAP_CARD" data-g9-component-items="2" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-1"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-trap-repair"><summary>Mistake clinic · diagnose and repair</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="wrong_path"><h4>A tempting wrong path</h4><ul><li>Equal and opposite partner forces cancel each other on one body&#x27;s free-body diagram.</li><li>After resolving weight on an incline, mg, mg sin(theta) and mg cos(theta) are three separate forces on the block.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="diagnose"><h4>Diagnose</h4><ul><li>A horse pulls a cart and the cart pulls back equally. Which of those two forces acts on the cart, and which acts on the horse?</li><li>If gravity is one Earth-on-block interaction, did choosing tilted axes create two new physical agents?</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="repair"><h4>Repair</h4><ul><li>Choose one body first. Put only forces acting on that body on its diagram; draw the partner force only when the other body is chosen.</li><li>No. Weight remains one physical force. Its parallel/perpendicular components are the coordinate representation used in the chosen equations; do not add the original mg again to those component sums.</li></ul></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-triad" data-g9-component="QUICK_CHECK" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="TRIAD" data-g9-component-items="2" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-1"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="independent_check"><h4>1-2-3 quick check</h4><ol class="g9-triad"><li data-g9-triad-role=""><span class="g9-triad-head">1 · Check</span><span class="g9-triad-text">Treat the touching bodies as one system: the external supporting force it predicts must equal the one found from the single body&#x27;s own diagram.</span></li><li data-g9-triad-role=""><span class="g9-triad-head">2 · Check</span><span class="g9-triad-text">Each interaction contributes exactly one arrow to a given diagram; a diagram holding both members of a pair has one arrow that belongs to the other body.</span></li></ol></div></div></section></section></div></div><div class="g9-split g9-split-support-only"><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-repair_closure" data-blueprint-slot="repair_closure" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-recall-card" data-g9-component="EXIT_RECALL" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="RECALL_CARD"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_task"><h4>Now you do one</h4><p>A person pushes a box. Describe the two contact-force partner arrows and state which one belongs on the box&#x27;s free-body diagram.</p></div><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your answer<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1A-MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-exit"><summary>Model answer</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1A-MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-exit"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_answer"><p>The person&#x27;s force on the box belongs on the box diagram. The box&#x27;s equal-and-opposite force on the person belongs on the person&#x27;s diagram.</p><ol><li>Choose the box first.</li><li>Ask which member of the contact pair acts on the box.</li><li>The partner acts on the person, so it cannot cancel the first force on the box diagram.</li></ol></div></template></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-chips" data-g9-component="LEARNING_TRANSITIONS" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="CHIPS"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="learning_transitions"><h4>Next steps</h4><div class="g9-transitions-row" data-g9-transitions><a class="g9-transition-chip g9-chip-practice" data-g9-transition="PRACTICE" href="core2.html">Practice this concept &rarr;</a><a class="g9-transition-chip g9-chip-qb" data-g9-transition="QUESTION_BANK" href="../../../public/question-bank/index.html?capability=MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP">All questions in Question Bank &rarr;</a></div></div></div></section></div></div></article><article id="g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1A" data-g9-search-text="Physics Laws of Motion An accelerating observer needs a declared pseudo-force convention Hard concept An accelerating observer needs a declared pseudo-force convention" class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-concept-header" data-g9-component="CONCEPT_HEADER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="CONCEPT_HEADER"><h2>An accelerating observer needs a declared pseudo-force convention</h2><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1A" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-NLM-FIRST-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Laws of Motion"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Laws of Motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE" data-g9-meta-value="An accelerating observer needs a declared pseudo-force convention"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>An accelerating observer needs a declared pseudo-force convention</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE" data-g9-meta-value="HARD"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Hard concept</span></span></div></div></section><div class="g9-split"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-construction" data-blueprint-slot="construction" data-required="true"><section id="g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-1" class="g9-cu" data-g9-cu="CU-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-1"><div class="g9-component g9-c-unit-header" data-g9-component="UNIT_HEADER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="UNIT_HEADER" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-1"><div class="g9-unit-head"><span class="g9-unit-no" aria-hidden="true">1</span><h3>Say whether the observer is accelerating; keep the physical forces identical in both descriptions and add −m a_frame only for the accelerating observer.</h3><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-concept">Hard concept</span></div><nav class="g9-cu-nav" data-g9-unit-navigation aria-label="Concept Book section navigation"><span data-g9-route-position>Section 4 of 14</span><span class="g9-cu-nav-links"><a data-g9-prev-section href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-1" aria-label="Previous section: Pick one body and admit only forces that answer &#x27;who acts on this body?&#x27;; a partner force, or the components of a force already listed, never joins that list as an extra force.">← Previous</a><a data-g9-next-section href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-1" aria-label="Next section: Choose one body and a signed axis, add every external force on that body, and divide the net force by the mass; on a fixed incline, choose axes along and across the contact.">Next →</a></span></nav></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-banner" data-g9-component="KEY_STEP" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="BANNER"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="inferential_jump"><h4>The key step</h4><p>A force-based account depends on the observer: a non-accelerating observer uses the physical interaction forces, while an accelerating observer must add the corresponding pseudo-force convention to describe the same observed motion consistently.</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-tile" data-g9-component="MODEL_CONTRACT" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_TILE"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-prerequisites"><summary>What you need before this</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="entry_assumptions"><h4>Prerequisites</h4><ul><li>Can build a force inventory for one chosen body.</li></ul><ul><li data-g9-prereq="CAP-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP" data-bridged="true"><a href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP">Assign each force arrow to the body on which that force acts.</a></li></ul></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-step-cards" data-g9-component="CONSTRUCTION_STEPS" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEP_CARDS" data-g9-component-items="3" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-1"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="construction"><ol><li data-g9-step="NLM4-1"><strong>Describe the same loose object first from the roadside and then from inside a bus, stating whether each observer is accelerating.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> The observer choice is part of the model; leaving it implicit makes two different descriptions look contradictory.<br><em>Result:</em> roadside: observer acceleration zero.  bus during start: observer acceleration forward.</li><li data-g9-step="NLM4-2"><strong>Keep the physical interactions unchanged and compare what the accelerating bus observer sees the loose object do relative to the bus.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> The apparent backward motion comes from the observer&#x27;s changing motion, not from a newly discovered physical contact on the object.<br><em>Result:</em> bus accelerates forward -&gt; loose object appears to shift backward relative to the bus.</li><li data-g9-step="NLM4-3"><strong>Add a backward pseudo-force only in the accelerating-observer description and compare it with the roadside account.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> The added convention compensates for the observer&#x27;s acceleration without inventing a new physical interaction.<br><em>Result:</em> roadside account: physical forces only.  accelerating-bus account: same physical forces plus backward pseudo-force.</li></ol></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-equation-card" data-g9-component="EQUATIONS" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_EQUATION_CARD" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-1"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-equations"><summary>Formula reference · meaning and conditions</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="equation_matrix"><h4>Equations and validity</h4><div class="g9-table-scroll" data-g9-equation-matrix><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Equation</th><th scope="col">What it tells you</th><th scope="col">When you can use it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr data-g9-relation-ref="REL-NLM-ELEVATOR-SUPPORT"><td><div class="g9-math" data-g9-math="mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></math></div></td><td><p>For a person that remains in contact with a horizontal scale in an inertial ground frame, with upward positive and g the positive magnitude of gravitational acceleration, the signed vertical force balance is N - m g = m a_y.</p></td><td><ul><li>The ground frame is treated as inertial and the positive vertical direction is upward.</li><li>The person remains in contact with the scale, so N &gt;= 0.</li><li>The person and scale share the stated vertical acceleration while contact persists.</li><li>g is the positive magnitude of the downward gravitational acceleration.</li></ul></td></tr><tr data-g9-relation-ref="REL-NLM-PSEUDO-FORCE"><td><div class="g9-math" data-g9-math="mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mtext>pseudo</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mtext>frame</mtext></msub></mrow></math></div></td><td><p>In a one-axis frame accelerating with signed acceleration a_frame relative to an inertial frame, the pseudo-force assigned to a mass m is opposite the frame acceleration and has magnitude m|a_frame|.</p></td><td><ul><li>The selected observer frame has nonzero translational acceleration relative to the inertial frame.</li><li>F_pseudo and a_frame use the same declared axis and sign convention.</li><li>The mass is positive and treated as constant.</li><li>The pseudo-force is a frame-dependent modelling term, not a physical interaction and not a Newton-third-law partner.</li></ul></td></tr><tr data-g9-relation-ref="REL-NLM-HANGING-BOB-TILT"><td><div class="g9-math" data-g9-math="mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">tan</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mtext>frame</mtext></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow><mi>g</mi></mfrac></mrow></math></div></td><td><p>For a bob stationary relative to a horizontally accelerating vehicle, with a massless taut string and no other horizontal interaction, the equilibrium string tilt magnitude theta from vertical satisfies tan(theta)=|a_frame|/g in the vehicle frame.</p></td><td><ul><li>The bob is stationary relative to the vehicle after transients, so its relative acceleration is zero.</li><li>The vehicle has constant horizontal acceleration over the represented state.</li><li>The string is massless and taut, and air resistance is neglected.</li><li>theta is measured from the vertical; the bob tilts opposite the vehicle acceleration.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-predict-reveal-worked-card" data-g9-component="WORKED_EXAMPLE" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="PREDICT_REVEAL_WORKED_CARD" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-1"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_anchor"><h4>Watch one · predict before reveal</h4><p>A 60 kg person stands on a scale in a lift that accelerates upward at 2 m/s². Take g = 10 m/s² and up as positive. Find the scale reading using the ground frame, then again using the lift frame with a pseudo-force, and compare.</p><p class="g9-worked-instruction">Before opening each step, say what you would do next.</p><ol class="g9-watch-steps"><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 1, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>Ground frame: the physical forces are the scale&#x27;s push N up and the weight 600 N down, and the person accelerates at +2 m/s².</p></div></details></li><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 2, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>Ground-frame equation: N − 600 = 60 × 2, so N = 720 N.</p></div></details></li><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 3, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>Lift frame: the person is at rest, and the frame accelerates at +2 m/s², so add F_pseudo = −m a_frame = −120 N.</p></div></details></li><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 4, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>Lift-frame equation: N − 600 − 120 = 0, so N = 720 N, the same reading.</p></div></details></li></ol><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="worked-result"><summary>Result and independent check</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_result"><h4>Result</h4><p>Both descriptions give a scale reading of 720 N.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_check"><h4>Check</h4><p>With the lift at rest or moving steadily, a_frame = 0, the pseudo-force vanishes and both frames give N = 600 N.</p></div></div></details></div></div></section></section></div><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-representation" data-blueprint-slot="representation" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-staged-visual" data-g9-component="STAGED_VISUAL" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="STAGED_VISUAL" data-g9-component-items="4" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-1"><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="CU-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-1-REP-NLM-LIFT-SCALE-60KG" data-g9-stage="TEACHING" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-LIFT-SCALE-60KG" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="4" data-g9-stages-total="4" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-LIFTSCALE-V0 VIS-NLM-LIFTSCALE-V1 VIS-NLM-LIFTSCALE-V2 VIS-NLM-LIFTSCALE-V3" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 840 380" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-1-REP-NLM-LIFT-SCALE-60KG-1--rep-nlm-liftscale-title g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-1-REP-NLM-LIFT-SCALE-60KG-1--rep-nlm-liftscale-desc">\n  <title id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-1-REP-NLM-LIFT-SCALE-60KG-1--rep-nlm-liftscale-title">A 60 kg person on a scale in a lift accelerating upward at 2 m/s²</title>\n  <desc id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-1-REP-NLM-LIFT-SCALE-60KG-1--rep-nlm-liftscale-desc">Stage 1: a 60 kg person stands on a scale in a lift accelerating upward at 2 m/s²; g = 10 m/s² and up is positive. Stage 2: in the ground frame the forces are the scale\'s push N up and the 600 N weight down, and N − 600 = 60 × 2 gives N = 720 N. Stage 3: in the lift frame the person is at rest and a pseudo-force −m a_frame = −120 N is added, so N − 600 − 120 = 0 and N = 720 N. Stage 4: both frames give the same 720 N scale reading.</desc>\n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-1-REP-NLM-LIFT-SCALE-60KG-1--rep-nlm-liftscale-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-1-REP-NLM-LIFT-SCALE-60KG-1--rep-nlm-liftscale-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-LIFTSCALE-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">A person on a scale in a lift accelerating upward</text>\n    <rect x="60" y="50" width="220" height="290" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <rect x="130" y="322" width="80" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="170" y="336" text-anchor="middle">scale</text>\n    <circle cx="170" cy="221" r="9" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="170" y1="230" x2="170" y2="294" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="170" y1="294" x2="160" y2="322" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="170" y1="294" x2="180" y2="322" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="156" y1="244" x2="184" y2="244" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="195" y="230">60 kg</text>\n    <line x1="305" y1="260" x2="305" y2="160" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-1-REP-NLM-LIFT-SCALE-60KG-1--rep-nlm-liftscale-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="290" y="285">a = 2 m/s² up</text>\n    <text x="20" y="370">g = 10 m/s²; up is positive.</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-LIFTSCALE-V1" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <circle cx="420" cy="170" r="6" fill="currentColor" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="420" y1="170" x2="420" y2="80" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-1-REP-NLM-LIFT-SCALE-60KG-1--rep-nlm-liftscale-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="430" y="90">N (scale on person)</text>\n    <line x1="420" y1="170" x2="420" y2="250" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-1-REP-NLM-LIFT-SCALE-60KG-1--rep-nlm-liftscale-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="430" y="246">weight 600 N (Earth)</text>\n    <text x="610" y="60" font-weight="600">Ground frame</text>\n    <text x="610" y="84">N − 600 = 60 × 2</text>\n    <text x="610" y="106">N = 720 N</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-LIFTSCALE-V2" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <line x1="395" y1="170" x2="395" y2="230" stroke="#1f6fb2" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-dasharray="6 4" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-1-REP-NLM-LIFT-SCALE-60KG-1--rep-nlm-liftscale-arrow-acc)"/>\n    <text x="360" y="320" fill="#1f6fb2">pseudo-force 120 N</text>\n    <text x="360" y="340" fill="#1f6fb2">(lift frame only)</text>\n    <text x="610" y="150" font-weight="600">Lift frame</text>\n    <text x="610" y="174">person at rest; add</text>\n    <text x="610" y="196">−m a_frame = −120 N</text>\n    <text x="610" y="218">N − 600 − 120 = 0,</text>\n    <text x="610" y="240">so N = 720 N</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-LIFTSCALE-V3" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="610" y="300" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">Both frames: the scale</text>\n    <text x="610" y="322" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">reads 720 N</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n<div class="g9-stage-controls"><div class="g9-stage-chips" role="group" aria-label="Stages"><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="0" data-g9-stage-desc="Show the person on the scale and the lift&#x27;s upward acceleration.">1: The lift</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="1" data-g9-stage-desc="Write Newton&#x27;s second law with the physical forces only.">2: Ground frame</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="2" data-g9-stage-desc="Add the pseudo-force for the accelerating observer.">3: Lift frame</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="3" data-g9-stage-desc="Compare the two descriptions&#x27; scale readings.">4: Same reading</button></div><p class="g9-stage-desc" data-g9-stage-desc-text></p><div class="g9-stage-stepper"><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="prev">Previous stage</button><span data-g9-stage-label></span><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="next">Next stage</button></div></div><figcaption data-g9-block="representation_bridge" data-g9-caption="purpose">Show the 60 kg person on the scale in the lift accelerating up at 2 m/s², described from the ground and from the lift.</figcaption></figure></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-repair_closure" data-blueprint-slot="repair_closure" data-required="true"><section class="g9-cu-support" data-g9-support-for="CU-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-1"><h3>Say whether the observer is accelerating; keep the physical forces identical in both descriptions and add −m a_frame only for the accelerating observer.</h3><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-trap-card" data-g9-component="TRAP_REPAIR" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_TRAP_CARD" data-g9-component-items="1" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-1"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-trap-repair"><summary>Mistake clinic · diagnose and repair</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="wrong_path"><h4>A tempting wrong path</h4><ul><li>The same ordinary-force diagram can be used unchanged in any accelerating observer&#x27;s frame.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="diagnose"><h4>Diagnose</h4><ul><li>A loose mass appears to accelerate backward inside a bus that starts forward. Can the bus observer use only the same physical-force inventory as the roadside observer?</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="repair"><h4>Repair</h4><ul><li>State the observer first. Use physical interactions alone for a non-accelerating observer; for an accelerating observer include the corresponding pseudo-force consistently.</li></ul></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-triad" data-g9-component="QUICK_CHECK" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="TRIAD" data-g9-component-items="2" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-1"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="independent_check"><h4>1-2-3 quick check</h4><ol class="g9-triad"><li data-g9-triad-role=""><span class="g9-triad-head">1 · Check</span><span class="g9-triad-text">Both descriptions must predict the same observable, such as the scale reading or the string direction.</span></li><li data-g9-triad-role=""><span class="g9-triad-head">2 · Check</span><span class="g9-triad-text">Set the frame&#x27;s acceleration to zero: the pseudo-force must vanish and the two descriptions must become identical.</span></li></ol></div></div></section></section></div></div><div class="g9-split g9-split-support-only"><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-repair_closure" data-blueprint-slot="repair_closure" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-recall-card" data-g9-component="EXIT_RECALL" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="RECALL_CARD"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_task"><h4>Now you do one</h4><p>A bus accelerates forward and a loose object appears to move backward relative to the bus. Compare the roadside and bus-observer force descriptions without calculating any magnitude.</p></div><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your answer<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1A-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-exit"><summary>Model answer</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1A-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-exit"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_answer"><p>The roadside description uses the physical interactions only. The accelerating bus description adds a backward pseudo-force convention to account for the observer&#x27;s acceleration.</p><ol><li>The physical interactions on the object do not change merely because the observer changes.</li><li>The bus observer is accelerating relative to the roadside observer.</li><li>The pseudo-force belongs to that accelerating-observer description rather than to a new physical agent.</li></ol></div></template></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-chips" data-g9-component="PRACTICE_LINKS" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="CHIPS"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="practice_navigation"><h4>Practice this concept in Core2</h4><ul><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2009-P2-Q40" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2009-P2-Q40">IIT-JEE · 2009 · Paper II · Q40</a></li></ul></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-chips" data-g9-component="LEARNING_TRANSITIONS" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="CHIPS"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="learning_transitions"><h4>Next steps</h4><div class="g9-transitions-row" data-g9-transitions><a class="g9-transition-chip g9-chip-practice" data-g9-transition="PRACTICE" href="core2.html">Practice this concept &rarr;</a><a class="g9-transition-chip g9-chip-qb" data-g9-transition="QUESTION_BANK" href="../../../public/question-bank/index.html?capability=MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE">All questions in Question Bank &rarr;</a></div></div></div></section></div></div></article><article id="g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1A" data-g9-search-text="Physics Laws of Motion Net force, not any one force, sets acceleration Medium concept Net force, not any one force, sets acceleration" class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-concept-header" data-g9-component="CONCEPT_HEADER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="CONCEPT_HEADER"><h2>Net force, not any one force, sets acceleration</h2><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1A" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-NLM-FIRST-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Laws of Motion"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Laws of Motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Net force, not any one force, sets acceleration"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Net force, not any one force, sets acceleration</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span></div></div></section><div class="g9-split"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-construction" data-blueprint-slot="construction" data-required="true"><section id="g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-1" class="g9-cu" data-g9-cu="CU-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-1"><div class="g9-component g9-c-unit-header" data-g9-component="UNIT_HEADER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="UNIT_HEADER" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-1"><div class="g9-unit-head"><span class="g9-unit-no" aria-hidden="true">1</span><h3>Choose one body and a signed axis, add every external force on that body, and divide the net force by the mass; on a fixed incline, choose axes along and across the contact.</h3><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-concept">Medium concept</span></div><nav class="g9-cu-nav" data-g9-unit-navigation aria-label="Concept Book section navigation"><span data-g9-route-position>Section 5 of 14</span><span class="g9-cu-nav-links"><a data-g9-prev-section href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-1" aria-label="Previous section: Say whether the observer is accelerating; keep the physical forces identical in both descriptions and add −m a_frame only for the accelerating observer.">← Previous</a><a data-g9-next-section href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-1" aria-label="Next section: Before giving friction a direction, imagine the contact made perfectly slippery and ask which way the surfaces would slide relative to each other; friction opposes that, and is zero if there is no such tendency.">Next →</a></span></nav></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-banner" data-g9-component="KEY_STEP" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="BANNER"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="inferential_jump"><h4>The key step</h4><p>For a chosen body of positive mass in the Grade 9 inertial-frame model, acceleration points with the signed net external force and satisfies F_net = m a.</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-tile" data-g9-component="MODEL_CONTRACT" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_TILE"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-prerequisites"><summary>What you need before this</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="entry_assumptions"><h4>Prerequisites</h4><ul><li>Can choose one body, draw its external forces, and distinguish zero from nonzero net force.</li></ul><ul><li data-g9-prereq="CAP-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP" data-bridged="true"><a href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP">Assign each force arrow to the body on which that force acts.</a></li></ul></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-step-cards" data-g9-component="CONSTRUCTION_STEPS" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEP_CARDS" data-g9-component-items="4" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-1"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="construction"><ol><li data-g9-step="NLM6-1"><strong>Choose one body, declare a positive direction, and list only external forces acting on that body.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> The net-force relation applies to one body&#x27;s external force sum.<br><em>Result:</em> one body; one signed axis; signed external-force list.</li><li data-g9-step="NLM6-2"><strong>Add the signed forces to obtain F_net, then use F_net = m a.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> The net external force on the chosen body sets its acceleration: F_net = m a holds for that one body, with m &gt; 0, in an inertial frame.<br><em>Result:</em> F_net = signed sum; a = F_net / m.</li><li data-g9-step="NLM6-3"><strong>Return to an equal-opposite force case.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> The relation must recover zero acceleration when the signed force sum is zero.<br><em>Result:</em> F_net = 0 -&gt; a = 0 for m &gt; 0.</li><li data-g9-step="NLM6-4"><strong>Choose axes that make the active constraint easy to express; for a fixed inclined contact, use parallel/perpendicular axes when helpful, resolve the physical forces into those axes, and write separate signed Newton-II equations.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> Newton II is coordinate-independent. Aligning one axis with the contact makes maintained contact explicit as a_perp=0 while leaving the tangential acceleration free to be nonzero.<br><em>Result:</em> contact-aligned model: Sigma F_parallel = m a_parallel; Sigma F_perp = m a_perp, with maintained fixed contact -&gt; a_perp = 0</li></ol></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-equation-card" data-g9-component="EQUATIONS" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_EQUATION_CARD" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-1"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-equations"><summary>Formula reference · meaning and conditions</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="equation_matrix"><h4>Equations and validity</h4><div class="g9-table-scroll" data-g9-equation-matrix><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Equation</th><th scope="col">What it tells you</th><th scope="col">When you can use it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr data-g9-relation-ref="REL-NEWTON-SECOND-LAW"><td><div class="g9-math" data-g9-math="mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mtext>net</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow></math></div></td><td><p>The vector net external force on a chosen body equals its mass times its acceleration.</p></td><td><ul><li>m &gt; 0.</li><li>F_net and a refer to the same chosen body.</li><li>All signed components use one declared axis.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-predict-reveal-worked-card" data-g9-component="WORKED_EXAMPLE" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="PREDICT_REVEAL_WORKED_CARD" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-1"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_anchor"><h4>Watch one · predict before reveal</h4><p>A 10 kg crate is pulled up a smooth ramp inclined at 30° by a rope parallel to the ramp with a force of 70 N. Take g = 10 m/s². Using axes along and perpendicular to the ramp, find the crate&#x27;s acceleration and the normal force, and the pull that would give zero acceleration.</p><p class="g9-worked-instruction">Before opening each step, say what you would do next.</p><ol class="g9-watch-steps"><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 1, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>Body: the crate. +x up the ramp, +y out of the ramp. Forces: the 70 N pull along +x, the 100 N weight vertically down, the normal force N along +y.</p></div></details></li><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 2, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>Resolve the weight: 100 × sin 30° = 50 N down the ramp and 100 × cos 30° ≈ 86.6 N into the ramp.</p></div></details></li><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 3, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>Along the ramp: F_net = 70 − 50 = 20 N, so a = 20/10 = 2 m/s² up the ramp.</p></div></details></li><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 4, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>Across the ramp the crate keeps contact, so a_y = 0 and N ≈ 86.6 N. A 50 N pull would make F_net = 0 along the ramp, so a = 0.</p></div></details></li></ol><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="worked-result"><summary>Result and independent check</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_result"><h4>Result</h4><p>a = 2 m/s² up the ramp, N ≈ 86.6 N, and a 50 N pull would give zero acceleration.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_check"><h4>Check</h4><p>Units: 20 N / 10 kg = 2 m/s². On level ground (0°) the same method gives N = 100 N and no down-slope weight component.</p></div></div></details></div></div></section></section></div><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-representation" data-blueprint-slot="representation" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-staged-visual" data-g9-component="STAGED_VISUAL" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="STAGED_VISUAL" data-g9-component-items="3" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-1"><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="CU-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-1-REP-NLM-RAMP-PULL-70N" data-g9-stage="TEACHING" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-RAMP-PULL-70N" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="3" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-RAMP70-V0 VIS-NLM-RAMP70-V1 VIS-NLM-RAMP70-V2" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 780 330" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-1-REP-NLM-RAMP-PULL-70N-1--rep-nlm-ramp70-title g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-1-REP-NLM-RAMP-PULL-70N-1--rep-nlm-ramp70-desc">\n  <title id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-1-REP-NLM-RAMP-PULL-70N-1--rep-nlm-ramp70-title">Crate on a smooth 30° ramp pulled by 70 N: axes along and across the contact</title>\n  <desc id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-1-REP-NLM-RAMP-PULL-70N-1--rep-nlm-ramp70-desc">Stage 1: A 10 kg crate on a smooth fixed ramp inclined at 30° is pulled by a rope parallel to the ramp with 70 N; g = 10 m/s². Stage 2: The forces on the crate are its 100 N weight, the normal force N and the 70 N tension; along the ramp 70 − 100 sin 30° = 10a, across it N − 100 cos 30° = 0 because the fixed contact gives a_perp = 0. Stage 3: a = 20/10 = 2 m/s² up the ramp, N ≈ 86.6 N, and a 50 N pull would give zero acceleration.</desc>\n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-1-REP-NLM-RAMP-PULL-70N-1--rep-nlm-ramp70-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-1-REP-NLM-RAMP-PULL-70N-1--rep-nlm-ramp70-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-RAMP70-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">A 10 kg crate pulled up a smooth 30° ramp</text>\n    <polygon points="40,270 370,270 370,79.5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="84" y="262" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14">30°</text>\n    <path d="M70 270 A30 30 0 0 0 66 255" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2"/>\n    <g transform="rotate(-30 178.7 170.3)"><rect x="150.7 " y="153.3" width="56" height="34" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/></g>\n    <text x="178.7" y="175.3" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14">10 kg</text>\n    <line x1="204.7" y1="155.3" x2="274" y2="115.3" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="281.7" y="114.3">rope, 70 N</text>\n    <text x="20" y="310">rope parallel to the ramp; g = 10 m/s²</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-RAMP70-V1" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <line x1="178.7" y1="170.3" x2="178.7" y2="240.3" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-1-REP-NLM-RAMP-PULL-70N-1--rep-nlm-ramp70-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="186.7" y="246.3">mg = 100 N</text>\n    <line x1="169.7" y1="154.7" x2="139.7" y2="102.7" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-1-REP-NLM-RAMP-PULL-70N-1--rep-nlm-ramp70-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="131.7" y="96.7" text-anchor="end">N</text>\n    <line x1="204.7" y1="155.3" x2="256.7" y2="125.3" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-1-REP-NLM-RAMP-PULL-70N-1--rep-nlm-ramp70-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="264.7" y="147.3">T = 70 N</text>\n    <line x1="178.7" y1="170.3" x2="109.4" y2="210.3" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3 4"/>\n    <line x1="178.7" y1="170.3" x2="208.7" y2="222.2" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3 4"/>\n    <text x="440" y="60" font-weight="600">axes along / across the ramp</text>\n    <text x="440" y="82">along (up +): 70 − 100 sin 30° = 10a</text>\n    <text x="440" y="104">across: N − 100 cos 30° = 0</text>\n    <text x="440" y="126">(fixed contact: a_perp = 0)</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-RAMP70-V2" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="440" y="170" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">a = 20/10 = 2 m/s² up the ramp</text>\n    <text x="440" y="192" fill="#1f6fb2">N = 100 cos 30° ≈ 86.6 N</text>\n    <text x="440" y="214">a = 0 needs a pull of 50 N</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n<div class="g9-stage-controls"><div class="g9-stage-chips" role="group" aria-label="Stages"><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="0" data-g9-stage-desc="Show the crate, the ramp and the rope.">1: The ramp</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="1" data-g9-stage-desc="Draw the forces on the crate and the contact-aligned axes.">2: Forces and axes</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="2" data-g9-stage-desc="Solve both axis equations.">3: Results</button></div><p class="g9-stage-desc" data-g9-stage-desc-text></p><div class="g9-stage-stepper"><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="prev">Previous stage</button><span data-g9-stage-label></span><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="next">Next stage</button></div></div><figcaption data-g9-block="representation_bridge" data-g9-caption="purpose">Show the 10 kg crate on the 30° ramp with its three forces and axes along and across the contact.</figcaption></figure></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-repair_closure" data-blueprint-slot="repair_closure" data-required="true"><section class="g9-cu-support" data-g9-support-for="CU-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-1"><h3>Choose one body and a signed axis, add every external force on that body, and divide the net force by the mass; on a fixed incline, choose axes along and across the contact.</h3><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-trap-card" data-g9-component="TRAP_REPAIR" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_TRAP_CARD" data-g9-component-items="2" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-1"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-trap-repair"><summary>Mistake clinic · diagnose and repair</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="wrong_path"><h4>A tempting wrong path</h4><ul><li>Acceleration points with whichever individual force looks largest in the diagram.</li><li>If a block on a fixed incline has zero acceleration perpendicular to the plane, its total acceleration must be zero.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="diagnose"><h4>Diagnose</h4><ul><li>Two 10 N forces act opposite each other on a body. Does either force make the body accelerate in its direction?</li><li>Can a block remain in contact with a fixed plane while speeding up down the plane?</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="repair"><h4>Repair</h4><ul><li>Add all signed external forces first; acceleration follows the net force, not an isolated arrow.</li><li>Yes. Maintained contact sets only a_perp=0. The parallel component a_parallel is determined independently by the net force along the plane.</li></ul></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-triad" data-g9-component="QUICK_CHECK" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="TRIAD" data-g9-component-items="2" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-1"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="independent_check"><h4>1-2-3 quick check</h4><ol class="g9-triad"><li data-g9-triad-role=""><span class="g9-triad-head">1 · Check</span><span class="g9-triad-text">The net force in newtons divided by the mass in kilograms must give m/s².</span></li><li data-g9-triad-role=""><span class="g9-triad-head">2 · Check</span><span class="g9-triad-text">Flatten the incline to 0°: the method must give N = mg and no down-slope weight component, the level-floor case.</span></li></ol></div></div></section></section></div></div><div class="g9-split g9-split-support-only"><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-repair_closure" data-blueprint-slot="repair_closure" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-recall-card" data-g9-component="EXIT_RECALL" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="RECALL_CARD"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_task"><h4>Now you do one</h4><p>A 4 kg body has a net external force of +12 N along a declared axis. Find its acceleration.</p></div><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your answer<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1A-MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-exit"><summary>Model answer</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1A-MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-exit"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_answer"><p>The acceleration is +3 m/s^2.</p><ol><li>Use F_net = m a for the chosen body.</li><li>a = +12 N / 4 kg = +3 m/s^2.</li><li>The positive sign matches the net-force direction.</li></ol></div></template></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-chips" data-g9-component="PRACTICE_LINKS" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="CHIPS"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="practice_navigation"><h4>Practice this concept in Core2</h4><ul><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q03" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q03">IIT-JEE · 2007 · Paper I · Q3</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q10" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q10">IIT-JEE · 2007 · Paper I · Q10</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33">IIT-JEE · 2008 · Paper II · Q33</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P1-Q41" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P1-Q41">IIT-JEE · 2011 · Paper I · Q41</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q34" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q34">IIT-JEE · 2011 · Paper II · Q34</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P2-Q19" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P2-Q19">JEE (Advanced) · 2014 · Paper 2 · Q19</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13">JEE (Advanced) · 2020 · Paper 1 · Q13</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P1-Q08" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P1-Q08">JEE (Advanced) · 2014 · Paper 1 · Q8</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-NEET-2020-E1-Q160" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-NEET-2020-E1-Q160">NEET (UG) · 2020 · English Set E1 · Q160</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2009-P1-Q49" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2009-P1-Q49">IIT-JEE · 2009 · Paper I · Q49</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2009-P2-Q40" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2009-P2-Q40">IIT-JEE · 2009 · Paper II · Q40</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2009-P2-Q55" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2009-P2-Q55">IIT-JEE · 2009 · Paper II · Q55</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEMAIN-2026-06APR-S2-Q46" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-JEEMAIN-2026-06APR-S2-Q46">JEE Main · 2026 · Session 2 · 06 Apr 2026 · Shift 2 · Q46</a></li></ul></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-chips" data-g9-component="LEARNING_TRANSITIONS" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="CHIPS"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="learning_transitions"><h4>Next steps</h4><div class="g9-transitions-row" data-g9-transitions><a class="g9-transition-chip g9-chip-practice" data-g9-transition="PRACTICE" href="core2.html">Practice this concept &rarr;</a><a class="g9-transition-chip g9-chip-qb" data-g9-transition="QUESTION_BANK" href="../../../public/question-bank/index.html?capability=MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW">All questions in Question Bank &rarr;</a></div></div></div></section></div></div></article><article id="g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1A" data-g9-search-text="Physics Laws of Motion Friction opposes relative sliding at a contact Medium concept Friction opposes relative sliding at a contact" class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-concept-header" data-g9-component="CONCEPT_HEADER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="CONCEPT_HEADER"><h2>Friction opposes relative sliding at a contact</h2><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1A" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-NLM-FIRST-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Laws of Motion"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Laws of Motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION" data-g9-meta-value="Friction opposes relative sliding at a contact"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Friction opposes relative sliding at a contact</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span></div></div></section><div class="g9-split"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-construction" data-blueprint-slot="construction" data-required="true"><section id="g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-1" class="g9-cu" data-g9-cu="CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-1"><div class="g9-component g9-c-unit-header" data-g9-component="UNIT_HEADER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="UNIT_HEADER" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-1"><div class="g9-unit-head"><span class="g9-unit-no" aria-hidden="true">1</span><h3>Before giving friction a direction, imagine the contact made perfectly slippery and ask which way the surfaces would slide relative to each other; friction opposes that, and is zero if there is no such tendency.</h3><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-concept">Medium concept</span></div><nav class="g9-cu-nav" data-g9-unit-navigation aria-label="Concept Book section navigation"><span data-g9-route-position>Section 6 of 14</span><span class="g9-cu-nav-links"><a data-g9-prev-section href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-1" aria-label="Previous section: Choose one body and a signed axis, add every external force on that body, and divide the net force by the mass; on a fixed incline, choose axes along and across the contact.">← Previous</a><a data-g9-next-section href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-1" aria-label="Next section: Name each force of an interaction as &#x27;A on B&#x27; or &#x27;B on A&#x27;, and put it on the diagram of the body it acts on, so the pair never meets in one sum.">Next →</a></span></nav></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-banner" data-g9-component="KEY_STEP" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="BANNER"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="inferential_jump"><h4>The key step</h4><p>Friction is a contact force along the surface that opposes relative sliding, or the tendency for relative sliding, between the contacting surfaces; it is not defined by the object&#x27;s overall velocity alone.</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-tile" data-g9-component="MODEL_CONTRACT" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_TILE"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-prerequisites"><summary>What you need before this</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="entry_assumptions"><h4>Prerequisites</h4><ul><li>Can choose one body and identify external contact interactions acting on it.</li></ul><ul><li data-g9-prereq="CAP-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP" data-bridged="true"><a href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP">Assign each force arrow to the body on which that force acts.</a></li></ul></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-info" data-g9-component="QUESTION_BRIDGE" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_INFO" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-1"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-question-bridge"><summary>Why this section matters for the question set</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="question_bridge"><h4>This unit builds toward</h4><ul><li data-g9-bridge-question="PYQ-PHY-INJSO-2023-Q24"><strong>INJSO|2023|28 Jan 2023|Physics|Q24</strong> · D2<br>The move learners miss: A tyre is part of the chosen system, so a force attributed to the tyre is not the external agent that slows the entire rider+cycle system; the ground is. <a class="g9-bridge-link" data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-INJSO-2023-Q24" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-INJSO-2023-Q24">Try it in Core2</a></li></ul></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-step-cards" data-g9-component="CONSTRUCTION_STEPS" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEP_CARDS" data-g9-component-items="4" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-1"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="construction"><ol><li data-g9-step="NLM5-1" data-g9-crux-step><span class="g9-crux-tag">The step the hard question turns on</span><strong>Choose the body or system and name the exact contact before deciding friction; for a contact between two bodies, keep the two friction forces as an equal-and-opposite third-law pair on different bodies.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> Friction belongs to one contact interaction. The force on A by B and the force on B by A are a third-law pair, so neither may be moved onto the other body&#x27;s free-body diagram.<br><em>Result:</em> chosen body/system; named contact and partner; tangent direction; friction owner and receiver fixed.</li><li data-g9-step="NLM5-2"><strong>Imagine the relative sliding, or tendency to slide, at that named contact if tangential friction were absent; point friction on the chosen body opposite that relative tendency.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> Contact friction resists relative sliding between the surfaces.<br><em>Result:</em> contact-specific slip tendency -&gt; friction direction; direction need not oppose the body&#x27;s velocity.</li><li data-g9-step="NLM5-3"><strong>Check a conveyor-belt case where the belt can frictionally accelerate an object in the same direction as the object&#x27;s motion.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> The counterexample falsifies the shortcut &#x27;friction always points opposite velocity&#x27;.<br><em>Result:</em> friction direction follows contact slip tendency, not a universal page-direction rule.</li><li data-g9-step="NLM5-4"><strong>Decide whether any tangential friction is required at all; allow friction to be zero when the contact has neither relative sliding nor a tendency to slide.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> Friction is a responsive tangential contact force, not an automatic nonzero force merely because two rough surfaces touch.<br><em>Result:</em> contact-state check -&gt; friction may be zero when no tangential force is required.</li></ol></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-equation-card" data-g9-component="EQUATIONS" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_EQUATION_CARD" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-1"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-equations"><summary>Formula reference · meaning and conditions</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="equation_matrix"><h4>Equations and validity</h4><div class="g9-table-scroll" data-g9-equation-matrix><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Equation</th><th scope="col">What it tells you</th><th scope="col">When you can use it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr data-g9-relation-ref="REL-NLM-STATIC-FRICTION-BOUND"><td><div class="g9-math" data-g9-math="mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>|</mo><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></div></td><td><p>For a contact that remains static, the static-friction magnitude adjusts to the value required by the tangential dynamics up to the limiting value mu_s N; equality applies only at impending slip.</p></td><td><ul><li>The surfaces are in contact and have no relative sliding during the state being tested.</li><li>N is obtained from the actual free-body dynamics, not assumed to equal mg.</li><li>Static friction opposes the impending relative slip direction.</li><li>Equality |f_s| = mu_s N is used only at impending slip.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-predict-reveal-worked-card" data-g9-component="WORKED_EXAMPLE" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="PREDICT_REVEAL_WORKED_CARD" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-1"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_anchor"><h4>Watch one · predict before reveal</h4><p>A book rests on a tray. A waiter first carries the tray at steady speed in a straight line, then speeds up forward; the book never slips. Find the friction force of the tray on the book in each stage, including whether it can be zero.</p><p class="g9-worked-instruction">Before opening each step, say what you would do next.</p><ol class="g9-watch-steps"><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 1, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>Choose the book as the body; the contact is the tray&#x27;s top surface, and friction acts along it.</p></div></details></li><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 2, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>Steady carry: if the tray became perfectly slippery, the book would keep moving with the tray, so there is no slip tendency and the friction is zero.</p></div></details></li><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 3, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>Speeding up: without friction the book would keep its old speed while the tray moved ahead, so the book would slip backward relative to the tray.</p></div></details></li><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 4, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>Static friction opposes that tendency, so the tray&#x27;s friction on the book points forward, the same way the book is moving.</p></div></details></li></ol><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="worked-result"><summary>Result and independent check</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_result"><h4>Result</h4><p>During the steady carry the friction is zero; while the tray speeds up, static friction on the book points forward.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_check"><h4>Check</h4><p>The book speeds up forward and friction is the only horizontal force on it, so by F_net = m a that force must point forward, which agrees.</p></div></div></details></div></div></section></section></div><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-representation" data-blueprint-slot="representation" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-staged-visual" data-g9-component="STAGED_VISUAL" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="STAGED_VISUAL" data-g9-component-items="3" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-1"><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-1-REP-NLM-TRAY-BOOK-FRICTION" data-g9-stage="TEACHING" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-TRAY-BOOK-FRICTION" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="3" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-TRAY-V0 VIS-NLM-TRAY-V1 VIS-NLM-TRAY-V2" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 440 260" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-1-REP-NLM-TRAY-BOOK-FRICTION-1--tray-title g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-1-REP-NLM-TRAY-BOOK-FRICTION-1--tray-desc">\n<title id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-1-REP-NLM-TRAY-BOOK-FRICTION-1--tray-title">Book on a tray: friction follows relative slip tendency</title><desc id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-1-REP-NLM-TRAY-BOOK-FRICTION-1--tray-desc">Three stages compare steady-speed motion and forward acceleration of a tray carrying a book without slip, then reveal slip tendency and friction.</desc>\n<defs><marker id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-1-REP-NLM-TRAY-BOOK-FRICTION-1--tray-a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker></defs>\n<g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-TRAY-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="14" fill="currentColor" stroke="currentColor"><text x="16" y="24" stroke="none" font-weight="700">Book never slips on the tray</text><text x="26" y="54" stroke="none" >steady speed</text><line x1="20" y1="138" x2="185" y2="138" stroke-width="2" /><rect x="72" y="98" width="60" height="40" fill="none" stroke-width="2" /><line x1="42" y1="78" x2="116" y2="78" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-1-REP-NLM-TRAY-BOOK-FRICTION-1--tray-a)"/><text x="48" y="67" stroke="none" >v constant</text><text x="250" y="54" stroke="none" >speeding up</text><line x1="232" y1="138" x2="415" y2="138" stroke-width="2" /><rect x="292" y="98" width="60" height="40" fill="none" stroke-width="2" /><line x1="254" y1="78" x2="340" y2="78" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-1-REP-NLM-TRAY-BOOK-FRICTION-1--tray-a)"/><text x="260" y="67" stroke="none" >a forward</text></g>\n<g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-TRAY-V1" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="14" fill="currentColor" stroke="currentColor"><text x="16" y="178" stroke="none" font-weight="700">Imagine the tray top frictionless:</text><text x="16" y="202" stroke="none" >steady speed → no relative slip tendency</text><text x="16" y="226" stroke="none">speeding up → book tends to slip backward</text><text x="16" y="246" stroke="none">relative to the tray</text></g>\n<g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-TRAY-V2" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="14" fill="currentColor" stroke="currentColor"><text x="16" y="178" stroke="none" font-weight="700">Friction of tray on book:</text><text x="16" y="202" stroke="none" >steady speed → f = 0</text><text x="16" y="226" stroke="none" >speeding up → static friction points forward</text><line x1="292" y1="92" x2="360" y2="92" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-1-REP-NLM-TRAY-BOOK-FRICTION-1--tray-a)"/></g></svg>\n<div class="g9-stage-controls"><div class="g9-stage-chips" role="group" aria-label="Stages"><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="0" data-g9-stage-desc="Show the tray carried at steady speed and then speeding up.">1: Two stages</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="1" data-g9-stage-desc="Imagine the contact slippery and ask which way the book would slide.">2: Slip tendency</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="2" data-g9-stage-desc="Give friction the direction opposing the tendency, or zero.">3: Friction</button></div><p class="g9-stage-desc" data-g9-stage-desc-text></p><div class="g9-stage-stepper"><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="prev">Previous stage</button><span data-g9-stage-label></span><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="next">Next stage</button></div></div><figcaption data-g9-block="representation_bridge" data-g9-caption="purpose">Show the book on the tray in its two stages so friction&#x27;s direction is found from the slip tendency, including the zero case.</figcaption></figure></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-repair_closure" data-blueprint-slot="repair_closure" data-required="true"><section class="g9-cu-support" data-g9-support-for="CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-1"><h3>Before giving friction a direction, imagine the contact made perfectly slippery and ask which way the surfaces would slide relative to each other; friction opposes that, and is zero if there is no such tendency.</h3><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-trap-card" data-g9-component="TRAP_REPAIR" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_TRAP_CARD" data-g9-component-items="2" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-1"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-trap-repair"><summary>Mistake clinic · diagnose and repair</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="wrong_path"><h4>A tempting wrong path</h4><ul><li>Friction always points opposite the object&#x27;s velocity.</li><li>Two rough surfaces in contact must always exert a nonzero friction force.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="diagnose"><h4>Diagnose</h4><ul><li>A moving conveyor belt speeds up a box resting on it. Can the friction on the box point in the same direction as the box&#x27;s motion?</li><li>A crate and rough platform already move together at constant velocity. If no horizontal force is otherwise required, must the friction force be nonzero merely because the surfaces are rough?</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="repair"><h4>Repair</h4><ul><li>Decide the relative sliding tendency at the contact first; friction opposes that tendency.</li><li>No. Ask what relative sliding or slip tendency would occur without friction. If none exists and no tangential force is required, friction can be zero.</li></ul></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-triad" data-g9-component="QUICK_CHECK" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="TRIAD" data-g9-component-items="3" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-1"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="independent_check"><h4>1-2-3 quick check</h4><ol class="g9-triad"><li data-g9-triad-role="CHECK"><span class="g9-triad-head">1 · Check</span><span class="g9-triad-text">CHECK: For a chosen body, name the other surface at the contact and state which way that surface would slip relative to the body if friction vanished.</span></li><li data-g9-triad-role="APPLY"><span class="g9-triad-head">2 · Apply</span><span class="g9-triad-text">APPLY: A conveyor belt moves right faster than a box on it. Predict the friction on the box and the equal-and-opposite friction on the belt.</span></li><li data-g9-triad-role="CONNECT"><span class="g9-triad-head">3 · Connect</span><span class="g9-triad-text">CONNECT: Carry this contact ownership into the quantitative test: after direction is fixed, the next question is how much friction the no-slip motion requires and whether the contact can supply it.</span></li></ol></div></div></section></section></div></div><div class="g9-split g9-split-support-only"><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-repair_closure" data-blueprint-slot="repair_closure" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-recall-card" data-g9-component="EXIT_RECALL" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="RECALL_CARD"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_task"><h4>Now you do one</h4><p>A box is placed on a conveyor belt moving to the right faster than the box. Before the box matches the belt speed, what direction is the friction force on the box?</p></div><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your answer<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1A-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-exit"><summary>Model answer</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1A-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-exit"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_answer"><p>The friction force on the box points to the right.</p><ol><li>Relative to the belt, the box tends to slip left at the contact.</li><li>Friction opposes that relative slip tendency.</li><li>Therefore the belt&#x27;s friction on the box points right and accelerates it.</li></ol></div></template></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-visual-card" data-g9-component="INTERACTIVE_BRIDGE" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="VISUAL_CARD"><aside class="g9-interactive-bridge" data-g9-interactive-bridge><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-interactive">Try Visually</span><h3 class="g9-interactive-title">Static-to-Kinetic Friction Threshold Explorer</h3><p class="g9-interactive-desc">Explore this concept with interactive controls and real-time visual response.</p><a class="g9-bridge-link g9-action-interactive" data-g9-interactive-link href="../../../public/physics/nlm/explorers/friction-threshold/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Try it visually &rarr;</a></aside></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-chips" data-g9-component="LEARNING_TRANSITIONS" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="CHIPS"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="learning_transitions"><h4>Next steps</h4><div class="g9-transitions-row" data-g9-transitions><a class="g9-transition-chip g9-chip-practice" data-g9-transition="PRACTICE" href="core2.html">Practice this concept &rarr;</a><a class="g9-transition-chip g9-chip-qb" data-g9-transition="QUESTION_BANK" href="../../../public/question-bank/index.html?capability=MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION">All questions in Question Bank &rarr;</a></div></div></div></section></div></div></article><article id="g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1A" data-g9-search-text="Physics Laws of Motion Third-law partner forces act on different bodies Medium concept Third-law partner forces act on different bodies" class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-concept-header" data-g9-component="CONCEPT_HEADER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="CONCEPT_HEADER"><h2>Third-law partner forces act on different bodies</h2><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1A" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-NLM-FIRST-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Laws of Motion"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Laws of Motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Third-law partner forces act on different bodies"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Third-law partner forces act on different bodies</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span></div></div></section><div class="g9-split"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-construction" data-blueprint-slot="construction" data-required="true"><section id="g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-1" class="g9-cu" data-g9-cu="CU-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-1"><div class="g9-component g9-c-unit-header" data-g9-component="UNIT_HEADER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="UNIT_HEADER" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-1"><div class="g9-unit-head"><span class="g9-unit-no" aria-hidden="true">1</span><h3>Name each force of an interaction as &#x27;A on B&#x27; or &#x27;B on A&#x27;, and put it on the diagram of the body it acts on, so the pair never meets in one sum.</h3><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-concept">Medium concept</span></div><nav class="g9-cu-nav" data-g9-unit-navigation aria-label="Concept Book section navigation"><span data-g9-route-position>Section 7 of 14</span><span class="g9-cu-nav-links"><a data-g9-prev-section href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-1" aria-label="Previous section: Before giving friction a direction, imagine the contact made perfectly slippery and ask which way the surfaces would slide relative to each other; friction opposes that, and is zero if there is no such tendency.">← Previous</a><a data-g9-next-section href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-1" aria-label="Next section: Find the actual normal force and the friction that rest would need, compare that need with μ_s N, and use μ N as the friction value only at impending slip or once sliding.">Next →</a></span></nav></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-banner" data-g9-component="KEY_STEP" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="BANNER"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="inferential_jump"><h4>The key step</h4><p>One interaction produces two partner forces of equal magnitude and opposite direction, but they act on different bodies and therefore do not cancel in a single body&#x27;s net-force sum.</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-tile" data-g9-component="MODEL_CONTRACT" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_TILE"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-prerequisites"><summary>What you need before this</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="entry_assumptions"><h4>Prerequisites</h4><ul><li>Can assign force arrows to the body on which they act.</li></ul><ul><li data-g9-prereq="CAP-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP" data-bridged="true"><a href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP">Assign each force arrow to the body on which that force acts.</a></li></ul></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-step-cards" data-g9-component="CONSTRUCTION_STEPS" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEP_CARDS" data-g9-component-items="3" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-1"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="construction"><ol><li data-g9-step="NLM7-1"><strong>Name the interaction as &#x27;A on B&#x27; and &#x27;B on A&#x27; before drawing arrows.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> The agent-target wording records which body owns each partner force.<br><em>Result:</em> force of A on B belongs to B; force of B on A belongs to A.</li><li data-g9-step="NLM7-2"><strong>Draw equal-length opposite arrows on the two different body diagrams.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> Newton&#x27;s third-law partners are equal in magnitude and opposite in direction for the same interaction.<br><em>Result:</em> A on B: +20 N on B&#x27;s diagram.  B on A: −20 N on A&#x27;s diagram.  Same size, opposite sign, different bodies.</li><li data-g9-step="NLM7-3"><strong>Ask whether either single body&#x27;s force sum contains both partner arrows.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> A force diagram for one body includes only forces acting on that body.<br><em>Result:</em> third-law partners do not cancel inside one body&#x27;s F_net unless another separate force happens to balance one.</li></ol></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-equation-card" data-g9-component="EQUATIONS" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_EQUATION_CARD" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-1"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-equations"><summary>Formula reference · meaning and conditions</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="equation_matrix"><h4>Equations and validity</h4><div class="g9-table-scroll" data-g9-equation-matrix><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Equation</th><th scope="col">What it tells you</th><th scope="col">When you can use it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr data-g9-relation-ref="REL-NEWTON-SECOND-LAW"><td><div class="g9-math" data-g9-math="mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mtext>net</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow></math></div></td><td><p>The vector net external force on a chosen body equals its mass times its acceleration.</p></td><td><ul><li>m &gt; 0.</li><li>F_net and a refer to the same chosen body.</li><li>All signed components use one declared axis.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-predict-reveal-worked-card" data-g9-component="WORKED_EXAMPLE" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="PREDICT_REVEAL_WORKED_CARD" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-1"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_anchor"><h4>Watch one · predict before reveal</h4><p>In space, a 70 kg astronaut pushes a 350 kg satellite with a force of 140 N. During the push, find the force on each body and each body&#x27;s acceleration.</p><p class="g9-worked-instruction">Before opening each step, say what you would do next.</p><ol class="g9-watch-steps"><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 1, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>The interaction is the contact between hand and satellite: astronaut on satellite and satellite on astronaut.</p></div></details></li><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 2, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>Astronaut on satellite: 140 N, on the satellite&#x27;s diagram. Satellite on astronaut: 140 N the opposite way, on the astronaut&#x27;s diagram.</p></div></details></li><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 3, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>Satellite: a = 140/350 = 0.4 m/s².</p></div></details></li><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 4, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>Astronaut: a = 140/70 = 2 m/s², in the opposite direction. Neither diagram contains both forces, so they do not cancel.</p></div></details></li></ol><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="worked-result"><summary>Result and independent check</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_result"><h4>Result</h4><p>Each body feels 140 N, in opposite directions: the satellite accelerates at 0.4 m/s² away from the astronaut, and the astronaut at 2 m/s² the other way.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_check"><h4>Check</h4><p>Mass times acceleration gives 350 × 0.4 = 140 N and 70 × 2 = 140 N: both equal the one shared interaction force.</p></div></div></details></div></div></section></section></div><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-representation" data-blueprint-slot="representation" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-staged-visual" data-g9-component="STAGED_VISUAL" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="STAGED_VISUAL" data-g9-component-items="3" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-1"><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="CU-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-1-REP-NLM-ASTRONAUT-SATELLITE" data-g9-stage="TEACHING" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-ASTRONAUT-SATELLITE" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="3" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-SPACE-V0 VIS-NLM-SPACE-V1 VIS-NLM-SPACE-V2" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 820 300" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-1-REP-NLM-ASTRONAUT-SATELLITE-1--rep-nlm-space-title g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-1-REP-NLM-ASTRONAUT-SATELLITE-1--rep-nlm-space-desc">\n  <title id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-1-REP-NLM-ASTRONAUT-SATELLITE-1--rep-nlm-space-title">Astronaut and satellite: one interaction, two forces on two bodies</title>\n  <desc id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-1-REP-NLM-ASTRONAUT-SATELLITE-1--rep-nlm-space-desc">Stage 1: In space a 70 kg astronaut pushes a 350 kg satellite with a force of 140 N; no other forces act and both start at rest. Stage 2: The astronaut pushes the satellite with 140 N away from the astronaut, and the satellite pushes the astronaut with 140 N the other way: same size, opposite direction, different bodies. Stage 3: The satellite accelerates at 140/350 = 0.4 m/s² away from the astronaut and the astronaut at 140/70 = 2 m/s² the other way.</desc>\n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-1-REP-NLM-ASTRONAUT-SATELLITE-1--rep-nlm-space-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-1-REP-NLM-ASTRONAUT-SATELLITE-1--rep-nlm-space-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-SPACE-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">In space: a 70 kg astronaut pushes a 350 kg satellite</text>\n    <circle cx="120" cy="120.8" r="10.8" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="120" y1="131.6" x2="120" y2="164" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="120" y1="164" x2="106.5" y2="200" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="120" y1="164" x2="133.5" y2="200" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="102" y1="144.2" x2="138" y2="144.2" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <rect x="200" y="110" width="130" height="80" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="265" y="146" text-anchor="middle">satellite</text>\n    <text x="265" y="168" text-anchor="middle">350 kg</text>\n    <line x1="128" y1="148" x2="200" y2="148" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="80" y="226">astronaut 70 kg</text>\n    <text x="20" y="285">push 140 N; no other forces; both start at rest</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-SPACE-V1" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <line x1="332" y1="150" x2="402" y2="150" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-1-REP-NLM-ASTRONAUT-SATELLITE-1--rep-nlm-space-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="200" y="96">astronaut on satellite: +140 N</text>\n    <line x1="100" y1="170" x2="30" y2="170" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-1-REP-NLM-ASTRONAUT-SATELLITE-1--rep-nlm-space-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="20" y="254" text-anchor="start">satellite on astronaut: −140 N</text>\n    <text x="460" y="60" font-weight="600">each force on its own body:</text>\n    <text x="460" y="82">on satellite: 140 N away</text>\n    <text x="460" y="104">on astronaut: 140 N back</text>\n    <text x="460" y="126">same size, opposite, different bodies</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-SPACE-V2" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="460" y="170" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">a_satellite = 140/350 = 0.4 m/s²</text>\n    <text x="460" y="192" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">a_astronaut = 140/70 = 2 m/s²</text>\n    <text x="460" y="214">opposite directions; the pair never</text>\n    <text x="460" y="236">sits in one body\'s sum</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n<div class="g9-stage-controls"><div class="g9-stage-chips" role="group" aria-label="Stages"><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="0" data-g9-stage-desc="Show the two bodies and the push.">1: The push</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="1" data-g9-stage-desc="Put each force of the interaction on the body it acts on.">2: The force pair</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="2" data-g9-stage-desc="Divide each body&#x27;s own force by its own mass.">3: Accelerations</button></div><p class="g9-stage-desc" data-g9-stage-desc-text></p><div class="g9-stage-stepper"><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="prev">Previous stage</button><span data-g9-stage-label></span><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="next">Next stage</button></div></div><figcaption data-g9-block="representation_bridge" data-g9-caption="purpose">Show the astronaut-satellite push with each force of the pair on its own body.</figcaption></figure></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-repair_closure" data-blueprint-slot="repair_closure" data-required="true"><section class="g9-cu-support" data-g9-support-for="CU-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-1"><h3>Name each force of an interaction as &#x27;A on B&#x27; or &#x27;B on A&#x27;, and put it on the diagram of the body it acts on, so the pair never meets in one sum.</h3><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-trap-card" data-g9-component="TRAP_REPAIR" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_TRAP_CARD" data-g9-component-items="1" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-1"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-trap-repair"><summary>Mistake clinic · diagnose and repair</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="wrong_path"><h4>A tempting wrong path</h4><ul><li>Action and reaction cancel each other, so interacting bodies cannot accelerate.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="diagnose"><h4>Diagnose</h4><ul><li>When a person pushes a cart, do the person&#x27;s force on the cart and the cart&#x27;s force on the person both appear on the cart&#x27;s free-body diagram?</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="repair"><h4>Repair</h4><ul><li>No. Put each partner on the body it acts on; only then sum forces for one body.</li></ul></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-triad" data-g9-component="QUICK_CHECK" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="TRIAD" data-g9-component-items="2" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-1"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="independent_check"><h4>1-2-3 quick check</h4><ol class="g9-triad"><li data-g9-triad-role=""><span class="g9-triad-head">1 · Check</span><span class="g9-triad-text">Multiply each body&#x27;s mass by its acceleration: both products must equal the size of the one shared interaction force.</span></li><li data-g9-triad-role=""><span class="g9-triad-head">2 · Check</span><span class="g9-triad-text">The two accelerations must point opposite ways, with sizes in the inverse ratio of the masses.</span></li></ol></div></div></section></section></div></div><div class="g9-split g9-split-support-only"><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-repair_closure" data-blueprint-slot="repair_closure" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-recall-card" data-g9-component="EXIT_RECALL" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="RECALL_CARD"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_task"><h4>Now you do one</h4><p>A swimmer pushes water backward. Identify the third-law partner force and state which body each force acts on.</p></div><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your answer<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1A-MIC-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-exit"><summary>Model answer</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1A-MIC-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-exit"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_answer"><p>The swimmer pushes the water backward, and the water pushes the swimmer forward with an equal-magnitude opposite force. The two forces act on different bodies.</p><ol><li>The interaction is swimmer-water contact.</li><li>Swimmer on water acts on the water.</li><li>Water on swimmer acts on the swimmer in the opposite direction.</li></ol></div></template></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-chips" data-g9-component="LEARNING_TRANSITIONS" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="CHIPS"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="learning_transitions"><h4>Next steps</h4><div class="g9-transitions-row" data-g9-transitions><a class="g9-transition-chip g9-chip-practice" data-g9-transition="PRACTICE" href="core2.html">Practice this concept &rarr;</a><a class="g9-transition-chip g9-chip-qb" data-g9-transition="QUESTION_BANK" href="../../../public/question-bank/index.html?capability=MIC-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW">All questions in Question Bank &rarr;</a></div></div></div></section></div></div></article><article id="g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1A" data-g9-search-text="Physics Laws of Motion Quantitative static and kinetic friction conditions Hard concept Quantitative static and kinetic friction conditions" class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-concept-header" data-g9-component="CONCEPT_HEADER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="CONCEPT_HEADER"><h2>Quantitative static and kinetic friction conditions</h2><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1A" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-NLM-FIRST-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Laws of Motion"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Laws of Motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT" data-g9-meta-value="Quantitative static and kinetic friction conditions"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Quantitative static and kinetic friction conditions</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT" data-g9-meta-value="HARD"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Hard concept</span></span></div></div></section><div class="g9-split"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-construction" data-blueprint-slot="construction" data-required="true"><section id="g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-1" class="g9-cu" data-g9-cu="CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-1"><div class="g9-component g9-c-unit-header" data-g9-component="UNIT_HEADER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="UNIT_HEADER" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-1"><div class="g9-unit-head"><span class="g9-unit-no" aria-hidden="true">1</span><h3>Find the actual normal force and the friction that rest would need, compare that need with μ_s N, and use μ N as the friction value only at impending slip or once sliding.</h3><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-concept">Hard concept</span></div><nav class="g9-cu-nav" data-g9-unit-navigation aria-label="Concept Book section navigation"><span data-g9-route-position>Section 8 of 14</span><span class="g9-cu-nav-links"><a data-g9-prev-section href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-1" aria-label="Previous section: Name each force of an interaction as &#x27;A on B&#x27; or &#x27;B on A&#x27;, and put it on the diagram of the body it acts on, so the pair never meets in one sum.">← Previous</a><a data-g9-next-section href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-1" aria-label="Next section: Name the physical link that fixes the separation, give both bodies one sign convention, and write a separate Newton-II equation for each body with the interaction force kept in it.">Next →</a></span></nav></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-banner" data-g9-component="KEY_STEP" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="BANNER"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="inferential_jump"><h4>The key step</h4><p>Static friction is a responsive contact force: solve for the friction required to prevent relative slip and compare it with the limiting value mu_s N. Equality is a threshold condition, while the kinetic model is used only after sliding is established.</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-tile" data-g9-component="MODEL_CONTRACT" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_TILE"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-prerequisites"><summary>What you need before this</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="entry_assumptions"><h4>Prerequisites</h4><ul><li>Can determine friction direction from relative slip tendency.</li><li>Can write Newton-II equations for a chosen body.</li></ul><ul><li data-g9-prereq="CAP-NLM-FRICTION" data-bridged="true"><a href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION">Identify friction as a contact force that opposes relative sliding or the tendency to slide.</a></li><li data-g9-prereq="CAP-NLM-SECOND-LAW" data-bridged="true"><a href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW">Relate signed net external force to acceleration with F_net = m a.</a></li></ul></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-info" data-g9-component="QUESTION_BRIDGE" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_INFO" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-1"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-question-bridge"><summary>Why this section matters for the question set</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="question_bridge"><h4>This unit builds toward</h4><ul><li data-g9-bridge-question="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13"><strong>JEE (Advanced)|2020|Paper 1|Physics|Q13</strong> · D4 · the hardest question in this set<br>The move learners miss: At the second stick-slip transition use kinetic friction at the slipping right contact and limiting static friction at the sticking left contact: μ_kN_R=μ_sN_L; this gives r-50=25.6 cm and swaps which finger slips. <a class="g9-bridge-link" data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13">Try it in Core2</a></li><li data-g9-bridge-question="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P2-Q19"><strong>JEE (Advanced)|2014|Paper 2|Physics|Q19</strong> · D3<br>The move learners miss: Compare the whole-system demand with m2&#x27;s contact capacity: static rest requires (m1+m2)tanθ≤0.3m2, hence tanθ≤0.2. <a class="g9-bridge-link" data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P2-Q19" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P2-Q19">Try it in Core2</a></li><li data-g9-bridge-question="PYQ-PHY-INJSO-2018-Q12"><strong>INJSO|2018|27 Jan 2018|Physics|Q12</strong> · D3<br>The move learners miss: Use the three-person trial: 3F-f_k=ma=50 N, then subtract the uniform-speed equation to isolate one person&#x27;s force. <a class="g9-bridge-link" data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-INJSO-2018-Q12" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-INJSO-2018-Q12">Try it in Core2</a></li></ul></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-step-cards" data-g9-component="CONSTRUCTION_STEPS" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEP_CARDS" data-g9-component-items="4" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-1"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="construction"><ol><li data-g9-step="NLM8-1"><strong>Build the chosen-body free-body diagram and solve the perpendicular dynamics for N before using any friction law: do not assume N = mg; N=mg only in the special level case with no other perpendicular force or acceleration; a downward added component raises N, an upward component lowers it, and if the required contact force would be negative the contact is lost so N=0 and friction=0.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> A unilateral contact can push but not pull. Its normal reaction is whatever the perpendicular Newton-II equation requires while contact persists; friction capacity is then tied to that actual N.<br><em>Result:</em> perpendicular equation -&gt; actual N≥0; level examples N=mg+F_down or N=mg-F_up; contact lost -&gt; N=0 and f=0.</li><li data-g9-step="NLM8-2"><strong>Assume no relative sliding provisionally, choose a body or a whole connected system deliberately, and solve the tangential Newton-II equation for the friction demand at each rough contact.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> Static friction responds to the no-slip dynamics. A system boundary can remove internal contact pairs, but it cannot create friction capacity at a smooth contact or move a rough contact from one body to another.<br><em>Result:</em> chosen boundary + no-slip dynamics -&gt; signed f_required at each actual rough contact.</li><li data-g9-step="NLM8-3"><strong>Compare each |f_required| with μ_sN; on an incline this comparison can be turned into the angle condition for the onset of sliding rather than setting f=μ_sN from the start.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> The static-contact model permits any required value only up to the limiting magnitude.<br><em>Result:</em> |f_required| &lt;= mu_s N -&gt; static feasible; equality only at impending slip; after sliding use the kinetic model. Equivalent symbolic form: |f_required|≤μ_sN.</li><li data-g9-step="NLM8-4" data-g9-crux-step><span class="g9-crux-tag">The step the hard question turns on</span><strong>At the threshold use |f_s|=μ_sN; once relative sliding is established use |f_k|=μ_kN opposite the sliding, and if several contacts change state, update N and the friction model at each contact before continuing.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> Static-below-limit, impending-slip, kinetic-sliding and contact-lost are different contact states. A multi-contact process may switch which contact is static or sliding, so stale μN values cannot be carried across a transition.<br><em>Result:</em> state map: static below limit -&gt; f_required; impending -&gt; μ_sN; sliding -&gt; μ_kN; contact lost -&gt; N=f=0.</li></ol></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-equation-card" data-g9-component="EQUATIONS" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_EQUATION_CARD" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-1"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-equations"><summary>Formula reference · meaning and conditions</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="equation_matrix"><h4>Equations and validity</h4><div class="g9-table-scroll" data-g9-equation-matrix><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Equation</th><th scope="col">What it tells you</th><th scope="col">When you can use it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr data-g9-relation-ref="REL-NLM-STATIC-FRICTION-BOUND"><td><div class="g9-math" data-g9-math="mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>|</mo><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></div></td><td><p>For a contact that remains static, the static-friction magnitude adjusts to the value required by the tangential dynamics up to the limiting value mu_s N; equality applies only at impending slip.</p></td><td><ul><li>The surfaces are in contact and have no relative sliding during the state being tested.</li><li>N is obtained from the actual free-body dynamics, not assumed to equal mg.</li><li>Static friction opposes the impending relative slip direction.</li><li>Equality |f_s| = mu_s N is used only at impending slip.</li></ul></td></tr><tr data-g9-relation-ref="REL-NLM-KINETIC-FRICTION"><td><div class="g9-math" data-g9-math="mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>|</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></div></td><td><p>Once relative sliding at the contact is established, the bounded dry-sliding model uses kinetic-friction magnitude mu_k N, directed opposite the relative sliding.</p></td><td><ul><li>Relative sliding between the two surfaces is established.</li><li>N is obtained from the actual free-body dynamics.</li><li>The friction direction opposes the relative sliding at the contact.</li><li>The simple dry-sliding coefficient model is being used.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-predict-reveal-worked-card" data-g9-component="WORKED_EXAMPLE" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="PREDICT_REVEAL_WORKED_CARD" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-1"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_anchor"><h4>Watch one · predict before reveal</h4><p class="g9-prov" data-g9-anchor-source>JEE (Advanced) · 2020 · Paper 1 · Q13 · Official past paper, faithful restatement of the original</p><p class="g9-lines">A uniform metre scale is supported horizontally on two index fingers initially at 0.00 cm and 90.00 cm. Both fingers are moved slowly toward the centre. The slipping finger alternates because static and kinetic friction differ. With mu_s=0.40 and mu_k=0.32, find the final distance x_R of the right finger from the 50.00 cm centre at the instant it stops and the left finger starts slipping again.</p><p class="g9-worked-instruction">Before opening each step, say what you would do next.</p><ol class="g9-watch-steps"><li data-g9-watch-step data-g9-move-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13-MOVE-1"><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 1, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><strong>Treat the slowly moved metre scale as quasistatic and label the two finger supports by their current coordinates and normal reactions N_L and N_R.</strong><p>Why valid: At every instant the scale has negligible translational and angular acceleration, but the support reactions change as the finger positions change.</p><p>Result: N_L+N_R=mg; their ratio is set by support positions.</p></div></details></li><li data-g9-watch-step data-g9-move-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13-MOVE-2"><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 2, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><strong>For each current pair of support positions, obtain N_L and N_R from force and torque balance before writing friction.</strong><p>Why valid: Friction capacities are μN, so a fixed normal reaction while a support moves would make the threshold condition inconsistent with the changing support geometry.</p><p>Result: With the right finger at 90 cm, N_L/N_R=40/(50-l); the first switch gives l=18 cm.</p></div></details></li><li data-g9-watch-step data-g9-move-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13-MOVE-3"><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 3, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><strong>After the first switch, hold the left finger at 18 cm while the right finger slips inward and recompute the support ratio N_R/N_L=32/(r-50).</strong><p>Why valid: The support geometry has changed, so the normal reactions—and therefore both friction capacities—must be recalculated for the second stage rather than carried over.</p><p>Result: N_R/N_L=32/(r-50).</p></div></details></li><li data-g9-watch-step data-g9-move-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13-MOVE-4"><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 4, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><strong>At the second stick-slip transition use kinetic friction at the slipping right contact and limiting static friction at the sticking left contact: μ_kN_R=μ_sN_L; this gives r-50=25.6 cm and swaps which finger slips.</strong><p>Why valid: The friction law is contact-state specific: a sliding contact uses μ_kN while a sticking contact can rise only to μ_sN. Equality marks the instant the roles switch.</p><p>Result: x_R=25.6 cm.</p></div></details></li></ol><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="worked-result"><summary>Result and independent check</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_result"><h4>Result</h4><p>25.6 cm.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_check"><h4>Check</h4><p>The result is less than the initial 40 cm offset of the right finger and greater than zero, consistent with inward motion.</p></div></div></details></div></div></section></section></div><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-representation" data-blueprint-slot="representation" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-staged-visual" data-g9-component="STAGED_VISUAL" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="STAGED_VISUAL" data-g9-component-items="4" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-1"><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-1-REP-NLM-FRICTION-STATE-MAP" data-g9-stage="TEACHING" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-FRICTION-STATE-MAP" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="4" data-g9-stages-total="4" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-FRICTION-STATE-V0 VIS-NLM-FRICTION-STATE-V1 VIS-NLM-FRICTION-STATE-V2 VIS-NLM-FRICTION-STATE-V3" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 440 330" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-1-REP-NLM-FRICTION-STATE-MAP-1--frs-title g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-1-REP-NLM-FRICTION-STATE-MAP-1--frs-desc">\n<title id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-1-REP-NLM-FRICTION-STATE-MAP-1--frs-title">Friction contact-state decision map</title><desc id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-1-REP-NLM-FRICTION-STATE-MAP-1--frs-desc">Four stages: solve the normal reaction and allow contact loss; find the no-slip friction demand; compare it with the static limit; after sliding starts use kinetic friction. Contact loss has N and friction zero.</desc>\n<defs><marker id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-1-REP-NLM-FRICTION-STATE-MAP-1--frs-a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker></defs>\n<g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-FRICTION-STATE-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="14" fill="currentColor" stroke="currentColor">\n<text x="16" y="24" stroke="none" font-weight="700">1 · Solve perpendicular dynamics first</text><line x1="20" y1="145" x2="190" y2="145" stroke-width="2" /><rect x="72" y="95" width="70" height="50" fill="none" stroke-width="2" /><line x1="107" y1="95" x2="107" y2="55" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-1-REP-NLM-FRICTION-STATE-MAP-1--frs-a)"/><text x="116" y="62" stroke="none" >N</text><line x1="107" y1="120" x2="107" y2="188" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-1-REP-NLM-FRICTION-STATE-MAP-1--frs-a)"/><text x="116" y="184" stroke="none" >mg</text><line x1="142" y1="110" x2="205" y2="78" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-1-REP-NLM-FRICTION-STATE-MAP-1--frs-a)"/><text x="211" y="82" stroke="none" >upward force</text>\n<text x="16" y="218" stroke="none" >Use ΣF⊥ = ma⊥. N = mg only when justified.</text><text x="16" y="242" stroke="none" >If the equation requires N &lt; 0: contact is lost.</text><text x="16" y="266" stroke="none" font-weight="700">CONTACT LOST → N = 0 and f = 0</text></g>\n<g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-FRICTION-STATE-V1" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="14" fill="currentColor" stroke="currentColor">\n<text x="16" y="24" stroke="none" font-weight="700">2 · Assume no slip and find f_required</text><line x1="20" y1="145" x2="250" y2="145" stroke-width="2" /><rect x="82" y="95" width="70" height="50" fill="none" stroke-width="2" /><line x1="152" y1="119" x2="230" y2="119" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-1-REP-NLM-FRICTION-STATE-MAP-1--frs-a)"/><text x="162" y="105" stroke="none" >applied force</text><line x1="82" y1="130" x2="34" y2="130" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-1-REP-NLM-FRICTION-STATE-MAP-1--frs-a)"/><text x="18" y="115" stroke="none" >f_required</text><text x="16" y="202" stroke="none" >Solve the tangential Newton-II equation.</text><text x="16" y="226" stroke="none" >Do not set static friction to μ_sN yet.</text></g>\n<g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-FRICTION-STATE-V2" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="14" fill="currentColor" stroke="currentColor">\n<text x="16" y="24" stroke="none" font-weight="700">3 · Compare demand with the static capacity</text><text x="28" y="78" stroke="none" >STATIC BELOW LIMIT</text><text x="238" y="78" stroke="none" >IMPENDING SLIP</text><text x="28" y="105" stroke="none" >|f_required| &lt; μ_sN</text><text x="238" y="105" stroke="none" >|f_s| = μ_sN</text><text x="28" y="132" stroke="none" >f_s = f_required</text><text x="238" y="132" stroke="none" >equality only here</text><line x1="45" y1="170" x2="386" y2="170" stroke-width="2" /><text x="45" y="194" stroke="none" >0</text><text x="350" y="194" stroke="none" >μ_sN</text><text x="16" y="240" stroke="none">On an incline this comparison gives the</text><text x="16" y="262" stroke="none">angle where sliding starts.</text></g>\n<g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-FRICTION-STATE-V3" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="14" fill="currentColor" stroke="currentColor">\n<text x="16" y="24" stroke="none" font-weight="700">4 · Once sliding starts, switch model</text><line x1="20" y1="145" x2="250" y2="145" stroke-width="2" /><rect x="88" y="95" width="70" height="50" fill="none" stroke-width="2" /><line x1="158" y1="118" x2="235" y2="118" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-1-REP-NLM-FRICTION-STATE-MAP-1--frs-a)"/><text x="165" y="105" stroke="none" >relative sliding</text><line x1="88" y1="132" x2="32" y2="132" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-1-REP-NLM-FRICTION-STATE-MAP-1--frs-a)"/><text x="18" y="116" stroke="none" >f_k</text><text x="16" y="205" stroke="none" >SLIDING → |f_k| = μ_kN, opposite relative sliding.</text><text x="16" y="230" stroke="none">For several contacts, update N and each state</text><text x="16" y="252" stroke="none">after a switch.</text><text x="16" y="282" stroke="none">CONTACT LOST remains separate: N = f = 0.</text></g></svg>\n<div class="g9-stage-controls"><div class="g9-stage-chips" role="group" aria-label="Stages"><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="0" data-g9-stage-desc="Choose the body and solve the perpendicular dynamics before touching a friction formula.">1: Find N and test contact</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="1" data-g9-stage-desc="Assume no slip and solve for the friction actually required.">2: Static demand</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="2" data-g9-stage-desc="Compare the no-slip demand with μ_sN and identify impending slip only at equality.">3: Threshold</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="3" data-g9-stage-desc="Switch to μ_kN only after sliding is established, while keeping contact loss as the N=0 branch.">4: Sliding branch</button></div><p class="g9-stage-desc" data-g9-stage-desc-text></p><div class="g9-stage-stepper"><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="prev">Previous stage</button><span data-g9-stage-label></span><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="next">Next stage</button></div></div><figcaption data-g9-block="representation_bridge" data-g9-caption="purpose">Build the complete dry-friction decision from actual normal reaction through static demand, impending slip, kinetic sliding and contact loss.</figcaption></figure></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-repair_closure" data-blueprint-slot="repair_closure" data-required="true"><section class="g9-cu-support" data-g9-support-for="CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-1"><h3>Find the actual normal force and the friction that rest would need, compare that need with μ_s N, and use μ N as the friction value only at impending slip or once sliding.</h3><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-trap-card" data-g9-component="TRAP_REPAIR" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_TRAP_CARD" data-g9-component-items="4" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-1"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-trap-repair"><summary>Mistake clinic · diagnose and repair</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="wrong_path"><h4>A tempting wrong path</h4><ul><li>Friction always equals mu N.</li><li>The normal force is always mg.</li><li>If both mu_s and mu_k are given, the coefficients themselves tell you which friction model to use.</li><li>Friction can keep acting after the surfaces lose contact, or a negative value of N can be used inside μN.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="diagnose"><h4>Diagnose</h4><ul><li>A small horizontal force is applied to a block that remains at rest. Must static friction already equal mu_s N?</li><li>If an extra force presses a block into a horizontal surface, can the normal reaction still be assumed equal to mg?</li><li>A problem gives both mu_s and mu_k but does not say whether the contact slips. Can you choose kinetic friction just because mu_k is supplied?</li><li>An upward pull becomes large enough that the floor would need a downward normal force to keep touching the block. What are N and friction after separation?</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="repair"><h4>Repair</h4><ul><li>No. First solve the force required for no slip. Static friction matches that requirement up to its limit; equality occurs only at impending slip.</li><li>Determine N from the perpendicular free-body equation. N = mg is only a special case.</li><li>No. Determine the contact state first: test whether the no-slip friction demand fits within mu_s N. Use the kinetic model only after sliding is established.</li><li>A contact can push, not pull. Once the perpendicular equation would require N&lt;0, contact is lost: set N=0 and therefore contact friction is zero.</li></ul></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-triad" data-g9-component="QUICK_CHECK" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="TRIAD" data-g9-component-items="3" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-1"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="independent_check"><h4>1-2-3 quick check</h4><ol class="g9-triad"><li data-g9-triad-role="CHECK"><span class="g9-triad-head">1 · Check</span><span class="g9-triad-text">CHECK: State the four contact states in order and the friction rule for each: static below its limit, impending slip, established sliding, and contact lost.</span></li><li data-g9-triad-role="APPLY"><span class="g9-triad-head">2 · Apply</span><span class="g9-triad-text">APPLY: A level block has weight 50 N and is pulled 20 N upward as well as 12 N sideways. With μ_s=0.5, find N, the static limit, and the actual friction if it stays at rest.</span></li><li data-g9-triad-role="CONNECT"><span class="g9-triad-head">3 · Connect</span><span class="g9-triad-text">CONNECT: In a two-contact problem, after one contact starts sliding, recompute the normal reactions and identify which contact is now static before writing the next μN equation.</span></li></ol></div></div></section></section></div></div><div class="g9-split g9-split-support-only"><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-repair_closure" data-blueprint-slot="repair_closure" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-recall-card" data-g9-component="EXIT_RECALL" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="RECALL_CARD"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_task"><h4>Now you do one</h4><p>A contact has known normal reaction N and coefficient mu_s. The no-slip force equation requires tangential friction f_required. State the test for whether static contact is possible, when equality with mu_s N is justified, and what changes once sliding is established.</p></div><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your answer<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1A-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-exit"><summary>Model answer</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1A-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-exit"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_answer"><p>Static contact is possible when |f_required| &lt;= mu_s N. Equality |f_s| = mu_s N is justified only at impending slip. After relative sliding is established, use the kinetic model |f_k| = mu_k N with direction opposite the relative sliding.</p><ol><li>Static friction supplies the required no-slip value rather than an automatic maximum.</li><li>The limiting value mu_s N is a bound and becomes an equality only at the threshold of slipping.</li><li>The kinetic model belongs to a different contact state: established relative sliding.</li></ol></div></template></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-chips" data-g9-component="PRACTICE_LINKS" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="CHIPS"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="practice_navigation"><h4>Practice this concept in Core2</h4><ul><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q10" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q10">IIT-JEE · 2007 · Paper I · Q10</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33">IIT-JEE · 2008 · Paper II · Q33</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P1-Q41" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P1-Q41">IIT-JEE · 2011 · Paper I · Q41</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q34" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q34">IIT-JEE · 2011 · Paper II · Q34</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P2-Q19" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P2-Q19">JEE (Advanced) · 2014 · Paper 2 · Q19</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13">JEE (Advanced) · 2020 · Paper 1 · Q13</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P1-Q08" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P1-Q08">JEE (Advanced) · 2014 · Paper 1 · Q8</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEMAIN-2026-06APR-S2-Q46" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-JEEMAIN-2026-06APR-S2-Q46">JEE Main · 2026 · Session 2 · 06 Apr 2026 · Shift 2 · Q46</a></li></ul></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-chips" data-g9-component="LEARNING_TRANSITIONS" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="CHIPS"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="learning_transitions"><h4>Next steps</h4><div class="g9-transitions-row" data-g9-transitions><a class="g9-transition-chip g9-chip-practice" data-g9-transition="PRACTICE" href="core2.html">Practice this concept &rarr;</a><a class="g9-transition-chip g9-chip-qb" data-g9-transition="QUESTION_BANK" href="../../../public/question-bank/index.html?capability=MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT">All questions in Question Bank &rarr;</a></div></div></div></section></div></div></article><article id="g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1A" data-g9-search-text="Physics Laws of Motion Connected bodies and a justified common acceleration Hard concept Connected bodies and a justified common acceleration" class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-concept-header" data-g9-component="CONCEPT_HEADER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="CONCEPT_HEADER"><h2>Connected bodies and a justified common acceleration</h2><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1A" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-NLM-FIRST-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Laws of Motion"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Laws of Motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL" data-g9-meta-value="Connected bodies and a justified common acceleration"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Connected bodies and a justified common acceleration</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL" data-g9-meta-value="HARD"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Hard concept</span></span></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-chips" data-g9-component="SECTION_ROUTE" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="CHIPS"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="section_route"><h4>Sections</h4><nav data-g9-section-route aria-label="Sections in this concept"><ol><li><a href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-1">Name the physical link that fixes the separation, give both bodies one sign convention, and write a separate Newton-II equation for each body with the interaction force kept in it.</a></li><li><a href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-2">Add the body equations into one system equation only where the internal pair should drop out, then isolate the body that makes the requested interaction force external.</a></li></ol></nav></div></div></section><div class="g9-split"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-construction" data-blueprint-slot="construction" data-required="true"><section id="g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-1" class="g9-cu" data-g9-cu="CU-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-1"><div class="g9-component g9-c-unit-header" data-g9-component="UNIT_HEADER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="UNIT_HEADER" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-1"><div class="g9-unit-head"><span class="g9-unit-no" aria-hidden="true">1</span><h3>Name the physical link that fixes the separation, give both bodies one sign convention, and write a separate Newton-II equation for each body with the interaction force kept in it.</h3><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-concept">Hard concept</span></div><nav class="g9-cu-nav" data-g9-unit-navigation aria-label="Concept Book section navigation"><span data-g9-route-position>Section 9 of 14</span><span class="g9-cu-nav-links"><a data-g9-prev-section href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-1" aria-label="Previous section: Find the actual normal force and the friction that rest would need, compare that need with μ_s N, and use μ N as the friction value only at impending slip or once sliding.">← Previous</a><a data-g9-next-section href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-2" aria-label="Next section: Add the body equations into one system equation only where the internal pair should drop out, then isolate the body that makes the requested interaction force external.">Next →</a></span></nav></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-banner" data-g9-component="KEY_STEP" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="BANNER"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="inferential_jump"><h4>The key step</h4><p>Bodies share an acceleration component only when a physical constraint keeps their relative separation fixed along that coordinate; the shared acceleration can link separate Newton-II equations, while internal partner forces cancel only if the bodies are deliberately treated as one system.</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-tile" data-g9-component="MODEL_CONTRACT" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_TILE"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-prerequisites"><summary>What you need before this</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="entry_assumptions"><h4>Prerequisites</h4><ul><li>Can draw one FBD per chosen body.</li><li>Can apply signed Newton II to one chosen body.</li></ul><ul><li data-g9-prereq="CAP-NLM-SECOND-LAW" data-bridged="true"><a href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW">Relate signed net external force to acceleration with F_net = m a.</a></li></ul></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-step-cards" data-g9-component="CONSTRUCTION_STEPS" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEP_CARDS" data-g9-component-items="3" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-1"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="construction"><ol><li data-g9-step="NLM9-1"><strong>Identify the connection/contact condition and state exactly which relative separation is fixed.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> A common acceleration is a kinematic consequence of a constraint, not a default property of nearby bodies.<br><em>Result:</em> fixed relative separation along chosen coordinate -&gt; common constrained acceleration component</li><li data-g9-step="NLM9-2"><strong>Choose one compatible sign convention for every body equation and assign the constrained acceleration component consistently.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> The same physical acceleration cannot change sign merely because the equation changed bodies.<br><em>Result:</em> same-sense coordinates -&gt; a_A = a_B = a</li><li data-g9-step="NLM9-3"><strong>Write one Newton-II force sum for each body, keeping every inter-body force on the body it acts on.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> The interaction forces are external to each individual body and therefore belong in its own equation.<br><em>Result:</em> body A: Sigma F_A = m_A a; body B: Sigma F_B = m_B a</li></ol></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-equation-card" data-g9-component="EQUATIONS" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_EQUATION_CARD" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-1"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-equations"><summary>Formula reference · meaning and conditions</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="equation_matrix"><h4>Equations and validity</h4><div class="g9-table-scroll" data-g9-equation-matrix><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Equation</th><th scope="col">What it tells you</th><th scope="col">When you can use it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr data-g9-relation-ref="REL-NLM-COMMON-ACCEL-CONSTRAINT"><td><div class="g9-math" data-g9-math="mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow></math></div></td><td><p>When an active connection keeps the relative separation of bodies A and B fixed along one shared coordinate and both use the same positive sense, their acceleration components along that coordinate are equal.</p></td><td><ul><li>The connection/contact actively keeps the relevant relative separation fixed.</li><li>Both acceleration components use the same coordinate direction and sign convention.</li><li>The constraint has not gone slack, separated, or otherwise ceased to apply.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-predict-reveal-worked-card" data-g9-component="WORKED_EXAMPLE" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="PREDICT_REVEAL_WORKED_CARD" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-1"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_anchor"><h4>Watch one · predict before reveal</h4><p>Blocks A (3 kg) and B (2 kg) touch on a smooth floor, A to the left of B. A 20 N force pushes A to the right into B, and a 5 N force pushes B to the left, back toward A. The blocks stay in contact. Write one Newton-II equation per block and solve for their acceleration and the contact force.</p><p class="g9-worked-instruction">Before opening each step, say what you would do next.</p><ol class="g9-watch-steps"><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 1, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>Maintained contact fixes the separation of A and B, so with rightward positive a_A = a_B = a.</p></div></details></li><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 2, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>For A: the 20 N push acts right and B pushes back on A with the contact force N, so 20 − N = 3a.</p></div></details></li><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 3, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>For B: A pushes B right with N and the 5 N force acts left, so N − 5 = 2a.</p></div></details></li><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 4, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>Adding the equations removes N: 15 = 5a, so a = 3 m/s². Then N = 5 + 2 × 3 = 11 N.</p></div></details></li></ol><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="worked-result"><summary>Result and independent check</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_result"><h4>Result</h4><p>The blocks accelerate together at 3 m/s² to the right, and the contact force between them is 11 N.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_check"><h4>Check</h4><p>Check A on its own: 20 − 11 = 9 N, and 3 kg × 3 m/s² = 9 N.</p></div></div></details></div></div></section></section></div><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-representation" data-blueprint-slot="representation" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-staged-visual" data-g9-component="STAGED_VISUAL" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="STAGED_VISUAL" data-g9-component-items="3" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-1"><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="CU-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-1-REP-NLM-BLOCKS-3KG-2KG" data-g9-stage="TEACHING" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-BLOCKS-3KG-2KG" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="3" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-AB32-V0 VIS-NLM-AB32-V1 VIS-NLM-AB32-V2" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 820 270" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-1-REP-NLM-BLOCKS-3KG-2KG-1--rep-nlm-ab32-title g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-1-REP-NLM-BLOCKS-3KG-2KG-1--rep-nlm-ab32-desc">\n  <title id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-1-REP-NLM-BLOCKS-3KG-2KG-1--rep-nlm-ab32-title">Blocks 3 kg and 2 kg pushed together: one equation per block</title>\n  <desc id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-1-REP-NLM-BLOCKS-3KG-2KG-1--rep-nlm-ab32-desc">Stage 1: Blocks A (3 kg) and B (2 kg) touch on a smooth floor, A on the left; a 20 N force pushes A to the right into B and a 5 N force pushes B to the left; rightward is positive. Stage 2: Each block is isolated: A: 20 − N = 3a and B: N − 5 = 2a, with one a because the contact fixes the separation. Stage 3: Adding gives 15 = 5a, so a = 3 m/s² to the right, and N = 20 − 3 × 3 = 11 N; on B, 11 − 5 = 2 × 3 checks.</desc>\n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-1-REP-NLM-BLOCKS-3KG-2KG-1--rep-nlm-ab32-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-1-REP-NLM-BLOCKS-3KG-2KG-1--rep-nlm-ab32-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-AB32-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">Blocks A (3 kg) and B (2 kg) touch on a smooth floor</text>\n    <line x1="20" y1="200" x2="420" y2="200" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="30" y1="200" x2="22" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="48" y1="200" x2="40" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="66" y1="200" x2="58" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="84" y1="200" x2="76" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="102" y1="200" x2="94" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="120" y1="200" x2="112" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="138" y1="200" x2="130" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="156" y1="200" x2="148" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="174" y1="200" x2="166" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="192" y1="200" x2="184" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="210" y1="200" x2="202" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="228" y1="200" x2="220" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="246" y1="200" x2="238" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="264" y1="200" x2="256" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="282" y1="200" x2="274" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="200" x2="292" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="318" y1="200" x2="310" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="336" y1="200" x2="328" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="354" y1="200" x2="346" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="372" y1="200" x2="364" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="390" y1="200" x2="382" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="408" y1="200" x2="400" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <rect x="140" y="130" width="80" height="70" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="180" y="170" text-anchor="middle">A 3 kg</text>\n    <rect x="220" y="145" width="76" height="55" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="258" y="177.5" text-anchor="middle">B 2 kg</text>\n    <line x1="60" y1="165" x2="136" y2="165" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-1-REP-NLM-BLOCKS-3KG-2KG-1--rep-nlm-ab32-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="60" y="155">20 N</text>\n    <line x1="376" y1="172" x2="300" y2="172" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-1-REP-NLM-BLOCKS-3KG-2KG-1--rep-nlm-ab32-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="376" y="162" text-anchor="end">5 N</text>\n    <text x="20" y="240">rightward positive; the blocks stay in contact</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-AB32-V1" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <rect x="128" y="118" width="104" height="94" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="6 4"/>\n    <rect x="214" y="136" width="90" height="74" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="6 4"/>\n    <text x="450" y="60" font-weight="600">one link, one a (contact fixes the gap)</text>\n    <text x="450" y="82">A: 20 − N = 3a</text>\n    <text x="450" y="104">B: N − 5 = 2a</text>\n    <text x="450" y="126">N = contact force, B on A / A on B</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-AB32-V2" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="450" y="170" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">add: 15 = 5a, a = 3 m/s² right</text>\n    <text x="450" y="192" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">N = 20 − 3 × 3 = 11 N</text>\n    <text x="450" y="214">check on B: 11 − 5 = 2 × 3 ✓</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n<div class="g9-stage-controls"><div class="g9-stage-chips" role="group" aria-label="Stages"><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="0" data-g9-stage-desc="Show the touching blocks and both pushes.">1: The two blocks</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="1" data-g9-stage-desc="Isolate each block with the contact force kept in its equation.">2: One equation per block</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="2" data-g9-stage-desc="Solve for a and N.">3: Solve</button></div><p class="g9-stage-desc" data-g9-stage-desc-text></p><div class="g9-stage-stepper"><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="prev">Previous stage</button><span data-g9-stage-label></span><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="next">Next stage</button></div></div><figcaption data-g9-block="representation_bridge" data-g9-caption="purpose">Show blocks A (3 kg) and B (2 kg) pushed together by 20 N and 5 N, each isolated with the contact force in its own equation.</figcaption></figure></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-repair_closure" data-blueprint-slot="repair_closure" data-required="true"><section class="g9-cu-support" data-g9-support-for="CU-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-1"><h3>Name the physical link that fixes the separation, give both bodies one sign convention, and write a separate Newton-II equation for each body with the interaction force kept in it.</h3><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-trap-card" data-g9-component="TRAP_REPAIR" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_TRAP_CARD" data-g9-component-items="3" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-1"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-trap-repair"><summary>Mistake clinic · diagnose and repair</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="wrong_path"><h4>A tempting wrong path</h4><ul><li>Any two touching or connected bodies automatically have the same acceleration.</li><li>The contact force between two bodies can be cancelled from each individual FBD because it is an internal force of the pair.</li><li>Bodies with the same acceleration must have the same net force.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="diagnose"><h4>Diagnose</h4><ul><li>If two blocks are touching but one loses contact, what keeps their accelerations equal after separation?</li><li>On body A alone, is the force from body B external to A?</li><li>Two connected bodies of different masses share acceleration a. Must their individual net forces be equal?</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="repair"><h4>Repair</h4><ul><li>Name the active constraint first. Equal acceleration is justified only while the relative-separation condition remains enforced.</li><li>Yes. Keep it in A&#x27;s equation. It cancels only after A and B are deliberately combined into one system equation.</li><li>No. The constraint can make their accelerations equal while Newton II gives different net forces m_A a and m_B a when the masses differ.</li></ul></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-triad" data-g9-component="QUICK_CHECK" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="TRIAD" data-g9-component-items="2" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-1"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="independent_check"><h4>1-2-3 quick check</h4><ol class="g9-triad"><li data-g9-triad-role=""><span class="g9-triad-head">1 · Check</span><span class="g9-triad-text">Substitute the acceleration and the contact force into both body equations; each must balance on its own.</span></li><li data-g9-triad-role=""><span class="g9-triad-head">2 · Check</span><span class="g9-triad-text">The contact force must come out positive in the pushing sense: an ordinary contact can only push, so a negative value means the bodies have separated.</span></li></ol></div></div></section></section></div></div><div class="g9-split"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-construction" data-blueprint-slot="construction" data-required="true"><section id="g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-2" class="g9-cu" data-g9-cu="CU-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-2"><div class="g9-component g9-c-unit-header" data-g9-component="UNIT_HEADER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="UNIT_HEADER" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-2"><div class="g9-unit-head"><span class="g9-unit-no" aria-hidden="true">2</span><h3>Add the body equations into one system equation only where the internal pair should drop out, then isolate the body that makes the requested interaction force external.</h3><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-concept">Hard concept</span></div><nav class="g9-cu-nav" data-g9-unit-navigation aria-label="Concept Book section navigation"><span data-g9-route-position>Section 10 of 14</span><span class="g9-cu-nav-links"><a data-g9-prev-section href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-1" aria-label="Previous section: Name the physical link that fixes the separation, give both bodies one sign convention, and write a separate Newton-II equation for each body with the interaction force kept in it.">← Previous</a><a data-g9-next-section href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-1" aria-label="Next section: Declare the ideal string before writing a single T, then draw that tension on each attached body, pulling away from the body along its own segment.">Next →</a></span></nav></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-step-cards" data-g9-component="CONSTRUCTION_STEPS" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEP_CARDS" data-g9-component-items="2" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-2"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="construction"><ol><li data-g9-step="NLM9-4"><strong>If treating A+B as one system, add the two body equations and cancel only the internal A-on-B/B-on-A pair in that system sum.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> Internal partner forces cancel in the combined system equation because both members are included, not because either force is absent from a body FBD.<br><em>Result:</em> system: Sigma F_external = (m_A + m_B) a; internal pair absent only after equations are combined</li><li data-g9-step="NLM9-5"><strong>Match the chosen system boundary to the requested quantity: use the combined constrained system when internal forces are irrelevant to the shared acceleration, then isolate the body or subsystem that makes a requested interaction force external.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> An internal interaction disappears from a whole-system force sum, so that boundary is efficient for acceleration but cannot by itself determine the internal force that was cancelled.<br><em>Result:</em> target shared acceleration -&gt; whole constrained system; target internal/contact force -&gt; isolate a body or subsystem while carrying the same justified acceleration.</li></ol></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-predict-reveal-worked-card" data-g9-component="WORKED_EXAMPLE" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="PREDICT_REVEAL_WORKED_CARD" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-2"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_anchor"><h4>Watch one · predict before reveal</h4><p>A 2 kg bucket hangs from a light rope tied under a 1 kg bucket. A second rope lifts the top bucket with a 45 N upward force. Take g = 10 m/s² and up as positive. Choose a system to find the acceleration, then a different system to find the tension in the rope between the buckets.</p><p class="g9-worked-instruction">Before opening each step, say what you would do next.</p><ol class="g9-watch-steps"><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 1, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>For the acceleration, take both buckets as one system: the connecting rope&#x27;s pulls are internal and drop out.</p></div></details></li><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 2, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>External forces on the system: 45 N up and weight (1 + 2) × 10 = 30 N down, so 45 − 30 = 3a and a = 5 m/s².</p></div></details></li><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 3, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>For the connecting rope, isolate the lower bucket: that rope&#x27;s pull is external to it.</p></div></details></li><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 4, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>Lower bucket: T − 2 × 10 = 2 × 5, so T = 30 N.</p></div></details></li></ol><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="worked-result"><summary>Result and independent check</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_result"><h4>Result</h4><p>The buckets accelerate upward at 5 m/s², and the rope between them carries 30 N.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_check"><h4>Check</h4><p>Check the top bucket alone: 45 − 10 − 30 = 5 N, and 1 kg × 5 m/s² = 5 N.</p></div></div></details></div></div></section></section></div><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-representation" data-blueprint-slot="representation" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-staged-visual" data-g9-component="STAGED_VISUAL" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="STAGED_VISUAL" data-g9-component-items="3" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-2"><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="CU-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-2-REP-NLM-BUCKETS-45N" data-g9-stage="TEACHING" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-BUCKETS-45N" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="3" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-BUCKETS-V0 VIS-NLM-BUCKETS-V1 VIS-NLM-BUCKETS-V2" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 820 310" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-2-REP-NLM-BUCKETS-45N-1--rep-nlm-buckets-title g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-2-REP-NLM-BUCKETS-45N-1--rep-nlm-buckets-desc">\n  <title id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-2-REP-NLM-BUCKETS-45N-1--rep-nlm-buckets-title">Two buckets lifted by 45 N: one system for a, another for the tension</title>\n  <desc id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-2-REP-NLM-BUCKETS-45N-1--rep-nlm-buckets-desc">Stage 1: A 2 kg bucket hangs from a light rope tied under a 1 kg bucket, and a second rope lifts the top bucket with 45 N; g = 10 m/s², up positive. Stage 2: With both buckets as one 3 kg system the rope between them is internal: 45 − 30 = 3a, so a = 5 m/s² upward. Stage 3: Isolating the lower bucket makes the rope force external: T − 20 = 2 × 5, so T = 30 N.</desc>\n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-2-REP-NLM-BUCKETS-45N-1--rep-nlm-buckets-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-2-REP-NLM-BUCKETS-45N-1--rep-nlm-buckets-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-BUCKETS-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">Two buckets on ropes, lifted by 45 N</text>\n    <line x1="170" y1="40" x2="170" y2="90" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <path d="M135 90 L143 140 H197 L205 90 Z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="170" y="120" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14">1 kg</text>\n    <line x1="170" y1="140" x2="170" y2="190" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <path d="M125 190 L133 250 H207 L215 190 Z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="170" y="225" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14">2 kg</text>\n    <line x1="170" y1="88" x2="170" y2="44" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-2-REP-NLM-BUCKETS-45N-1--rep-nlm-buckets-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="180" y="58">45 N</text>\n    <text x="185" y="170">rope between</text>\n    <text x="20" y="290">g = 10 m/s², up positive</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-BUCKETS-V1" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <rect x="115" y="80" width="110" height="180" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="6 4"/>\n    <text x="40" y="120">system 1:</text>\n    <text x="40" y="142">both buckets</text>\n    <text x="450" y="60" font-weight="600">system 1 (both, 3 kg): tension inside</text>\n    <text x="450" y="82">45 − 30 = 3a</text>\n    <text x="450" y="104" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">a = 5 m/s² up</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-BUCKETS-V2" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <rect x="118" y="182" width="104" height="76" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="6 4"/>\n    <line x1="150" y1="192" x2="150" y2="156" stroke="#1f6fb2" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-2-REP-NLM-BUCKETS-45N-1--rep-nlm-buckets-arrow-acc)"/>\n    <text x="140" y="172" text-anchor="end" fill="#1f6fb2">T</text>\n    <text x="450" y="150" font-weight="600">system 2 (lower bucket): T is external</text>\n    <text x="450" y="172">T − 20 = 2 × 5</text>\n    <text x="450" y="194" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">T = 30 N</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n<div class="g9-stage-controls"><div class="g9-stage-chips" role="group" aria-label="Stages"><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="0" data-g9-stage-desc="Show the two buckets, the ropes and the 45 N lift.">1: The buckets</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="1" data-g9-stage-desc="Box both buckets so the inner rope drops out.">2: Whole system</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="2" data-g9-stage-desc="Box the lower bucket so the tension is external.">3: Lower bucket</button></div><p class="g9-stage-desc" data-g9-stage-desc-text></p><div class="g9-stage-stepper"><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="prev">Previous stage</button><span data-g9-stage-label></span><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="next">Next stage</button></div></div><figcaption data-g9-block="representation_bridge" data-g9-caption="purpose">Show the two buckets and the 45 N lift, with one system boundary for the acceleration and another for the rope tension.</figcaption></figure></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-repair_closure" data-blueprint-slot="repair_closure" data-required="true"><section class="g9-cu-support" data-g9-support-for="CU-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-2"><h3>Add the body equations into one system equation only where the internal pair should drop out, then isolate the body that makes the requested interaction force external.</h3><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-trap-card" data-g9-component="TRAP_REPAIR" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_TRAP_CARD" data-g9-component-items="2" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-2"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-trap-repair"><summary>Mistake clinic · diagnose and repair</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="wrong_path"><h4>A tempting wrong path</h4><ul><li>The contact force between two bodies can be cancelled from each individual FBD because it is an internal force of the pair.</li><li>Bodies with the same acceleration must have the same net force.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="diagnose"><h4>Diagnose</h4><ul><li>On body A alone, is the force from body B external to A?</li><li>Two connected bodies of different masses share acceleration a. Must their individual net forces be equal?</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="repair"><h4>Repair</h4><ul><li>Yes. Keep it in A&#x27;s equation. It cancels only after A and B are deliberately combined into one system equation.</li><li>No. The constraint can make their accelerations equal while Newton II gives different net forces m_A a and m_B a when the masses differ.</li></ul></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-triad" data-g9-component="QUICK_CHECK" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="TRIAD" data-g9-component-items="2" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-2"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="independent_check"><h4>1-2-3 quick check</h4><ol class="g9-triad"><li data-g9-triad-role=""><span class="g9-triad-head">1 · Check</span><span class="g9-triad-text">Check the body you did not use: its own forces must give its mass times the same acceleration.</span></li><li data-g9-triad-role=""><span class="g9-triad-head">2 · Check</span><span class="g9-triad-text">Reduce the driving force until the acceleration is zero: the internal force must then equal what simple balance of the isolated body gives.</span></li></ol></div></div></section></section></div></div><div class="g9-split g9-split-support-only"><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-repair_closure" data-blueprint-slot="repair_closure" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-recall-card" data-g9-component="EXIT_RECALL" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="RECALL_CARD"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_task"><h4>Now you do one</h4><p>Two bodies are constrained to keep a fixed separation along one straight coordinate. State the acceleration relation, write the form of the two body equations, and explain when their interaction force can disappear from an equation.</p></div><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your answer<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1A-MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-exit"><summary>Model answer</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1A-MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-exit"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_answer"><p>With same-sense coordinates the constraint gives a_A = a_B = a. Each body keeps the interaction force in its own Newton-II equation; the equal-and-opposite pair cancels only in a combined A+B system equation.</p><ol><li>Fixed relative separation implies equal velocity and acceleration components along the constrained coordinate.</li><li>Each individual body experiences the partner force as an external force.</li><li>When both bodies are included in one system, the internal partner pair cancels in the summed equation.</li></ol></div></template></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-chips" data-g9-component="PRACTICE_LINKS" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="CHIPS"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="practice_navigation"><h4>Practice this concept in Core2</h4><ul><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2009-P2-Q55" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2009-P2-Q55">IIT-JEE · 2009 · Paper II · Q55</a></li></ul></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-chips" data-g9-component="LEARNING_TRANSITIONS" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="CHIPS"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="learning_transitions"><h4>Next steps</h4><div class="g9-transitions-row" data-g9-transitions><a class="g9-transition-chip g9-chip-practice" data-g9-transition="PRACTICE" href="core2.html">Practice this concept &rarr;</a><a class="g9-transition-chip g9-chip-qb" data-g9-transition="QUESTION_BANK" href="../../../public/question-bank/index.html?capability=MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL">All questions in Question Bank &rarr;</a></div></div></div></section></div></div></article><article id="g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1A" data-g9-search-text="Physics Laws of Motion Ideal-string tension belongs to each body-specific FBD Hard concept Ideal-string tension belongs to each body-specific FBD" class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-concept-header" data-g9-component="CONCEPT_HEADER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="CONCEPT_HEADER"><h2>Ideal-string tension belongs to each body-specific FBD</h2><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1A" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-NLM-FIRST-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Laws of Motion"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Laws of Motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION" data-g9-meta-value="Ideal-string tension belongs to each body-specific FBD"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Ideal-string tension belongs to each body-specific FBD</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION" data-g9-meta-value="HARD"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Hard concept</span></span></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-chips" data-g9-component="SECTION_ROUTE" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="CHIPS"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="section_route"><h4>Sections</h4><nav data-g9-section-route aria-label="Sections in this concept"><ol><li><a href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-1">Declare the ideal string before writing a single T, then draw that tension on each attached body, pulling away from the body along its own segment.</a></li><li><a href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-2">Check that nothing breaks the ideal model, then take the tension from a chosen body&#x27;s signed Newton-II equation instead of setting it equal to a weight or an applied force.</a></li></ol></nav></div></div></section><div class="g9-split"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-construction" data-blueprint-slot="construction" data-required="true"><section id="g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-1" class="g9-cu" data-g9-cu="CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-1"><div class="g9-component g9-c-unit-header" data-g9-component="UNIT_HEADER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="UNIT_HEADER" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-1"><div class="g9-unit-head"><span class="g9-unit-no" aria-hidden="true">1</span><h3>Declare the ideal string before writing a single T, then draw that tension on each attached body, pulling away from the body along its own segment.</h3><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-concept">Hard concept</span></div><nav class="g9-cu-nav" data-g9-unit-navigation aria-label="Concept Book section navigation"><span data-g9-route-position>Section 11 of 14</span><span class="g9-cu-nav-links"><a data-g9-prev-section href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-2" aria-label="Previous section: Add the body equations into one system equation only where the internal pair should drop out, then isolate the body that makes the requested interaction force external.">← Previous</a><a data-g9-next-section href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-2" aria-label="Next section: Check that nothing breaks the ideal model, then take the tension from a chosen body&#x27;s signed Newton-II equation instead of setting it equal to a weight or an applied force.">Next →</a></span></nav></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-banner" data-g9-component="KEY_STEP" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="BANNER"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="inferential_jump"><h4>The key step</h4><p>For one taut massless ideal string, with any redirection treated as frictionless and massless, the tension magnitude can be represented by one T throughout that same string; each body still receives its own tension force along the local string direction.</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-tile" data-g9-component="MODEL_CONTRACT" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_TILE"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-prerequisites"><summary>What you need before this</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="entry_assumptions"><h4>Prerequisites</h4><ul><li>Can assign forces to the body they act on.</li><li>Can apply Newton II to a chosen body.</li></ul><ul><li data-g9-prereq="CAP-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP" data-bridged="true"><a href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP">Assign each force arrow to the body on which that force acts.</a></li><li data-g9-prereq="CAP-NLM-SECOND-LAW" data-bridged="true"><a href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW">Relate signed net external force to acceleration with F_net = m a.</a></li></ul></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-step-cards" data-g9-component="CONSTRUCTION_STEPS" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEP_CARDS" data-g9-component-items="3" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-1"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="construction"><ol><li data-g9-step="NLM10-1"><strong>State the idealization before using one tension symbol: taut inextensible massless string, with any pulley/redirection frictionless and massless for this bounded model.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> Uniform tension is a consequence of the ideal model and must not be silently transferred to a nonideal rope or pulley.<br><em>Result:</em> ideal single string model declared -&gt; one tension magnitude T is admissible throughout that string</li><li data-g9-step="NLM10-2"><strong>For each attached body, draw the string&#x27;s pull along the local string direction away from the body.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> Tension is a force exerted by the string on that body and belongs on that body&#x27;s FBD.<br><em>Result:</em> body A FBD contains string-on-A force of magnitude T; body B FBD contains string-on-B force of magnitude T</li><li data-g9-step="NLM10-3"><strong>Use the same symbol T for those magnitudes only while the declared ideal assumptions hold.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> A massless string segment cannot sustain a nonzero net force in this idealized model, and a frictionless massless redirection does not create a tension-magnitude jump.<br><em>Result:</em> T_A = T_B = T under the declared ideal-string/redirection conditions</li></ol></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-equation-card" data-g9-component="EQUATIONS" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_EQUATION_CARD" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-1"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-equations"><summary>Formula reference · meaning and conditions</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="equation_matrix"><h4>Equations and validity</h4><div class="g9-table-scroll" data-g9-equation-matrix><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Equation</th><th scope="col">What it tells you</th><th scope="col">When you can use it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr data-g9-relation-ref="REL-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION"><td><div class="g9-math" data-g9-math="mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></math></div></td><td><p>For one taut massless ideal string, with any direction-changing pulley/support treated as frictionless and massless in this bounded model, the tension magnitude is uniform throughout that same string.</p></td><td><ul><li>The same taut string connects the stated segments.</li><li>The string is massless and inextensible in the ideal model.</li><li>Any pulley/redirection used here is frictionless and massless and only changes string direction.</li><li>No nonideal feature is introduced that would require different tension magnitudes.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-predict-reveal-worked-card" data-g9-component="WORKED_EXAMPLE" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="PREDICT_REVEAL_WORKED_CARD" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-1"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_anchor"><h4>Watch one · predict before reveal</h4><p>A light, taut, inextensible string runs from a 2 kg cart on a smooth table, over a frictionless light pulley at the table&#x27;s edge, down to a 1 kg hanging mass. Take g = 10 m/s². State the model that allows one tension T, list the forces on each body, and find the acceleration and T.</p><p class="g9-worked-instruction">Before opening each step, say what you would do next.</p><ol class="g9-watch-steps"><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 1, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>The string is light, taut and inextensible and the pulley is light and frictionless, so one tension T acts along the whole string.</p></div></details></li><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 2, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>On the cart: T pulls it toward the pulley; its weight and the table&#x27;s normal force balance vertically.</p></div></details></li><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 3, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>On the hanging mass: T pulls it up and its 10 N weight pulls it down; both bodies share one acceleration magnitude a.</p></div></details></li><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 4, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>Cart: T = 2a. Hanging mass, down positive: 10 − T = 1 × a. Adding gives 10 = 3a, so a = 10/3 m/s² and T = 20/3 N.</p></div></details></li></ol><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="worked-result"><summary>Result and independent check</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_result"><h4>Result</h4><p>One T is allowed by the ideal string and pulley. The acceleration is 10/3 ≈ 3.33 m/s² and T = 20/3 ≈ 6.67 N.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_check"><h4>Check</h4><p>T ≈ 6.67 N is less than the 10 N weight, as it must be for a mass that accelerates downward.</p></div></div></details></div></div></section></section></div><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-representation" data-blueprint-slot="representation" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-staged-visual" data-g9-component="STAGED_VISUAL" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="STAGED_VISUAL" data-g9-component-items="3" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-1"><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-1-REP-NLM-TABLE-CART-HANGING" data-g9-stage="TEACHING" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-TABLE-CART-HANGING" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="3" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-TABLE-V0 VIS-NLM-TABLE-V1 VIS-NLM-TABLE-V2" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 900 320" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-1-REP-NLM-TABLE-CART-HANGING-1--rep-nlm-table-title g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-1-REP-NLM-TABLE-CART-HANGING-1--rep-nlm-table-desc">\n  <title id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-1-REP-NLM-TABLE-CART-HANGING-1--rep-nlm-table-title">Cart on a table and a hanging mass: one ideal string, one T on each body</title>\n  <desc id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-1-REP-NLM-TABLE-CART-HANGING-1--rep-nlm-table-desc">Stage 1: A light, taut, inextensible string runs from a 2 kg cart on a smooth table over a light frictionless pulley at the edge down to a 1 kg hanging mass; g = 10 m/s². Stage 2: The ideal string and pulley allow one tension T: it pulls the cart toward the pulley and pulls the mass up, while the mass\'s 10 N weight pulls down; cart: T = 2a, mass: 10 − T = 1a. Stage 3: Adding gives 10 = 3a, so a = 10/3 ≈ 3.33 m/s² and T = 20/3 ≈ 6.67 N, less than the 10 N weight.</desc>\n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-1-REP-NLM-TABLE-CART-HANGING-1--rep-nlm-table-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-1-REP-NLM-TABLE-CART-HANGING-1--rep-nlm-table-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-TABLE-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">2 kg cart on a smooth table, 1 kg hanging mass</text>\n    <line x1="20" y1="150" x2="300" y2="150" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="150" x2="300" y2="300" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <rect x="80" y="88" width="90" height="44" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="125" y="115" text-anchor="middle">2 kg</text>\n    <circle cx="98" cy="141" r="9" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="152" cy="141" r="9" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="310" cy="140" r="14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="310" cy="140" r="3" fill="currentColor" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="170" y1="110" x2="310" y2="126" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8"/>\n    <line x1="324" y1="140" x2="324" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8"/>\n    <rect x="302" y="220" width="44" height="40" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="324" y="245" text-anchor="middle">1 kg</text>\n    <text x="20" y="190">light, taut, inextensible string</text>\n    <text x="20" y="212">light frictionless pulley; g = 10 m/s²</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-TABLE-V1" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <line x1="172" y1="118" x2="232" y2="118" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-1-REP-NLM-TABLE-CART-HANGING-1--rep-nlm-table-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="150" y="100">T (string on cart)</text>\n    <line x1="324" y1="222" x2="324" y2="182" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-1-REP-NLM-TABLE-CART-HANGING-1--rep-nlm-table-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="356" y="214">T (string on mass)</text>\n    <line x1="324" y1="262" x2="324" y2="302" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-1-REP-NLM-TABLE-CART-HANGING-1--rep-nlm-table-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="334" y="300">10 N</text>\n    <text x="500" y="60" font-weight="600">ideal string + ideal pulley:</text>\n    <text x="500" y="82">one tension T on both bodies,</text>\n    <text x="500" y="104">each pulling away along its segment</text>\n    <text x="500" y="126">cart: T = 2a</text>\n    <text x="500" y="148">mass (down +): 10 − T = 1a</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-TABLE-V2" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="500" y="190" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">add: 10 = 3a, a = 10/3 ≈ 3.33 m/s²</text>\n    <text x="500" y="212" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">T = 2a = 20/3 ≈ 6.67 N</text>\n    <text x="500" y="234">T &lt; 10 N: the mass accelerates</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n<div class="g9-stage-controls"><div class="g9-stage-chips" role="group" aria-label="Stages"><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="0" data-g9-stage-desc="Show the cart, pulley, string and hanging mass.">1: The set-up</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="1" data-g9-stage-desc="Declare the ideal string and draw T on each attached body.">2: Tension on each body</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="2" data-g9-stage-desc="Solve the two equations.">3: Solve</button></div><p class="g9-stage-desc" data-g9-stage-desc-text></p><div class="g9-stage-stepper"><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="prev">Previous stage</button><span data-g9-stage-label></span><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="next">Next stage</button></div></div><figcaption data-g9-block="representation_bridge" data-g9-caption="purpose">Show the 2 kg cart and 1 kg hanging mass on one ideal string, with the one tension drawn on each body along its own segment.</figcaption></figure></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-repair_closure" data-blueprint-slot="repair_closure" data-required="true"><section class="g9-cu-support" data-g9-support-for="CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-1"><h3>Declare the ideal string before writing a single T, then draw that tension on each attached body, pulling away from the body along its own segment.</h3><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-trap-card" data-g9-component="TRAP_REPAIR" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_TRAP_CARD" data-g9-component-items="2" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-1"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-trap-repair"><summary>Mistake clinic · diagnose and repair</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="wrong_path"><h4>A tempting wrong path</h4><ul><li>Tension forces at the two ends of a string cancel because they are equal and opposite.</li><li>Every rope over every pulley has the same tension on both sides.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="diagnose"><h4>Diagnose</h4><ul><li>Do the two end tensions act on the same chosen body?</li><li>What model assumptions justify using one symbol T throughout the rope?</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="repair"><h4>Repair</h4><ul><li>No. Put the tension force acting on each body in that body&#x27;s own FBD. Equal magnitudes across the ideal string do not make them a cancelling pair on one body.</li><li>State the ideal massless string and frictionless/massless redirection assumptions first. Without them, equal tension is not automatic.</li></ul></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-triad" data-g9-component="QUICK_CHECK" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="TRIAD" data-g9-component-items="2" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-1"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="independent_check"><h4>1-2-3 quick check</h4><ol class="g9-triad"><li data-g9-triad-role=""><span class="g9-triad-head">1 · Check</span><span class="g9-triad-text">The hanging mass accelerates downward, so the tension on it must come out smaller than its weight; a tension above the weight would make it rise.</span></li><li data-g9-triad-role=""><span class="g9-triad-head">2 · Check</span><span class="g9-triad-text">Every term of each body equation must be a force in newtons: a mass times an acceleration, a weight, or the tension.</span></li></ol></div></div></section></section></div></div><div class="g9-split"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-construction" data-blueprint-slot="construction" data-required="true"><section id="g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-2" class="g9-cu" data-g9-cu="CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-2"><div class="g9-component g9-c-unit-header" data-g9-component="UNIT_HEADER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="UNIT_HEADER" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-2"><div class="g9-unit-head"><span class="g9-unit-no" aria-hidden="true">2</span><h3>Check that nothing breaks the ideal model, then take the tension from a chosen body&#x27;s signed Newton-II equation instead of setting it equal to a weight or an applied force.</h3><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-concept">Hard concept</span></div><nav class="g9-cu-nav" data-g9-unit-navigation aria-label="Concept Book section navigation"><span data-g9-route-position>Section 12 of 14</span><span class="g9-cu-nav-links"><a data-g9-prev-section href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-1" aria-label="Previous section: Declare the ideal string before writing a single T, then draw that tension on each attached body, pulling away from the body along its own segment.">← Previous</a><a data-g9-next-section href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-1" aria-label="Next section: Measure both coordinates away from the fixed pulley, write the changing length as y_A + y_B = constant, and differentiate that one relation.">Next →</a></span></nav></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-step-cards" data-g9-component="CONSTRUCTION_STEPS" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEP_CARDS" data-g9-component-items="2" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-2"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="construction"><ol><li data-g9-step="NLM10-4"><strong>Check whether the problem introduces rope mass, elasticity, pulley friction, or rotational inertia; if it does, stop using equal tension unless a different model justifies it.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> Those changes alter the force transmission assumptions that supported one common T.<br><em>Result:</em> nonideal feature present -&gt; equal-tension relation not licensed by this capability</li><li data-g9-step="NLM10-5"><strong>Determine the tension magnitude from a chosen body&#x27;s signed Newton-II equation; do not replace T by mg or by an applied force merely because those forces appear in the same setup.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> Uniform tension says the ideal string transmits one magnitude throughout itself; it does not say that magnitude equals some different force. For a hanging body, T=mg only in the special zero-acceleration case.<br><em>Result:</em> for +y upward on a hanging mass: T - mg = ma_y; therefore a_y=0 -&gt; T=mg, a_y&gt;0 -&gt; T&gt;mg, and a_y&lt;0 -&gt; T&lt;mg.</li></ol></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-predict-reveal-worked-card" data-g9-component="WORKED_EXAMPLE" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="PREDICT_REVEAL_WORKED_CARD" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-2"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_anchor"><h4>Watch one · predict before reveal</h4><p>A 2 kg lamp hangs from a light cord inside a lift. Take up as positive and g = 10 m/s². Find the cord tension when the lift (a) moves up at steady speed, (b) accelerates upward at 2 m/s², and (c) accelerates downward at 3 m/s².</p><p class="g9-worked-instruction">Before opening each step, say what you would do next.</p><ol class="g9-watch-steps"><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 1, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>Only the cord and the lamp&#x27;s weight act on the lamp, and nothing makes the cord non-ideal, so T comes from the lamp&#x27;s own equation: T − 20 = 2a.</p></div></details></li><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 2, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>(a) Steady speed means a = 0, so T = 20 N.</p></div></details></li><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 3, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>(b) a = +2 m/s², so T = 20 + 2 × 2 = 24 N.</p></div></details></li><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 4, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>(c) a = −3 m/s², so T = 20 + 2 × (−3) = 14 N.</p></div></details></li></ol><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="worked-result"><summary>Result and independent check</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_result"><h4>Result</h4><p>The tension is 20 N, 24 N and 14 N in the three cases.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_check"><h4>Check</h4><p>In free fall, a = −10 m/s², the same equation gives T = 20 − 20 = 0: the cord goes slack, as expected.</p></div></div></details></div></div></section></section></div><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-representation" data-blueprint-slot="representation" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-staged-visual" data-g9-component="STAGED_VISUAL" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="STAGED_VISUAL" data-g9-component-items="3" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-2"><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-2-REP-NLM-LIFT-LAMP-TENSION" data-g9-stage="TEACHING" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-LIFT-LAMP-TENSION" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="3" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-LIFTLAMP-V0 VIS-NLM-LIFTLAMP-V1 VIS-NLM-LIFTLAMP-V2" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 820 300" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-2-REP-NLM-LIFT-LAMP-TENSION-1--rep-nlm-liftlamp-title g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-2-REP-NLM-LIFT-LAMP-TENSION-1--rep-nlm-liftlamp-desc">\n  <title id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-2-REP-NLM-LIFT-LAMP-TENSION-1--rep-nlm-liftlamp-title">Lamp on a cord in a lift: tension from T − mg = m a_y in three cases</title>\n  <desc id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-2-REP-NLM-LIFT-LAMP-TENSION-1--rep-nlm-liftlamp-desc">Stage 1: A 2 kg lamp hangs from a light cord inside a lift that (a) moves up at steady speed, (b) accelerates upward at 2 m/s², (c) accelerates downward at 3 m/s²; up positive, g = 10 m/s². Stage 2: Two forces act on the lamp, the cord\'s T up and its 20 N weight down, so T − mg = m a_y and T = 2(10 + a_y). Stage 3: T = 20 N at steady speed, 24 N when accelerating up at 2 m/s² and 14 N when accelerating down at 3 m/s².</desc>\n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-2-REP-NLM-LIFT-LAMP-TENSION-1--rep-nlm-liftlamp-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-2-REP-NLM-LIFT-LAMP-TENSION-1--rep-nlm-liftlamp-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-LIFTLAMP-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">A 2 kg lamp on a light cord inside a lift</text>\n    <line x1="80" y1="40" x2="80" y2="70" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <rect x="20" y="70" width="120" height="170" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="80" y1="70" x2="80" y2="130" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8"/>\n    <path d="M64 156 L72 130 H88 L96 156 Z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="80" y="262" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14">(a) up, steady</text>\n    <line x1="220" y1="40" x2="220" y2="70" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <rect x="160" y="70" width="120" height="170" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="220" y1="70" x2="220" y2="130" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8"/>\n    <path d="M204 156 L212 130 H228 L236 156 Z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="220" y="262" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14">(b) a = +2 m/s²</text>\n    <line x1="360" y1="40" x2="360" y2="70" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <rect x="300" y="70" width="120" height="170" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="360" y1="70" x2="360" y2="130" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8"/>\n    <path d="M344 156 L352 130 H368 L376 156 Z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="360" y="262" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14">(c) a = −3 m/s²</text>\n    <line x1="150" y1="150" x2="150" y2="110" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-2-REP-NLM-LIFT-LAMP-TENSION-1--rep-nlm-liftlamp-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="146" y="170" text-anchor="end">v</text>\n    <line x1="290" y1="170" x2="290" y2="120" stroke="#1f6fb2" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-2-REP-NLM-LIFT-LAMP-TENSION-1--rep-nlm-liftlamp-arrow-acc)"/>\n    <text x="286" y="186" text-anchor="end" fill="#1f6fb2">a</text>\n    <line x1="430" y1="120" x2="430" y2="170" stroke="#1f6fb2" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-2-REP-NLM-LIFT-LAMP-TENSION-1--rep-nlm-liftlamp-arrow-acc)"/>\n    <text x="434" y="112" fill="#1f6fb2">a</text>\n    <text x="20" y="290">up positive; g = 10 m/s²</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-LIFTLAMP-V1" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <line x1="80" y1="128" x2="80" y2="92" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-2-REP-NLM-LIFT-LAMP-TENSION-1--rep-nlm-liftlamp-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="88" y="104">T</text>\n    <line x1="80" y1="158" x2="80" y2="198" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-2-REP-NLM-LIFT-LAMP-TENSION-1--rep-nlm-liftlamp-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="88" y="192">20 N</text>\n    <text x="470" y="60" font-weight="600">lamp, +y up:</text>\n    <text x="470" y="82">T − mg = m a_y</text>\n    <text x="470" y="104">T = 2(10 + a_y)</text>\n    <text x="470" y="126">T comes from the equation, not mg</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-LIFTLAMP-V2" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="80" y="230" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">T = 20 N</text>\n    <text x="220" y="230" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">T = 24 N</text>\n    <text x="360" y="230" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">T = 14 N</text>\n    <text x="470" y="170" fill="#1f6fb2">(a) a_y = 0: T = 20 N = mg</text>\n    <text x="470" y="192" fill="#1f6fb2">(b) a_y = +2: T = 24 N &gt; mg</text>\n    <text x="470" y="214" fill="#1f6fb2">(c) a_y = −3: T = 14 N &lt; mg</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n<div class="g9-stage-controls"><div class="g9-stage-chips" role="group" aria-label="Stages"><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="0" data-g9-stage-desc="Show the lamp in the lift moving up steadily, accelerating up and accelerating down.">1: Three lift motions</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="1" data-g9-stage-desc="Draw T and the 20 N weight and write the signed equation.">2: Forces on the lamp</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="2" data-g9-stage-desc="Evaluate T for each acceleration.">3: Tension in each case</button></div><p class="g9-stage-desc" data-g9-stage-desc-text></p><div class="g9-stage-stepper"><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="prev">Previous stage</button><span data-g9-stage-label></span><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="next">Next stage</button></div></div><figcaption data-g9-block="representation_bridge" data-g9-caption="purpose">Show the lamp in the lift in its three motion cases so the tension is taken from the lamp&#x27;s signed equation, not set equal to its weight.</figcaption></figure></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-repair_closure" data-blueprint-slot="repair_closure" data-required="true"><section class="g9-cu-support" data-g9-support-for="CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-2"><h3>Check that nothing breaks the ideal model, then take the tension from a chosen body&#x27;s signed Newton-II equation instead of setting it equal to a weight or an applied force.</h3><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-trap-card" data-g9-component="TRAP_REPAIR" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_TRAP_CARD" data-g9-component-items="2" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-2"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-trap-repair"><summary>Mistake clinic · diagnose and repair</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="wrong_path"><h4>A tempting wrong path</h4><ul><li>Every rope over every pulley has the same tension on both sides.</li><li>The tension in a string holding a hanging mass is always equal to the mass&#x27;s weight.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="diagnose"><h4>Diagnose</h4><ul><li>What model assumptions justify using one symbol T throughout the rope?</li><li>A hanging mass accelerates upward. Can T still equal mg while Newton II gives a nonzero upward net force?</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="repair"><h4>Repair</h4><ul><li>State the ideal massless string and frictionless/massless redirection assumptions first. Without them, equal tension is not automatic.</li><li>Write the signed body equation. With upward positive, T-mg=ma, so upward acceleration requires T&gt;mg; equality belongs only to zero vertical acceleration.</li></ul></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-triad" data-g9-component="QUICK_CHECK" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="TRIAD" data-g9-component-items="2" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-2"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="independent_check"><h4>1-2-3 quick check</h4><ol class="g9-triad"><li data-g9-triad-role=""><span class="g9-triad-head">1 · Check</span><span class="g9-triad-text">Let the downward acceleration reach g: the equation must give zero tension, a slack cord.</span></li><li data-g9-triad-role=""><span class="g9-triad-head">2 · Check</span><span class="g9-triad-text">Upward acceleration must raise the tension above the weight and downward acceleration must lower it; a tension on the wrong side of mg shows a sign error.</span></li></ol></div></div></section></section></div></div><div class="g9-split g9-split-support-only"><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-repair_closure" data-blueprint-slot="repair_closure" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-recall-card" data-g9-component="EXIT_RECALL" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="RECALL_CARD"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_task"><h4>Now you do one</h4><p>Two bodies are attached to the same taut ideal string, redirected only by a frictionless massless fixed pulley. State the tension relation and explain where the two tension forces appear.</p></div><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your answer<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1A-MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-exit"><summary>Model answer</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1A-MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-exit"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_answer"><p>Under the declared ideal model, both string segments have the same tension magnitude T. Each body has its own string-on-body tension force on its own FBD; the forces are not cancelled across separate body diagrams.</p><ol><li>The ideal assumptions license one common tension magnitude throughout the same string.</li><li>Each tension force acts on a different body and points along that body&#x27;s local string segment.</li><li>Equal magnitude does not mean the forces cancel in an individual-body equation.</li></ol></div></template></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-chips" data-g9-component="PRACTICE_LINKS" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="CHIPS"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="practice_navigation"><h4>Practice this concept in Core2</h4><ul><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q03" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q03">IIT-JEE · 2007 · Paper I · Q3</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-NEET-2020-E1-Q160" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-NEET-2020-E1-Q160">NEET (UG) · 2020 · English Set E1 · Q160</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2009-P2-Q55" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2009-P2-Q55">IIT-JEE · 2009 · Paper II · Q55</a></li></ul></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-chips" data-g9-component="LEARNING_TRANSITIONS" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="CHIPS"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="learning_transitions"><h4>Next steps</h4><div class="g9-transitions-row" data-g9-transitions><a class="g9-transition-chip g9-chip-practice" data-g9-transition="PRACTICE" href="core2.html">Practice this concept &rarr;</a><a class="g9-transition-chip g9-chip-qb" data-g9-transition="QUESTION_BANK" href="../../../public/question-bank/index.html?capability=MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION">All questions in Question Bank &rarr;</a></div></div></div></section></div></div></article><article id="g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1A" data-g9-search-text="Physics Laws of Motion One fixed-pulley string gives one length constraint Hard concept One fixed-pulley string gives one length constraint" class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-concept-header" data-g9-component="CONCEPT_HEADER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="CONCEPT_HEADER"><h2>One fixed-pulley string gives one length constraint</h2><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1A" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-NLM-FIRST-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Laws of Motion"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Laws of Motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT" data-g9-meta-value="One fixed-pulley string gives one length constraint"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>One fixed-pulley string gives one length constraint</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT" data-g9-meta-value="HARD"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Hard concept</span></span></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-chips" data-g9-component="SECTION_ROUTE" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="CHIPS"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="section_route"><h4>Sections</h4><nav data-g9-section-route aria-label="Sections in this concept"><ol><li><a href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-1">Measure both coordinates away from the fixed pulley, write the changing length as y_A + y_B = constant, and differentiate that one relation.</a></li><li><a href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-2">Read the signed result back as motion, and keep the length argument for the accelerations separate from the ideal-pulley argument for equal tension.</a></li></ol></nav></div></div></section><div class="g9-split"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-construction" data-blueprint-slot="construction" data-required="true"><section id="g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-1" class="g9-cu" data-g9-cu="CU-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-1"><div class="g9-component g9-c-unit-header" data-g9-component="UNIT_HEADER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="UNIT_HEADER" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-1"><div class="g9-unit-head"><span class="g9-unit-no" aria-hidden="true">1</span><h3>Measure both coordinates away from the fixed pulley, write the changing length as y_A + y_B = constant, and differentiate that one relation.</h3><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-concept">Hard concept</span></div><nav class="g9-cu-nav" data-g9-unit-navigation aria-label="Concept Book section navigation"><span data-g9-route-position>Section 13 of 14</span><span class="g9-cu-nav-links"><a data-g9-prev-section href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-2" aria-label="Previous section: Check that nothing breaks the ideal model, then take the tension from a chosen body&#x27;s signed Newton-II equation instead of setting it equal to a weight or an applied force.">← Previous</a><a data-g9-next-section href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-2" aria-label="Next section: Read the signed result back as motion, and keep the length argument for the accelerations separate from the ideal-pulley argument for equal tension.">Next →</a></span></nav></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-banner" data-g9-component="KEY_STEP" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="BANNER"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="inferential_jump"><h4>The key step</h4><p>For one taut inextensible string over one fixed ideal pulley, the two changing straight segment lengths add to a constant; with coordinates measured away from the pulley this gives y_A + y_B = constant and therefore a_A + a_B = 0.</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-tile" data-g9-component="MODEL_CONTRACT" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_TILE"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-prerequisites"><summary>What you need before this</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="entry_assumptions"><h4>Prerequisites</h4><ul><li>Can identify a common-acceleration connection when separation is fixed.</li><li>Can use an ideal single-string tension model when forces are also being solved.</li></ul><ul><li data-g9-prereq="CAP-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL" data-bridged="true"><a href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL">Identify which bodies are constrained to share one acceleration component and write consistent body-specific or whole-system Newton-II equations.</a></li><li data-g9-prereq="CAP-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION" data-bridged="true"><a href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION">Use one declared ideal-string model to assign tension forces and, where justified, one common tension magnitude throughout that same string.</a></li></ul></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-step-cards" data-g9-component="CONSTRUCTION_STEPS" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEP_CARDS" data-g9-component-items="3" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-1"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="construction"><ol><li data-g9-step="NLM11-1"><strong>Restrict the model to one taut inextensible string over one fixed ideal pulley and choose y_A and y_B positive away from the pulley along the two straight segments.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> The coordinate declaration determines the signs in the length relation and blocks accidental import of movable-pulley rules.<br><em>Result:</em> single fixed pulley; coordinates y_A,y_B measured away from pulley</li><li data-g9-step="NLM11-2"><strong>Write the variable part of the string length as y_A + y_B and set its total equal to a constant.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> An inextensible string has fixed total length; the wrapped portion around a fixed pulley is constant in this bounded geometry.<br><em>Result:</em> y_A + y_B = L_free = constant</li><li data-g9-step="NLM11-3"><strong>Differentiate the fixed-length relation at the kinematic level needed by the problem.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> A constant length has zero time derivative at each order.<br><em>Result:</em> v_A + v_B = 0 and a_A + a_B = 0</li></ol></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-equation-card" data-g9-component="EQUATIONS" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_EQUATION_CARD" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-1"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-equations"><summary>Formula reference · meaning and conditions</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="equation_matrix"><h4>Equations and validity</h4><div class="g9-table-scroll" data-g9-equation-matrix><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Equation</th><th scope="col">What it tells you</th><th scope="col">When you can use it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr data-g9-relation-ref="REL-NLM-FIXED-PULLEY-STRING-LENGTH"><td><div class="g9-math" data-g9-math="mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>free</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mtext>constant</mtext><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></div></td><td><p>For one taut inextensible string over one fixed ideal pulley, with both coordinates measured away from the pulley along the two straight segments, the variable segment lengths sum to a constant and the signed accelerations sum to zero.</p></td><td><ul><li>There is one fixed pulley and one taut inextensible string.</li><li>Only the two declared straight segments change length; the wrapped/fixed portions are constant.</li><li>Both coordinates are measured away from the pulley along their local string segments.</li><li>The pulley itself does not translate.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-predict-reveal-worked-card" data-g9-component="WORKED_EXAMPLE" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="PREDICT_REVEAL_WORKED_CARD" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-1"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_anchor"><h4>Watch one · predict before reveal</h4><p>One taut inextensible string passes over a fixed pulley and holds A and B. With y_A and y_B measured downward from the pulley along each segment, y_A = 1.2 m and y_B = 0.8 m. A then moves down by 0.3 m. Find B&#x27;s new position, and B&#x27;s velocity at an instant when A moves down at 0.5 m/s.</p><p class="g9-worked-instruction">Before opening each step, say what you would do next.</p><ol class="g9-watch-steps"><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 1, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>Both coordinates are measured away from the pulley, so the changing length is y_A + y_B = 1.2 + 0.8 = 2.0 m, fixed.</p></div></details></li><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 2, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>After A moves down 0.3 m, y_A = 1.5 m, so y_B = 2.0 − 1.5 = 0.5 m: B has risen 0.3 m.</p></div></details></li><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 3, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>Differentiating y_A + y_B = 2.0 m gives v_A + v_B = 0.</p></div></details></li><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 4, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>With v_A = +0.5 m/s, v_B = −0.5 m/s: B moves toward the pulley at 0.5 m/s.</p></div></details></li></ol><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="worked-result"><summary>Result and independent check</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_result"><h4>Result</h4><p>B rises to y_B = 0.5 m; when A moves down at 0.5 m/s, v_B = −0.5 m/s, that is 0.5 m/s upward.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_check"><h4>Check</h4><p>The new lengths 1.5 m and 0.5 m still add to 2.0 m, so the string has neither stretched nor gone slack.</p></div></div></details></div></div></section></section></div><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-representation" data-blueprint-slot="representation" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-staged-visual" data-g9-component="STAGED_VISUAL" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="STAGED_VISUAL" data-g9-component-items="3" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-1"><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="CU-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-1-REP-NLM-STRING-LENGTH-12-08" data-g9-stage="TEACHING" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-STRING-LENGTH-12-08" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="3" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-YLEN-V0 VIS-NLM-YLEN-V1 VIS-NLM-YLEN-V2" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 820 370" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-1-REP-NLM-STRING-LENGTH-12-08-1--rep-nlm-ylen-title g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-1-REP-NLM-STRING-LENGTH-12-08-1--rep-nlm-ylen-desc">\n  <title id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-1-REP-NLM-STRING-LENGTH-12-08-1--rep-nlm-ylen-title">One string over a fixed pulley: y_A + y_B stays 2.0 m</title>\n  <desc id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-1-REP-NLM-STRING-LENGTH-12-08-1--rep-nlm-ylen-desc">Stage 1: One taut inextensible string over a fixed pulley holds A and B; measured downward from the pulley, y_A = 1.2 m and y_B = 0.8 m. Stage 2: The string length fixes y_A + y_B = 2.0 m, so v_A + v_B = 0 and a_A + a_B = 0. Stage 3: When A moves down 0.3 m to y_A = 1.5 m, y_B = 0.5 m, so B rises; when A moves down at 0.5 m/s, v_B = −0.5 m/s, 0.5 m/s upward.</desc>\n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-1-REP-NLM-STRING-LENGTH-12-08-1--rep-nlm-ylen-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-1-REP-NLM-STRING-LENGTH-12-08-1--rep-nlm-ylen-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-YLEN-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">One string over a fixed pulley holds A and B</text>\n    <line x1="120" y1="40" x2="240" y2="40" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="130" y1="40" x2="122" y2="32" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="148" y1="40" x2="140" y2="32" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="166" y1="40" x2="158" y2="32" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="184" y1="40" x2="176" y2="32" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="202" y1="40" x2="194" y2="32" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="220" y1="40" x2="212" y2="32" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="238" y1="40" x2="230" y2="32" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <circle cx="180" cy="70" r="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="180" cy="70" r="3" fill="currentColor" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="180" y1="70" x2="180" y2="40" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="160" y1="70" x2="160" y2="250" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8"/>\n    <line x1="200" y1="70" x2="200" y2="190" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8"/>\n    <rect x="140" y="250" width="40" height="30" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="160" y="270" text-anchor="middle">A</text>\n    <rect x="180" y="190" width="40" height="30" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="200" y="210" text-anchor="middle">B</text>\n    <line x1="110" y1="70" x2="110" y2="250" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <text x="104" y="205" text-anchor="end">y_A = 1.2 m</text>\n    <line x1="250" y1="70" x2="250" y2="190" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <text x="256" y="130">y_B = 0.8 m</text>\n    <text x="20" y="350">y_A and y_B measured downward from the pulley</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-YLEN-V1" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="470" y="60" font-weight="600">fixed length:</text>\n    <text x="470" y="82">y_A + y_B = 2.0 m (constant)</text>\n    <text x="470" y="104">so v_A + v_B = 0, a_A + a_B = 0</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-YLEN-V2" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <rect x="140" y="295" width="40" height="30" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/>\n    <rect x="180" y="145" width="40" height="30" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/>\n    <text x="128" y="315" text-anchor="end" fill="#1f6fb2">A +0.3 m</text>\n    <text x="262" y="167" fill="#1f6fb2">B: y_B = 0.5 m</text>\n    <text x="470" y="150" fill="#1f6fb2">A down 0.3 m: y_A = 1.5 m</text>\n    <text x="470" y="172" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">y_B = 2.0 − 1.5 = 0.5 m (B rises)</text>\n    <text x="470" y="194" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">v_A = +0.5 m/s → v_B = −0.5 m/s</text>\n    <text x="470" y="216">B moves up at 0.5 m/s</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n<div class="g9-stage-controls"><div class="g9-stage-chips" role="group" aria-label="Stages"><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="0" data-g9-stage-desc="Show A and B with y_A = 1.2 m and y_B = 0.8 m measured down from the pulley.">1: Coordinates</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="1" data-g9-stage-desc="Write the fixed-length relation and differentiate it.">2: Length relation</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="2" data-g9-stage-desc="Apply the relation to the 0.3 m move and the 0.5 m/s instant.">3: New position and velocity</button></div><p class="g9-stage-desc" data-g9-stage-desc-text></p><div class="g9-stage-stepper"><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="prev">Previous stage</button><span data-g9-stage-label></span><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="next">Next stage</button></div></div><figcaption data-g9-block="representation_bridge" data-g9-caption="purpose">Show A and B on one string over a fixed pulley with both coordinates measured away from the pulley, so their sum stays fixed.</figcaption></figure></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-repair_closure" data-blueprint-slot="repair_closure" data-required="true"><section class="g9-cu-support" data-g9-support-for="CU-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-1"><h3>Measure both coordinates away from the fixed pulley, write the changing length as y_A + y_B = constant, and differentiate that one relation.</h3><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-trap-card" data-g9-component="TRAP_REPAIR" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_TRAP_CARD" data-g9-component-items="2" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-1"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-trap-repair"><summary>Mistake clinic · diagnose and repair</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="wrong_path"><h4>A tempting wrong path</h4><ul><li>Every pulley problem has the same acceleration ratio.</li><li>Bodies on opposite sides of a fixed pulley have the same signed acceleration.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="diagnose"><h4>Diagnose</h4><ul><li>What part of the string length changes in this exact geometry, and is any pulley itself moving?</li><li>If both coordinates are positive away from the pulley, can both segment lengths increase while total string length stays fixed?</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="repair"><h4>Repair</h4><ul><li>Write the string-length equation from the actual geometry. The equal-magnitude result here is only for one fixed pulley with two changing straight segments.</li><li>No. Their signed changes are opposite: a_A + a_B = 0 in these coordinates.</li></ul></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-triad" data-g9-component="QUICK_CHECK" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="TRIAD" data-g9-component-items="2" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-1"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="independent_check"><h4>1-2-3 quick check</h4><ol class="g9-triad"><li data-g9-triad-role=""><span class="g9-triad-head">1 · Check</span><span class="g9-triad-text">Add the new segment lengths: they must still give the original total.</span></li><li data-g9-triad-role=""><span class="g9-triad-head">2 · Check</span><span class="g9-triad-text">If both coordinates grew at once the string would have to stretch, so one must shrink while the other grows.</span></li></ol></div></div></section></section></div></div><div class="g9-split"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-construction" data-blueprint-slot="construction" data-required="true"><section id="g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-2" class="g9-cu" data-g9-cu="CU-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-2"><div class="g9-component g9-c-unit-header" data-g9-component="UNIT_HEADER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="UNIT_HEADER" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-2"><div class="g9-unit-head"><span class="g9-unit-no" aria-hidden="true">2</span><h3>Read the signed result back as motion, and keep the length argument for the accelerations separate from the ideal-pulley argument for equal tension.</h3><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-concept">Hard concept</span></div><nav class="g9-cu-nav" data-g9-unit-navigation aria-label="Concept Book section navigation"><span data-g9-route-position>Section 14 of 14</span><span class="g9-cu-nav-links"><a data-g9-prev-section href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-1" aria-label="Previous section: Measure both coordinates away from the fixed pulley, write the changing length as y_A + y_B = constant, and differentiate that one relation.">← Previous</a></span></nav></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-step-cards" data-g9-component="CONSTRUCTION_STEPS" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEP_CARDS" data-g9-component-items="2" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-2"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="construction"><ol><li data-g9-step="NLM11-4"><strong>Translate the signed result back into motion: if A accelerates farther from the pulley, B accelerates toward it with equal magnitude.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> The two segment changes must cancel to preserve total string length.<br><em>Result:</em> |a_A| = |a_B| with opposite signs in the declared away-from-pulley coordinates</li><li data-g9-step="NLM11-5"><strong>Keep the kinematic string-length claim separate from the force-transmission claim: derive the acceleration relation from inextensibility and fixed geometry, and use equal tension only when the independent ideal-string/redirection assumptions license it.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> The acceleration relation comes from constant string length, whereas tension equality comes from the force model. One statement is not the proof of the other.<br><em>Result:</em> fixed-length geometry -&gt; a_A + a_B = 0 in the declared coordinates; ideal force-transmission model -&gt; T_A = T_B. Treat these as separate justified statements.</li></ol></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-predict-reveal-worked-card" data-g9-component="WORKED_EXAMPLE" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="PREDICT_REVEAL_WORKED_CARD" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-2"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_anchor"><h4>Watch one · predict before reveal</h4><p>Masses A (3 kg) and B (1 kg) hang from one light inextensible string over a fixed, frictionless, light pulley, with y_A and y_B measured downward from the pulley. Take g = 10 m/s². Using a_A + a_B = 0 and one tension T, find a_A, a_B and T, and name the assumption each of those two relations rests on.</p><p class="g9-worked-instruction">Before opening each step, say what you would do next.</p><ol class="g9-watch-steps"><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 1, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>The fixed total length gives a_A + a_B = 0 in these coordinates; this uses geometry only.</p></div></details></li><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 2, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>One tension T is licensed separately, because the string is light and the pulley is light and frictionless.</p></div></details></li><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 3, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>Down positive for each mass: A: 30 − T = 3a_A. B: 10 − T = 1 × a_B = −a_A.</p></div></details></li><li data-g9-watch-step><details class="g9-worked-step" data-g9-worked-predict><summary>Predict step 4, then reveal</summary><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>Subtracting B&#x27;s equation from A&#x27;s: 20 = 4a_A, so a_A = 5 m/s², a_B = −5 m/s², and T = 30 − 3 × 5 = 15 N.</p></div></details></li></ol><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="worked-result"><summary>Result and independent check</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_result"><h4>Result</h4><p>a_A = +5 m/s² (down), a_B = −5 m/s² (up) and T = 15 N. The acceleration relation rests on the fixed string length; the single T rests on the ideal pulley and string.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_check"><h4>Check</h4><p>As one system driven by the weight difference, (3 − 1) × 10 = 20 N on 4 kg gives 5 m/s², matching the two body equations.</p></div></div></details></div></div></section></section></div><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-representation" data-blueprint-slot="representation" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-staged-visual" data-g9-component="STAGED_VISUAL" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="STAGED_VISUAL" data-g9-component-items="3" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-2"><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="CU-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-2-REP-NLM-ATWOOD-3KG-1KG" data-g9-stage="TEACHING" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-ATWOOD-3KG-1KG" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="3" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-ATWOOD31-V0 VIS-NLM-ATWOOD31-V1 VIS-NLM-ATWOOD31-V2" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 820 370" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-2-REP-NLM-ATWOOD-3KG-1KG-1--rep-nlm-atwood31-title g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-2-REP-NLM-ATWOOD-3KG-1KG-1--rep-nlm-atwood31-desc">\n  <title id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-2-REP-NLM-ATWOOD-3KG-1KG-1--rep-nlm-atwood31-title">Atwood machine 3 kg and 1 kg: length gives a_A + a_B = 0, the ideal pulley gives one T</title>\n  <desc id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-2-REP-NLM-ATWOOD-3KG-1KG-1--rep-nlm-atwood31-desc">Stage 1: Masses A (3 kg) and B (1 kg) hang from one light inextensible string over a fixed, frictionless, light pulley, with y_A and y_B measured downward from the pulley; g = 10 m/s². Stage 2: The fixed string length gives a_A + a_B = 0 and the ideal pulley and string give one T; A: 30 − T = 3a_A and B: 10 − T = 1a_B, down positive. Stage 3: a_A = +5 m/s² (down), a_B = −5 m/s² (up) and T = 15 N; the two relations rest on two separate assumptions.</desc>\n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-2-REP-NLM-ATWOOD-3KG-1KG-1--rep-nlm-atwood31-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-2-REP-NLM-ATWOOD-3KG-1KG-1--rep-nlm-atwood31-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-ATWOOD31-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">Atwood machine: A 3 kg and B 1 kg</text>\n    <line x1="120" y1="40" x2="240" y2="40" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="130" y1="40" x2="122" y2="32" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="148" y1="40" x2="140" y2="32" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="166" y1="40" x2="158" y2="32" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="184" y1="40" x2="176" y2="32" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="202" y1="40" x2="194" y2="32" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="220" y1="40" x2="212" y2="32" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="238" y1="40" x2="230" y2="32" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <circle cx="180" cy="70" r="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="180" cy="70" r="3" fill="currentColor" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="180" y1="70" x2="180" y2="40" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="160" y1="70" x2="160" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8"/>\n    <line x1="200" y1="70" x2="200" y2="160" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8"/>\n    <rect x="128" y="220" width="64" height="44" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="160" y="247" text-anchor="middle">A 3 kg</text>\n    <rect x="172" y="160" width="56" height="36" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="200" y="183" text-anchor="middle">B 1 kg</text>\n    <text x="20" y="336">light string, fixed frictionless light pulley</text>\n    <text x="20" y="358">y_A, y_B measured down from the pulley; g = 10 m/s²</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-ATWOOD31-V1" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <line x1="140" y1="218" x2="140" y2="178" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-2-REP-NLM-ATWOOD-3KG-1KG-1--rep-nlm-atwood31-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="132" y="196" text-anchor="end">T</text>\n    <line x1="160" y1="266" x2="160" y2="302" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-2-REP-NLM-ATWOOD-3KG-1KG-1--rep-nlm-atwood31-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="150" y="300" text-anchor="end">30 N</text>\n    <line x1="220" y1="158" x2="220" y2="122" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-2-REP-NLM-ATWOOD-3KG-1KG-1--rep-nlm-atwood31-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="228" y="136">T</text>\n    <line x1="200" y1="198" x2="200" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-2-REP-NLM-ATWOOD-3KG-1KG-1--rep-nlm-atwood31-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="208" y="226">10 N</text>\n    <text x="470" y="60" font-weight="600">fixed length → a_A + a_B = 0</text>\n    <text x="470" y="82" font-weight="600">ideal pulley and string → one T</text>\n    <text x="470" y="104">A (down +): 30 − T = 3a_A</text>\n    <text x="470" y="126">B (down +): 10 − T = 1a_B</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-ATWOOD31-V2" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="470" y="170" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">a_A = +5 m/s² (down)</text>\n    <text x="470" y="192" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">a_B = −5 m/s² (up)</text>\n    <text x="470" y="214" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">T = 15 N</text>\n    <text x="470" y="236">two relations, two separate reasons</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n<div class="g9-stage-controls"><div class="g9-stage-chips" role="group" aria-label="Stages"><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="0" data-g9-stage-desc="Show A and B on one string over the fixed pulley.">1: The machine</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="1" data-g9-stage-desc="Name the reason for each relation and write each body&#x27;s equation.">2: Two relations, two equations</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="2" data-g9-stage-desc="Solve for the accelerations and T.">3: Solve</button></div><p class="g9-stage-desc" data-g9-stage-desc-text></p><div class="g9-stage-stepper"><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="prev">Previous stage</button><span data-g9-stage-label></span><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="next">Next stage</button></div></div><figcaption data-g9-block="representation_bridge" data-g9-caption="purpose">Show the 3 kg and 1 kg Atwood machine with the length constraint and the equal-tension model kept as separate statements.</figcaption></figure></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-repair_closure" data-blueprint-slot="repair_closure" data-required="true"><section class="g9-cu-support" data-g9-support-for="CU-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-2"><h3>Read the signed result back as motion, and keep the length argument for the accelerations separate from the ideal-pulley argument for equal tension.</h3><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-trap-card" data-g9-component="TRAP_REPAIR" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_TRAP_CARD" data-g9-component-items="2" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-2"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-trap-repair"><summary>Mistake clinic · diagnose and repair</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="wrong_path"><h4>A tempting wrong path</h4><ul><li>Bodies on opposite sides of a fixed pulley have the same signed acceleration.</li><li>The two end accelerations are equal and opposite because the string tension is equal on both sides.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="diagnose"><h4>Diagnose</h4><ul><li>If both coordinates are positive away from the pulley, can both segment lengths increase while total string length stays fixed?</li><li>If equal tension were no longer guaranteed but the same fixed, taut, inextensible string still could not change length, which relation would you re-check from geometry?</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="repair"><h4>Repair</h4><ul><li>No. Their signed changes are opposite: a_A + a_B = 0 in these coordinates.</li><li>Derive the acceleration relation from the fixed-length equation. Equal tension is a separate force-model statement and is not the cause of the kinematic constraint.</li></ul></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-triad" data-g9-component="QUICK_CHECK" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="TRIAD" data-g9-component-items="2" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-2"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="independent_check"><h4>1-2-3 quick check</h4><ol class="g9-triad"><li data-g9-triad-role=""><span class="g9-triad-head">1 · Check</span><span class="g9-triad-text">Treat both masses as one system driven by the difference of their weights: the acceleration must match the one from the two body equations.</span></li><li data-g9-triad-role=""><span class="g9-triad-head">2 · Check</span><span class="g9-triad-text">Make the two masses equal: both accelerations must be zero and the tension must equal the common weight.</span></li></ol></div></div></section></section></div></div><div class="g9-split g9-split-support-only"><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-repair_closure" data-blueprint-slot="repair_closure" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-recall-card" data-g9-component="EXIT_RECALL" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="RECALL_CARD"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_task"><h4>Now you do one</h4><p>One taut inextensible string passes over one fixed ideal pulley, with y_A and y_B measured away from the pulley along the two hanging segments. Write the length relation and its acceleration consequence.</p></div><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your answer<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1A-MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-exit"><summary>Model answer</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1A-MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-exit"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_answer"><p>The variable string length is y_A + y_B = constant, so a_A + a_B = 0. The two acceleration magnitudes are equal and their signs are opposite in the declared coordinates.</p><ol><li>The fixed pulley contributes no changing geometric length in this bounded model.</li><li>Because the string is inextensible, the sum of the two straight segment lengths is constant.</li><li>Two time derivatives give the acceleration relation.</li></ol></div></template></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-chips" data-g9-component="LEARNING_TRANSITIONS" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="CHIPS"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="learning_transitions"><h4>Next steps</h4><div class="g9-transitions-row" data-g9-transitions><a class="g9-transition-chip g9-chip-practice" data-g9-transition="PRACTICE" href="core2.html">Practice this concept &rarr;</a><a class="g9-transition-chip g9-chip-qb" data-g9-transition="QUESTION_BANK" href="../../../public/question-bank/index.html?capability=MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT">All questions in Question Bank &rarr;</a></div></div></div></section></div></div></article></section><section id="g9-role-CORE1B" data-g9-role-section="CORE1B"><h1>Force and laws of motion: Reconstruction</h1><article id="g9-CORE1B--MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1B" data-g9-search-text="Physics Laws of Motion Zero net force allows steady motion Medium concept Zero net force allows steady motion" class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><h2>Zero net force allows steady motion</h2><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1B" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-NLM-FIRST-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Laws of Motion"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Laws of Motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION" data-g9-meta-value="Zero net force allows steady motion"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Zero net force allows steady motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span></div></section><div class="g9-split g9-split-primary-only"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-attempt" data-blueprint-slot="attempt" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="predict"><h4>Predict</h4><p>A car cruises at a steady speed along a straight, level road with its engine running. Must the forward driving force be larger than the backward resistance just to keep it moving?</p></div><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-REP-NLM-CAR-CRUISE-2000N" data-g9-stage="PRE_ATTEMPT" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-CAR-CRUISE-2000N" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="1" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-CRUISE-V0" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg role="img" aria-label="The car" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 840 300">\n  \n  \n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-REP-NLM-CAR-CRUISE-2000N-1--rep-nlm-cruise-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-REP-NLM-CAR-CRUISE-2000N-1--rep-nlm-cruise-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-CRUISE-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">A car cruising along a straight, level road</text>\n    <line x1="20" y1="220" x2="500" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="30" y1="220" x2="22" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="48" y1="220" x2="40" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="66" y1="220" x2="58" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="84" y1="220" x2="76" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="102" y1="220" x2="94" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="120" y1="220" x2="112" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="138" y1="220" x2="130" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="156" y1="220" x2="148" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="174" y1="220" x2="166" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="192" y1="220" x2="184" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="210" y1="220" x2="202" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="228" y1="220" x2="220" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="246" y1="220" x2="238" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="264" y1="220" x2="256" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="282" y1="220" x2="274" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="220" x2="292" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="318" y1="220" x2="310" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="336" y1="220" x2="328" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="354" y1="220" x2="346" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="372" y1="220" x2="364" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="390" y1="220" x2="382" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="408" y1="220" x2="400" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="426" y1="220" x2="418" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="444" y1="220" x2="436" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="462" y1="220" x2="454" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="480" y1="220" x2="472" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="498" y1="220" x2="490" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <rect x="180" y="158" width="140" height="44" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <path d="M205 158 L225 130 L275 130 L295 158" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="210" cy="208" r="12" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="290" cy="208" r="12" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="250.0" y="186" text-anchor="middle">1500 kg</text>\n    <line x1="190" y1="100" x2="310" y2="100" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-REP-NLM-CAR-CRUISE-2000N-1--rep-nlm-cruise-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="190" y="88">v = 25 m/s, steady, in a straight line</text>\n    <line x1="320" y1="180" x2="430" y2="180" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-REP-NLM-CAR-CRUISE-2000N-1--rep-nlm-cruise-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="330" y="168">driving force 2000 N</text>\n    <text x="330" y="200">(road on car)</text>\n    <text x="20" y="262">Air and rolling resistance act backward on the car (size to find).</text>\n    <text x="20" y="284">Find the resistive force, the net force and the velocity 10 s later.</text>\n  </g>\n  \n  \n</svg>\n<figcaption data-g9-block="stage_caption" data-g9-caption="stages">The car</figcaption></figure><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="attempt_prompt"><h4>Attempt</h4><p>A 1500 kg car cruises at a steady 25 m/s along a straight, level road. The road pushes it forward with a driving force of 2000 N. Find the total resistive force and the net force on the car, and say what its velocity will be 10 s later if nothing changes.</p><ul><li>Mass 1500 kg; speed a steady 25 m/s in a straight line.</li><li>Driving force 2000 N forward.</li><li>Resistive forces (air and rolling) act backward.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your attempt<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-reconstruction" data-blueprint-slot="reconstruction" data-required="true"><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-reconstruct"><summary>Reconstruct</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-reconstruct"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="reconstruct"><ol><li>What does &#x27;a steady 25 m/s in a straight line&#x27; tell you about the car&#x27;s acceleration?</li><li>So what must the net force on the car be?</li><li>The road pushes forward with 2000 N. What total backward force makes the net force zero?</li><li>If nothing changes for 10 s, can the velocity change? What is it then?</li></ol></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="diagnose"><h4>Diagnose</h4><ul><li>A low-friction cart is pushed and then released. After the hand loses contact, what horizontal force is required merely to keep it moving steadily?</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="repair"><h4>Repair</h4><ul><li>Separate velocity from change of velocity: a net force is required for acceleration, not for the existence of motion.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="success_criteria"><h4>What your answer should contain</h4><p>The total resistive force on the car with its direction, the net force on the car, and the car&#x27;s velocity 10 s later, each justified from the steady 25 m/s.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="model_response"><h4>What a complete answer does</h4><p>Reads &#x27;a steady 25 m/s in a straight line&#x27; as zero acceleration, so the net force is zero.; Finds the resistive total, 2000 N backward, from that balance rather than from the size of the speed.; Keeps the velocity at 25 m/s in the same direction after 10 s.</p><ul><li>The velocity is not changing, so the net force is zero and the resistive forces total 2000 N backward. With nothing changed, the car is still moving at 25 m/s in the same direction 10 s later.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="rejoin_jump"><h4>The step you rebuilt</h4><p>Zero net force does not mean zero motion; it means velocity does not change, so rest and steady motion are both compatible with the same condition.</p></div><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-full-REP-NLM-CAR-CRUISE-2000N" data-g9-stage="POST_ATTEMPT" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-CAR-CRUISE-2000N" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="3" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-CRUISE-V0 VIS-NLM-CRUISE-V1 VIS-NLM-CRUISE-V2" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 840 300" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-full-REP-NLM-CAR-CRUISE-2000N-1--rep-nlm-cruise-title g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-full-REP-NLM-CAR-CRUISE-2000N-1--rep-nlm-cruise-desc">\n  <title id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-full-REP-NLM-CAR-CRUISE-2000N-1--rep-nlm-cruise-title">A 1500 kg car cruising at a steady 25 m/s with a 2000 N driving force</title>\n  <desc id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-full-REP-NLM-CAR-CRUISE-2000N-1--rep-nlm-cruise-desc">Stage 1: a 1500 kg car moves at a steady 25 m/s in a straight line on a level road; the road pushes it forward with a 2000 N driving force, and air and rolling resistance act backward. Stage 2: the velocity is not changing, so the acceleration and the net force are zero. Stage 3: the resistive forces total 2000 N backward, the net force is zero, and 10 s later the car still moves at 25 m/s in the same direction.</desc>\n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-full-REP-NLM-CAR-CRUISE-2000N-1--rep-nlm-cruise-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-full-REP-NLM-CAR-CRUISE-2000N-1--rep-nlm-cruise-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-CRUISE-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">A car cruising along a straight, level road</text>\n    <line x1="20" y1="220" x2="500" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="30" y1="220" x2="22" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="48" y1="220" x2="40" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="66" y1="220" x2="58" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="84" y1="220" x2="76" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="102" y1="220" x2="94" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="120" y1="220" x2="112" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="138" y1="220" x2="130" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="156" y1="220" x2="148" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="174" y1="220" x2="166" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="192" y1="220" x2="184" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="210" y1="220" x2="202" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="228" y1="220" x2="220" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="246" y1="220" x2="238" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="264" y1="220" x2="256" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="282" y1="220" x2="274" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="220" x2="292" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="318" y1="220" x2="310" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="336" y1="220" x2="328" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="354" y1="220" x2="346" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="372" y1="220" x2="364" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="390" y1="220" x2="382" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="408" y1="220" x2="400" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="426" y1="220" x2="418" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="444" y1="220" x2="436" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="462" y1="220" x2="454" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="480" y1="220" x2="472" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="498" y1="220" x2="490" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <rect x="180" y="158" width="140" height="44" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <path d="M205 158 L225 130 L275 130 L295 158" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="210" cy="208" r="12" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="290" cy="208" r="12" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="250.0" y="186" text-anchor="middle">1500 kg</text>\n    <line x1="190" y1="100" x2="310" y2="100" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-full-REP-NLM-CAR-CRUISE-2000N-1--rep-nlm-cruise-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="190" y="88">v = 25 m/s, steady, in a straight line</text>\n    <line x1="320" y1="180" x2="430" y2="180" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-full-REP-NLM-CAR-CRUISE-2000N-1--rep-nlm-cruise-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="330" y="168">driving force 2000 N</text>\n    <text x="330" y="200">(road on car)</text>\n    <text x="20" y="262">Air and rolling resistance act backward on the car (size to find).</text>\n    <text x="20" y="284">Find the resistive force, the net force and the velocity 10 s later.</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-CRUISE-V1" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="540" y="70">Velocity not changing:</text>\n    <text x="540" y="92">steady speed, straight line,</text>\n    <text x="540" y="114">so a = 0 and the net force is zero</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-CRUISE-V2" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <line x1="180" y1="180" x2="70" y2="180" stroke="#1f6fb2" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-full-REP-NLM-CAR-CRUISE-2000N-1--rep-nlm-cruise-arrow-acc)"/>\n    <text x="40" y="150" fill="#1f6fb2">resistance 2000 N</text>\n    <text x="540" y="160">resistive forces total 2000 N backward</text>\n    <text x="540" y="182">net force = 2000 − 2000 = 0</text>\n    <text x="540" y="214" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">10 s later: still 25 m/s,</text>\n    <text x="540" y="236" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">in the same direction</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n<div class="g9-stage-controls"><div class="g9-stage-chips" role="group" aria-label="Stages"><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="0" data-g9-stage-desc="Show the car, its steady velocity and the forces along the road.">1: The car</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="1" data-g9-stage-desc="Read the unchanging velocity as zero acceleration.">2: Steady velocity</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="2" data-g9-stage-desc="Size the resistance and predict the velocity 10 s later.">3: Balance and prediction</button></div><p class="g9-stage-desc" data-g9-stage-desc-text></p><div class="g9-stage-stepper"><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="prev">Previous stage</button><span data-g9-stage-label></span><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="next">Next stage</button></div></div><figcaption data-g9-block="representation_bridge" data-g9-caption="purpose">Show the 1500 kg car cruising at a steady 25 m/s with its 2000 N driving force and the resistance acting backward.</figcaption></figure></template><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="boundary_test"><h4>Boundary test</h4><p>The driver eases off and the driving force drops to 500 N while the resistance is still 2000 N. Is the net force still zero, and what happens to the speed?</p></div><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-boundary"><summary>Boundary answer</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION-boundary"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="boundary_answer"><p>No. The net force is 2000 − 500 = 1500 N backward, so the car slows at 1500/1500 = 1 m/s². Zero net force described only the steady interval.</p><p>Zero net force goes with unchanged velocity, not with motion as such.</p></div></template></section></div></div></article><article id="g9-CORE1B--MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1B" data-g9-search-text="Physics Laws of Motion Nonzero forces can cancel Medium concept Nonzero forces can cancel" class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><h2>Nonzero forces can cancel</h2><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1B" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-NLM-FIRST-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Laws of Motion"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Laws of Motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO" data-g9-meta-value="Nonzero forces can cancel"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Nonzero forces can cancel</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span></div></section><div class="g9-split g9-split-primary-only"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-attempt" data-blueprint-slot="attempt" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="predict"><h4>Predict</h4><p>A string pulls up on a box that still rests on the floor. Does the floor still push up on the box with a force equal to the box&#x27;s weight?</p></div><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-REP-NLM-BOX3-STRING-10N" data-g9-stage="PRE_ATTEMPT" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-BOX3-STRING-10N" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="1" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-BOX3-V0" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg role="img" aria-label="The box" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 840 390">\n  \n  \n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-REP-NLM-BOX3-STRING-10N-1--rep-nlm-box3-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-REP-NLM-BOX3-STRING-10N-1--rep-nlm-box3-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-BOX3-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">A box on the floor with a string pulling straight up</text>\n    <text x="20" y="50">The box stays at rest on the floor; g = 10 m/s².</text>\n    <text x="20" y="72">List every force on the box, then find the net force.</text>\n    <line x1="20" y1="280" x2="440" y2="280" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="30" y1="280" x2="22" y2="288" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="48" y1="280" x2="40" y2="288" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="66" y1="280" x2="58" y2="288" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="84" y1="280" x2="76" y2="288" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="102" y1="280" x2="94" y2="288" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="120" y1="280" x2="112" y2="288" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="138" y1="280" x2="130" y2="288" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="156" y1="280" x2="148" y2="288" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="174" y1="280" x2="166" y2="288" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="192" y1="280" x2="184" y2="288" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="210" y1="280" x2="202" y2="288" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="228" y1="280" x2="220" y2="288" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="246" y1="280" x2="238" y2="288" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="264" y1="280" x2="256" y2="288" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="282" y1="280" x2="274" y2="288" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="280" x2="292" y2="288" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="318" y1="280" x2="310" y2="288" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="336" y1="280" x2="328" y2="288" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="354" y1="280" x2="346" y2="288" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="372" y1="280" x2="364" y2="288" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="390" y1="280" x2="382" y2="288" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="408" y1="280" x2="400" y2="288" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="426" y1="280" x2="418" y2="288" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <rect x="170" y="210" width="100" height="70" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="220" y="250" text-anchor="middle">3 kg box</text>\n    <line x1="230" y1="210" x2="230" y2="100" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n    <line x1="230" y1="210" x2="230" y2="180" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="3" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-REP-NLM-BOX3-STRING-10N-1--rep-nlm-box3-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="240" y="120">string pulls up with 10 N</text>\n    <text x="240" y="195">10 N</text>\n  </g>\n  \n  \n</svg>\n<figcaption data-g9-block="stage_caption" data-g9-caption="stages">The box</figcaption></figure><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="attempt_prompt"><h4>Attempt</h4><p>A 3 kg box rests on the floor while a string pulls straight up on it with 10 N; the box does not move. Take g = 10 m/s². List every force on the box with its agent, direction and size, and find the net force.</p><ul><li>Mass 3 kg; g = 10 m/s².</li><li>String pull 10 N vertically upward.</li><li>The box stays at rest on the floor.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your attempt<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-reconstruction" data-blueprint-slot="reconstruction" data-required="true"><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-reconstruct"><summary>Reconstruct</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-reconstruct"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="reconstruct"><ol><li>Which bodies touch or pull on the box? Name each agent.</li><li>Which way does each force point, and which sizes do you already know?</li><li>The box stays at rest. What must the upward forces add up to?</li><li>So how hard must the floor push? Is that the box&#x27;s weight?</li></ol></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="diagnose"><h4>Diagnose</h4><ul><li>A book rests on a table. Are there no forces on the book, or can nonzero forces add to zero?</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="repair"><h4>Repair</h4><ul><li>List every interaction force on the body, then combine their directions; cancellation belongs to the net result, not to the individual forces.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="success_criteria"><h4>What your answer should contain</h4><p>A three-force inventory for the box (Earth&#x27;s pull, the string&#x27;s pull and the floor&#x27;s push), each with its agent, direction and size, and a separate statement that the net force is zero.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="model_response"><h4>What a complete answer does</h4><p>Lists all three forces with their agents, including the floor&#x27;s push.; Finds the floor&#x27;s push, 20 N up, from zero net force instead of setting it equal to the weight.; Labels only the sum as zero; each force stays nonzero.</p><ul><li>Earth pulls it down with 30 N, the string pulls up with 10 N and the floor pushes up with 20 N. The net force is zero, although all three forces are nonzero; the floor&#x27;s push is 20 N, not 30 N.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="rejoin_jump"><h4>The step you rebuilt</h4><p>Several nonzero forces can act on one body while their directed sum is zero; the motion responds to the net result rather than to the mere presence of forces.</p></div><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-full-REP-NLM-BOX3-STRING-10N" data-g9-stage="POST_ATTEMPT" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-BOX3-STRING-10N" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="3" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-BOX3-V0 VIS-NLM-BOX3-V1 VIS-NLM-BOX3-V2" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 840 390" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-full-REP-NLM-BOX3-STRING-10N-1--rep-nlm-box3-title g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-full-REP-NLM-BOX3-STRING-10N-1--rep-nlm-box3-desc">\n  <title id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-full-REP-NLM-BOX3-STRING-10N-1--rep-nlm-box3-title">A 3 kg box at rest on the floor with a string pulling up with 10 N</title>\n  <desc id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-full-REP-NLM-BOX3-STRING-10N-1--rep-nlm-box3-desc">Stage 1: a 3 kg box rests on the floor while a string pulls straight up on it with 10 N; g = 10 m/s². Stage 2: the forces on the box are its weight 30 N down (Earth), the string\'s 10 N up and the floor\'s push N up. Stage 3: the floor pushes up with 20 N, not 30 N; the net force is 10 + 20 − 30 = 0 although all three forces are nonzero.</desc>\n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-full-REP-NLM-BOX3-STRING-10N-1--rep-nlm-box3-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-full-REP-NLM-BOX3-STRING-10N-1--rep-nlm-box3-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-BOX3-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">A box on the floor with a string pulling straight up</text>\n    <text x="20" y="50">The box stays at rest on the floor; g = 10 m/s².</text>\n    <text x="20" y="72">List every force on the box, then find the net force.</text>\n    <line x1="20" y1="280" x2="440" y2="280" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="30" y1="280" x2="22" y2="288" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="48" y1="280" x2="40" y2="288" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="66" y1="280" x2="58" y2="288" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="84" y1="280" x2="76" y2="288" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="102" y1="280" x2="94" y2="288" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="120" y1="280" x2="112" y2="288" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="138" y1="280" x2="130" y2="288" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="156" y1="280" x2="148" y2="288" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="174" y1="280" x2="166" y2="288" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="192" y1="280" x2="184" y2="288" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="210" y1="280" x2="202" y2="288" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="228" y1="280" x2="220" y2="288" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="246" y1="280" x2="238" y2="288" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="264" y1="280" x2="256" y2="288" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="282" y1="280" x2="274" y2="288" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="280" x2="292" y2="288" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="318" y1="280" x2="310" y2="288" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="336" y1="280" x2="328" y2="288" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="354" y1="280" x2="346" y2="288" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="372" y1="280" x2="364" y2="288" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="390" y1="280" x2="382" y2="288" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="408" y1="280" x2="400" y2="288" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="426" y1="280" x2="418" y2="288" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <rect x="170" y="210" width="100" height="70" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="220" y="250" text-anchor="middle">3 kg box</text>\n    <line x1="230" y1="210" x2="230" y2="100" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n    <line x1="230" y1="210" x2="230" y2="180" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="3" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-full-REP-NLM-BOX3-STRING-10N-1--rep-nlm-box3-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="240" y="120">string pulls up with 10 N</text>\n    <text x="240" y="195">10 N</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-BOX3-V1" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <line x1="200" y1="280" x2="200" y2="370" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-full-REP-NLM-BOX3-STRING-10N-1--rep-nlm-box3-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="210" y="368">weight 30 N (Earth on box)</text>\n    <line x1="185" y1="210" x2="185" y2="150" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-full-REP-NLM-BOX3-STRING-10N-1--rep-nlm-box3-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="177" y="165" text-anchor="end">floor\'s push N (up)</text>\n    <text x="500" y="110">forces on the box:</text>\n    <text x="500" y="132">Earth: 30 N down</text>\n    <text x="500" y="154">string: 10 N up</text>\n    <text x="500" y="176">floor: N up</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-BOX3-V2" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="500" y="220">floor\'s push N = 20 N up, not 30 N</text>\n    <text x="500" y="242">net force = 10 + 20 − 30 = 0</text>\n    <text x="500" y="274" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">three nonzero forces, zero net</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n<div class="g9-stage-controls"><div class="g9-stage-chips" role="group" aria-label="Stages"><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="0" data-g9-stage-desc="Show the box, the floor and the string&#x27;s 10 N pull.">1: The box</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="1" data-g9-stage-desc="List each force on the box with its agent.">2: Every force</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="2" data-g9-stage-desc="Size the floor&#x27;s push and combine the forces.">3: Net force</button></div><p class="g9-stage-desc" data-g9-stage-desc-text></p><div class="g9-stage-stepper"><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="prev">Previous stage</button><span data-g9-stage-label></span><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="next">Next stage</button></div></div><figcaption data-g9-block="representation_bridge" data-g9-caption="purpose">Show the 3 kg box at rest on the floor with a string pulling up on it with 10 N.</figcaption></figure></template><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="boundary_test"><h4>Boundary test</h4><p>The string pull is raised to 30 N, and then to 35 N. What is the floor&#x27;s push in each case, and is the net force still zero?</p></div><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-boundary"><summary>Boundary answer</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO-boundary"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="boundary_answer"><p>At 30 N the floor&#x27;s push is zero: the string alone balances the 30 N weight. At 35 N the floor cannot pull the box down, so the net force is 5 N upward and the box accelerates upward at 5/3 ≈ 1.7 m/s².</p><p>Zero net force came from a balance the floor&#x27;s push could adjust to; once that force cannot adjust, the forces no longer cancel.</p></div></template></section></div></div></article><article id="g9-CORE1B--MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1B" data-g9-search-text="Physics Laws of Motion A free-body diagram belongs to one chosen body Hard concept A free-body diagram belongs to one chosen body" class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><h2>A free-body diagram belongs to one chosen body</h2><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1B" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-NLM-FIRST-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Laws of Motion"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Laws of Motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP" data-g9-meta-value="A free-body diagram belongs to one chosen body"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>A free-body diagram belongs to one chosen body</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP" data-g9-meta-value="HARD"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Hard concept</span></span></div></section><div class="g9-split g9-split-primary-only"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-attempt" data-blueprint-slot="attempt" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="predict"><h4>Predict</h4><p>A man stands in a boat. Should the man&#x27;s weight appear on the boat&#x27;s free-body diagram?</p></div><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-REP-NLM-MAN-IN-BOAT" data-g9-stage="PRE_ATTEMPT" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-MAN-IN-BOAT" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="1" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-BOAT-V0" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg role="img" aria-label="The man and the boat" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 840 340">\n  \n  \n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-REP-NLM-MAN-IN-BOAT-1--rep-nlm-boat-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-REP-NLM-MAN-IN-BOAT-1--rep-nlm-boat-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-BOAT-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">A man standing in a boat on a lake</text>\n    <path d="M20 210 Q45 200 70 210 T120 210 T170 210 T220 210 T270 210 T320 210 T370 210 T420 210" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <path d="M110 170 L370 170 L330 225 L150 225 Z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="240" cy="99" r="9" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="240" y1="108" x2="240" y2="142" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="240" y1="142" x2="230" y2="170" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="240" y1="142" x2="250" y2="170" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="226" y1="122" x2="254" y2="122" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="265" y="110">man 70 kg</text>\n    <text x="240" y="200" text-anchor="middle">boat 130 kg</text>\n    <text x="20" y="270">Everything is at rest; g = 10 m/s².</text>\n    <text x="20" y="292">Build the free-body inventory of the boat.</text>\n  </g>\n  \n  \n</svg>\n<figcaption data-g9-block="stage_caption" data-g9-caption="stages">The man and the boat</figcaption></figure><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="attempt_prompt"><h4>Attempt</h4><p>A 70 kg man stands in a 130 kg boat floating at rest on a lake. Take g = 10 m/s². Build the free-body inventory of the boat: every force, its agent, its direction and its size.</p><ul><li>Man 70 kg; boat 130 kg; g = 10 m/s².</li><li>Everything is at rest.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your attempt<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-reconstruction" data-blueprint-slot="reconstruction" data-required="true"><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-reconstruct"><summary>Reconstruct</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-reconstruct"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="reconstruct"><ol><li>Which body is the diagram for?</li><li>Which bodies act on the boat: Earth, the man, the water? Which way does each one push or pull?</li><li>Is the man&#x27;s weight on the boat&#x27;s diagram, or the man&#x27;s push on the boat? How big is that push while he stands still?</li><li>What must the water&#x27;s push be for the boat to stay at rest?</li></ol></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="diagnose"><h4>Diagnose</h4><ul><li>A horse pulls a cart and the cart pulls back equally. Which of those two forces acts on the cart, and which acts on the horse?</li><li>If gravity is one Earth-on-block interaction, did choosing tilted axes create two new physical agents?</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="repair"><h4>Repair</h4><ul><li>Choose one body first. Put only forces acting on that body on its diagram; draw the partner force only when the other body is chosen.</li><li>No. Weight remains one physical force. Its parallel/perpendicular components are the coordinate representation used in the chosen equations; do not add the original mg again to those component sums.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="success_criteria"><h4>What your answer should contain</h4><p>One free-body inventory for the boat only: Earth&#x27;s pull, the man&#x27;s push and the water&#x27;s push, each with its agent, direction and size, with no force that acts on the man.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="model_response"><h4>What a complete answer does</h4><p>Every force on the list acts on the boat and has a named agent.; Uses the man&#x27;s push on the boat (700 N down), not the man&#x27;s weight, which acts on the man.; Finds the water&#x27;s push, 2000 N up, from zero net force on the boat.</p><ul><li>On the boat: its weight 1300 N down (Earth), the man&#x27;s push 700 N down (man) and the water&#x27;s push 2000 N up (water). The man&#x27;s weight acts on the man, not on the boat.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="rejoin_jump"><h4>The step you rebuilt</h4><p>A free-body diagram contains forces acting on one chosen body only; the equal-and-opposite partner of an interaction acts on the other body and belongs on that other body&#x27;s diagram.</p></div><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-full-REP-NLM-MAN-IN-BOAT" data-g9-stage="POST_ATTEMPT" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-MAN-IN-BOAT" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="3" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-BOAT-V0 VIS-NLM-BOAT-V1 VIS-NLM-BOAT-V2" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 840 340" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-full-REP-NLM-MAN-IN-BOAT-1--rep-nlm-boat-title g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-full-REP-NLM-MAN-IN-BOAT-1--rep-nlm-boat-desc">\n  <title id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-full-REP-NLM-MAN-IN-BOAT-1--rep-nlm-boat-title">A 70 kg man standing in a 130 kg boat at rest on a lake</title>\n  <desc id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-full-REP-NLM-MAN-IN-BOAT-1--rep-nlm-boat-desc">Stage 1: a 70 kg man stands in a 130 kg boat floating at rest on a lake; g = 10 m/s². Stage 2: the boat is chosen as the body; the agents acting on it are Earth, the man (by contact) and the water (by contact). Stage 3: the boat\'s diagram carries its weight 1300 N down (Earth), the man\'s push 700 N down (man) and the water\'s push 2000 N up (water); the man\'s own weight acts on the man, not on the boat.</desc>\n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-full-REP-NLM-MAN-IN-BOAT-1--rep-nlm-boat-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-full-REP-NLM-MAN-IN-BOAT-1--rep-nlm-boat-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-BOAT-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">A man standing in a boat on a lake</text>\n    <path d="M20 210 Q45 200 70 210 T120 210 T170 210 T220 210 T270 210 T320 210 T370 210 T420 210" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <path d="M110 170 L370 170 L330 225 L150 225 Z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="240" cy="99" r="9" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="240" y1="108" x2="240" y2="142" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="240" y1="142" x2="230" y2="170" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="240" y1="142" x2="250" y2="170" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="226" y1="122" x2="254" y2="122" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="265" y="110">man 70 kg</text>\n    <text x="240" y="200" text-anchor="middle">boat 130 kg</text>\n    <text x="20" y="270">Everything is at rest; g = 10 m/s².</text>\n    <text x="20" y="292">Build the free-body inventory of the boat.</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-BOAT-V1" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <path d="M100 162 L380 162 L336 233 L144 233 Z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="6 4"/>\n    <text x="470" y="40" font-weight="600">chosen body: the boat</text>\n    <text x="470" y="62">who acts on the boat?</text>\n    <text x="470" y="84">agents: Earth, the man (contact),</text>\n    <text x="470" y="106">the water (contact)</text>\n    <path d="M560 190 L720 190 L690 230 L590 230 Z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="640" y="216" text-anchor="middle">boat</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-BOAT-V2" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <line x1="600" y1="190" x2="600" y2="140" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-full-REP-NLM-MAN-IN-BOAT-1--rep-nlm-boat-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="592" y="160" text-anchor="end">water on boat 2000 N</text>\n    <line x1="690" y1="145" x2="690" y2="188" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-full-REP-NLM-MAN-IN-BOAT-1--rep-nlm-boat-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="700" y="160">man on boat</text>\n    <text x="700" y="180">700 N</text>\n    <line x1="640" y1="230" x2="640" y2="290" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-full-REP-NLM-MAN-IN-BOAT-1--rep-nlm-boat-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="650" y="285">Earth on boat 1300 N</text>\n    <text x="20" y="324" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">The man\'s 700 N weight acts on the man, not on the boat.</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n<div class="g9-stage-controls"><div class="g9-stage-chips" role="group" aria-label="Stages"><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="0" data-g9-stage-desc="Show the man standing in the boat at rest on the lake.">1: The man and the boat</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="1" data-g9-stage-desc="Isolate the boat and name the agents that act on it.">2: Choose the boat</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="2" data-g9-stage-desc="Draw each force on the boat with its agent and size.">3: Forces on the boat</button></div><p class="g9-stage-desc" data-g9-stage-desc-text></p><div class="g9-stage-stepper"><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="prev">Previous stage</button><span data-g9-stage-label></span><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="next">Next stage</button></div></div><figcaption data-g9-block="representation_bridge" data-g9-caption="purpose">Show the 70 kg man in the 130 kg boat at rest, with the boat chosen as the one body whose forces are listed.</figcaption></figure></template><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="boundary_test"><h4>Boundary test</h4><p>Now draw the man&#x27;s diagram. Does the boat&#x27;s upward push on him belong there? Does the water&#x27;s push?</p></div><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-boundary"><summary>Boundary answer</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-boundary"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="boundary_answer"><p>The boat&#x27;s push on the man, 700 N up, belongs there with his weight, 700 N down. The water does not touch the man, so its push does not belong there.</p><p>Each force goes on the diagram of the body it acts on; switching the body switches the inventory.</p></div></template></section></div></div></article><article id="g9-CORE1B--MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1B" data-g9-search-text="Physics Laws of Motion An accelerating observer needs a declared pseudo-force convention Hard concept An accelerating observer needs a declared pseudo-force convention" class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><h2>An accelerating observer needs a declared pseudo-force convention</h2><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1B" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-NLM-FIRST-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Laws of Motion"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Laws of Motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE" data-g9-meta-value="An accelerating observer needs a declared pseudo-force convention"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>An accelerating observer needs a declared pseudo-force convention</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE" data-g9-meta-value="HARD"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Hard concept</span></span></div></section><div class="g9-split g9-split-primary-only"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-attempt" data-blueprint-slot="attempt" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="predict"><h4>Predict</h4><p>A train brakes and a ball on its smooth floor rolls toward the front. Did something start pushing the ball forward?</p></div><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-REP-NLM-TRAIN-BALL-BRAKING" data-g9-stage="PRE_ATTEMPT" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-TRAIN-BALL-BRAKING" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="1" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-TRAINBALL-V0" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg role="img" aria-label="The braking train" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 840 320">\n  \n  \n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-REP-NLM-TRAIN-BALL-BRAKING-1--rep-nlm-trainball-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-REP-NLM-TRAIN-BALL-BRAKING-1--rep-nlm-trainball-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-TRAINBALL-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">A ball on the smooth floor of a braking train</text>\n    <line x1="20" y1="260" x2="500" y2="260" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="30" y1="260" x2="22" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="48" y1="260" x2="40" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="66" y1="260" x2="58" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="84" y1="260" x2="76" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="102" y1="260" x2="94" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="120" y1="260" x2="112" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="138" y1="260" x2="130" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="156" y1="260" x2="148" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="174" y1="260" x2="166" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="192" y1="260" x2="184" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="210" y1="260" x2="202" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="228" y1="260" x2="220" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="246" y1="260" x2="238" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="264" y1="260" x2="256" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="282" y1="260" x2="274" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="260" x2="292" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="318" y1="260" x2="310" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="336" y1="260" x2="328" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="354" y1="260" x2="346" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="372" y1="260" x2="364" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="390" y1="260" x2="382" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="408" y1="260" x2="400" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="426" y1="260" x2="418" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="444" y1="260" x2="436" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="462" y1="260" x2="454" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="480" y1="260" x2="472" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="498" y1="260" x2="490" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <rect x="60" y="120" width="400" height="110" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="110" cy="245" r="14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="410" cy="245" r="14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="260" cy="216" r="14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="200" y="190">ball 0.5 kg, smooth floor</text>\n    <line x1="80" y1="100" x2="200" y2="100" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-REP-NLM-TRAIN-BALL-BRAKING-1--rep-nlm-trainball-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="80" y="88">train\'s velocity (forward)</text>\n    <line x1="440" y1="75" x2="360" y2="75" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-REP-NLM-TRAIN-BALL-BRAKING-1--rep-nlm-trainball-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="180" y="60">acceleration 2 m/s² backward (braking)</text>\n    <text x="20" y="290">Describe the ball from the platform, then from inside the train.</text>\n  </g>\n  \n  \n</svg>\n<figcaption data-g9-block="stage_caption" data-g9-caption="stages">The braking train</figcaption></figure><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="attempt_prompt"><h4>Attempt</h4><p>A 0.5 kg ball rests on the smooth floor of a train moving along a straight track. The train brakes, slowing at 2 m/s². Describe the ball&#x27;s horizontal forces and motion as seen from the platform, then as seen from inside the train, and give the size and direction of any pseudo-force.</p><ul><li>Ball 0.5 kg on a frictionless train floor.</li><li>Train deceleration 2 m/s² (acceleration 2 m/s² backward).</li></ul></div><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your attempt<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-reconstruction" data-blueprint-slot="reconstruction" data-required="true"><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-reconstruct"><summary>Reconstruct</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-reconstruct"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="reconstruct"><ol><li>Which bodies touch the ball, and can any of them push it horizontally on a smooth floor?</li><li>Seen from the platform, what does the ball&#x27;s velocity do while the train brakes?</li><li>Which observer accelerates, and in which direction?</li><li>Seen from inside the train, which way does the ball accelerate and how fast? What pseudo-force, size and direction, accounts for that?</li></ol></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="diagnose"><h4>Diagnose</h4><ul><li>A loose mass appears to accelerate backward inside a bus that starts forward. Can the bus observer use only the same physical-force inventory as the roadside observer?</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="repair"><h4>Repair</h4><ul><li>State the observer first. Use physical interactions alone for a non-accelerating observer; for an accelerating observer include the corresponding pseudo-force consistently.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="success_criteria"><h4>What your answer should contain</h4><p>Two labelled descriptions of the ball, one from the platform and one from the braking train, each with the ball&#x27;s horizontal forces and acceleration, and the size and direction of the pseudo-force in the train description.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="model_response"><h4>What a complete answer does</h4><p>Platform: no horizontal physical force acts on the ball, so it keeps its velocity while the train slows.; Train: the ball accelerates forward at 2 m/s² relative to the train.; Accounts for that with a pseudo-force of 0.5 × 2 = 1 N pointing forward, opposite the train&#x27;s acceleration, supplied by no body.</p><ul><li>Platform: no horizontal force acts, so the ball keeps its speed while the train slows under it. Train: the ball accelerates forward at 2 m/s², explained by a pseudo-force of 0.5 × 2 = 1 N pointing forward. No physical force has been added.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="rejoin_jump"><h4>The step you rebuilt</h4><p>A force-based account depends on the observer: a non-accelerating observer uses the physical interaction forces, while an accelerating observer must add the corresponding pseudo-force convention to describe the same observed motion consistently.</p></div><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-full-REP-NLM-TRAIN-BALL-BRAKING" data-g9-stage="POST_ATTEMPT" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-TRAIN-BALL-BRAKING" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="3" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-TRAINBALL-V0 VIS-NLM-TRAINBALL-V1 VIS-NLM-TRAINBALL-V2" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 840 320" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-full-REP-NLM-TRAIN-BALL-BRAKING-1--rep-nlm-trainball-title g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-full-REP-NLM-TRAIN-BALL-BRAKING-1--rep-nlm-trainball-desc">\n  <title id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-full-REP-NLM-TRAIN-BALL-BRAKING-1--rep-nlm-trainball-title">A 0.5 kg ball on the smooth floor of a train braking at 2 m/s²</title>\n  <desc id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-full-REP-NLM-TRAIN-BALL-BRAKING-1--rep-nlm-trainball-desc">Stage 1: a 0.5 kg ball rests on the frictionless floor of a train moving forward; the train brakes, so its acceleration is 2 m/s² backward. Stage 2: from the platform no horizontal force acts on the ball, so it keeps its speed while the train slows under it. Stage 3: from inside the train the ball accelerates forward at 2 m/s², explained by a pseudo-force of 0.5 × 2 = 1 N forward; no physical force has been added.</desc>\n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-full-REP-NLM-TRAIN-BALL-BRAKING-1--rep-nlm-trainball-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-full-REP-NLM-TRAIN-BALL-BRAKING-1--rep-nlm-trainball-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-TRAINBALL-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">A ball on the smooth floor of a braking train</text>\n    <line x1="20" y1="260" x2="500" y2="260" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="30" y1="260" x2="22" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="48" y1="260" x2="40" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="66" y1="260" x2="58" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="84" y1="260" x2="76" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="102" y1="260" x2="94" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="120" y1="260" x2="112" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="138" y1="260" x2="130" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="156" y1="260" x2="148" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="174" y1="260" x2="166" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="192" y1="260" x2="184" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="210" y1="260" x2="202" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="228" y1="260" x2="220" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="246" y1="260" x2="238" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="264" y1="260" x2="256" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="282" y1="260" x2="274" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="260" x2="292" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="318" y1="260" x2="310" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="336" y1="260" x2="328" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="354" y1="260" x2="346" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="372" y1="260" x2="364" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="390" y1="260" x2="382" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="408" y1="260" x2="400" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="426" y1="260" x2="418" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="444" y1="260" x2="436" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="462" y1="260" x2="454" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="480" y1="260" x2="472" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="498" y1="260" x2="490" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <rect x="60" y="120" width="400" height="110" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="110" cy="245" r="14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="410" cy="245" r="14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="260" cy="216" r="14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="200" y="190">ball 0.5 kg, smooth floor</text>\n    <line x1="80" y1="100" x2="200" y2="100" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-full-REP-NLM-TRAIN-BALL-BRAKING-1--rep-nlm-trainball-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="80" y="88">train\'s velocity (forward)</text>\n    <line x1="440" y1="75" x2="360" y2="75" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-full-REP-NLM-TRAIN-BALL-BRAKING-1--rep-nlm-trainball-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="180" y="60">acceleration 2 m/s² backward (braking)</text>\n    <text x="20" y="290">Describe the ball from the platform, then from inside the train.</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-TRAINBALL-V1" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="530" y="60" font-weight="600">From the platform</text>\n    <text x="530" y="82">no horizontal force acts on the ball,</text>\n    <text x="530" y="104">so it keeps its speed;</text>\n    <text x="530" y="126">the train slows under it</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-TRAINBALL-V2" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <line x1="276" y1="216" x2="340" y2="216" stroke="#1f6fb2" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-dasharray="6 4" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-full-REP-NLM-TRAIN-BALL-BRAKING-1--rep-nlm-trainball-arrow-acc)"/>\n    <text x="290" y="150" fill="#1f6fb2">pseudo-force 1 N</text>\n    <text x="290" y="170" fill="#1f6fb2">(train frame only)</text>\n    <text x="530" y="170" font-weight="600">From inside the train</text>\n    <text x="530" y="192">the ball accelerates forward at 2 m/s²</text>\n    <text x="530" y="214">pseudo-force = 0.5 × 2 = 1 N,</text>\n    <text x="530" y="236">forward</text>\n    <text x="530" y="266" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">No physical force has been added.</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n<div class="g9-stage-controls"><div class="g9-stage-chips" role="group" aria-label="Stages"><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="0" data-g9-stage-desc="Show the ball, the train&#x27;s velocity and its braking acceleration.">1: The braking train</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="1" data-g9-stage-desc="Describe the ball with physical forces only.">2: Platform view</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="2" data-g9-stage-desc="Describe the ball in the train frame with its pseudo-force.">3: Train view</button></div><p class="g9-stage-desc" data-g9-stage-desc-text></p><div class="g9-stage-stepper"><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="prev">Previous stage</button><span data-g9-stage-label></span><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="next">Next stage</button></div></div><figcaption data-g9-block="representation_bridge" data-g9-caption="purpose">Show the 0.5 kg ball on the smooth floor of the train braking at 2 m/s², seen by a platform observer and by a train observer.</figcaption></figure></template><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="boundary_test"><h4>Boundary test</h4><p>The train now runs at a steady speed. Does a passenger need a pseudo-force to describe the ball?</p></div><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-boundary"><summary>Boundary answer</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRAME-CHOICE-boundary"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="boundary_answer"><p>No. The train frame is not accelerating, so the ball stays at rest relative to the floor and no pseudo-force is needed.</p><p>The trigger is observer acceleration, not observer motion in general.</p></div></template></section></div></div></article><article id="g9-CORE1B--MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1B" data-g9-search-text="Physics Laws of Motion Net force, not any one force, sets acceleration Medium concept Net force, not any one force, sets acceleration" class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><h2>Net force, not any one force, sets acceleration</h2><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1B" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-NLM-FIRST-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Laws of Motion"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Laws of Motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Net force, not any one force, sets acceleration"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Net force, not any one force, sets acceleration</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span></div></section><div class="g9-split g9-split-primary-only"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-attempt" data-blueprint-slot="attempt" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="predict"><h4>Predict</h4><p>A body moves right while its net external force points left. Which way is its acceleration?</p></div><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-REP-NLM-CAR-BRAKING-1200KG" data-g9-stage="PRE_ATTEMPT" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-CAR-BRAKING-1200KG" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="1" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-CARBRAKE-V0" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg role="img" aria-label="The braking car" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 840 300">\n  \n  \n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-REP-NLM-CAR-BRAKING-1200KG-1--rep-nlm-carbrake-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-REP-NLM-CAR-BRAKING-1200KG-1--rep-nlm-carbrake-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-CARBRAKE-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">A car braking on a level road</text>\n    <line x1="20" y1="220" x2="520" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="30" y1="220" x2="22" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="48" y1="220" x2="40" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="66" y1="220" x2="58" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="84" y1="220" x2="76" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="102" y1="220" x2="94" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="120" y1="220" x2="112" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="138" y1="220" x2="130" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="156" y1="220" x2="148" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="174" y1="220" x2="166" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="192" y1="220" x2="184" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="210" y1="220" x2="202" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="228" y1="220" x2="220" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="246" y1="220" x2="238" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="264" y1="220" x2="256" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="282" y1="220" x2="274" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="220" x2="292" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="318" y1="220" x2="310" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="336" y1="220" x2="328" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="354" y1="220" x2="346" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="372" y1="220" x2="364" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="390" y1="220" x2="382" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="408" y1="220" x2="400" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="426" y1="220" x2="418" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="444" y1="220" x2="436" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="462" y1="220" x2="454" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="480" y1="220" x2="472" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="498" y1="220" x2="490" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="516" y1="220" x2="508" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <rect x="240" y="158" width="140" height="44" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <path d="M265 158 L285 130 L335 130 L355 158" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="270" cy="208" r="12" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="350" cy="208" r="12" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="310.0" y="186" text-anchor="middle">1200 kg</text>\n    <line x1="250" y1="100" x2="380" y2="100" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-REP-NLM-CAR-BRAKING-1200KG-1--rep-nlm-carbrake-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="250" y="88">v = +20 m/s</text>\n    <line x1="240" y1="214" x2="60" y2="214" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-REP-NLM-CAR-BRAKING-1200KG-1--rep-nlm-carbrake-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="40" y="206">road friction 5400 N</text>\n    <line x1="240" y1="150" x2="220" y2="150" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-REP-NLM-CAR-BRAKING-1200KG-1--rep-nlm-carbrake-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="150" y="140">air drag 600 N</text>\n    <line x1="420" y1="60" x2="500" y2="60" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-REP-NLM-CAR-BRAKING-1200KG-1--rep-nlm-carbrake-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="420" y="50">forward = +</text>\n    <text x="20" y="262">Find the net force, the acceleration and the time to stop.</text>\n  </g>\n  \n  \n</svg>\n<figcaption data-g9-block="stage_caption" data-g9-caption="stages">The braking car</figcaption></figure><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="attempt_prompt"><h4>Attempt</h4><p>A 1200 kg car moving at 20 m/s brakes. The road&#x27;s friction on it is 5400 N backward and air drag is 600 N backward. Take forward as positive. Find the net force and the acceleration, and how long the car takes to stop if these forces stay constant.</p><ul><li>Mass 1200 kg; initial velocity +20 m/s.</li><li>Road friction 5400 N backward; air drag 600 N backward.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your attempt<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-reconstruction" data-blueprint-slot="reconstruction" data-required="true"><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-reconstruct"><summary>Reconstruct</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-reconstruct"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="reconstruct"><ol><li>Which body is the equation about, and which horizontal forces act on it?</li><li>With forward positive, what is the signed sum of the road friction and the drag?</li><li>What acceleration does that net force give for the 1200 kg car, with its sign?</li><li>The velocity is +20 m/s and the acceleration is negative. How long until the velocity reaches zero?</li></ol></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="diagnose"><h4>Diagnose</h4><ul><li>Two 10 N forces act opposite each other on a body. Does either force make the body accelerate in its direction?</li><li>Can a block remain in contact with a fixed plane while speeding up down the plane?</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="repair"><h4>Repair</h4><ul><li>Add all signed external forces first; acceleration follows the net force, not an isolated arrow.</li><li>Yes. Maintained contact sets only a_perp=0. The parallel component a_parallel is determined independently by the net force along the plane.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="success_criteria"><h4>What your answer should contain</h4><p>The signed net force on the car, its signed acceleration, and the time the car takes to stop.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="model_response"><h4>What a complete answer does</h4><p>Adds the friction and the drag with their signs before using F = ma.; Keeps the acceleration negative (backward) while the velocity is positive.; Uses the signed acceleration to find the stopping time, 20/5 = 4 s.</p><ul><li>F_net = −5400 − 600 = −6000 N, so a = −6000/1200 = −5 m/s². It stops after 20/5 = 4 s. The acceleration points backward while the velocity points forward.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="rejoin_jump"><h4>The step you rebuilt</h4><p>For a chosen body of positive mass in the Grade 9 inertial-frame model, acceleration points with the signed net external force and satisfies F_net = m a.</p></div><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-full-REP-NLM-CAR-BRAKING-1200KG" data-g9-stage="POST_ATTEMPT" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-CAR-BRAKING-1200KG" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="3" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-CARBRAKE-V0 VIS-NLM-CARBRAKE-V1 VIS-NLM-CARBRAKE-V2" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 840 300" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-full-REP-NLM-CAR-BRAKING-1200KG-1--rep-nlm-carbrake-title g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-full-REP-NLM-CAR-BRAKING-1200KG-1--rep-nlm-carbrake-desc">\n  <title id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-full-REP-NLM-CAR-BRAKING-1200KG-1--rep-nlm-carbrake-title">A 1200 kg car braking from 20 m/s under road friction and air drag</title>\n  <desc id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-full-REP-NLM-CAR-BRAKING-1200KG-1--rep-nlm-carbrake-desc">Stage 1: a 1200 kg car moves forward at +20 m/s while road friction 5400 N and air drag 600 N act backward; forward is positive. Stage 2: the net force is −5400 − 600 = −6000 N. Stage 3: the acceleration is −6000 / 1200 = −5 m/s², pointing backward while the velocity points forward, and the car stops after 20 / 5 = 4 s.</desc>\n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-full-REP-NLM-CAR-BRAKING-1200KG-1--rep-nlm-carbrake-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-full-REP-NLM-CAR-BRAKING-1200KG-1--rep-nlm-carbrake-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-CARBRAKE-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">A car braking on a level road</text>\n    <line x1="20" y1="220" x2="520" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="30" y1="220" x2="22" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="48" y1="220" x2="40" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="66" y1="220" x2="58" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="84" y1="220" x2="76" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="102" y1="220" x2="94" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="120" y1="220" x2="112" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="138" y1="220" x2="130" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="156" y1="220" x2="148" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="174" y1="220" x2="166" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="192" y1="220" x2="184" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="210" y1="220" x2="202" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="228" y1="220" x2="220" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="246" y1="220" x2="238" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="264" y1="220" x2="256" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="282" y1="220" x2="274" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="220" x2="292" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="318" y1="220" x2="310" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="336" y1="220" x2="328" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="354" y1="220" x2="346" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="372" y1="220" x2="364" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="390" y1="220" x2="382" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="408" y1="220" x2="400" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="426" y1="220" x2="418" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="444" y1="220" x2="436" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="462" y1="220" x2="454" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="480" y1="220" x2="472" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="498" y1="220" x2="490" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="516" y1="220" x2="508" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <rect x="240" y="158" width="140" height="44" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <path d="M265 158 L285 130 L335 130 L355 158" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="270" cy="208" r="12" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="350" cy="208" r="12" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="310.0" y="186" text-anchor="middle">1200 kg</text>\n    <line x1="250" y1="100" x2="380" y2="100" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-full-REP-NLM-CAR-BRAKING-1200KG-1--rep-nlm-carbrake-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="250" y="88">v = +20 m/s</text>\n    <line x1="240" y1="214" x2="60" y2="214" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-full-REP-NLM-CAR-BRAKING-1200KG-1--rep-nlm-carbrake-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="40" y="206">road friction 5400 N</text>\n    <line x1="240" y1="150" x2="220" y2="150" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-full-REP-NLM-CAR-BRAKING-1200KG-1--rep-nlm-carbrake-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="150" y="140">air drag 600 N</text>\n    <line x1="420" y1="60" x2="500" y2="60" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-full-REP-NLM-CAR-BRAKING-1200KG-1--rep-nlm-carbrake-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="420" y="50">forward = +</text>\n    <text x="20" y="262">Find the net force, the acceleration and the time to stop.</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-CARBRAKE-V1" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="560" y="80" font-weight="600">Net force</text>\n    <text x="560" y="102">F_net = −5400 − 600 = −6000 N</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-CARBRAKE-V2" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <line x1="480" y1="118" x2="410" y2="118" stroke="#1f6fb2" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-full-REP-NLM-CAR-BRAKING-1200KG-1--rep-nlm-carbrake-arrow-acc)"/>\n    <text x="420" y="110" fill="#1f6fb2">a</text>\n    <text x="560" y="150">a = −6000 / 1200 = −5 m/s²</text>\n    <text x="560" y="172">a points backward, v points forward</text>\n    <text x="560" y="204" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">stops after 20 / 5 = 4 s</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n<div class="g9-stage-controls"><div class="g9-stage-chips" role="group" aria-label="Stages"><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="0" data-g9-stage-desc="Show the car, its velocity, the axis and the two backward forces.">1: The braking car</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="1" data-g9-stage-desc="Add the signed forces.">2: Net force</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="2" data-g9-stage-desc="Divide by the mass and find the stopping time.">3: Acceleration</button></div><p class="g9-stage-desc" data-g9-stage-desc-text></p><div class="g9-stage-stepper"><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="prev">Previous stage</button><span data-g9-stage-label></span><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="next">Next stage</button></div></div><figcaption data-g9-block="representation_bridge" data-g9-caption="purpose">Show the 1200 kg car moving at +20 m/s with road friction 5400 N and air drag 600 N acting backward.</figcaption></figure></template><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="boundary_test"><h4>Boundary test</h4><p>Suppose the forces forward on the car add up to exactly 6000 N while the same 6000 N acts backward. What acceleration does F_net = ma give?</p></div><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-boundary"><summary>Boundary answer</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW-boundary"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="boundary_answer"><p>Zero. The net force is zero, so the car keeps whatever velocity it has.</p><p>The second-law relation agrees with the first-law constant-velocity case.</p></div></template></section></div></div></article><article id="g9-CORE1B--MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1B" data-g9-search-text="Physics Laws of Motion Friction opposes relative sliding at a contact Medium concept Friction opposes relative sliding at a contact" class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><h2>Friction opposes relative sliding at a contact</h2><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1B" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-NLM-FIRST-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Laws of Motion"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Laws of Motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION" data-g9-meta-value="Friction opposes relative sliding at a contact"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Friction opposes relative sliding at a contact</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span></div></section><div class="g9-split g9-split-primary-only"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-attempt" data-blueprint-slot="attempt" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="predict"><h4>Predict</h4><p>A block is pressed against a wall and does not slide. Which way does the friction on it point, and does pressing harder make that friction larger?</p></div><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-REP-NLM-BLOCK-WALL-50N" data-g9-stage="PRE_ATTEMPT" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-BLOCK-WALL-50N" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="1" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-BLOCKWALL-V0" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg role="img" aria-label="The block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 840 340">\n  \n  \n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-REP-NLM-BLOCK-WALL-50N-1--rep-nlm-blockwall-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-REP-NLM-BLOCK-WALL-50N-1--rep-nlm-blockwall-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-BLOCKWALL-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">A block pressed against a vertical wall</text>\n    <line x1="300" y1="50" x2="300" y2="320" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="60" x2="308" y2="52" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="78" x2="308" y2="70" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="96" x2="308" y2="88" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="114" x2="308" y2="106" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="132" x2="308" y2="124" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="150" x2="308" y2="142" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="168" x2="308" y2="160" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="186" x2="308" y2="178" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="204" x2="308" y2="196" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="222" x2="308" y2="214" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="240" x2="308" y2="232" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="258" x2="308" y2="250" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="276" x2="308" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="294" x2="308" y2="286" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="312" x2="308" y2="304" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <rect x="220" y="150" width="80" height="70" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="260" y="190" text-anchor="middle">2 kg</text>\n    <line x1="100" y1="205" x2="218" y2="205" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-REP-NLM-BLOCK-WALL-50N-1--rep-nlm-blockwall-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="20" y="240">push 50 N, then 80 N</text>\n    <text x="360" y="70">μ_s = 0.6 between block and wall</text>\n    <text x="360" y="92">g = 10 m/s²</text>\n    <text x="360" y="114">the block does not slide</text>\n    <text x="330" y="320">Find the friction\'s direction and size, then at 80 N.</text>\n  </g>\n  \n  \n</svg>\n<figcaption data-g9-block="stage_caption" data-g9-caption="stages">The block</figcaption></figure><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="attempt_prompt"><h4>Attempt</h4><p>A 2 kg block is pressed against a vertical wall by a 50 N horizontal push and does not slide. μ_s = 0.6 and g = 10 m/s². Give the direction and size of the friction on the block, then say what happens to the friction when the push is increased to 80 N.</p><ul><li>Block 2 kg; g = 10 m/s².</li><li>Horizontal push 50 N, then 80 N.</li><li>μ_s = 0.6 between block and wall.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your attempt<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-reconstruction" data-blueprint-slot="reconstruction" data-required="true"><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-reconstruct"><summary>Reconstruct</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-reconstruct"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="reconstruct"><ol><li>Which two surfaces are in contact, and which force presses them together?</li><li>Without friction, which way would the block slide along the wall?</li><li>Which way must friction point, and how large must it be to keep the block still?</li><li>Is that within μ_s N? When the push rises to 80 N, which of the two numbers changes?</li></ol></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="diagnose"><h4>Diagnose</h4><ul><li>A moving conveyor belt speeds up a box resting on it. Can the friction on the box point in the same direction as the box&#x27;s motion?</li><li>A crate and rough platform already move together at constant velocity. If no horizontal force is otherwise required, must the friction force be nonzero merely because the surfaces are rough?</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="repair"><h4>Repair</h4><ul><li>Decide the relative sliding tendency at the contact first; friction opposes that tendency.</li><li>No. Ask what relative sliding or slip tendency would occur without friction. If none exists and no tangential force is required, friction can be zero.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="success_criteria"><h4>What your answer should contain</h4><p>The friction&#x27;s direction from the block&#x27;s tendency to slide, its size from the vertical balance, a check against μ_s N, and what changes and what does not when the push rises to 80 N.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="model_response"><h4>What a complete answer does</h4><p>Names the slip tendency: without friction the block would slide down the wall, so friction points up.; Takes the friction&#x27;s size, 20 N, from the vertical balance and checks it against μ_s N = 30 N.; At 80 N, says the limit rises to 48 N but the friction stays 20 N.</p><ul><li>Without friction the block would slide down the wall, so friction points up. It must balance the 20 N weight, so f = 20 N (the limit is 0.6 × 50 = 30 N). At 80 N the limit rises to 48 N, but the friction stays 20 N up because the need has not changed.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="rejoin_jump"><h4>The step you rebuilt</h4><p>Friction is a contact force along the surface that opposes relative sliding, or the tendency for relative sliding, between the contacting surfaces; it is not defined by the object&#x27;s overall velocity alone.</p></div><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-full-REP-NLM-BLOCK-WALL-50N" data-g9-stage="POST_ATTEMPT" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-BLOCK-WALL-50N" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="3" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-BLOCKWALL-V0 VIS-NLM-BLOCKWALL-V1 VIS-NLM-BLOCKWALL-V2" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 840 340" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-full-REP-NLM-BLOCK-WALL-50N-1--rep-nlm-blockwall-title g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-full-REP-NLM-BLOCK-WALL-50N-1--rep-nlm-blockwall-desc">\n  <title id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-full-REP-NLM-BLOCK-WALL-50N-1--rep-nlm-blockwall-title">A 2 kg block pressed against a vertical wall by a horizontal push</title>\n  <desc id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-full-REP-NLM-BLOCK-WALL-50N-1--rep-nlm-blockwall-desc">Stage 1: a 2 kg block is pressed against a vertical wall by a 50 N horizontal push, later 80 N; μ_s = 0.6, g = 10 m/s², and the block does not slide. Stage 2: the block\'s weight is 20 N down and the wall pushes back with 50 N; without friction the block would slide down, and the static limit is 0.6 × 50 = 30 N. Stage 3: friction is 20 N up, balancing the weight; with an 80 N push the limit rises to 48 N but the friction stays 20 N up.</desc>\n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-full-REP-NLM-BLOCK-WALL-50N-1--rep-nlm-blockwall-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-full-REP-NLM-BLOCK-WALL-50N-1--rep-nlm-blockwall-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-BLOCKWALL-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">A block pressed against a vertical wall</text>\n    <line x1="300" y1="50" x2="300" y2="320" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="60" x2="308" y2="52" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="78" x2="308" y2="70" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="96" x2="308" y2="88" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="114" x2="308" y2="106" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="132" x2="308" y2="124" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="150" x2="308" y2="142" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="168" x2="308" y2="160" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="186" x2="308" y2="178" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="204" x2="308" y2="196" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="222" x2="308" y2="214" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="240" x2="308" y2="232" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="258" x2="308" y2="250" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="276" x2="308" y2="268" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="294" x2="308" y2="286" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="312" x2="308" y2="304" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <rect x="220" y="150" width="80" height="70" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="260" y="190" text-anchor="middle">2 kg</text>\n    <line x1="100" y1="205" x2="218" y2="205" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-full-REP-NLM-BLOCK-WALL-50N-1--rep-nlm-blockwall-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="20" y="240">push 50 N, then 80 N</text>\n    <text x="360" y="70">μ_s = 0.6 between block and wall</text>\n    <text x="360" y="92">g = 10 m/s²</text>\n    <text x="360" y="114">the block does not slide</text>\n    <text x="330" y="320">Find the friction\'s direction and size, then at 80 N.</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-BLOCKWALL-V1" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <line x1="250" y1="220" x2="250" y2="290" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-full-REP-NLM-BLOCK-WALL-50N-1--rep-nlm-blockwall-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="130" y="290">weight 20 N</text>\n    <line x1="220" y1="165" x2="150" y2="165" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-full-REP-NLM-BLOCK-WALL-50N-1--rep-nlm-blockwall-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="80" y="150">wall\'s push 50 N</text>\n    <text x="360" y="160">without friction the block would slide down</text>\n    <text x="360" y="182">static limit = 0.6 × 50 = 30 N</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-BLOCKWALL-V2" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <line x1="285" y1="150" x2="285" y2="90" stroke="#1f6fb2" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-full-REP-NLM-BLOCK-WALL-50N-1--rep-nlm-blockwall-arrow-acc)"/>\n    <text x="277" y="80" fill="#1f6fb2" text-anchor="end">friction 20 N up</text>\n    <text x="360" y="230">friction 20 N up balances the 20 N weight</text>\n    <text x="360" y="252">push 80 N: limit rises to 48 N,</text>\n    <text x="360" y="274" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">friction stays 20 N up</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n<div class="g9-stage-controls"><div class="g9-stage-chips" role="group" aria-label="Stages"><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="0" data-g9-stage-desc="Show the block, the wall and the horizontal push.">1: The block</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="1" data-g9-stage-desc="Draw the weight and the wall&#x27;s push and test the slip tendency.">2: Forces and slip tendency</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="2" data-g9-stage-desc="Size the friction from the need and compare with the limit.">3: Friction</button></div><p class="g9-stage-desc" data-g9-stage-desc-text></p><div class="g9-stage-stepper"><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="prev">Previous stage</button><span data-g9-stage-label></span><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="next">Next stage</button></div></div><figcaption data-g9-block="representation_bridge" data-g9-caption="purpose">Show the 2 kg block held against the vertical wall by a 50 N, then 80 N, horizontal push.</figcaption></figure></template><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="boundary_test"><h4>Boundary test</h4><p>The push is lowered to 30 N. Does the block still stay put?</p></div><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-boundary"><summary>Boundary answer</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-boundary"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="boundary_answer"><p>No. The limit becomes 0.6 × 30 = 18 N, less than the 20 N needed, so the block slides down the wall.</p><p>Friction supplies what the contact needs only up to μ_s N; beyond that the no-slip state fails.</p></div></template></section></div></div></article><article id="g9-CORE1B--MIC-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1B" data-g9-search-text="Physics Laws of Motion Third-law partner forces act on different bodies Medium concept Third-law partner forces act on different bodies" class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><h2>Third-law partner forces act on different bodies</h2><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1B" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-NLM-FIRST-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Laws of Motion"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Laws of Motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Third-law partner forces act on different bodies"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Third-law partner forces act on different bodies</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span></div></section><div class="g9-split g9-split-primary-only"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-attempt" data-blueprint-slot="attempt" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="predict"><h4>Predict</h4><p>A rocket&#x27;s engine pushes exhaust gas backward. If the gas pushes the rocket forward equally hard, why do the two forces not cancel and leave the rocket at rest?</p></div><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="MIC-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-REP-NLM-ROCKET-EXHAUST-5000N" data-g9-stage="PRE_ATTEMPT" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-ROCKET-EXHAUST-5000N" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="1" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-ROCKET-V0" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg role="img" aria-label="The rocket and gas" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 840 300">\n  \n  \n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-REP-NLM-ROCKET-EXHAUST-5000N-1--rep-nlm-rocket-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-REP-NLM-ROCKET-EXHAUST-5000N-1--rep-nlm-rocket-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-ROCKET-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">A rocket firing its engine far from any planet</text>\n    <path d="M260 130 L460 130 L520 160 L460 190 L260 190 Z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <path d="M260 145 L240 138 L240 182 L260 175" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="360" y="166" text-anchor="middle">rocket 400 kg</text>\n    <circle cx="200" cy="160" r="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2"/>\n    <circle cx="170" cy="148" r="14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2"/>\n    <circle cx="172" cy="174" r="13" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2"/>\n    <circle cx="140" cy="160" r="12" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2"/>\n    <text x="120" y="205">exhaust gas</text>\n    <line x1="210" y1="110" x2="90" y2="110" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-REP-NLM-ROCKET-EXHAUST-5000N-1--rep-nlm-rocket-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="40" y="90">engine on gas: 5000 N backward</text>\n    <text x="20" y="280">No other forces act. Name the partner force and find the rocket\'s acceleration.</text>\n  </g>\n  \n  \n</svg>\n<figcaption data-g9-block="stage_caption" data-g9-caption="stages">The rocket and gas</figcaption></figure><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="attempt_prompt"><h4>Attempt</h4><p>A 400 kg rocket far from any planet fires its engine, which pushes exhaust gas backward with a force of 5000 N. Name the partner force and the body it acts on, and find the rocket&#x27;s acceleration.</p><ul><li>Rocket mass 400 kg (take it as constant for this instant).</li><li>Engine&#x27;s push on the gas: 5000 N backward.</li><li>No other forces act.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your attempt<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-reconstruction" data-blueprint-slot="reconstruction" data-required="true"><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-reconstruct"><summary>Reconstruct</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-reconstruct"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="reconstruct"><ol><li>Which two bodies interact when the engine fires?</li><li>The engine pushes the gas backward. Which body receives that push?</li><li>What is its partner: which body pushes, which receives, in which direction and how hard?</li><li>Which of the two forces belongs in the rocket&#x27;s force sum, and what acceleration does it give?</li></ol></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="diagnose"><h4>Diagnose</h4><ul><li>When a person pushes a cart, do the person&#x27;s force on the cart and the cart&#x27;s force on the person both appear on the cart&#x27;s free-body diagram?</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="repair"><h4>Repair</h4><ul><li>No. Put each partner on the body it acts on; only then sum forces for one body.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="success_criteria"><h4>What your answer should contain</h4><p>The partner force named with its agent and target (gas on rocket), the body each of the two forces acts on, and the rocket&#x27;s acceleration from the one force acting on it.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="model_response"><h4>What a complete answer does</h4><p>Names the partner: the gas pushes the rocket forward with 5000 N.; Places the engine&#x27;s push on the gas and the gas&#x27;s push on the rocket.; Uses only the force on the rocket: a = 5000/400 = 12.5 m/s² forward.</p><ul><li>The partner is the gas pushing on the rocket, 5000 N forward. It acts on the rocket, while the engine&#x27;s push acts on the gas. The rocket&#x27;s acceleration is 5000/400 = 12.5 m/s² forward.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="rejoin_jump"><h4>The step you rebuilt</h4><p>One interaction produces two partner forces of equal magnitude and opposite direction, but they act on different bodies and therefore do not cancel in a single body&#x27;s net-force sum.</p></div><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="MIC-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-full-REP-NLM-ROCKET-EXHAUST-5000N" data-g9-stage="POST_ATTEMPT" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-ROCKET-EXHAUST-5000N" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="3" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-ROCKET-V0 VIS-NLM-ROCKET-V1 VIS-NLM-ROCKET-V2" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 840 300" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-full-REP-NLM-ROCKET-EXHAUST-5000N-1--rep-nlm-rocket-title g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-full-REP-NLM-ROCKET-EXHAUST-5000N-1--rep-nlm-rocket-desc">\n  <title id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-full-REP-NLM-ROCKET-EXHAUST-5000N-1--rep-nlm-rocket-title">A 400 kg rocket far from any planet pushing its exhaust gas backward with 5000 N</title>\n  <desc id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-full-REP-NLM-ROCKET-EXHAUST-5000N-1--rep-nlm-rocket-desc">Stage 1: a 400 kg rocket far from any planet fires its engine, which pushes the exhaust gas backward with 5000 N; no other forces act. Stage 2: the partner force is the gas pushing on the rocket, 5000 N forward; it acts on the rocket, while the engine\'s push acts on the gas. Stage 3: the rocket\'s acceleration is 5000 / 400 = 12.5 m/s² forward.</desc>\n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-full-REP-NLM-ROCKET-EXHAUST-5000N-1--rep-nlm-rocket-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-full-REP-NLM-ROCKET-EXHAUST-5000N-1--rep-nlm-rocket-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-ROCKET-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">A rocket firing its engine far from any planet</text>\n    <path d="M260 130 L460 130 L520 160 L460 190 L260 190 Z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <path d="M260 145 L240 138 L240 182 L260 175" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="360" y="166" text-anchor="middle">rocket 400 kg</text>\n    <circle cx="200" cy="160" r="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2"/>\n    <circle cx="170" cy="148" r="14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2"/>\n    <circle cx="172" cy="174" r="13" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2"/>\n    <circle cx="140" cy="160" r="12" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2"/>\n    <text x="120" y="205">exhaust gas</text>\n    <line x1="210" y1="110" x2="90" y2="110" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-full-REP-NLM-ROCKET-EXHAUST-5000N-1--rep-nlm-rocket-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="40" y="90">engine on gas: 5000 N backward</text>\n    <text x="20" y="280">No other forces act. Name the partner force and find the rocket\'s acceleration.</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-ROCKET-V1" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <line x1="300" y1="225" x2="430" y2="225" stroke="#1f6fb2" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-full-REP-NLM-ROCKET-EXHAUST-5000N-1--rep-nlm-rocket-arrow-acc)"/>\n    <text x="300" y="250" fill="#1f6fb2">gas on rocket: 5000 N forward</text>\n    <text x="560" y="60">partner pair:</text>\n    <text x="560" y="82">engine pushes gas</text>\n    <text x="560" y="104">(acts on the gas)</text>\n    <text x="560" y="126">gas pushes rocket</text>\n    <text x="560" y="148">(acts on the rocket)</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-ROCKET-V2" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="560" y="190" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">a = 5000 / 400</text>\n    <text x="560" y="212" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">= 12.5 m/s² forward</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n<div class="g9-stage-controls"><div class="g9-stage-chips" role="group" aria-label="Stages"><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="0" data-g9-stage-desc="Show the rocket, the gas and the engine&#x27;s push on the gas.">1: The rocket and gas</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="1" data-g9-stage-desc="Name the partner and the body it acts on.">2: The partner force</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="2" data-g9-stage-desc="Find the rocket&#x27;s acceleration from the force on it.">3: Acceleration</button></div><p class="g9-stage-desc" data-g9-stage-desc-text></p><div class="g9-stage-stepper"><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="prev">Previous stage</button><span data-g9-stage-label></span><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="next">Next stage</button></div></div><figcaption data-g9-block="representation_bridge" data-g9-caption="purpose">Show the 400 kg rocket far from any planet and the engine&#x27;s 5000 N push on the exhaust gas.</figcaption></figure></template><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="boundary_test"><h4>Boundary test</h4><p>Can a third-law partner force ever be the force that balances another force on the same body?</p></div><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-boundary"><summary>Boundary answer</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-THIRD-LAW-boundary"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="boundary_answer"><p>A separate force may happen to balance it, but its own third-law partner acts on the other body and is not the balancing term on the same diagram.</p><p>Equal-and-opposite third-law pairing is different from same-body force balance.</p></div></template></section></div></div></article><article id="g9-CORE1B--MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1B" data-g9-search-text="Physics Laws of Motion Quantitative static and kinetic friction conditions Hard concept Quantitative static and kinetic friction conditions" class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><h2>Quantitative static and kinetic friction conditions</h2><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1B" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-NLM-FIRST-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Laws of Motion"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Laws of Motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT" data-g9-meta-value="Quantitative static and kinetic friction conditions"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Quantitative static and kinetic friction conditions</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT" data-g9-meta-value="HARD"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Hard concept</span></span></div></section><div class="g9-split g9-split-primary-only"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-attempt" data-blueprint-slot="attempt" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="predict"><h4>Predict</h4><p>A block remains at rest while the applied tangential force is gradually increased. Before impending slip, must static friction already equal mu_s N?</p></div><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-REP-NLM-BOX5-PUSH-30N-PRESS-20N" data-g9-stage="PRE_ATTEMPT" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-BOX5-PUSH-30N-PRESS-20N" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="1" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-BOX5-V0" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg role="img" aria-label="Two trials" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 840 360">\n  \n  \n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-REP-NLM-BOX5-PUSH-30N-PRESS-20N-1--rep-nlm-box5-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-REP-NLM-BOX5-PUSH-30N-PRESS-20N-1--rep-nlm-box5-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-BOX5-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">A 5 kg box pushed with 30 N, without and with a downward press</text>\n    <text x="20" y="60" font-weight="600">Trial 1</text>\n    <line x1="20" y1="210" x2="380" y2="210" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="30" y1="210" x2="22" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="48" y1="210" x2="40" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="66" y1="210" x2="58" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="84" y1="210" x2="76" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="102" y1="210" x2="94" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="120" y1="210" x2="112" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="138" y1="210" x2="130" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="156" y1="210" x2="148" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="174" y1="210" x2="166" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="192" y1="210" x2="184" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="210" y1="210" x2="202" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="228" y1="210" x2="220" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="246" y1="210" x2="238" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="264" y1="210" x2="256" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="282" y1="210" x2="274" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="210" x2="292" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="318" y1="210" x2="310" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="336" y1="210" x2="328" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="354" y1="210" x2="346" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="372" y1="210" x2="364" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <rect x="160" y="140" width="90" height="70" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="205" y="180" text-anchor="middle">5 kg</text>\n    <line x1="50" y1="175" x2="158" y2="175" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-REP-NLM-BOX5-PUSH-30N-PRESS-20N-1--rep-nlm-box5-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="40" y="160">push 30 N</text>\n    <text x="440" y="60" font-weight="600">Trial 2</text>\n    <line x1="440" y1="210" x2="820" y2="210" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="450" y1="210" x2="442" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="468" y1="210" x2="460" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="486" y1="210" x2="478" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="504" y1="210" x2="496" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="522" y1="210" x2="514" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="540" y1="210" x2="532" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="558" y1="210" x2="550" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="576" y1="210" x2="568" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="594" y1="210" x2="586" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="612" y1="210" x2="604" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="630" y1="210" x2="622" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="648" y1="210" x2="640" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="666" y1="210" x2="658" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="684" y1="210" x2="676" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="702" y1="210" x2="694" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="720" y1="210" x2="712" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="738" y1="210" x2="730" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="756" y1="210" x2="748" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="774" y1="210" x2="766" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="792" y1="210" x2="784" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="810" y1="210" x2="802" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <rect x="580" y="140" width="90" height="70" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="625" y="180" text-anchor="middle">5 kg</text>\n    <line x1="470" y1="175" x2="578" y2="175" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-REP-NLM-BOX5-PUSH-30N-PRESS-20N-1--rep-nlm-box5-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="460" y="160">push 30 N</text>\n    <line x1="625" y1="80" x2="625" y2="138" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-REP-NLM-BOX5-PUSH-30N-PRESS-20N-1--rep-nlm-box5-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="635" y="100">press 20 N</text>\n    <text x="20" y="340">μ_s = 0.5, μ_k = 0.4, g = 10 m/s². Does the box move in each trial?</text>\n  </g>\n  \n  \n</svg>\n<figcaption data-g9-block="stage_caption" data-g9-caption="stages">Two trials</figcaption></figure><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="attempt_prompt"><h4>Attempt</h4><p>A 5 kg box rests on a floor with μ_s = 0.5 and μ_k = 0.4; take g = 10 m/s². A 30 N horizontal push is applied. Decide whether the box moves, and find the friction and the acceleration. Then repeat with a second child pressing straight down on the lid with 20 N while the same 30 N push acts.</p><ul><li>Mass 5 kg; μ_s = 0.5; μ_k = 0.4; g = 10 m/s².</li><li>Horizontal push 30 N.</li><li>Second trial: an extra 20 N downward press.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your attempt<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-reconstruction" data-blueprint-slot="reconstruction" data-required="true"><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-reconstruct"><summary>Reconstruct</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-reconstruct"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="reconstruct"><ol><li>In each trial, what normal force does the vertical balance give?</li><li>If the box did not slip, what friction would the 30 N push require?</li><li>Is 30 N within μ_s N in each trial?</li><li>In the trial where the box slides, which friction model applies, and what acceleration results?</li></ol></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="diagnose"><h4>Diagnose</h4><ul><li>A small horizontal force is applied to a block that remains at rest. Must static friction already equal mu_s N?</li><li>If an extra force presses a block into a horizontal surface, can the normal reaction still be assumed equal to mg?</li><li>A problem gives both mu_s and mu_k but does not say whether the contact slips. Can you choose kinetic friction just because mu_k is supplied?</li><li>An upward pull becomes large enough that the floor would need a downward normal force to keep touching the block. What are N and friction after separation?</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="repair"><h4>Repair</h4><ul><li>No. First solve the force required for no slip. Static friction matches that requirement up to its limit; equality occurs only at impending slip.</li><li>Determine N from the perpendicular free-body equation. N = mg is only a special case.</li><li>No. Determine the contact state first: test whether the no-slip friction demand fits within mu_s N. Use the kinetic model only after sliding is established.</li><li>A contact can push, not pull. Once the perpendicular equation would require N&lt;0, contact is lost: set N=0 and therefore contact friction is zero.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="success_criteria"><h4>What your answer should contain</h4><p>For each trial: the normal force, the static limit, a decision whether the box moves, the friction and the acceleration.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="model_response"><h4>What a complete answer does</h4><p>Find N from the FBD, solve for f_required under no slip, compare |f_required| with mu_s N, and use the kinetic model only after sliding is established.</p><ul><li>Trial 1: N = 50 N, limit 25 N &lt; 30 N, so the box slides; friction is 0.4 × 50 = 20 N and a = (30 − 20)/5 = 2 m/s². Trial 2: N = 70 N, limit 35 N ≥ 30 N, so the box stays at rest with 30 N of static friction.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="rejoin_jump"><h4>The step you rebuilt</h4><p>Static friction is a responsive contact force: solve for the friction required to prevent relative slip and compare it with the limiting value mu_s N. Equality is a threshold condition, while the kinetic model is used only after sliding is established.</p></div><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-full-REP-NLM-BOX5-PUSH-30N-PRESS-20N" data-g9-stage="POST_ATTEMPT" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-BOX5-PUSH-30N-PRESS-20N" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="3" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-BOX5-V0 VIS-NLM-BOX5-V1 VIS-NLM-BOX5-V2" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 840 360" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-full-REP-NLM-BOX5-PUSH-30N-PRESS-20N-1--rep-nlm-box5-title g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-full-REP-NLM-BOX5-PUSH-30N-PRESS-20N-1--rep-nlm-box5-desc">\n  <title id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-full-REP-NLM-BOX5-PUSH-30N-PRESS-20N-1--rep-nlm-box5-title">A 5 kg box on a rough floor pushed with 30 N, without and with a 20 N downward press</title>\n  <desc id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-full-REP-NLM-BOX5-PUSH-30N-PRESS-20N-1--rep-nlm-box5-desc">Stage 1: a 5 kg box on a floor with μ_s = 0.5 and μ_k = 0.4 (g = 10 m/s²) is pushed horizontally with 30 N; in a second trial a 20 N downward press is added. Stage 2: trial 1 has N = 50 N and a static limit of 25 N; trial 2 has N = 70 N and a limit of 35 N. Stage 3: in trial 1 the 30 N push exceeds 25 N, so the box slides with friction 0.4 × 50 = 20 N and a = (30 − 20) / 5 = 2 m/s²; in trial 2 the limit 35 N is at least 30 N, so the box stays at rest with 30 N of static friction.</desc>\n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-full-REP-NLM-BOX5-PUSH-30N-PRESS-20N-1--rep-nlm-box5-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-full-REP-NLM-BOX5-PUSH-30N-PRESS-20N-1--rep-nlm-box5-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-BOX5-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">A 5 kg box pushed with 30 N, without and with a downward press</text>\n    <text x="20" y="60" font-weight="600">Trial 1</text>\n    <line x1="20" y1="210" x2="380" y2="210" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="30" y1="210" x2="22" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="48" y1="210" x2="40" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="66" y1="210" x2="58" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="84" y1="210" x2="76" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="102" y1="210" x2="94" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="120" y1="210" x2="112" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="138" y1="210" x2="130" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="156" y1="210" x2="148" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="174" y1="210" x2="166" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="192" y1="210" x2="184" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="210" y1="210" x2="202" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="228" y1="210" x2="220" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="246" y1="210" x2="238" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="264" y1="210" x2="256" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="282" y1="210" x2="274" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="210" x2="292" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="318" y1="210" x2="310" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="336" y1="210" x2="328" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="354" y1="210" x2="346" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="372" y1="210" x2="364" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <rect x="160" y="140" width="90" height="70" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="205" y="180" text-anchor="middle">5 kg</text>\n    <line x1="50" y1="175" x2="158" y2="175" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-full-REP-NLM-BOX5-PUSH-30N-PRESS-20N-1--rep-nlm-box5-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="40" y="160">push 30 N</text>\n    <text x="440" y="60" font-weight="600">Trial 2</text>\n    <line x1="440" y1="210" x2="820" y2="210" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="450" y1="210" x2="442" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="468" y1="210" x2="460" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="486" y1="210" x2="478" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="504" y1="210" x2="496" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="522" y1="210" x2="514" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="540" y1="210" x2="532" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="558" y1="210" x2="550" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="576" y1="210" x2="568" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="594" y1="210" x2="586" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="612" y1="210" x2="604" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="630" y1="210" x2="622" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="648" y1="210" x2="640" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="666" y1="210" x2="658" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="684" y1="210" x2="676" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="702" y1="210" x2="694" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="720" y1="210" x2="712" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="738" y1="210" x2="730" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="756" y1="210" x2="748" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="774" y1="210" x2="766" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="792" y1="210" x2="784" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="810" y1="210" x2="802" y2="218" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <rect x="580" y="140" width="90" height="70" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="625" y="180" text-anchor="middle">5 kg</text>\n    <line x1="470" y1="175" x2="578" y2="175" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-full-REP-NLM-BOX5-PUSH-30N-PRESS-20N-1--rep-nlm-box5-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="460" y="160">push 30 N</text>\n    <line x1="625" y1="80" x2="625" y2="138" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-full-REP-NLM-BOX5-PUSH-30N-PRESS-20N-1--rep-nlm-box5-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="635" y="100">press 20 N</text>\n    <text x="20" y="340">μ_s = 0.5, μ_k = 0.4, g = 10 m/s². Does the box move in each trial?</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-BOX5-V1" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="260">N = 50 N; limit 0.5 × 50 = 25 N</text>\n    <text x="440" y="260">N = 70 N; limit 0.5 × 70 = 35 N</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-BOX5-V2" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <line x1="220" y1="226" x2="160" y2="226" stroke="#1f6fb2" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-full-REP-NLM-BOX5-PUSH-30N-PRESS-20N-1--rep-nlm-box5-arrow-acc)"/>\n    <line x1="640" y1="226" x2="580" y2="226" stroke="#1f6fb2" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-full-REP-NLM-BOX5-PUSH-30N-PRESS-20N-1--rep-nlm-box5-arrow-acc)"/>\n    <text x="20" y="284" fill="#1f6fb2">30 N &gt; 25 N: slides; friction 0.4 × 50 = 20 N;</text>\n    <text x="20" y="306" fill="#1f6fb2">a = (30 − 20) / 5 = 2 m/s²</text>\n    <text x="440" y="284" fill="#1f6fb2">30 N ≤ 35 N: stays at rest;</text>\n    <text x="440" y="306" fill="#1f6fb2">static friction 30 N</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n<div class="g9-stage-controls"><div class="g9-stage-chips" role="group" aria-label="Stages"><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="0" data-g9-stage-desc="Show both trials with their pushes and the friction coefficients.">1: Two trials</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="1" data-g9-stage-desc="Find each trial&#x27;s normal force and static limit.">2: Normal force and limit</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="2" data-g9-stage-desc="Compare the need with the limit and choose the friction model.">3: Decision</button></div><p class="g9-stage-desc" data-g9-stage-desc-text></p><div class="g9-stage-stepper"><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="prev">Previous stage</button><span data-g9-stage-label></span><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="next">Next stage</button></div></div><figcaption data-g9-block="representation_bridge" data-g9-caption="purpose">Show the 5 kg box pushed with 30 N in two trials, the second with an extra 20 N downward press.</figcaption></figure></template><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="boundary_test"><h4>Boundary test</h4><p>If the no-slip equation requires zero friction while mu_s N is positive, what is the static-friction magnitude?</p></div><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-boundary"><summary>Boundary answer</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT-boundary"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="boundary_answer"><p>Zero. Static friction need not sit at its maximum.</p><p>The static relation is an inequality/response rule, not an unconditional equality.</p></div></template></section></div></div></article><article id="g9-CORE1B--MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1B" data-g9-search-text="Physics Laws of Motion Connected bodies and a justified common acceleration Hard concept Connected bodies and a justified common acceleration" class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><h2>Connected bodies and a justified common acceleration</h2><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1B" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-NLM-FIRST-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Laws of Motion"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Laws of Motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL" data-g9-meta-value="Connected bodies and a justified common acceleration"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Connected bodies and a justified common acceleration</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL" data-g9-meta-value="HARD"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Hard concept</span></span></div></section><div class="g9-split g9-split-primary-only"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-attempt" data-blueprint-slot="attempt" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="predict"><h4>Predict</h4><p>Two blocks move together without separating. Can you assign different accelerations along their common line while their separation stays fixed?</p></div><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-REP-NLM-CAR-TRAILER-TOWBAR" data-g9-stage="PRE_ATTEMPT" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-CAR-TRAILER-TOWBAR" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="1" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-TOWBAR-V0" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg role="img" aria-label="Car and trailer" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 840 440">\n  \n  \n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-REP-NLM-CAR-TRAILER-TOWBAR-1--rep-nlm-towbar-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-REP-NLM-CAR-TRAILER-TOWBAR-1--rep-nlm-towbar-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-TOWBAR-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">A car towing a trailer with a rigid bar on a level road</text>\n    <line x1="20" y1="220" x2="700" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="30" y1="220" x2="22" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="48" y1="220" x2="40" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="66" y1="220" x2="58" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="84" y1="220" x2="76" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="102" y1="220" x2="94" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="120" y1="220" x2="112" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="138" y1="220" x2="130" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="156" y1="220" x2="148" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="174" y1="220" x2="166" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="192" y1="220" x2="184" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="210" y1="220" x2="202" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="228" y1="220" x2="220" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="246" y1="220" x2="238" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="264" y1="220" x2="256" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="282" y1="220" x2="274" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="220" x2="292" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="318" y1="220" x2="310" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="336" y1="220" x2="328" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="354" y1="220" x2="346" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="372" y1="220" x2="364" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="390" y1="220" x2="382" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="408" y1="220" x2="400" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="426" y1="220" x2="418" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="444" y1="220" x2="436" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="462" y1="220" x2="454" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="480" y1="220" x2="472" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="498" y1="220" x2="490" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="516" y1="220" x2="508" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="534" y1="220" x2="526" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="552" y1="220" x2="544" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="570" y1="220" x2="562" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="588" y1="220" x2="580" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="606" y1="220" x2="598" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="624" y1="220" x2="616" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="642" y1="220" x2="634" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="660" y1="220" x2="652" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="678" y1="220" x2="670" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="696" y1="220" x2="688" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <rect x="80" y="150" width="150" height="58" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="155" cy="208" r="12" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="155" y="184" text-anchor="middle">trailer 500 kg</text>\n    <line x1="230" y1="190" x2="290" y2="190" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="4"/>\n    <text x="225" y="240">rigid tow bar</text>\n    <rect x="290" y="158" width="180" height="44" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <path d="M315 158 L335 130 L425 130 L445 158" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="320" cy="208" r="12" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="440" cy="208" r="12" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="380.0" y="186" text-anchor="middle">car 1000 kg</text>\n    <line x1="480" y1="180" x2="590" y2="180" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-REP-NLM-CAR-TRAILER-TOWBAR-1--rep-nlm-towbar-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="490" y="168">driving force 3000 N</text>\n    <text x="20" y="270">Ignore all resistance.</text>\n  </g>\n  \n  \n</svg>\n<figcaption data-g9-block="stage_caption" data-g9-caption="stages">Car and trailer</figcaption></figure><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="attempt_prompt"><h4>Attempt</h4><p>A 1000 kg car tows a 500 kg trailer with a light rigid tow bar on a level road. The road pushes the car&#x27;s drive wheels forward with 3000 N; ignore all resistance. State the constraint, write one Newton-II equation for the car and one for the trailer, and find the acceleration and the tow-bar force.</p><ul><li>Car 1000 kg; trailer 500 kg.</li><li>Driving force on the car 3000 N forward.</li><li>Rigid bar: the separation stays fixed.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your attempt<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-reconstruction" data-blueprint-slot="reconstruction" data-required="true"><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-reconstruct"><summary>Reconstruct</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-reconstruct"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="reconstruct"><ol><li>What does the rigid tow bar keep fixed between the car and the trailer?</li><li>With forward positive, what must be true of their two accelerations?</li><li>Which forces go in the car&#x27;s equation, and which in the trailer&#x27;s? Where does the bar force appear?</li><li>After adding the two equations, which pair cancels, and what are a and the bar force?</li></ol></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="diagnose"><h4>Diagnose</h4><ul><li>If two blocks are touching but one loses contact, what keeps their accelerations equal after separation?</li><li>On body A alone, is the force from body B external to A?</li><li>Two connected bodies of different masses share acceleration a. Must their individual net forces be equal?</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="repair"><h4>Repair</h4><ul><li>Name the active constraint first. Equal acceleration is justified only while the relative-separation condition remains enforced.</li><li>Yes. Keep it in A&#x27;s equation. It cancels only after A and B are deliberately combined into one system equation.</li><li>No. The constraint can make their accelerations equal while Newton II gives different net forces m_A a and m_B a when the masses differ.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="success_criteria"><h4>What your answer should contain</h4><p>The tow-bar constraint, one signed Newton-II equation for the car and one for the trailer, the common acceleration and the tow-bar force.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="model_response"><h4>What a complete answer does</h4><p>State the fixed-separation constraint first, use one sign convention, set the constrained acceleration components equal, and cancel internal partner forces only after combining both body equations.</p><ul><li>The fixed bar gives one acceleration. Car: 3000 − T = 1000a. Trailer: T = 500a. Adding gives a = 2 m/s², so T = 1000 N; check with the car: 3000 − 1000 = 2000 = 1000 × 2.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="rejoin_jump"><h4>The step you rebuilt</h4><p>Bodies share an acceleration component only when a physical constraint keeps their relative separation fixed along that coordinate; the shared acceleration can link separate Newton-II equations, while internal partner forces cancel only if the bodies are deliberately treated as one system.</p></div><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-full-REP-NLM-CAR-TRAILER-TOWBAR" data-g9-stage="POST_ATTEMPT" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-CAR-TRAILER-TOWBAR" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="3" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-TOWBAR-V0 VIS-NLM-TOWBAR-V1 VIS-NLM-TOWBAR-V2" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 840 440" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-full-REP-NLM-CAR-TRAILER-TOWBAR-1--rep-nlm-towbar-title g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-full-REP-NLM-CAR-TRAILER-TOWBAR-1--rep-nlm-towbar-desc">\n  <title id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-full-REP-NLM-CAR-TRAILER-TOWBAR-1--rep-nlm-towbar-title">A 1000 kg car towing a 500 kg trailer with a rigid bar and a 3000 N driving force</title>\n  <desc id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-full-REP-NLM-CAR-TRAILER-TOWBAR-1--rep-nlm-towbar-desc">Stage 1: a 1000 kg car tows a 500 kg trailer with a light rigid tow bar on a level road; the road pushes the car\'s drive wheels forward with 3000 N and resistance is ignored. Stage 2: the bar keeps the separation fixed, so both share one acceleration a; the bar pulls the trailer forward with T and the car backward with T, giving 3000 − T = 1000 a for the car and T = 500 a for the trailer. Stage 3: a = 2 m/s² and T = 1000 N; check with the car: 3000 − 1000 = 2000 = 1000 × 2.</desc>\n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-full-REP-NLM-CAR-TRAILER-TOWBAR-1--rep-nlm-towbar-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-full-REP-NLM-CAR-TRAILER-TOWBAR-1--rep-nlm-towbar-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-TOWBAR-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">A car towing a trailer with a rigid bar on a level road</text>\n    <line x1="20" y1="220" x2="700" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="30" y1="220" x2="22" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="48" y1="220" x2="40" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="66" y1="220" x2="58" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="84" y1="220" x2="76" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="102" y1="220" x2="94" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="120" y1="220" x2="112" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="138" y1="220" x2="130" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="156" y1="220" x2="148" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="174" y1="220" x2="166" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="192" y1="220" x2="184" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="210" y1="220" x2="202" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="228" y1="220" x2="220" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="246" y1="220" x2="238" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="264" y1="220" x2="256" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="282" y1="220" x2="274" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="220" x2="292" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="318" y1="220" x2="310" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="336" y1="220" x2="328" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="354" y1="220" x2="346" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="372" y1="220" x2="364" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="390" y1="220" x2="382" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="408" y1="220" x2="400" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="426" y1="220" x2="418" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="444" y1="220" x2="436" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="462" y1="220" x2="454" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="480" y1="220" x2="472" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="498" y1="220" x2="490" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="516" y1="220" x2="508" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="534" y1="220" x2="526" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="552" y1="220" x2="544" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="570" y1="220" x2="562" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="588" y1="220" x2="580" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="606" y1="220" x2="598" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="624" y1="220" x2="616" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="642" y1="220" x2="634" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="660" y1="220" x2="652" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="678" y1="220" x2="670" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="696" y1="220" x2="688" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <rect x="80" y="150" width="150" height="58" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="155" cy="208" r="12" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="155" y="184" text-anchor="middle">trailer 500 kg</text>\n    <line x1="230" y1="190" x2="290" y2="190" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="4"/>\n    <text x="225" y="240">rigid tow bar</text>\n    <rect x="290" y="158" width="180" height="44" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <path d="M315 158 L335 130 L425 130 L445 158" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="320" cy="208" r="12" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="440" cy="208" r="12" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="380.0" y="186" text-anchor="middle">car 1000 kg</text>\n    <line x1="480" y1="180" x2="590" y2="180" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-full-REP-NLM-CAR-TRAILER-TOWBAR-1--rep-nlm-towbar-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="490" y="168">driving force 3000 N</text>\n    <text x="20" y="270">Ignore all resistance.</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-TOWBAR-V1" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="300" font-weight="600">One acceleration a for both (the bar keeps the separation fixed).</text>\n    <rect x="80" y="320" width="110" height="40" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="135" y="345" text-anchor="middle">trailer</text>\n    <line x1="190" y1="340" x2="250" y2="340" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-full-REP-NLM-CAR-TRAILER-TOWBAR-1--rep-nlm-towbar-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="200" y="330">T</text>\n    <rect x="380" y="320" width="130" height="40" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="445" y="345" text-anchor="middle">car</text>\n    <line x1="380" y1="340" x2="320" y2="340" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-full-REP-NLM-CAR-TRAILER-TOWBAR-1--rep-nlm-towbar-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="330" y="330">T</text>\n    <line x1="510" y1="340" x2="610" y2="340" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-full-REP-NLM-CAR-TRAILER-TOWBAR-1--rep-nlm-towbar-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="520" y="330">3000 N</text>\n    <text x="20" y="392">Car: 3000 − T = 1000 a</text>\n    <text x="300" y="392">Trailer: T = 500 a</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-TOWBAR-V2" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="420" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">a = 2 m/s², T = 1000 N; check: 3000 − 1000 = 2000 = 1000 × 2</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n<div class="g9-stage-controls"><div class="g9-stage-chips" role="group" aria-label="Stages"><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="0" data-g9-stage-desc="Show the two bodies, the rigid bar and the driving force.">1: Car and trailer</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="1" data-g9-stage-desc="State the shared acceleration and write one equation per body.">2: Constraint and equations</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="2" data-g9-stage-desc="Solve for a and T and check with the car.">3: Solve and check</button></div><p class="g9-stage-desc" data-g9-stage-desc-text></p><div class="g9-stage-stepper"><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="prev">Previous stage</button><span data-g9-stage-label></span><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="next">Next stage</button></div></div><figcaption data-g9-block="representation_bridge" data-g9-caption="purpose">Show the 1000 kg car towing the 500 kg trailer with a rigid bar under a 3000 N driving force.</figcaption></figure></template><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="boundary_test"><h4>Boundary test</h4><p>The tow bar snaps. May the car and the trailer still be given one acceleration?</p></div><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-boundary"><summary>Boundary answer</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-CONNECTED-COMMON-ACCEL-boundary"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="boundary_answer"><p>No. With the bar gone, each body has its own force sum: the car accelerates at 3000/1000 = 3 m/s², and the trailer, with no forces along the road, keeps its velocity.</p><p>A common acceleration holds only while the constraint is active.</p></div></template></section></div></div></article><article id="g9-CORE1B--MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1B" data-g9-search-text="Physics Laws of Motion Ideal-string tension belongs to each body-specific FBD Hard concept Ideal-string tension belongs to each body-specific FBD" class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><h2>Ideal-string tension belongs to each body-specific FBD</h2><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1B" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-NLM-FIRST-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Laws of Motion"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Laws of Motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION" data-g9-meta-value="Ideal-string tension belongs to each body-specific FBD"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Ideal-string tension belongs to each body-specific FBD</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION" data-g9-meta-value="HARD"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Hard concept</span></span></div></section><div class="g9-split g9-split-primary-only"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-attempt" data-blueprint-slot="attempt" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="predict"><h4>Predict</h4><p>A hand pulls a cart with a string, and a second string ties it to a cart behind. Must both strings carry the same tension?</p></div><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-REP-NLM-CARTS-2KG-3KG-15N" data-g9-stage="PRE_ATTEMPT" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-CARTS-2KG-3KG-15N" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="1" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-CARTS15-V0" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg role="img" aria-label="The carts" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 840 440">\n  \n  \n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-REP-NLM-CARTS-2KG-3KG-15N-1--rep-nlm-carts15-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-REP-NLM-CARTS-2KG-3KG-15N-1--rep-nlm-carts15-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-CARTS15-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">Two carts on a smooth track joined by light strings</text>\n    <line x1="20" y1="220" x2="700" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="30" y1="220" x2="22" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="48" y1="220" x2="40" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="66" y1="220" x2="58" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="84" y1="220" x2="76" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="102" y1="220" x2="94" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="120" y1="220" x2="112" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="138" y1="220" x2="130" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="156" y1="220" x2="148" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="174" y1="220" x2="166" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="192" y1="220" x2="184" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="210" y1="220" x2="202" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="228" y1="220" x2="220" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="246" y1="220" x2="238" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="264" y1="220" x2="256" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="282" y1="220" x2="274" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="220" x2="292" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="318" y1="220" x2="310" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="336" y1="220" x2="328" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="354" y1="220" x2="346" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="372" y1="220" x2="364" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="390" y1="220" x2="382" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="408" y1="220" x2="400" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="426" y1="220" x2="418" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="444" y1="220" x2="436" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="462" y1="220" x2="454" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="480" y1="220" x2="472" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="498" y1="220" x2="490" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="516" y1="220" x2="508" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="534" y1="220" x2="526" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="552" y1="220" x2="544" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="570" y1="220" x2="562" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="588" y1="220" x2="580" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="606" y1="220" x2="598" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="624" y1="220" x2="616" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="642" y1="220" x2="634" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="660" y1="220" x2="652" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="678" y1="220" x2="670" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="696" y1="220" x2="688" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <rect x="80" y="160" width="120" height="48" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="110" cy="208" r="10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="170" cy="208" r="10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="140" y="190" text-anchor="middle">rear 3 kg</text>\n    <line x1="200" y1="184" x2="290" y2="184" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n    <text x="205" y="170">light string</text>\n    <rect x="290" y="160" width="120" height="48" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="320" cy="208" r="10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="380" cy="208" r="10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="350" y="190" text-anchor="middle">front 2 kg</text>\n    <line x1="410" y1="184" x2="510" y2="184" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n    <line x1="510" y1="184" x2="600" y2="184" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-REP-NLM-CARTS-2KG-3KG-15N-1--rep-nlm-carts15-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="500" y="170">hand pulls with 15 N</text>\n    <text x="20" y="270">Smooth track; both strings light and taut.</text>\n  </g>\n  \n  \n</svg>\n<figcaption data-g9-block="stage_caption" data-g9-caption="stages">The carts</figcaption></figure><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="attempt_prompt"><h4>Attempt</h4><p>On a smooth track a hand pulls a light string attached to a 2 kg cart with 15 N. A second light string ties that cart to a 3 kg cart behind it. State the model, find the acceleration and the tension in each string, and say which body each tension force acts on.</p><ul><li>Front cart 2 kg; rear cart 3 kg; smooth track.</li><li>Hand&#x27;s pull 15 N on the front string.</li><li>Both strings light and taut.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your attempt<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-reconstruction" data-blueprint-slot="reconstruction" data-required="true"><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-reconstruct"><summary>Reconstruct</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-reconstruct"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="reconstruct"><ol><li>What does &#x27;light and taut&#x27; let you say about the tension along one string?</li><li>Are the two strings one string? May one T be used for both?</li><li>Which body does the rear string pull on at each end, and what does the rear cart&#x27;s equation give for its tension?</li><li>Which forces act on the front cart, and does 15 − 9 equal its mass times a?</li></ol></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="diagnose"><h4>Diagnose</h4><ul><li>Do the two end tensions act on the same chosen body?</li><li>What model assumptions justify using one symbol T throughout the rope?</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="repair"><h4>Repair</h4><ul><li>No. Put the tension force acting on each body in that body&#x27;s own FBD. Equal magnitudes across the ideal string do not make them a cancelling pair on one body.</li><li>State the ideal massless string and frictionless/massless redirection assumptions first. Without them, equal tension is not automatic.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="success_criteria"><h4>What your answer should contain</h4><p>The model (light, taut strings on a smooth track), the common acceleration, the tension in each string, and the bodies each string&#x27;s pull acts on.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="model_response"><h4>What a complete answer does</h4><p>Declare each string light and taut, so each has one tension along its own length. Find a from the whole train, find each string&#x27;s tension from what that string alone pulls, and put each tension arrow on the body at each end of its string.</p><ul><li>Each light string has one tension along its length. a = 15/5 = 3 m/s². The front string&#x27;s tension is 15 N and acts on the front cart. The rear string&#x27;s tension is 3 × 3 = 9 N and acts backward on the front cart and forward on the rear cart. The two strings have different tensions.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="rejoin_jump"><h4>The step you rebuilt</h4><p>For one taut massless ideal string, with any redirection treated as frictionless and massless, the tension magnitude can be represented by one T throughout that same string; each body still receives its own tension force along the local string direction.</p></div><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-full-REP-NLM-CARTS-2KG-3KG-15N" data-g9-stage="POST_ATTEMPT" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-CARTS-2KG-3KG-15N" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="3" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-CARTS15-V0 VIS-NLM-CARTS15-V1 VIS-NLM-CARTS15-V2" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 840 440" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-full-REP-NLM-CARTS-2KG-3KG-15N-1--rep-nlm-carts15-title g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-full-REP-NLM-CARTS-2KG-3KG-15N-1--rep-nlm-carts15-desc">\n  <title id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-full-REP-NLM-CARTS-2KG-3KG-15N-1--rep-nlm-carts15-title">A hand pulling a 2 kg cart with 15 N, with a 3 kg cart tied behind it</title>\n  <desc id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-full-REP-NLM-CARTS-2KG-3KG-15N-1--rep-nlm-carts15-desc">Stage 1: on a smooth track a hand pulls a light string attached to a 2 kg front cart with 15 N; a second light string ties that cart to a 3 kg rear cart. Stage 2: each light string has one tension along its length: T_1 in the front string pulls the front cart forward, T_2 in the rear string pulls the front cart backward and the rear cart forward. Stage 3: a = 15 / 5 = 3 m/s², T_1 = 15 N and T_2 = 3 × 3 = 9 N, so the two strings have different tensions.</desc>\n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-full-REP-NLM-CARTS-2KG-3KG-15N-1--rep-nlm-carts15-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-full-REP-NLM-CARTS-2KG-3KG-15N-1--rep-nlm-carts15-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-CARTS15-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">Two carts on a smooth track joined by light strings</text>\n    <line x1="20" y1="220" x2="700" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="30" y1="220" x2="22" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="48" y1="220" x2="40" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="66" y1="220" x2="58" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="84" y1="220" x2="76" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="102" y1="220" x2="94" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="120" y1="220" x2="112" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="138" y1="220" x2="130" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="156" y1="220" x2="148" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="174" y1="220" x2="166" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="192" y1="220" x2="184" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="210" y1="220" x2="202" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="228" y1="220" x2="220" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="246" y1="220" x2="238" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="264" y1="220" x2="256" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="282" y1="220" x2="274" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="220" x2="292" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="318" y1="220" x2="310" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="336" y1="220" x2="328" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="354" y1="220" x2="346" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="372" y1="220" x2="364" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="390" y1="220" x2="382" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="408" y1="220" x2="400" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="426" y1="220" x2="418" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="444" y1="220" x2="436" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="462" y1="220" x2="454" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="480" y1="220" x2="472" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="498" y1="220" x2="490" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="516" y1="220" x2="508" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="534" y1="220" x2="526" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="552" y1="220" x2="544" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="570" y1="220" x2="562" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="588" y1="220" x2="580" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="606" y1="220" x2="598" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="624" y1="220" x2="616" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="642" y1="220" x2="634" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="660" y1="220" x2="652" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="678" y1="220" x2="670" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="696" y1="220" x2="688" y2="228" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <rect x="80" y="160" width="120" height="48" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="110" cy="208" r="10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="170" cy="208" r="10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="140" y="190" text-anchor="middle">rear 3 kg</text>\n    <line x1="200" y1="184" x2="290" y2="184" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n    <text x="205" y="170">light string</text>\n    <rect x="290" y="160" width="120" height="48" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="320" cy="208" r="10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="380" cy="208" r="10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="350" y="190" text-anchor="middle">front 2 kg</text>\n    <line x1="410" y1="184" x2="510" y2="184" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n    <line x1="510" y1="184" x2="600" y2="184" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-full-REP-NLM-CARTS-2KG-3KG-15N-1--rep-nlm-carts15-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="500" y="170">hand pulls with 15 N</text>\n    <text x="20" y="270">Smooth track; both strings light and taut.</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-CARTS15-V1" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="300" font-weight="600">Each light string has one tension along its length.</text>\n    <rect x="80" y="320" width="110" height="40" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="135" y="345" text-anchor="middle">rear</text>\n    <line x1="190" y1="340" x2="250" y2="340" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-full-REP-NLM-CARTS-2KG-3KG-15N-1--rep-nlm-carts15-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="200" y="330">T_2</text>\n    <rect x="390" y="320" width="110" height="40" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="445" y="345" text-anchor="middle">front</text>\n    <line x1="390" y1="340" x2="330" y2="340" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-full-REP-NLM-CARTS-2KG-3KG-15N-1--rep-nlm-carts15-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="340" y="330">T_2</text>\n    <line x1="500" y1="340" x2="580" y2="340" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-full-REP-NLM-CARTS-2KG-3KG-15N-1--rep-nlm-carts15-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="510" y="330">T_1</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-CARTS15-V2" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="392" fill="#1f6fb2">a = 15 / 5 = 3 m/s²; T_1 = 15 N (on the front cart)</text>\n    <text x="20" y="416" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">T_2 = 3 × 3 = 9 N: backward on the front cart, forward on the rear cart</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n<div class="g9-stage-controls"><div class="g9-stage-chips" role="group" aria-label="Stages"><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="0" data-g9-stage-desc="Show the two carts, the two strings and the hand&#x27;s pull.">1: The carts</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="1" data-g9-stage-desc="Put each string&#x27;s tension on the bodies it acts on.">2: Tensions</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="2" data-g9-stage-desc="Find the acceleration and each tension.">3: Values</button></div><p class="g9-stage-desc" data-g9-stage-desc-text></p><div class="g9-stage-stepper"><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="prev">Previous stage</button><span data-g9-stage-label></span><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="next">Next stage</button></div></div><figcaption data-g9-block="representation_bridge" data-g9-caption="purpose">Show the 2 kg and 3 kg carts on the smooth track, joined by one light string and pulled by the hand&#x27;s 15 N on another.</figcaption></figure></template><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="boundary_test"><h4>Boundary test</h4><p>The rear string is replaced by a 1 kg rope. Is the tension still the same all along it?</p></div><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-boundary"><summary>Boundary answer</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION-boundary"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="boundary_answer"><p>No. The rope has mass, so the light-string model no longer holds and equal tension is no longer guaranteed: with a = 15/6 = 2.5 m/s², the rope pulls the rear cart with 3 × 2.5 = 7.5 N but is pulled at its front end with 4 × 2.5 = 10 N.</p><p>One tension along a string is a result of the light-string model, not a universal rule.</p></div></template></section></div></div></article><article id="g9-CORE1B--MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1B" data-g9-search-text="Physics Laws of Motion One fixed-pulley string gives one length constraint Hard concept One fixed-pulley string gives one length constraint" class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><h2>One fixed-pulley string gives one length constraint</h2><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1B" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-NLM-FIRST-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Laws of Motion"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Laws of Motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT" data-g9-meta-value="One fixed-pulley string gives one length constraint"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>One fixed-pulley string gives one length constraint</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT" data-g9-meta-value="HARD"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Hard concept</span></span></div></section><div class="g9-split g9-split-primary-only"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-attempt" data-blueprint-slot="attempt" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="predict"><h4>Predict</h4><p>If mass A moves 1 cm farther from a fixed pulley on one side of a taut inextensible string, what must happen to the straight segment on the other side?</p></div><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-REP-NLM-PULLEY-YA06-YB10" data-g9-stage="PRE_ATTEMPT" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-PULLEY-YA06-YB10" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="1" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-PULLEY16-V0" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg role="img" aria-label="The string and coordinates" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 840 380">\n  \n  \n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-REP-NLM-PULLEY-YA06-YB10-1--rep-nlm-pulley16-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-REP-NLM-PULLEY-YA06-YB10-1--rep-nlm-pulley16-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-PULLEY16-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">One taut string over a fixed pulley</text>\n    <line x1="200" y1="40" x2="400" y2="40" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="210" y1="40" x2="202" y2="32" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="228" y1="40" x2="220" y2="32" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="246" y1="40" x2="238" y2="32" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="264" y1="40" x2="256" y2="32" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="282" y1="40" x2="274" y2="32" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="40" x2="292" y2="32" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="318" y1="40" x2="310" y2="32" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="336" y1="40" x2="328" y2="32" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="354" y1="40" x2="346" y2="32" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="372" y1="40" x2="364" y2="32" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="390" y1="40" x2="382" y2="32" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="40" x2="300" y2="80" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="300" cy="80" r="30" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="270" y1="80" x2="270" y2="200" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n    <line x1="330" y1="80" x2="330" y2="280" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n    <rect x="250" y="200" width="40" height="40" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="270" y="225" text-anchor="middle">A</text>\n    <rect x="310" y="280" width="40" height="40" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="330" y="305" text-anchor="middle">B</text>\n    <line x1="200" y1="80" x2="240" y2="80" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="5 4"/>\n    <line x1="220" y1="80" x2="220" y2="198" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-REP-NLM-PULLEY-YA06-YB10-1--rep-nlm-pulley16-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="212" y="145" text-anchor="end">y_A = 0.6 m</text>\n    <line x1="360" y1="80" x2="400" y2="80" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="5 4"/>\n    <line x1="380" y1="80" x2="380" y2="278" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-REP-NLM-PULLEY-YA06-YB10-1--rep-nlm-pulley16-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="390" y="185">y_B = 1.0 m</text>\n    <text x="510" y="60">y measured downward from the pulley</text>\n    <text x="510" y="82">(down positive)</text>\n    <text x="510" y="104">A rises by 0.1 m: find y_B</text>\n    <text x="510" y="126">instant: v_A = −0.2 m/s, a_A = +1.5 m/s²</text>\n  </g>\n  \n  \n</svg>\n<figcaption data-g9-block="stage_caption" data-g9-caption="stages">The string and coordinates</figcaption></figure><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="attempt_prompt"><h4>Attempt</h4><p>A taut inextensible string over a fixed pulley holds masses A and B, with y_A and y_B measured downward from the pulley. Now y_A = 0.6 m and y_B = 1.0 m. Find y_B after A has risen by 0.1 m. At an instant when A moves up at 0.2 m/s with an acceleration of 1.5 m/s² downward, find B&#x27;s velocity and acceleration.</p><ul><li>y_A = 0.6 m and y_B = 1.0 m, both measured downward from the pulley.</li><li>A rises by 0.1 m.</li><li>Instant: v_A = −0.2 m/s, a_A = +1.5 m/s² (down positive).</li></ul></div><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your attempt<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-reconstruction" data-blueprint-slot="reconstruction" data-required="true"><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-reconstruct"><summary>Reconstruct</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-reconstruct"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="reconstruct"><ol><li>Which two straight parts of the string change length, and what is their sum now?</li><li>A rises by 0.1 m. Which way does y_A change, and what must y_B be?</li><li>What does the fixed sum give for v_B and a_B, in the same down-positive coordinates?</li><li>Translate the signs back: which way is B moving, and which way is its acceleration?</li></ol></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="diagnose"><h4>Diagnose</h4><ul><li>What part of the string length changes in this exact geometry, and is any pulley itself moving?</li><li>If both coordinates are positive away from the pulley, can both segment lengths increase while total string length stays fixed?</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="repair"><h4>Repair</h4><ul><li>Write the string-length equation from the actual geometry. The equal-magnitude result here is only for one fixed pulley with two changing straight segments.</li><li>No. Their signed changes are opposite: a_A + a_B = 0 in these coordinates.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="success_criteria"><h4>What your answer should contain</h4><p>The fixed-length equation with its value, y_B after A rises 0.1 m, and B&#x27;s signed velocity and acceleration at the instant.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="model_response"><h4>What a complete answer does</h4><p>Choose both coordinates away from the pulley, write y_A + y_B = constant, then infer v_A + v_B = 0 and a_A + a_B = 0.</p><ul><li>y_A + y_B = 1.6 m, so y_B = 1.6 − 0.5 = 1.1 m. v_B = −v_A = +0.2 m/s (moving down), and a_B = −a_A = −1.5 m/s² (1.5 m/s² upward).</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="rejoin_jump"><h4>The step you rebuilt</h4><p>For one taut inextensible string over one fixed ideal pulley, the two changing straight segment lengths add to a constant; with coordinates measured away from the pulley this gives y_A + y_B = constant and therefore a_A + a_B = 0.</p></div><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-full-REP-NLM-PULLEY-YA06-YB10" data-g9-stage="POST_ATTEMPT" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-PULLEY-YA06-YB10" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="3" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-PULLEY16-V0 VIS-NLM-PULLEY16-V1 VIS-NLM-PULLEY16-V2" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 840 380" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-full-REP-NLM-PULLEY-YA06-YB10-1--rep-nlm-pulley16-title g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-full-REP-NLM-PULLEY-YA06-YB10-1--rep-nlm-pulley16-desc">\n  <title id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-full-REP-NLM-PULLEY-YA06-YB10-1--rep-nlm-pulley16-title">Masses A and B on one string over a fixed pulley with y_A = 0.6 m and y_B = 1.0 m</title>\n  <desc id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-full-REP-NLM-PULLEY-YA06-YB10-1--rep-nlm-pulley16-desc">Stage 1: a taut inextensible string over a fixed pulley holds A and B, with y_A = 0.6 m and y_B = 1.0 m measured downward from the pulley; A then rises by 0.1 m, and at one instant v_A = −0.2 m/s and a_A = +1.5 m/s² (down positive). Stage 2: the length gives y_A + y_B = 1.6 m, a constant, so v_A + v_B = 0 and a_A + a_B = 0. Stage 3: after A rises, y_A = 0.5 m and y_B = 1.6 − 0.5 = 1.1 m (dashed positions); v_B = +0.2 m/s (moving down) and a_B = −1.5 m/s² (upward).</desc>\n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-full-REP-NLM-PULLEY-YA06-YB10-1--rep-nlm-pulley16-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-full-REP-NLM-PULLEY-YA06-YB10-1--rep-nlm-pulley16-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-PULLEY16-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">One taut string over a fixed pulley</text>\n    <line x1="200" y1="40" x2="400" y2="40" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="210" y1="40" x2="202" y2="32" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="228" y1="40" x2="220" y2="32" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="246" y1="40" x2="238" y2="32" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="264" y1="40" x2="256" y2="32" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="282" y1="40" x2="274" y2="32" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="40" x2="292" y2="32" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="318" y1="40" x2="310" y2="32" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="336" y1="40" x2="328" y2="32" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="354" y1="40" x2="346" y2="32" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="372" y1="40" x2="364" y2="32" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="390" y1="40" x2="382" y2="32" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="40" x2="300" y2="80" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="300" cy="80" r="30" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="270" y1="80" x2="270" y2="200" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n    <line x1="330" y1="80" x2="330" y2="280" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n    <rect x="250" y="200" width="40" height="40" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="270" y="225" text-anchor="middle">A</text>\n    <rect x="310" y="280" width="40" height="40" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="330" y="305" text-anchor="middle">B</text>\n    <line x1="200" y1="80" x2="240" y2="80" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="5 4"/>\n    <line x1="220" y1="80" x2="220" y2="198" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-full-REP-NLM-PULLEY-YA06-YB10-1--rep-nlm-pulley16-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="212" y="145" text-anchor="end">y_A = 0.6 m</text>\n    <line x1="360" y1="80" x2="400" y2="80" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="5 4"/>\n    <line x1="380" y1="80" x2="380" y2="278" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-full-REP-NLM-PULLEY-YA06-YB10-1--rep-nlm-pulley16-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="390" y="185">y_B = 1.0 m</text>\n    <text x="510" y="60">y measured downward from the pulley</text>\n    <text x="510" y="82">(down positive)</text>\n    <text x="510" y="104">A rises by 0.1 m: find y_B</text>\n    <text x="510" y="126">instant: v_A = −0.2 m/s, a_A = +1.5 m/s²</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-PULLEY16-V1" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="510" y="170" font-weight="600">y_A + y_B = 1.6 m, constant</text>\n    <text x="510" y="192">so v_A + v_B = 0 and a_A + a_B = 0</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-PULLEY16-V2" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <rect x="250" y="180" width="40" height="40" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="6 4"/>\n    <rect x="310" y="300" width="40" height="40" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="6 4"/>\n    <text x="510" y="240" fill="#1f6fb2">y_A = 0.5 m, so y_B = 1.6 − 0.5 = 1.1 m</text>\n    <text x="510" y="262" fill="#1f6fb2">v_B = +0.2 m/s (moving down)</text>\n    <text x="510" y="284" fill="#1f6fb2">a_B = −1.5 m/s² (1.5 m/s² upward)</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n<div class="g9-stage-controls"><div class="g9-stage-chips" role="group" aria-label="Stages"><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="0" data-g9-stage-desc="Show the pulley, the two masses and both coordinates.">1: The string and coordinates</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="1" data-g9-stage-desc="Write the fixed-length relation and its rates.">2: Length constraint</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="2" data-g9-stage-desc="Find y_B after the move, then v_B and a_B.">3: New position and rates</button></div><p class="g9-stage-desc" data-g9-stage-desc-text></p><div class="g9-stage-stepper"><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="prev">Previous stage</button><span data-g9-stage-label></span><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="next">Next stage</button></div></div><figcaption data-g9-block="representation_bridge" data-g9-caption="purpose">Show A and B on one string over a fixed pulley with y_A = 0.6 m and y_B = 1.0 m measured downward from the pulley.</figcaption></figure></template><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="boundary_test"><h4>Boundary test</h4><p>A pulley itself is allowed to move. Can you keep using y_A + y_B = constant without re-deriving the geometry?</p></div><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-boundary"><summary>Boundary answer</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-NLM-SINGLE-STRING-CONSTRAINT-boundary"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="boundary_answer"><p>No. A movable pulley changes which string segments vary, so the length equation must be rebuilt.</p><p>This capability is intentionally bounded to one fixed pulley.</p></div></template></section></div></div></article></section><section id="g9-role-CORE2" data-g9-role-section="CORE2"><h1>Force and laws of motion: Source questions</h1><article id="g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q03" data-g9-unit="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q03" data-g9-kind="QUESTION" data-g9-role="CORE2" data-g9-search-text="Physics Laws of Motion Net force, not any one force, sets acceleration Medium concept Multi-step reasoning Newton-law and observer-frame practice Single-correct multiple choice IIT-JEE 2007 · Paper I · Q3 Verified past-exam adaptation Two equal masses m lie on a smooth horizontal surface and are joined by a light string of total length 2a. The midpoint of the initially taut string is pulled vertically upward by a small constant force F. When the masses are separated by 2x, determine the magnitude of the acceleration of either mass. Horizontal surface is frictionless. String is light and remains taut. Each half-string has length a. (A) (F/2m)·a/sqrt(a^2-x^2) (B) (F/2m)·x/sqrt(a^2-x^2) (C) (F/2m)·x/a (D) (F/2m)·sqrt(a^2-x^2)/x" class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-header" data-g9-component="IDENTITY" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="HEADER"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="source_identity"><h2>IIT-JEE · 2007 · Paper I · Q3</h2><p class="g9-prov">Official past paper, faithful restatement of the original</p></div><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE2" data-g9-meta-record="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q03" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-NLM-FIRST-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Laws of Motion"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Laws of Motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Net force, not any one force, sets acceleration"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Net force, not any one force, sets acceleration</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="question-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q03" data-g9-meta-value="D3"><strong>Question difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Multi-step reasoning</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="family" data-g9-meta-ref="FAM-PHY-NLM-PRACTICE" data-g9-meta-value="Newton-law and observer-frame practice"><strong>Family:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Newton-law and observer-frame practice</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="question-type" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q03" data-g9-meta-value="single_correct_mcq"><strong>Question type:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Single-correct multiple choice</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="source" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q03" data-g9-meta-value="IIT-JEE 2007 · Paper I · Q3"><strong>Source:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>IIT-JEE 2007 · Paper I · Q3</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="provenance" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q03" data-g9-meta-value="PYQ_ADAPTED"><strong>Provenance:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Verified past-exam adaptation</span></span></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-link-list" data-g9-component="SOURCE_PDF" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="LINK_LIST"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="source_pdf"><a class="g9-pdf-link g9-source-pdf" data-g9-source-pdf href="https://jeeadv.ac.in/past_qps/2007_1.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" type="application/pdf" aria-label="Open the original past paper as a PDF to print it"><svg class="g9-pdf-icon" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="24" height="24"><path d="M6 2.5h8l4.5 4.5v14.5H6z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linejoin="round"/><path d="M14 2.5V7h4.5M9 12h6.5M9 15.5h6.5M9 19h4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg><span>Source paper (PDF)</span></a></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-grid" data-g9-component="DIFFICULTY_WHY" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_GRID"><button type="button" class="g9-why-toggle" data-g9-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="g9-why-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q03"><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-band" data-g9-band="D3">D3 · 6/10 · about 3 min</span><span>Why this difficulty?</span></button><div id="g9-why-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q03" class="g9-why" hidden><p>Changing string geometry must be translated into tension components before Newton II is applied.</p><div class=g9-dgrid><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Choosing the model</span><b>2/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Representation</span><b>2/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Reasoning chain</span><b>1/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Algebra and numbers</span><b>1/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Traps and exceptions</span><b>0/2</b></div></div></div></div></section><div class="g9-split"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-attempt" data-blueprint-slot="attempt" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-stem" data-g9-component="STEM" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEM"><div class="g9-eyebrow">Attempt first</div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="stem"><p>Two equal masses m lie on a smooth horizontal surface and are joined by a light string of total length 2a. The midpoint of the initially taut string is pulled vertically upward by a small constant force F. When the masses are separated by 2x, determine the magnitude of the acceleration of either mass.</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-callout-info" data-g9-component="CONDITIONS" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CALLOUT_INFO"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="conditions"><h4>Conditions</h4><ul><li>Horizontal surface is frictionless.</li><li>String is light and remains taut.</li><li>Each half-string has length a.</li></ul></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-callout-warn" data-g9-component="TRAP" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CALLOUT_WARN"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core2-wrong-route"><summary>Common wrong route · open if you want a warning</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="common_wrong_route"><h4>Common wrong route</h4><p>Taking T=F/2 without resolving the angled string segments.</p></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-attempt" data-g9-component="ATTEMPT" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="ATTEMPT"><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your answer<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div></div></section></div><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-representation" data-blueprint-slot="representation" data-required="false"></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-support" data-blueprint-slot="support" data-required="false"><div class="g9-component g9-c-ladder" data-g9-component="HINT_LADDER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="LADDER" data-g9-component-items="3"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core2-hints"><summary>Need a hint? · guided support</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-eyebrow">Hint ladder · reveal only what you need</div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="authored_core2_support"><h4>Guided support</h4><div class="g9-ladder" data-g9-ladder-ref="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q03-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-group="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL"><ol data-g9-ladder></ol><ol class="g9-rung-map" aria-label="Rungs still to come"><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H0</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Represent</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H1</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Connect</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H2</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Carry out</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li></ol><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q03-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-1"><li data-g9-rung="1" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[0]" data-g9-support-reveals="CONCEPT" data-g9-support-kind="REPRESENT" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q03-MOVE-1"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H0</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Represent</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><div data-g9-support-reveal><p>Sketch one half-string as a right triangle with horizontal projection x and vertical rise y.</p></div></li></template><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q03-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-2"><li data-g9-rung="2" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[1]" data-g9-support-reveals="METHOD" data-g9-support-kind="CONNECT" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q03-MOVE-2"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H1</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Connect</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><div data-g9-support-reveal><p>At the pulled midpoint, which components of the two equal tensions balance F?</p></div></li></template><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q03-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-3"><li data-g9-rung="3" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[2]" data-g9-support-reveals="METHOD" data-g9-support-kind="EXECUTE" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q03-MOVE-3"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H2</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Carry out</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><div data-g9-support-reveal><p>After finding T, resolve only its horizontal component on either mass.</p></div></li></template><button type="button" data-g9-next-rung>Show guided support</button></div></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-link-list" data-g9-component="CONCEPT_NAV" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="LINK_LIST"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="concept_navigation"><h4>Need the concept again?</h4><ul><li><a data-g9-concept-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q03" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW" href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW">Net force, not any one force, sets acceleration</a></li><li><a data-g9-concept-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q03" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION" href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-IDEAL-STRING-TENSION">Ideal-string tension belongs to each body-specific FBD</a></li></ul></div></div></section></div></div><section class="blueprint-slot slot-solution" data-blueprint-slot="solution" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure" data-g9-component="SOLUTION" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE"><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q03-solution"><summary>Answer and working</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q03-solution"><div class="g9-component g9-c-step-list" data-g9-component="SOLUTION_STEPS" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEP_LIST" data-g9-component-items="3"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="structured_working"><h4>Reasoning route</h4><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="1" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q03-MOVE-1" data-g9-solution-kind="REPRESENT" data-g9-solution-stage="REPRESENT" data-g9-solution-crux="false"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">1</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Represent</h4><p data-g9-solution-action>Let the midpoint rise by y. String geometry gives x^2 + y^2 = a^2, so y = sqrt(a^2-x^2).</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>stated source quantities</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> This follows from the stated physical or chemical model and the quantities preserved in the source-matched task.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> intermediate result 1</p></div></section><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="2" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q03-MOVE-2" data-g9-solution-kind="CONNECT" data-g9-solution-stage="CONNECT" data-g9-solution-crux="true"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">2</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Connect</h4><p class="g9-prov" data-g9-crux-label>Key move</p><p data-g9-solution-action>At the massless midpoint, vertical force balance gives 2T(y/a)=F, hence T=Fa/(2y).</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>stated source quantities</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> This follows from the stated physical or chemical model and the quantities preserved in the source-matched task.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> intermediate result 2</p></div></section><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="3" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q03-MOVE-3" data-g9-solution-kind="TRANSFORM" data-g9-solution-stage="CALCULATE" data-g9-solution-crux="false"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">3</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Calculate</h4><p data-g9-solution-action>The horizontal force on either mass is T(x/a), so its acceleration is F x/[2m sqrt(a^2-x^2)].</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>stated source quantities</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> This follows from the stated physical or chemical model and the quantities preserved in the source-matched task.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> Option (B): a_mass = F x / [2m sqrt(a^2-x^2)].</p></div></section></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-answer-box" data-g9-component="ANSWER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="ANSWER_BOX"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="answer"><p>Option (B): <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{mass}</annotation></semantics></math></span> = F x / [2m <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\\sqrt{a^{2}-x^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></span>].</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-check-box" data-g9-component="CHECK" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CHECK_BOX"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="independent_check"><h4>Independent check</h4><p>As x approaches zero, the horizontal acceleration approaches zero because the string segments become nearly vertical.</p></div></div></template></div></section></article><article id="g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q10" data-g9-unit="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q10" data-g9-kind="QUESTION" data-g9-role="CORE2" data-g9-search-text="Physics Laws of Motion Quantitative static and kinetic friction conditions Hard concept Connected reasoning Newton-law and observer-frame practice Assertion–reason multiple choice IIT-JEE 2007 · Paper I · Q10 Verified past-exam adaptation A block moving on a rough horizontal surface stops after losing its kinetic energy to friction. The same surface is then inclined at 30 degrees and the block is projected up the incline with the same initial speed. Judge these claims: (1) the energy dissipated by friction before stopping is smaller on the incline; (2) the coefficient of friction becomes smaller when the plane is inclined. Same pair of surfaces is used. Initial speed is the same in both trials. Incline angle is 30 degrees. (A) Both true and (2) explains (1) (B) Both true but (2) does not explain (1) (C) (1) true, (2) false (D) (1) false, (2) true" class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-header" data-g9-component="IDENTITY" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="HEADER"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="source_identity"><h2>IIT-JEE · 2007 · Paper I · Q10</h2><p class="g9-prov">Official past paper, faithful restatement of the original</p></div><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE2" data-g9-meta-record="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q10" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-NLM-FIRST-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Laws of Motion"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Laws of Motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT" data-g9-meta-value="Quantitative static and kinetic friction conditions"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Quantitative static and kinetic friction conditions</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT" data-g9-meta-value="HARD"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Hard concept</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="question-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q10" data-g9-meta-value="D2"><strong>Question difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Connected reasoning</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="family" data-g9-meta-ref="FAM-PHY-NLM-PRACTICE" data-g9-meta-value="Newton-law and observer-frame practice"><strong>Family:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Newton-law and observer-frame practice</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="question-type" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q10" data-g9-meta-value="assertion_reason_mcq"><strong>Question type:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Assertion–reason multiple choice</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="source" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q10" data-g9-meta-value="IIT-JEE 2007 · Paper I · Q10"><strong>Source:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>IIT-JEE 2007 · Paper I · Q10</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="provenance" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q10" data-g9-meta-value="PYQ_ADAPTED"><strong>Provenance:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Verified past-exam adaptation</span></span></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-link-list" data-g9-component="SOURCE_PDF" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="LINK_LIST"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="source_pdf"><a class="g9-pdf-link g9-source-pdf" data-g9-source-pdf href="https://jeeadv.ac.in/past_qps/2007_1.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" type="application/pdf" aria-label="Open the original past paper as a PDF to print it"><svg class="g9-pdf-icon" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="24" height="24"><path d="M6 2.5h8l4.5 4.5v14.5H6z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linejoin="round"/><path d="M14 2.5V7h4.5M9 12h6.5M9 15.5h6.5M9 19h4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg><span>Source paper (PDF)</span></a></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-grid" data-g9-component="DIFFICULTY_WHY" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_GRID"><button type="button" class="g9-why-toggle" data-g9-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="g9-why-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q10"><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-band" data-g9-band="D2">D2 · 4/10 · about 2 min</span><span>Why this difficulty?</span></button><div id="g9-why-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q10" class="g9-why" hidden><p>The task separates friction coefficient from friction work and uses an energy comparison.</p><div class=g9-dgrid><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Choosing the model</span><b>1/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Representation</span><b>1/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Reasoning chain</span><b>1/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Algebra and numbers</span><b>0/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Traps and exceptions</span><b>1/2</b></div></div></div></div></section><div class="g9-split"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-attempt" data-blueprint-slot="attempt" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-stem" data-g9-component="STEM" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEM"><div class="g9-eyebrow">Attempt first</div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="stem"><p>A block moving on a rough horizontal surface stops after losing its kinetic energy to friction. The same surface is then inclined at 30 degrees and the block is projected up the incline with the same initial speed. Judge these claims: (1) the energy dissipated by friction before stopping is smaller on the incline; (2) the coefficient of friction becomes smaller when the plane is inclined.</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-callout-info" data-g9-component="CONDITIONS" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CALLOUT_INFO"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="conditions"><h4>Conditions</h4><ul><li>Same pair of surfaces is used.</li><li>Initial speed is the same in both trials.</li><li>Incline angle is 30 degrees.</li></ul></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-callout-warn" data-g9-component="TRAP" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CALLOUT_WARN"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core2-wrong-route"><summary>Common wrong route · open if you want a warning</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="common_wrong_route"><h4>Common wrong route</h4><p>Assuming a smaller friction force means the coefficient itself has changed.</p></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-attempt" data-g9-component="ATTEMPT" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="ATTEMPT"><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your answer<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div></div></section></div><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-representation" data-blueprint-slot="representation" data-required="false"><div class="g9-component g9-c-visual-card" data-g9-component="REPRESENTATION" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="VISUAL_CARD" data-g9-component-items="1"><div class="g9-card-head"><strong>Representation</strong></div><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q10-REP-PYQ-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q10-FRICTION" data-g9-stage="PRE_ATTEMPT" data-g9-representation="REP-PYQ-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q10-FRICTION" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="1" data-g9-stages-total="1" data-g9-stages=""><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 440 250" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q10-REP-PYQ-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q10-FRICTION-1--j07q10-title g9fig-CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q10-REP-PYQ-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q10-FRICTION-1--j07q10-desc">\n<title id="g9fig-CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q10-REP-PYQ-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q10-FRICTION-1--j07q10-title">Same block stopping on a level surface and up a 30 degree incline</title><desc id="g9fig-CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q10-REP-PYQ-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q10-FRICTION-1--j07q10-desc">The same block starts with the same speed in two trials. It stops on a rough level surface or after moving up the same rough surface inclined at 30 degrees. No result is shown.</desc>\n<defs><marker id="g9fig-CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q10-REP-PYQ-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q10-FRICTION-1--j07q10-a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker></defs>\n<g font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="14" fill="currentColor" stroke="currentColor"><text x="16" y="24" stroke="none" font-weight="700">same block · same initial speed</text><text x="38" y="52" stroke="none" >level trial</text><line x1="20" y1="150" x2="196" y2="150" stroke-width="2" /><rect x="70" y="108" width="58" height="42" fill="none" stroke-width="2" /><line x1="128" y1="128" x2="178" y2="128" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q10-REP-PYQ-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q10-FRICTION-1--j07q10-a)"/><text x="145" y="116" stroke="none" >v₀</text><text x="250" y="52" stroke="none" >30° incline trial</text><line x1="228" y1="174" x2="414" y2="68" stroke-width="2" /><rect x="300" y="104" width="58" height="42" fill="none" stroke-width="2" transform="rotate(-30 329 125)"/><line x1="290" y1="120" x2="346" y2="88" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q10-REP-PYQ-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q10-FRICTION-1--j07q10-a)"/><text x="349" y="83" stroke="none" >v₀</text><text x="232" y="188" stroke="none" >30°</text><text x="16" y="222" stroke="none">rough contact in both trials</text><text x="16" y="242" stroke="none">block eventually stops</text></g></svg>\n<figcaption data-g9-block="stage_caption" data-g9-caption="stages"></figcaption></figure></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-support" data-blueprint-slot="support" data-required="false"><div class="g9-component g9-c-ladder" data-g9-component="HINT_LADDER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="LADDER" data-g9-component-items="3"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core2-hints"><summary>Need a hint? · guided support</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-eyebrow">Hint ladder · reveal only what you need</div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="authored_core2_support"><h4>Guided support</h4><div class="g9-ladder" data-g9-ladder-ref="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q10-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-group="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL"><ol data-g9-ladder></ol><ol class="g9-rung-map" aria-label="Rungs still to come"><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H0</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Representation</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H1</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Key concept</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H2</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Crux</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li></ol><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q10-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-1"><li data-g9-rung="1" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[0]" data-g9-support-reveals="CONCEPT" data-g9-support-stage="REPRESENTATION" data-g9-support-kind="REPRESENT" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q10-MOVE-1"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H0</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Representation</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><p data-g9-support-prompt>What energy is present initially in both trials?</p><details data-g9-support-reveal><summary>Reveal support</summary><p>Sketch the horizontal and uphill trials with the same starting speed and a final stop.</p></details></li></template><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q10-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-2"><li data-g9-rung="2" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[1]" data-g9-support-reveals="CONCEPT" data-g9-support-stage="KEY_CONCEPT" data-g9-support-kind="CONNECT" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q10-MOVE-2"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H1</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Key concept</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><p data-g9-support-prompt>Where must the initial kinetic energy go by the stopping point?</p><details data-g9-support-reveal><summary>Reveal support</summary><p>In the level trial there is no height gain.</p></details></li></template><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q10-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-3"><li data-g9-rung="3" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[2]" data-g9-support-reveals="METHOD" data-g9-support-stage="CRUX" data-g9-support-kind="CONNECT" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q10-MOVE-3"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H2</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Crux</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><p data-g9-support-prompt>Which claim changes a force magnitude, and which claim incorrectly changes a surface property?</p><details data-g9-support-reveal><summary>Reveal support</summary><p>In the incline trial, reserve part of the initial kinetic energy for gravitational potential energy; do not change μ just because N changes.</p></details></li></template><button type="button" data-g9-next-rung>Show guided support</button></div></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-link-list" data-g9-component="CONCEPT_NAV" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="LINK_LIST"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="concept_navigation"><h4>Need the concept again?</h4><ul><li><a data-g9-concept-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q10" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT" href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT">Quantitative static and kinetic friction conditions</a></li><li><a data-g9-concept-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q10" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW" href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW">Net force, not any one force, sets acceleration</a></li></ul></div></div></section></div></div><section class="blueprint-slot slot-solution" data-blueprint-slot="solution" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure" data-g9-component="SOLUTION" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE"><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q10-solution"><summary>Answer and working</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q10-solution"><div class="g9-component g9-c-step-list" data-g9-component="SOLUTION_STEPS" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEP_LIST" data-g9-component-items="3"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="structured_working"><h4>Reasoning route</h4><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="1" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q10-MOVE-1" data-g9-solution-kind="REPRESENT" data-g9-solution-stage="REPRESENT" data-g9-solution-crux="false"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">1</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Represent</h4><p data-g9-solution-action>Compare the energy destinations in the level and incline trials while keeping the same initial kinetic energy.</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>same block</li><li>same initial speed</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> The same block starts both trials with the same speed, so both trials start with the same kinetic energy.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> Same initial kinetic energy in both trials.</p></div></section><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="2" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q10-MOVE-2" data-g9-solution-kind="CONNECT" data-g9-solution-stage="CONNECT" data-g9-solution-crux="false"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">2</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Connect</h4><p data-g9-solution-action>On the horizontal surface the stopping point has no gravitational-potential change, so friction dissipates all the initial kinetic energy.</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>horizontal trial</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> The block stops and the source states no other mechanical-energy store on the level surface.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> E_friction,horizontal=K_initial.</p></div></section><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="3" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2007-P1-Q10-MOVE-3" data-g9-solution-kind="VERIFY" data-g9-solution-stage="INTERPRET" data-g9-solution-crux="true"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">3</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Interpret</h4><p class="g9-prov" data-g9-crux-label>Key move</p><p data-g9-solution-action>On the incline, some initial kinetic energy becomes gravitational potential energy before the block stops, so friction dissipates less; the coefficient does not change because the same surfaces remain in contact.</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>30° incline</li><li>same surfaces</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> For the incline K_initial=ΔU_g+E_friction. Tilting changes N and therefore friction magnitude, but not the coefficient assigned to the same pair of surfaces.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> Statement 1 true; statement 2 false → option C.</p></div></section></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-answer-box" data-g9-component="ANSWER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="ANSWER_BOX"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="answer"><p>Option (C): statement (1) is true and statement (2) is false.</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-check-box" data-g9-component="CHECK" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CHECK_BOX"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="independent_check"><h4>Independent check</h4><p>The incline case satisfies K_initial = gain in gravitational potential energy + energy dissipated by friction.</p></div></div></template></div></section></article><article id="g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33" data-g9-unit="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33" data-g9-kind="QUESTION" data-g9-role="CORE2" data-g9-search-text="Physics Laws of Motion Quantitative static and kinetic friction conditions Hard concept Connected reasoning Newton-law and observer-frame practice Assertion–reason multiple choice IIT-JEE 2008 · Paper II · Q33 Verified past-exam adaptation Judge the pair of statements: (1) pulling a heavy body across level ground is easier than pushing it when the applied force is oblique; (2) frictional force depends on the nature of the surfaces in contact. Compare oblique pull and oblique push on the same level surface. (A) Both true and (2) explains (1) (B) Both true but (2) does not explain (1) (C) (1) true, (2) false (D) (1) false, (2) true" class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-header" data-g9-component="IDENTITY" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="HEADER"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="source_identity"><h2>IIT-JEE · 2008 · Paper II · Q33</h2><p class="g9-prov">Official past paper, faithful restatement of the original</p></div><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE2" data-g9-meta-record="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-NLM-FIRST-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Laws of Motion"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Laws of Motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT" data-g9-meta-value="Quantitative static and kinetic friction conditions"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Quantitative static and kinetic friction conditions</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT" data-g9-meta-value="HARD"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Hard concept</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="question-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33" data-g9-meta-value="D2"><strong>Question difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Connected reasoning</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="family" data-g9-meta-ref="FAM-PHY-NLM-PRACTICE" data-g9-meta-value="Newton-law and observer-frame practice"><strong>Family:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Newton-law and observer-frame practice</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="question-type" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33" data-g9-meta-value="assertion_reason_mcq"><strong>Question type:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Assertion–reason multiple choice</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="source" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33" data-g9-meta-value="IIT-JEE 2008 · Paper II · Q33"><strong>Source:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>IIT-JEE 2008 · Paper II · Q33</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="provenance" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33" data-g9-meta-value="PYQ_ADAPTED"><strong>Provenance:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Verified past-exam adaptation</span></span></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-link-list" data-g9-component="SOURCE_PDF" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="LINK_LIST"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="source_pdf"><a class="g9-pdf-link g9-source-pdf" data-g9-source-pdf href="https://jeeadv.ac.in/past_qps/2008_2.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" type="application/pdf" aria-label="Open the original past paper as a PDF to print it"><svg class="g9-pdf-icon" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="24" height="24"><path d="M6 2.5h8l4.5 4.5v14.5H6z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linejoin="round"/><path d="M14 2.5V7h4.5M9 12h6.5M9 15.5h6.5M9 19h4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg><span>Source paper (PDF)</span></a></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-grid" data-g9-component="DIFFICULTY_WHY" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_GRID"><button type="button" class="g9-why-toggle" data-g9-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="g9-why-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33"><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-band" data-g9-band="D2">D2 · 3/10 · about 2 min</span><span>Why this difficulty?</span></button><div id="g9-why-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33" class="g9-why" hidden><p>Requires identifying the normal-force mechanism and separating it from the coefficient-of-friction statement.</p><div class=g9-dgrid><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Choosing the model</span><b>1/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Representation</span><b>1/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Reasoning chain</span><b>1/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Algebra and numbers</span><b>0/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Traps and exceptions</span><b>0/2</b></div></div></div></div></section><div class="g9-split"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-attempt" data-blueprint-slot="attempt" dat