data-g9-component-items="1"><div class="g9-card-head"><strong>Representation</strong></div><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33-REP-PYQ-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33-FRICTION" data-g9-stage="PRE_ATTEMPT" data-g9-representation="REP-PYQ-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33-FRICTION" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="1" data-g9-stages-total="1" data-g9-stages=""><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 440 240" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33-REP-PYQ-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33-FRICTION-1--j08q33-title g9fig-CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33-REP-PYQ-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33-FRICTION-1--j08q33-desc">\n<title id="g9fig-CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33-REP-PYQ-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33-FRICTION-1--j08q33-title">Oblique pull compared with oblique push</title><desc id="g9fig-CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33-REP-PYQ-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33-FRICTION-1--j08q33-desc">The same body is on the same level rough surface. A force is angled upward while pulling and downward while pushing. Weight and normal force are labelled without evaluating them.</desc>\n<defs><marker id="g9fig-CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33-REP-PYQ-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33-FRICTION-1--j08q33-a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker></defs>\n<g font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="14" fill="currentColor" stroke="currentColor"><text x="16" y="24" stroke="none" font-weight="700">same body · same rough level surface</text><text x="70" y="54" stroke="none" >oblique pull</text><line x1="18" y1="170" x2="204" y2="170" stroke-width="2" /><rect x="74" y="120" width="60" height="50" fill="none" stroke-width="2" /><line x1="134" y1="130" x2="190" y2="92" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33-REP-PYQ-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33-FRICTION-1--j08q33-a)"/><text x="166" y="82" stroke="none" >F</text><line x1="104" y1="120" x2="104" y2="78" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33-REP-PYQ-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33-FRICTION-1--j08q33-a)"/><text x="112" y="86" stroke="none" >N</text><line x1="104" y1="145" x2="104" y2="208" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33-REP-PYQ-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33-FRICTION-1--j08q33-a)"/><text x="112" y="205" stroke="none" >mg</text><text x="286" y="54" stroke="none" >oblique push</text><line x1="226" y1="170" x2="422" y2="170" stroke-width="2" /><rect x="286" y="120" width="60" height="50" fill="none" stroke-width="2" /><line x1="346" y1="130" x2="402" y2="164" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33-REP-PYQ-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33-FRICTION-1--j08q33-a)"/><text x="388" y="180" stroke="none" >F</text><line x1="316" y1="120" x2="316" y2="78" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33-REP-PYQ-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33-FRICTION-1--j08q33-a)"/><text x="324" y="86" stroke="none" >N</text><line x1="316" y1="145" x2="316" y2="208" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33-REP-PYQ-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33-FRICTION-1--j08q33-a)"/><text x="324" y="205" stroke="none" >mg</text></g></svg>\n<figcaption data-g9-block="stage_caption" data-g9-caption="stages"></figcaption></figure></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-support" data-blueprint-slot="support" data-required="false"><div class="g9-component g9-c-ladder" data-g9-component="HINT_LADDER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="LADDER" data-g9-component-items="3"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core2-hints"><summary>Need a hint? · guided support</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-eyebrow">Hint ladder · reveal only what you need</div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="authored_core2_support"><h4>Guided support</h4><div class="g9-ladder" data-g9-ladder-ref="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-group="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL"><ol data-g9-ladder></ol><ol class="g9-rung-map" aria-label="Rungs still to come"><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H0</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Representation</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H1</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Key concept</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H2</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Crux</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li></ol><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-1"><li data-g9-rung="1" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[0]" data-g9-support-reveals="CONCEPT" data-g9-support-stage="REPRESENTATION" data-g9-support-kind="REPRESENT" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33-MOVE-1"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H0</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Representation</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><p data-g9-support-prompt>Which force component changes the normal reaction?</p><details data-g9-support-reveal><summary>Reveal support</summary><p>Draw the body twice: once with an oblique pull and once with an oblique push.</p></details></li></template><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-2"><li data-g9-rung="2" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[1]" data-g9-support-reveals="CONCEPT" data-g9-support-stage="KEY_CONCEPT" data-g9-support-kind="CONNECT" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33-MOVE-2"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H1</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Key concept</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><p data-g9-support-prompt>Does an upward component make N larger or smaller than mg? What about a downward component?</p><details data-g9-support-reveal><summary>Reveal support</summary><p>Write only the vertical force balance first.</p></details></li></template><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-3"><li data-g9-rung="3" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[2]" data-g9-support-reveals="METHOD" data-g9-support-stage="CRUX" data-g9-support-kind="CONNECT" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33-MOVE-3"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H2</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Crux</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><p data-g9-support-prompt>Does &#x27;friction depends on surface nature&#x27; actually explain why pull and push differ here?</p><details data-g9-support-reveal><summary>Reveal support</summary><p>Keep μ fixed because the pair of surfaces is the same; compare friction through the changed N.</p></details></li></template><button type="button" data-g9-next-rung>Show guided support</button></div></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-link-list" data-g9-component="CONCEPT_NAV" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="LINK_LIST"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="concept_navigation"><h4>Need the concept again?</h4><ul><li><a data-g9-concept-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT" href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT">Quantitative static and kinetic friction conditions</a></li><li><a data-g9-concept-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW" href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW">Net force, not any one force, sets acceleration</a></li></ul></div></div></section></div></div><section class="blueprint-slot slot-solution" data-blueprint-slot="solution" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure" data-g9-component="SOLUTION" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE"><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33-solution"><summary>Answer and working</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33-solution"><div class="g9-component g9-c-step-list" data-g9-component="SOLUTION_STEPS" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEP_LIST" data-g9-component-items="3"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="structured_working"><h4>Reasoning route</h4><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="1" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33-MOVE-1" data-g9-solution-kind="REPRESENT" data-g9-solution-stage="REPRESENT" data-g9-solution-crux="false"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">1</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Represent</h4><p data-g9-solution-action>Resolve the oblique applied force into horizontal and vertical components for a pull and for a push.</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>same level surface</li><li>oblique force</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> The vertical component changes the perpendicular force balance even when the horizontal component is compared like-for-like.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> Pull has an upward component; push has a downward component.</p></div></section><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="2" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33-MOVE-2" data-g9-solution-kind="CONNECT" data-g9-solution-stage="CONNECT" data-g9-solution-crux="false"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">2</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Connect</h4><p data-g9-solution-action>Use vertical balance: an upward pull reduces N below mg, while a downward push increases N above mg.</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>vertical balance</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> With no vertical acceleration, N plus upward applied components must balance mg; downward applied components add to the load the floor supports.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> N_pull=mg-F_y; N_push=mg+F_y.</p></div></section><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="3" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2008-P2-Q33-MOVE-3" data-g9-solution-kind="VERIFY" data-g9-solution-stage="INTERPRET" data-g9-solution-crux="true"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">3</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Interpret</h4><p class="g9-prov" data-g9-crux-label>Key move</p><p data-g9-solution-action>Because limiting/sliding friction scales with N, pulling can require less effort; surface nature affects μ but does not explain the pull-versus-push difference for the same surfaces.</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>same surfaces</li><li>different normal reactions</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> The comparison keeps the same contacting surfaces, so μ is unchanged; only N differs between pull and push.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> Both statements true, but statement 2 is not the explanation → option B.</p></div></section></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-answer-box" data-g9-component="ANSWER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="ANSWER_BOX"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="answer"><p>Option (B): both statements are true, but statement (2) is not the explanation of statement (1).</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-check-box" data-g9-component="CHECK" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CHECK_BOX"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="independent_check"><h4>Independent check</h4><p>If the applied force were exactly horizontal, the normal-reaction distinction between pulling and pushing would disappear.</p></div></div></template></div></section></article><article id="g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P1-Q41" data-g9-unit="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P1-Q41" data-g9-kind="QUESTION" data-g9-role="CORE2" data-g9-search-text="Physics Laws of Motion Quantitative static and kinetic friction conditions Hard concept Connected reasoning Newton-law and observer-frame practice Integer answer IIT-JEE 2011 · Paper I · Q41 Verified past-exam adaptation A block is on a plane inclined at 45 degrees. The coefficient of friction is mu. The force needed to just push the block upward is three times the force needed to just prevent it from sliding downward. If N = 10 mu, find N. Both forces act parallel to the incline. Each case is at limiting equilibrium." class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-header" data-g9-component="IDENTITY" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="HEADER"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="source_identity"><h2>IIT-JEE · 2011 · Paper I · Q41</h2><p class="g9-prov">Official past paper, faithful restatement of the original</p></div><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE2" data-g9-meta-record="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P1-Q41" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-NLM-FIRST-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Laws of Motion"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Laws of Motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT" data-g9-meta-value="Quantitative static and kinetic friction conditions"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Quantitative static and kinetic friction conditions</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT" data-g9-meta-value="HARD"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Hard concept</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="question-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P1-Q41" data-g9-meta-value="D2"><strong>Question difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Connected reasoning</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="family" data-g9-meta-ref="FAM-PHY-NLM-PRACTICE" data-g9-meta-value="Newton-law and observer-frame practice"><strong>Family:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Newton-law and observer-frame practice</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="question-type" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P1-Q41" data-g9-meta-value="integer_answer"><strong>Question type:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Integer answer</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="source" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P1-Q41" data-g9-meta-value="IIT-JEE 2011 · Paper I · Q41"><strong>Source:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>IIT-JEE 2011 · Paper I · Q41</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="provenance" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P1-Q41" data-g9-meta-value="PYQ_ADAPTED"><strong>Provenance:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Verified past-exam adaptation</span></span></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-link-list" data-g9-component="SOURCE_PDF" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="LINK_LIST"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="source_pdf"><a class="g9-pdf-link g9-source-pdf" data-g9-source-pdf href="https://jeeadv.ac.in/past_qps/2011_1.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" type="application/pdf" aria-label="Open the original past paper as a PDF to print it"><svg class="g9-pdf-icon" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="24" height="24"><path d="M6 2.5h8l4.5 4.5v14.5H6z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linejoin="round"/><path d="M14 2.5V7h4.5M9 12h6.5M9 15.5h6.5M9 19h4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg><span>Source paper (PDF)</span></a></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-grid" data-g9-component="DIFFICULTY_WHY" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_GRID"><button type="button" class="g9-why-toggle" data-g9-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="g9-why-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P1-Q41"><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-band" data-g9-band="D2">D2 · 5/10 · about 2 min</span><span>Why this difficulty?</span></button><div id="g9-why-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P1-Q41" class="g9-why" hidden><p>Two limiting-friction states must be written with opposite friction directions.</p><div class=g9-dgrid><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Choosing the model</span><b>1/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Representation</span><b>1/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Reasoning chain</span><b>1/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Algebra and numbers</span><b>1/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Traps and exceptions</span><b>1/2</b></div></div></div></div></section><div class="g9-split"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-attempt" data-blueprint-slot="attempt" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-stem" data-g9-component="STEM" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEM"><div class="g9-eyebrow">Attempt first</div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="stem"><p>A block is on a plane inclined at 45 degrees. The coefficient of friction is mu. The force needed to just push the block upward is three times the force needed to just prevent it from sliding downward. If N = 10 mu, find N.</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-callout-info" data-g9-component="CONDITIONS" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CALLOUT_INFO"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="conditions"><h4>Conditions</h4><ul><li>Both forces act parallel to the incline.</li><li>Each case is at limiting equilibrium.</li></ul></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-callout-warn" data-g9-component="TRAP" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CALLOUT_WARN"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core2-wrong-route"><summary>Common wrong route · open if you want a warning</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="common_wrong_route"><h4>Common wrong route</h4><p>Using the same friction direction in both limiting cases.</p></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-attempt" data-g9-component="ATTEMPT" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="ATTEMPT"><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your answer<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div></div></section></div><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-representation" data-blueprint-slot="representation" data-required="false"><div class="g9-component g9-c-visual-card" data-g9-component="REPRESENTATION" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="VISUAL_CARD" data-g9-component-items="1"><div class="g9-card-head"><strong>Representation</strong></div><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P1-Q41-REP-PYQ-IITJEE-2011-P1-Q41-FRICTION" data-g9-stage="PRE_ATTEMPT" data-g9-representation="REP-PYQ-IITJEE-2011-P1-Q41-FRICTION" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="1" data-g9-stages-total="1" data-g9-stages=""><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 440 255" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P1-Q41-REP-PYQ-IITJEE-2011-P1-Q41-FRICTION-1--j11q41-title g9fig-CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P1-Q41-REP-PYQ-IITJEE-2011-P1-Q41-FRICTION-1--j11q41-desc">\n<title id="g9fig-CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P1-Q41-REP-PYQ-IITJEE-2011-P1-Q41-FRICTION-1--j11q41-title">Two limiting cases on a 45 degree rough incline</title><desc id="g9fig-CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P1-Q41-REP-PYQ-IITJEE-2011-P1-Q41-FRICTION-1--j11q41-desc">The same block is shown in two source-given limiting cases on a 45 degree incline: a parallel force just pushes it upward, or just prevents downward sliding. No friction direction or coefficient is solved.</desc>\n<defs><marker id="g9fig-CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P1-Q41-REP-PYQ-IITJEE-2011-P1-Q41-FRICTION-1--j11q41-a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker></defs>\n<g font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="14" fill="currentColor" stroke="currentColor"><text x="16" y="24" stroke="none" font-weight="700">45° rough incline</text><text x="16" y="44" stroke="none">applied force parallel to plane</text><text x="26" y="55" stroke="none" >case A: just pushes upward</text><line x1="20" y1="178" x2="205" y2="73" stroke-width="2" /><rect x="82" y="120" width="55" height="40" fill="none" stroke-width="2" transform="rotate(-30 109 140)"/><line x1="132" y1="121" x2="181" y2="93" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P1-Q41-REP-PYQ-IITJEE-2011-P1-Q41-FRICTION-1--j11q41-a)"/><text x="183" y="88" stroke="none" >P_up</text><text x="26" y="194" stroke="none" >45°</text><text x="238" y="62" stroke="none">case B: prevents</text><text x="238" y="80" stroke="none">downward sliding</text><line x1="228" y1="178" x2="420" y2="69" stroke-width="2" /><rect x="292" y="120" width="55" height="40" fill="none" stroke-width="2" transform="rotate(-30 319 140)"/><line x1="339" y1="121" x2="388" y2="93" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P1-Q41-REP-PYQ-IITJEE-2011-P1-Q41-FRICTION-1--j11q41-a)"/><text x="390" y="88" stroke="none" >P_hold</text><text x="234" y="194" stroke="none" >45°</text><text x="16" y="230" stroke="none">coefficient = μ</text><text x="16" y="250" stroke="none">both cases at limiting equilibrium</text></g></svg>\n<figcaption data-g9-block="stage_caption" data-g9-caption="stages"></figcaption></figure></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-support" data-blueprint-slot="support" data-required="false"><div class="g9-component g9-c-ladder" data-g9-component="HINT_LADDER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="LADDER" data-g9-component-items="3"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core2-hints"><summary>Need a hint? · guided support</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-eyebrow">Hint ladder · reveal only what you need</div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="authored_core2_support"><h4>Guided support</h4><div class="g9-ladder" data-g9-ladder-ref="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P1-Q41-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-group="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL"><ol data-g9-ladder></ol><ol class="g9-rung-map" aria-label="Rungs still to come"><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H0</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Representation</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H1</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Key concept</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H2</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Crux</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li></ol><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P1-Q41-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-1"><li data-g9-rung="1" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[0]" data-g9-support-reveals="CONCEPT" data-g9-support-stage="REPRESENTATION" data-g9-support-kind="REPRESENT" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P1-Q41-MOVE-1"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H0</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Representation</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><p data-g9-support-prompt>Which way would the block move in each limiting case if friction were removed?</p><details data-g9-support-reveal><summary>Reveal support</summary><p>Make two incline sketches rather than reusing one friction arrow.</p></details></li></template><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P1-Q41-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-2"><li data-g9-rung="2" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[1]" data-g9-support-reveals="CONCEPT" data-g9-support-stage="KEY_CONCEPT" data-g9-support-kind="CONNECT" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P1-Q41-MOVE-2"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H1</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Key concept</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><p data-g9-support-prompt>What are N and the limiting friction magnitude?</p><details data-g9-support-reveal><summary>Reveal support</summary><p>The applied forces are parallel to the incline, so they do not change N.</p></details></li></template><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P1-Q41-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-3"><li data-g9-rung="3" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[2]" data-g9-support-reveals="METHOD" data-g9-support-stage="CRUX" data-g9-support-kind="CONNECT" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P1-Q41-MOVE-3"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H2</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Crux</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><p data-g9-support-prompt>Can you write both limiting-force equations before using the 3:1 ratio?</p><details data-g9-support-reveal><summary>Reveal support</summary><p>The same μN term enters with opposite signs in the two along-slope balances.</p></details></li></template><button type="button" data-g9-next-rung>Show guided support</button></div></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-link-list" data-g9-component="CONCEPT_NAV" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="LINK_LIST"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="concept_navigation"><h4>Need the concept again?</h4><ul><li><a data-g9-concept-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P1-Q41" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT" href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT">Quantitative static and kinetic friction conditions</a></li><li><a data-g9-concept-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P1-Q41" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW" href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW">Net force, not any one force, sets acceleration</a></li></ul></div></div></section></div></div><section class="blueprint-slot slot-solution" data-blueprint-slot="solution" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure" data-g9-component="SOLUTION" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE"><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P1-Q41-solution"><summary>Answer and working</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P1-Q41-solution"><div class="g9-component g9-c-step-list" data-g9-component="SOLUTION_STEPS" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEP_LIST" data-g9-component-items="3"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="structured_working"><h4>Reasoning route</h4><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="1" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P1-Q41-MOVE-1" data-g9-solution-kind="REPRESENT" data-g9-solution-stage="REPRESENT" data-g9-solution-crux="false"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">1</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Represent</h4><p data-g9-solution-action>Draw the two limiting cases separately and identify the impending motion in each.</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>45° incline</li><li>limiting equilibrium</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> Friction opposes the impending relative slip, so reversing the impending motion reverses the friction direction.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> Impending up → friction down slope; impending down → friction up slope.</p></div></section><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="2" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P1-Q41-MOVE-2" data-g9-solution-kind="CONNECT" data-g9-solution-stage="CONNECT" data-g9-solution-crux="false"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">2</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Connect</h4><p data-g9-solution-action>Because each applied force is parallel to the plane, N=mg cos45°. At the limit the friction magnitude is μN.</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>parallel applied force</li><li>45°</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> The applied force has no perpendicular component and each case is explicitly at impending slip.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> N=mg cos45° and f_lim=μmg cos45°.</p></div></section><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="3" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P1-Q41-MOVE-3" data-g9-solution-kind="TRANSFORM" data-g9-solution-stage="CALCULATE" data-g9-solution-crux="true"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">3</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Calculate</h4><p class="g9-prov" data-g9-crux-label>Key move</p><p data-g9-solution-action>Write P_up=mg(sin45°+μcos45°) and P_hold=mg(sin45°-μcos45°), then impose P_up=3P_hold.</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>P_up=3P_hold</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> The same limiting friction magnitude enters with opposite signs because its direction reverses between the two impending motions.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> 1+μ=3(1-μ) → μ=1/2 → N=5.</p></div></section></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-answer-box" data-g9-component="ANSWER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="ANSWER_BOX"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="answer"><p>5.</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-check-box" data-g9-component="CHECK" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CHECK_BOX"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="independent_check"><h4>Independent check</h4><p>With mu=1/2, the two force factors are 1.5 and 0.5, a 3:1 ratio.</p></div></div></template></div></section></article><article id="g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q34" data-g9-unit="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q34" data-g9-kind="QUESTION" data-g9-role="CORE2" data-g9-search-text="Physics Laws of Motion Quantitative static and kinetic friction conditions Hard concept Connected reasoning Newton-law and observer-frame practice Integer answer IIT-JEE 2011 · Paper II · Q34 Verified past-exam adaptation A 0.18 kg block on a rough horizontal surface is attached to a spring of force constant 2 N/m. The spring is initially unstretched. The block is given a horizontal impulse and first comes to rest after moving 0.06 m. With coefficient of friction 0.10 and g = 10 m/s^2, its initial speed is N/10 m/s. Find N. Spring is initially unstretched. Kinetic friction coefficient is 0.10. Use g=10 m/s^2." class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-header" data-g9-component="IDENTITY" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="HEADER"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="source_identity"><h2>IIT-JEE · 2011 · Paper II · Q34</h2><p class="g9-prov">Official past paper, faithful restatement of the original</p></div><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE2" data-g9-meta-record="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q34" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-NLM-FIRST-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Laws of Motion"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Laws of Motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT" data-g9-meta-value="Quantitative static and kinetic friction conditions"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Quantitative static and kinetic friction conditions</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT" data-g9-meta-value="HARD"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Hard concept</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="question-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q34" data-g9-meta-value="D2"><strong>Question difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Connected reasoning</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="family" data-g9-meta-ref="FAM-PHY-NLM-PRACTICE" data-g9-meta-value="Newton-law and observer-frame practice"><strong>Family:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Newton-law and observer-frame practice</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="question-type" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q34" data-g9-meta-value="integer_answer"><strong>Question type:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Integer answer</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="source" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q34" data-g9-meta-value="IIT-JEE 2011 · Paper II · Q34"><strong>Source:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>IIT-JEE 2011 · Paper II · Q34</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="provenance" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q34" data-g9-meta-value="PYQ_ADAPTED"><strong>Provenance:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Verified past-exam adaptation</span></span></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-link-list" data-g9-component="SOURCE_PDF" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="LINK_LIST"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="source_pdf"><a class="g9-pdf-link g9-source-pdf" data-g9-source-pdf href="https://jeeadv.ac.in/past_qps/2011_2.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" type="application/pdf" aria-label="Open the original past paper as a PDF to print it"><svg class="g9-pdf-icon" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="24" height="24"><path d="M6 2.5h8l4.5 4.5v14.5H6z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linejoin="round"/><path d="M14 2.5V7h4.5M9 12h6.5M9 15.5h6.5M9 19h4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg><span>Source paper (PDF)</span></a></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-grid" data-g9-component="DIFFICULTY_WHY" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_GRID"><button type="button" class="g9-why-toggle" data-g9-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="g9-why-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q34"><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-band" data-g9-band="D2">D2 · 5/10 · about 2 min</span><span>Why this difficulty?</span></button><div id="g9-why-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q34" class="g9-why" hidden><p>Requires an energy balance containing both spring storage and dissipative friction.</p><div class=g9-dgrid><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Choosing the model</span><b>1/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Representation</span><b>1/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Reasoning chain</span><b>1/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Algebra and numbers</span><b>1/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Traps and exceptions</span><b>1/2</b></div></div></div></div></section><div class="g9-split"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-attempt" data-blueprint-slot="attempt" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-stem" data-g9-component="STEM" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEM"><div class="g9-eyebrow">Attempt first</div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="stem"><p>A 0.18 kg block on a rough horizontal surface is attached to a spring of force constant 2 N/m. The spring is initially unstretched. The block is given a horizontal impulse and first comes to rest after moving 0.06 m. With coefficient of friction 0.10 and g = 10 m/<span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, its initial speed is N/10 m/s. Find N.</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-callout-info" data-g9-component="CONDITIONS" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CALLOUT_INFO"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="conditions"><h4>Conditions</h4><ul><li>Spring is initially unstretched.</li><li>Kinetic friction coefficient is 0.10.</li><li>Use g=10 m/<span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</li></ul></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-callout-warn" data-g9-component="TRAP" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CALLOUT_WARN"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core2-wrong-route"><summary>Common wrong route · open if you want a warning</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="common_wrong_route"><h4>Common wrong route</h4><p>Equating all initial kinetic energy to spring energy and forgetting friction work.</p></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-attempt" data-g9-component="ATTEMPT" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="ATTEMPT"><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your answer<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div></div></section></div><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-representation" data-blueprint-slot="representation" data-required="false"></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-support" data-blueprint-slot="support" data-required="false"><div class="g9-component g9-c-ladder" data-g9-component="HINT_LADDER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="LADDER" data-g9-component-items="2"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core2-hints"><summary>Need a hint? · guided support</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-eyebrow">Hint ladder · reveal only what you need</div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="authored_core2_support"><h4>Guided support</h4><div class="g9-ladder" data-g9-ladder-ref="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q34-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-group="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL"><ol data-g9-ladder></ol><ol class="g9-rung-map" aria-label="Rungs still to come"><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H0</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Represent</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H1</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Connect</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li></ol><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q34-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-1"><li data-g9-rung="1" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[0]" data-g9-support-reveals="CONCEPT" data-g9-support-kind="REPRESENT" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q34-MOVE-1"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H0</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Represent</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><div data-g9-support-reveal><p>List every sink of the block&#x27;s initial kinetic energy before it first stops.</p></div></li></template><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q34-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-2"><li data-g9-rung="2" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[1]" data-g9-support-reveals="METHOD" data-g9-support-kind="CONNECT" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q34-MOVE-2"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H1</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Connect</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><div data-g9-support-reveal><p>Use the stopping displacement for both spring extension and friction work.</p></div></li></template><button type="button" data-g9-next-rung>Show guided support</button></div></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-link-list" data-g9-component="CONCEPT_NAV" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="LINK_LIST"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="concept_navigation"><h4>Need the concept again?</h4><ul><li><a data-g9-concept-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q34" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT" href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT">Quantitative static and kinetic friction conditions</a></li><li><a data-g9-concept-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q34" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW" href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW">Net force, not any one force, sets acceleration</a></li></ul></div></div></section></div></div><section class="blueprint-slot slot-solution" data-blueprint-slot="solution" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure" data-g9-component="SOLUTION" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE"><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q34-solution"><summary>Answer and working</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q34-solution"><div class="g9-component g9-c-step-list" data-g9-component="SOLUTION_STEPS" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEP_LIST" data-g9-component-items="3"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="structured_working"><h4>Reasoning route</h4><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="1" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q34-MOVE-1" data-g9-solution-kind="REPRESENT" data-g9-solution-stage="REPRESENT" data-g9-solution-crux="false"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">1</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Represent</h4><p data-g9-solution-action>At the first stopping point, the initial kinetic energy has become spring energy plus work against friction.</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>stated source quantities</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> This follows from the stated physical or chemical model and the quantities preserved in the source-matched task.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> intermediate result 1</p></div></section><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="2" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q34-MOVE-2" data-g9-solution-kind="CONNECT" data-g9-solution-stage="CONNECT" data-g9-solution-crux="true"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">2</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Connect</h4><p class="g9-prov" data-g9-crux-label>Key move</p><p data-g9-solution-action>Write (1/2)mv^2 = (1/2)kx^2 + mu m g x with m=0.18, k=2, x=0.06 and mu=0.10.</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>stated source quantities</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> This follows from the stated physical or chemical model and the quantities preserved in the source-matched task.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> intermediate result 2</p></div></section><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="3" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q34-MOVE-3" data-g9-solution-kind="TRANSFORM" data-g9-solution-stage="CALCULATE" data-g9-solution-crux="false"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">3</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Calculate</h4><p data-g9-solution-action>The right side is 0.0036 + 0.0108 = 0.0144 J, so 0.09v^2=0.0144 and v=0.4 m/s; hence N=4.</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>stated source quantities</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> This follows from the stated physical or chemical model and the quantities preserved in the source-matched task.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> 4.</p></div></section></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-answer-box" data-g9-component="ANSWER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="ANSWER_BOX"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="answer"><p>4.</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-check-box" data-g9-component="CHECK" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CHECK_BOX"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="independent_check"><h4>Independent check</h4><p>Substituting v=0.4 m/s gives initial kinetic energy 0.0144 J, exactly the spring-plus-friction energy.</p></div></div></template></div></section></article><article id="g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P2-Q19" data-g9-unit="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P2-Q19" data-g9-kind="QUESTION" data-g9-role="CORE2" data-g9-search-text="Physics Laws of Motion Quantitative static and kinetic friction conditions Hard concept Multi-step reasoning Newton-law and observer-frame practice Matrix match JEE (Advanced) 2014 · Paper 2 · Q19 Verified past-exam adaptation Two touching blocks m1=1 kg and m2=2 kg rest on an incline of angle theta, with m1 placed just above m2 (up the slope), so m1 presses on m2. The plane is smooth under m1; for m2, static and kinetic friction coefficients are both 0.3. Determine which friction expression applies to m2 at theta = 5°, 10°, 15° and 20°. Candidate expressions are: (1) m2 g sin theta; (2) (m1+m2)g sin theta; (3) 0.3 m2 g cos theta; (4) 0.3(m1+m2)g cos theta. m1=1 kg; m2=2 kg. Only m2 has friction with the plane, mu=0.3. Use tan5.5°≈0.1, tan11.5°≈0.2, tan16.5°≈0.3. m1 is directly up-slope of m2 and pushes on it; m2 is the lower block. (A) P-1, Q-1, R-1, S-3 (B) P-2, Q-2, R-2, S-3 (C) P-2, Q-2, R-2, S-4 (D) P-2, Q-2, R-3, S-3" class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-header" data-g9-component="IDENTITY" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="HEADER"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="source_identity"><h2>JEE (Advanced) · 2014 · Paper 2 · Q19</h2><p class="g9-prov">Official past paper, faithful restatement of the original</p></div><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE2" data-g9-meta-record="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P2-Q19" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-NLM-FIRST-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Laws of Motion"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Laws of Motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT" data-g9-meta-value="Quantitative static and kinetic friction conditions"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Quantitative static and kinetic friction conditions</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT" data-g9-meta-value="HARD"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Hard concept</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="question-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P2-Q19" data-g9-meta-value="D3"><strong>Question difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Multi-step reasoning</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="family" data-g9-meta-ref="FAM-PHY-NLM-PRACTICE" data-g9-meta-value="Newton-law and observer-frame practice"><strong>Family:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Newton-law and observer-frame practice</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="question-type" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P2-Q19" data-g9-meta-value="matrix_match_mcq"><strong>Question type:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Matrix match</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="source" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P2-Q19" data-g9-meta-value="JEE (Advanced) 2014 · Paper 2 · Q19"><strong>Source:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>JEE (Advanced) 2014 · Paper 2 · Q19</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="provenance" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P2-Q19" data-g9-meta-value="PYQ_ADAPTED"><strong>Provenance:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Verified past-exam adaptation</span></span></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-link-list" data-g9-component="SOURCE_PDF" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="LINK_LIST"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="source_pdf"><a class="g9-pdf-link g9-source-pdf" data-g9-source-pdf href="https://jeeadv.ac.in/past_qps/2014_2.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" type="application/pdf" aria-label="Open the original past paper as a PDF to print it"><svg class="g9-pdf-icon" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="24" height="24"><path d="M6 2.5h8l4.5 4.5v14.5H6z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linejoin="round"/><path d="M14 2.5V7h4.5M9 12h6.5M9 15.5h6.5M9 19h4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg><span>Source paper (PDF)</span></a></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-grid" data-g9-component="DIFFICULTY_WHY" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_GRID"><button type="button" class="g9-why-toggle" data-g9-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="g9-why-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P2-Q19"><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-band" data-g9-band="D3">D3 · 7/10 · about 3 min</span><span>Why this difficulty?</span></button><div id="g9-why-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P2-Q19" class="g9-why" hidden><p>Requires a whole-system equilibrium demand, a friction-capacity threshold, and a switch to kinetic friction.</p><div class=g9-dgrid><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Choosing the model</span><b>2/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Representation</span><b>2/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Reasoning chain</span><b>1/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Algebra and numbers</span><b>1/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Traps and exceptions</span><b>1/2</b></div></div></div></div></section><div class="g9-split"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-attempt" data-blueprint-slot="attempt" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-stem" data-g9-component="STEM" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEM"><div class="g9-eyebrow">Attempt first</div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="stem"><p>Two touching blocks m1=1 kg and m2=2 kg rest on an incline of angle theta, with m1 placed just above m2 (up the slope), so m1 presses on m2. The plane is smooth under m1; for m2, static and kinetic friction coefficients are both 0.3. Determine which friction expression applies to m2 at theta = 5°, 10°, 15° and 20°. Candidate expressions are: (1) m2 g sin theta; (2) (m1+m2)g sin theta; (3) 0.3 m2 g cos theta; (4) 0.3(m1+m2)g cos theta.</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-callout-info" data-g9-component="CONDITIONS" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CALLOUT_INFO"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="conditions"><h4>Conditions</h4><ul><li>m1=1 kg; m2=2 kg.</li><li>Only m2 has friction with the plane, mu=0.3.</li><li>Use tan5.5°≈0.1, tan11.5°≈0.2, tan16.5°≈0.3.</li><li>m1 is directly up-slope of m2 and pushes on it; m2 is the lower block.</li></ul></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-callout-warn" data-g9-component="TRAP" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CALLOUT_WARN"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core2-wrong-route"><summary>Common wrong route · open if you want a warning</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="common_wrong_route"><h4>Common wrong route</h4><p>Using m2 g sin theta as the required static friction and ignoring the contact force transmitted by m1.</p></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-attempt" data-g9-component="ATTEMPT" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="ATTEMPT"><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your answer<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div></div></section></div><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-representation" data-blueprint-slot="representation" data-required="false"><div class="g9-component g9-c-visual-card" data-g9-component="REPRESENTATION" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="VISUAL_CARD" data-g9-component-items="1"><div class="g9-card-head"><strong>Representation</strong></div><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P2-Q19-REP-PYQ-JEEADV-2014-P2-Q19-FRICTION" data-g9-stage="PRE_ATTEMPT" data-g9-representation="REP-PYQ-JEEADV-2014-P2-Q19-FRICTION" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="1" data-g9-stages-total="1" data-g9-stages=""><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 440 270" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P2-Q19-REP-PYQ-JEEADV-2014-P2-Q19-FRICTION-1--ja14q19-title g9fig-CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P2-Q19-REP-PYQ-JEEADV-2014-P2-Q19-FRICTION-1--ja14q19-desc">\n<title id="g9fig-CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P2-Q19-REP-PYQ-JEEADV-2014-P2-Q19-FRICTION-1--ja14q19-title">Two touching blocks with only the lower block on rough support</title><desc id="g9fig-CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P2-Q19-REP-PYQ-JEEADV-2014-P2-Q19-FRICTION-1--ja14q19-desc">Block m1 is immediately uphill of m2. The plane is smooth under m1 and rough under m2 with coefficient 0.3. Source angles 5, 10, 15 and 20 degrees are listed; no threshold is shown.</desc>\n<defs><marker id="g9fig-CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P2-Q19-REP-PYQ-JEEADV-2014-P2-Q19-FRICTION-1--ja14q19-a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker></defs>\n<g font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="14" fill="currentColor" stroke="currentColor"><text x="16" y="24" stroke="none" font-weight="700">m₁ = 1 kg above m₂ = 2 kg</text><line x1="24" y1="206" x2="416" y2="82" stroke-width="2" /><rect x="146" y="145" width="58" height="42" fill="none" stroke-width="2" transform="rotate(-18 175 166)"/><rect x="208" y="126" width="66" height="46" fill="none" stroke-width="2" transform="rotate(-18 241 149)"/><text x="126" y="120" stroke="none" >m₁ · smooth</text><text x="270" y="116" stroke="none" >m₂ · rough</text><text x="22" y="226" stroke="none" >θ</text><text x="16" y="250" stroke="none" >μ = 0.3 only under m₂ · θ = 5°, 10°, 15°, 20°</text></g></svg>\n<figcaption data-g9-block="stage_caption" data-g9-caption="stages"></figcaption></figure></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-support" data-blueprint-slot="support" data-required="false"><div class="g9-component g9-c-ladder" data-g9-component="HINT_LADDER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="LADDER" data-g9-component-items="5"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core2-hints"><summary>Need a hint? · guided support</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-eyebrow">Hint ladder · reveal only what you need</div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="authored_core2_support"><h4>Guided support</h4><div class="g9-ladder" data-g9-ladder-ref="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P2-Q19-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-group="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL"><ol data-g9-ladder></ol><ol class="g9-rung-map" aria-label="Rungs still to come"><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H0</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Representation</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H1</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Key concept</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H2</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Crux</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H3</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Equation / formal model</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H4</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Assembly checkpoint</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li></ol><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P2-Q19-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-1"><li data-g9-rung="1" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[0]" data-g9-support-reveals="CONCEPT" data-g9-support-stage="REPRESENTATION" data-g9-support-kind="REPRESENT" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P2-Q19-MOVE-1"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H0</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Representation</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><p data-g9-support-prompt>Which downslope weight components must the one rough contact ultimately support?</p><details data-g9-support-reveal><summary>Reveal support</summary><p>Start with a system boundary around both touching blocks while testing rest.</p></details></li></template><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P2-Q19-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-2"><li data-g9-rung="2" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[1]" data-g9-support-reveals="CONCEPT" data-g9-support-stage="KEY_CONCEPT" data-g9-support-kind="CONNECT" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P2-Q19-MOVE-2"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H1</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Key concept</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><p data-g9-support-prompt>What is N at that contact, and therefore what is its maximum static-friction capacity?</p><details data-g9-support-reveal><summary>Reveal support</summary><p>Only m2 has a rough contact with the plane.</p></details></li></template><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P2-Q19-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-3"><li data-g9-rung="3" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[2]" data-g9-support-reveals="METHOD" data-g9-support-stage="CRUX" data-g9-support-kind="CONNECT" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P2-Q19-MOVE-3"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H2</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Crux</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><p data-g9-support-prompt>What inequality marks the static-to-sliding threshold?</p><details data-g9-support-reveal><summary>Reveal support</summary><p>Do not compare m2g sinθ with μm2g cosθ; compare the whole-system demand with the capacity available under m2.</p></details></li></template><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P2-Q19-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-4"><li data-g9-rung="4" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[3]" data-g9-support-reveals="METHOD" data-g9-support-stage="FORMAL_MODEL" data-g9-support-kind="EXECUTE" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P2-Q19-MOVE-3"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H3</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Equation / formal model</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><p data-g9-support-prompt>What numerical tanθ threshold follows from m1=1, m2=2 and μ=0.3?</p><details data-g9-support-reveal><summary>Reveal support</summary><p>Reduce the threshold to a condition on tanθ before checking the four angles.</p></details></li></template><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P2-Q19-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-5"><li data-g9-rung="5" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[4]" data-g9-support-reveals="METHOD" data-g9-support-stage="CHECKPOINT" data-g9-support-kind="EXECUTE" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P2-Q19-MOVE-4"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H4</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Assembly checkpoint</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><p data-g9-support-prompt>Which expression belongs on each side of about 11.5°?</p><details data-g9-support-reveal><summary>Reveal support</summary><p>After threshold use the sliding model at m2; below it use only the static friction actually required.</p></details></li></template><button type="button" data-g9-next-rung>Show guided support</button></div></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-link-list" data-g9-component="CONCEPT_NAV" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="LINK_LIST"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="concept_navigation"><h4>Need the concept again?</h4><ul><li><a data-g9-concept-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P2-Q19" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT" href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT">Quantitative static and kinetic friction conditions</a></li><li><a data-g9-concept-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P2-Q19" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW" href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW">Net force, not any one force, sets acceleration</a></li></ul></div></div></section></div></div><section class="blueprint-slot slot-solution" data-blueprint-slot="solution" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure" data-g9-component="SOLUTION" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE"><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P2-Q19-solution"><summary>Answer and working</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P2-Q19-solution"><div class="g9-component g9-c-step-list" data-g9-component="SOLUTION_STEPS" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEP_LIST" data-g9-component-items="4"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="structured_working"><h4>Reasoning route</h4><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="1" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P2-Q19-MOVE-1" data-g9-solution-kind="REPRESENT" data-g9-solution-stage="REPRESENT" data-g9-solution-crux="false"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">1</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Represent</h4><p data-g9-solution-action>While testing whether rest is possible, choose m1+m2 as one system along the incline.</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>m1=1 kg</li><li>m2=2 kg</li><li>incline</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> Only m2 touches the rough part of the plane, but both blocks contribute downslope weight to the system&#x27;s equilibrium demand.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> f_required=(m1+m2)g sinθ.</p></div></section><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="2" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P2-Q19-MOVE-2" data-g9-solution-kind="CONNECT" data-g9-solution-stage="CONNECT" data-g9-solution-crux="false"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">2</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Connect</h4><p data-g9-solution-action>Find the capacity of the only rough support: for m2, N=m2g cosθ, so f_max=0.3m2g cosθ.</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>μ=0.3</li><li>rough support only under m2</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> m1 lies on a smooth part of the plane and supplies no plane friction; m2&#x27;s normal follows its perpendicular balance.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> f_max=0.3m2g cosθ.</p></div></section><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="3" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P2-Q19-MOVE-3" data-g9-solution-kind="CONNECT" data-g9-solution-stage="CONNECT" data-g9-solution-crux="true"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">3</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Connect</h4><p class="g9-prov" data-g9-crux-label>Key move</p><p data-g9-solution-action>Compare the whole-system demand with m2&#x27;s contact capacity: static rest requires (m1+m2)tanθ≤0.3m2, hence tanθ≤0.2.</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>f_required</li><li>f_max</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> Static friction supplies the required value only while that demand does not exceed μ_sN at the actual rough contact.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> Threshold tanθ=0.2, about 11.5°.</p></div></section><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="4" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P2-Q19-MOVE-4" data-g9-solution-kind="VERIFY" data-g9-solution-stage="INTERPRET" data-g9-solution-crux="false"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">4</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Interpret</h4><p data-g9-solution-action>Below threshold use the required static friction; above threshold the system slides and m2&#x27;s friction is 0.3m2g cosθ.</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>θ=5°,10°,15°,20°</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> Although the source gives equal static and kinetic coefficients, the expression still switches because the contact state changes from no-slip demand to established sliding.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> 5°,10° → expression 2; 15°,20° → expression 3; option D.</p></div></section></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-answer-box" data-g9-component="ANSWER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="ANSWER_BOX"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="answer"><p>Option (D): 5°→2, 10°→2, 15°→3, 20°→3.</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-check-box" data-g9-component="CHECK" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CHECK_BOX"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="independent_check"><h4>Independent check</h4><p>The transition occurs near tan theta=0.2, i.e. theta≈11.5°, between 10° and 15°.</p></div></div></template></div></section></article><article id="g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13" data-g9-unit="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13" data-g9-kind="QUESTION" data-g9-role="CORE2" data-g9-search-text="Physics Laws of Motion Quantitative static and kinetic friction conditions Hard concept High-transfer / exception-sensitive Newton-law and observer-frame practice Numerical value JEE (Advanced) 2020 · Paper 1 · Q13 Verified past-exam adaptation A uniform metre scale is supported horizontally on two index fingers initially at 0.00 cm and 90.00 cm. Both fingers are moved slowly toward the centre. The slipping finger alternates because static and kinetic friction differ. With mu_s=0.40 and mu_k=0.32, find the final distance x_R of the right finger from the 50.00 cm centre at the instant it stops and the left finger starts slipping again. Scale is uniform. Finger motion is quasistatic. mu_s=0.40; mu_k=0.32." class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-header" data-g9-component="IDENTITY" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="HEADER"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="source_identity"><h2>JEE (Advanced) · 2020 · Paper 1 · Q13</h2><p class="g9-prov">Official past paper, faithful restatement of the original</p></div><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE2" data-g9-meta-record="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-NLM-FIRST-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Laws of Motion"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Laws of Motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT" data-g9-meta-value="Quantitative static and kinetic friction conditions"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Quantitative static and kinetic friction conditions</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT" data-g9-meta-value="HARD"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Hard concept</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="question-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13" data-g9-meta-value="D4"><strong>Question difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>High-transfer / exception-sensitive</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="family" data-g9-meta-ref="FAM-PHY-NLM-PRACTICE" data-g9-meta-value="Newton-law and observer-frame practice"><strong>Family:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Newton-law and observer-frame practice</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="question-type" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13" data-g9-meta-value="numerical_value"><strong>Question type:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Numerical value</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="source" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13" data-g9-meta-value="JEE (Advanced) 2020 · Paper 1 · Q13"><strong>Source:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>JEE (Advanced) 2020 · Paper 1 · Q13</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="provenance" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13" data-g9-meta-value="PYQ_ADAPTED"><strong>Provenance:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Verified past-exam adaptation</span></span></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-link-list" data-g9-component="SOURCE_PDF" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="LINK_LIST"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="source_pdf"><a class="g9-pdf-link g9-source-pdf" data-g9-source-pdf href="https://jeeadv.ac.in/past_qps/2020_1_English.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" type="application/pdf" aria-label="Open the original past paper as a PDF to print it"><svg class="g9-pdf-icon" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="24" height="24"><path d="M6 2.5h8l4.5 4.5v14.5H6z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linejoin="round"/><path d="M14 2.5V7h4.5M9 12h6.5M9 15.5h6.5M9 19h4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg><span>Source paper (PDF)</span></a></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-grid" data-g9-component="DIFFICULTY_WHY" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_GRID"><button type="button" class="g9-why-toggle" data-g9-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="g9-why-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13"><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-band" data-g9-band="D4">D4 · 9/10 · about 4 min</span><span>Why this difficulty?</span></button><div id="g9-why-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13" class="g9-why" hidden><p>A two-stage quasistatic friction process must be combined with changing support reactions from torque balance.</p><div class=g9-dgrid><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Choosing the model</span><b>2/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Representation</span><b>2/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Reasoning chain</span><b>2/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Algebra and numbers</span><b>2/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Traps and exceptions</span><b>1/2</b></div></div></div></div></section><div class="g9-split"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-attempt" data-blueprint-slot="attempt" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-stem" data-g9-component="STEM" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEM"><div class="g9-eyebrow">Attempt first</div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="stem"><p>A uniform metre scale is supported horizontally on two index fingers initially at 0.00 cm and 90.00 cm. Both fingers are moved slowly toward the centre. The slipping finger alternates because static and kinetic friction differ. With mu_s=0.40 and mu_k=0.32, find the final distance x_R of the right finger from the 50.00 cm centre at the instant it stops and the left finger starts slipping again.</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-callout-info" data-g9-component="CONDITIONS" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CALLOUT_INFO"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="conditions"><h4>Conditions</h4><ul><li>Scale is uniform.</li><li>Finger motion is quasistatic.</li><li>mu_s=0.40; mu_k=0.32.</li></ul></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-callout-warn" data-g9-component="TRAP" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CALLOUT_WARN"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core2-wrong-route"><summary>Common wrong route · open if you want a warning</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="common_wrong_route"><h4>Common wrong route</h4><p>Using one fixed normal reaction or one friction state throughout both stages, so the second stick-slip threshold is written with stale contact data.</p></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-attempt" data-g9-component="ATTEMPT" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="ATTEMPT"><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your answer<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div></div></section></div><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-representation" data-blueprint-slot="representation" data-required="false"><div class="g9-component g9-c-visual-card" data-g9-component="REPRESENTATION" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="VISUAL_CARD" data-g9-component-items="1"><div class="g9-card-head"><strong>Representation</strong></div><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13-REP-PYQ-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13-FRICTION" data-g9-stage="PRE_ATTEMPT" data-g9-representation="REP-PYQ-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13-FRICTION" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="1" data-g9-stages-total="1" data-g9-stages=""><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 440 240" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13-REP-PYQ-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13-FRICTION-1--ja20q13-title g9fig-CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13-REP-PYQ-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13-FRICTION-1--ja20q13-desc">\n<title id="g9fig-CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13-REP-PYQ-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13-FRICTION-1--ja20q13-title">Uniform metre scale on two fingers moving inward</title><desc id="g9fig-CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13-REP-PYQ-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13-FRICTION-1--ja20q13-desc">A uniform metre scale is horizontal. Its centre is 50 centimetres. Initially the left finger is at 0 and the right at 90 centimetres. Both move slowly inward. Static and kinetic coefficients are 0.40 and 0.32. No solved transition is shown.</desc>\n<defs><marker id="g9fig-CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13-REP-PYQ-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13-FRICTION-1--ja20q13-a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker></defs>\n<g font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="14" fill="currentColor" stroke="currentColor"><text x="16" y="24" stroke="none" font-weight="700">uniform metre scale · μ_s = 0.40 · μ_k = 0.32</text><rect x="30" y="104" width="360" height="34" fill="none" stroke-width="2" /><text x="28" y="92" stroke="none" >0 cm</text><text x="198" y="92" stroke="none" >50 cm</text><text x="326" y="92" stroke="none">90 cm</text><text x="378" y="92" stroke="none">100 cm</text><line x1="210" y1="100" x2="210" y2="142" stroke-width="2" /><text x="164" y="165" stroke="none" >centre of mass</text><line x1="58" y1="190" x2="132" y2="190" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13-REP-PYQ-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13-FRICTION-1--ja20q13-a)"/><line x1="356" y1="190" x2="282" y2="190" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13-REP-PYQ-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13-FRICTION-1--ja20q13-a)"/><text x="134" y="214" stroke="none" >both fingers move slowly inward</text><line x1="46" y1="138" x2="46" y2="188" stroke-width="2" /><text x="20" y="207" stroke="none" >left finger</text><line x1="354" y1="138" x2="354" y2="188" stroke-width="2" /><text x="326" y="207" stroke="none" >right finger</text></g></svg>\n<figcaption data-g9-block="stage_caption" data-g9-caption="stages"></figcaption></figure></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-support" data-blueprint-slot="support" data-required="false"><div class="g9-component g9-c-ladder" data-g9-component="HINT_LADDER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="LADDER" data-g9-component-items="5"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core2-hints"><summary>Need a hint? · guided support</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-eyebrow">Hint ladder · reveal only what you need</div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="authored_core2_support"><h4>Guided support</h4><div class="g9-ladder" data-g9-ladder-ref="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-group="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL"><ol data-g9-ladder></ol><ol class="g9-rung-map" aria-label="Rungs still to come"><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H0</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Representation</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H1</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Key concept</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H2</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Crux</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H3</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Equation / formal model</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H4</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Assembly checkpoint</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li></ol><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-1"><li data-g9-rung="1" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[0]" data-g9-support-reveals="CONCEPT" data-g9-support-stage="REPRESENTATION" data-g9-support-kind="REPRESENT" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13-MOVE-1"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H0</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Representation</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><p data-g9-support-prompt>What are the two contact states during the first stage?</p><details data-g9-support-reveal><summary>Reveal support</summary><p>Label each finger as its own contact and track which one is slipping and which one is sticking; do not assign one friction law to both.</p></details></li></template><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-2"><li data-g9-rung="2" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[1]" data-g9-support-reveals="CONCEPT" data-g9-support-stage="KEY_CONCEPT" data-g9-support-kind="CONNECT" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13-MOVE-2"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H1</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Key concept</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><p data-g9-support-prompt>Why must N_L/N_R change as a finger moves toward the centre?</p><details data-g9-support-reveal><summary>Reveal support</summary><p>Before using μN, update the normal reactions from the current support geometry.</p></details></li></template><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-3"><li data-g9-rung="3" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[2]" data-g9-support-reveals="METHOD" data-g9-support-stage="CRUX" data-g9-support-kind="CONNECT" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13-MOVE-3"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H2</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Crux</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><p data-g9-support-prompt>Which finger is fixed after the first switch, and which coordinate is still changing?</p><details data-g9-support-reveal><summary>Reveal support</summary><p>The process has two stages. After the first transition, the contact roles reverse and the support reactions must be rebuilt for the new geometry.</p></details></li></template><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-4"><li data-g9-rung="4" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[3]" data-g9-support-reveals="METHOD" data-g9-support-stage="FORMAL_MODEL" data-g9-support-kind="EXECUTE" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13-MOVE-4"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H3</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Equation / formal model</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><p data-g9-support-prompt>What equality compares those two friction magnitudes at the second transition?</p><details data-g9-support-reveal><summary>Reveal support</summary><p>At a switch, the currently slipping contact uses μ_kN while the currently sticking contact has reached μ_sN.</p></details></li></template><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-5"><li data-g9-rung="5" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[4]" data-g9-support-reveals="METHOD" data-g9-support-stage="CHECKPOINT" data-g9-support-kind="EXECUTE" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13-MOVE-4"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H4</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Assembly checkpoint</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><p data-g9-support-prompt>What offset r-50 results, and is it between 0 and the initial 40 cm?</p><details data-g9-support-reveal><summary>Reveal support</summary><p>Use the updated ratio N_R/N_L=32/(r-50) in μ_kN_R=μ_sN_L.</p></details></li></template><button type="button" data-g9-next-rung>Show guided support</button></div></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-link-list" data-g9-component="CONCEPT_NAV" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="LINK_LIST"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="concept_navigation"><h4>Need the concept again?</h4><ul><li><a data-g9-concept-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT" href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT">Quantitative static and kinetic friction conditions</a></li><li><a data-g9-concept-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW" href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW">Net force, not any one force, sets acceleration</a></li></ul></div></div></section></div></div><section class="blueprint-slot slot-solution" data-blueprint-slot="solution" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure" data-g9-component="SOLUTION" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE"><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13-solution"><summary>Answer and working</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13-solution"><div class="g9-component g9-c-step-list" data-g9-component="SOLUTION_STEPS" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEP_LIST" data-g9-component-items="4"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="structured_working"><h4>Reasoning route</h4><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="1" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13-MOVE-1" data-g9-solution-kind="REPRESENT" data-g9-solution-stage="REPRESENT" data-g9-solution-crux="false"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">1</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Represent</h4><p data-g9-solution-action>Treat the slowly moved metre scale as quasistatic and label the two finger supports by their current coordinates and normal reactions N_L and N_R.</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>uniform scale</li><li>quasistatic motion</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> At every instant the scale has negligible translational and angular acceleration, but the support reactions change as the finger positions change.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> N_L+N_R=mg; their ratio is set by support positions.</p></div></section><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="2" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13-MOVE-2" data-g9-solution-kind="CONNECT" data-g9-solution-stage="CONNECT" data-g9-solution-crux="false"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">2</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Connect</h4><p data-g9-solution-action>For each current pair of support positions, obtain N_L and N_R from force and torque balance before writing friction.</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>current support positions</li><li>centre of mass at 50 cm</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> Friction capacities are μN, so a fixed normal reaction while a support moves would make the threshold condition inconsistent with the changing support geometry.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> With the right finger at 90 cm, N_L/N_R=40/(50-l); the first switch gives l=18 cm.</p></div></section><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="3" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13-MOVE-3" data-g9-solution-kind="TRANSFORM" data-g9-solution-stage="CALCULATE" data-g9-solution-crux="false"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">3</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Calculate</h4><p data-g9-solution-action>After the first switch, hold the left finger at 18 cm while the right finger slips inward and recompute the support ratio N_R/N_L=32/(r-50).</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>left support 18 cm</li><li>right support r</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> The support geometry has changed, so the normal reactions—and therefore both friction capacities—must be recalculated for the second stage rather than carried over.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> N_R/N_L=32/(r-50).</p></div></section><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="4" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2020-P1-Q13-MOVE-4" data-g9-solution-kind="VERIFY" data-g9-solution-stage="INTERPRET" data-g9-solution-crux="true"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">4</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Interpret</h4><p class="g9-prov" data-g9-crux-label>Key move</p><p data-g9-solution-action>At the second stick-slip transition use kinetic friction at the slipping right contact and limiting static friction at the sticking left contact: μ_kN_R=μ_sN_L; this gives r-50=25.6 cm and swaps which finger slips.</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>μ_s=0.40</li><li>μ_k=0.32</li><li>updated N_R/N_L</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> The friction law is contact-state specific: a sliding contact uses μ_kN while a sticking contact can rise only to μ_sN. Equality marks the instant the roles switch.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> x_R=25.6 cm.</p></div></section></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-answer-box" data-g9-component="ANSWER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="ANSWER_BOX"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="answer"><p>25.6 cm.</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-check-box" data-g9-component="CHECK" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CHECK_BOX"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="independent_check"><h4>Independent check</h4><p>The result is less than the initial 40 cm offset of the right finger and greater than zero, consistent with inward motion.</p></div></div></template></div></section></article><article id="g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P1-Q08" data-g9-unit="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P1-Q08" data-g9-kind="QUESTION" data-g9-role="CORE2" data-g9-search-text="Physics Laws of Motion Quantitative static and kinetic friction conditions Hard concept Multi-step reasoning Newton-law and observer-frame practice Multiple-correct multiple choice JEE (Advanced) 2014 · Paper 1 · Q8 Verified past-exam adaptation A uniform ladder of mass m leans against a vertical wall, making angle theta with the horizontal floor, and is on the verge of slipping. Let the wall and floor friction coefficients be mu1 and mu2, with normal reactions N1 at the wall and N2 at the floor. Identify the source-correct limiting-equilibrium statements. The ladder is in static limiting equilibrium. Friction acts at the impending-slip limits where present. theta is measured between the ladder and the horizontal floor. (A) mu1=0, mu2!=0 and N2 tan(theta)=mg/2 (B) mu1!=0, mu2=0 and N1 tan(theta)=mg/2 (C) mu1!=0, mu2!=0 and N2=mg/(1+mu1 mu2) (D) mu1=0, mu2!=0 and N1 tan(theta)=mg/2" class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-header" data-g9-component="IDENTITY" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="HEADER"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="source_identity"><h2>JEE (Advanced) · 2014 · Paper 1 · Q8</h2><p class="g9-prov">Official past paper, faithful restatement of the original</p></div><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE2" data-g9-meta-record="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P1-Q08" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-NLM-FIRST-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Laws of Motion"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Laws of Motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT" data-g9-meta-value="Quantitative static and kinetic friction conditions"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Quantitative static and kinetic friction conditions</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT" data-g9-meta-value="HARD"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Hard concept</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="question-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P1-Q08" data-g9-meta-value="D3"><strong>Question difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Multi-step reasoning</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="family" data-g9-meta-ref="FAM-PHY-NLM-PRACTICE" data-g9-meta-value="Newton-law and observer-frame practice"><strong>Family:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Newton-law and observer-frame practice</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="question-type" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P1-Q08" data-g9-meta-value="multi_correct_mcq"><strong>Question type:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Multiple-correct multiple choice</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="source" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P1-Q08" data-g9-meta-value="JEE (Advanced) 2014 · Paper 1 · Q8"><strong>Source:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>JEE (Advanced) 2014 · Paper 1 · Q8</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="provenance" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P1-Q08" data-g9-meta-value="PYQ_ADAPTED"><strong>Provenance:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Verified past-exam adaptation</span></span></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-link-list" data-g9-component="SOURCE_PDF" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="LINK_LIST"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="source_pdf"><a class="g9-pdf-link g9-source-pdf" data-g9-source-pdf href="https://jeeadv.ac.in/past_qps/2014_1.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" type="application/pdf" aria-label="Open the original past paper as a PDF to print it"><svg class="g9-pdf-icon" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="24" height="24"><path d="M6 2.5h8l4.5 4.5v14.5H6z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linejoin="round"/><path d="M14 2.5V7h4.5M9 12h6.5M9 15.5h6.5M9 19h4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg><span>Source paper (PDF)</span></a></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-grid" data-g9-component="DIFFICULTY_WHY" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_GRID"><button type="button" class="g9-why-toggle" data-g9-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="g9-why-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P1-Q08"><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-band" data-g9-band="D3">D3 · 7/10 · about 3 min</span><span>Why this difficulty?</span></button><div id="g9-why-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P1-Q08" class="g9-why" hidden><p>Requires force balance plus torque balance and careful handling of the smooth-wall limiting case.</p><div class=g9-dgrid><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Choosing the model</span><b>2/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Representation</span><b>2/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Reasoning chain</span><b>1/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Algebra and numbers</span><b>1/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Traps and exceptions</span><b>1/2</b></div></div></div></div></section><div class="g9-split"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-attempt" data-blueprint-slot="attempt" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-stem" data-g9-component="STEM" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEM"><div class="g9-eyebrow">Attempt first</div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="stem"><p>A uniform ladder of mass m leans against a vertical wall, making angle theta with the horizontal floor, and is on the verge of slipping. Let the wall and floor friction coefficients be mu1 and mu2, with normal reactions N1 at the wall and N2 at the floor. Identify the source-correct limiting-equilibrium statements.</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-callout-info" data-g9-component="CONDITIONS" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CALLOUT_INFO"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="conditions"><h4>Conditions</h4><ul><li>The ladder is in static limiting equilibrium.</li><li>Friction acts at the impending-slip limits where present.</li><li>theta is measured between the ladder and the horizontal floor.</li></ul></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-callout-warn" data-g9-component="TRAP" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CALLOUT_WARN"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core2-wrong-route"><summary>Common wrong route · open if you want a warning</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="common_wrong_route"><h4>Common wrong route</h4><p>Applying one friction formula mechanically without separating the general and smooth-wall cases.</p></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-attempt" data-g9-component="ATTEMPT" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="ATTEMPT"><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your answer<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div></div></section></div><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-representation" data-blueprint-slot="representation" data-required="false"></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-support" data-blueprint-slot="support" data-required="false"><div class="g9-component g9-c-ladder" data-g9-component="HINT_LADDER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="LADDER" data-g9-component-items="3"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core2-hints"><summary>Need a hint? · guided support</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-eyebrow">Hint ladder · reveal only what you need</div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="authored_core2_support"><h4>Guided support</h4><div class="g9-ladder" data-g9-ladder-ref="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P1-Q08-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-group="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL"><ol data-g9-ladder></ol><ol class="g9-rung-map" aria-label="Rungs still to come"><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H0</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Represent</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H1</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Connect</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H2</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Carry out</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li></ol><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P1-Q08-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-1"><li data-g9-rung="1" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[0]" data-g9-support-reveals="CONCEPT" data-g9-support-kind="REPRESENT" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P1-Q08-MOVE-1"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H0</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Represent</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><div data-g9-support-reveal><p>Draw all four possible contact forces and mark their limiting directions.</p></div></li></template><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P1-Q08-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-2"><li data-g9-rung="2" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[1]" data-g9-support-reveals="METHOD" data-g9-support-kind="CONNECT" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P1-Q08-MOVE-2"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H1</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Connect</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><div data-g9-support-reveal><p>Use horizontal and vertical force balance for the two-friction case.</p></div></li></template><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P1-Q08-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-3"><li data-g9-rung="3" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[2]" data-g9-support-reveals="METHOD" data-g9-support-kind="EXECUTE" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P1-Q08-MOVE-3"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H2</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Carry out</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><div data-g9-support-reveal><p>For the smooth-wall case, take moments about the foot to eliminate floor forces.</p></div></li></template><button type="button" data-g9-next-rung>Show guided support</button></div></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-link-list" data-g9-component="CONCEPT_NAV" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="LINK_LIST"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="concept_navigation"><h4>Need the concept again?</h4><ul><li><a data-g9-concept-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P1-Q08" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT" href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT">Quantitative static and kinetic friction conditions</a></li><li><a data-g9-concept-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P1-Q08" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW" href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW">Net force, not any one force, sets acceleration</a></li></ul></div></div></section></div></div><section class="blueprint-slot slot-solution" data-blueprint-slot="solution" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure" data-g9-component="SOLUTION" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE"><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P1-Q08-solution"><summary>Answer and working</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P1-Q08-solution"><div class="g9-component g9-c-step-list" data-g9-component="SOLUTION_STEPS" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEP_LIST" data-g9-component-items="3"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="structured_working"><h4>Reasoning route</h4><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="1" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P1-Q08-MOVE-1" data-g9-solution-kind="REPRESENT" data-g9-solution-stage="REPRESENT" data-g9-solution-crux="false"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">1</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Represent</h4><p data-g9-solution-action>With friction at both contacts, horizontal balance gives N1=mu2 N2 and vertical balance gives N2+mu1 N1=mg.</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>stated source quantities</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> This follows from the stated model, conservation law, or stoichiometric relation preserved in the source-matched task.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> intermediate result 1</p></div></section><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="2" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P1-Q08-MOVE-2" data-g9-solution-kind="CONNECT" data-g9-solution-stage="CONNECT" data-g9-solution-crux="true"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">2</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Connect</h4><p class="g9-prov" data-g9-crux-label>Key move</p><p data-g9-solution-action>Eliminating N1 yields N2=mg/(1+mu1 mu2), establishing option (C).</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>stated source quantities</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> This follows from the stated model, conservation law, or stoichiometric relation preserved in the source-matched task.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> intermediate result 2</p></div></section><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="3" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P1-Q08-MOVE-3" data-g9-solution-kind="TRANSFORM" data-g9-solution-stage="CALCULATE" data-g9-solution-crux="false"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">3</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Calculate</h4><p data-g9-solution-action>If the wall is smooth (mu1=0), taking moments about the foot gives N1 L sin(theta)=mg(L/2)cos(theta), hence N1 tan(theta)=mg/2, establishing option (D).</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>stated source quantities</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> This follows from the stated model, conservation law, or stoichiometric relation preserved in the source-matched task.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> Options (C) and (D).</p></div></section></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-answer-box" data-g9-component="ANSWER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="ANSWER_BOX"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="answer"><p>Options (C) and (D).</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-check-box" data-g9-component="CHECK" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CHECK_BOX"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="independent_check"><h4>Independent check</h4><p>The smooth-wall special case follows independently from torque balance and does not require a wall friction force.</p></div></div></template></div></section></article><article id="g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-NEET-2020-E1-Q160" data-g9-unit="PYQ-PHY-NEET-2020-E1-Q160" data-g9-kind="QUESTION" data-g9-role="CORE2" data-g9-search-text="Physics Laws of Motion Net force, not any one force, sets acceleration Medium concept Connected reasoning Newton-law and observer-frame practice Single-correct multiple choice NEET (UG) 2020 · English Set E1 · Q160 Verified past-exam adaptation Two masses, 4 kg and 6 kg, are connected by a massless string over a frictionless pulley. Find the magnitude of the system acceleration in terms of g. String is massless. Pulley is frictionless. (1) g (2) g/2 (3) g/5 (4) g/10" class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-header" data-g9-component="IDENTITY" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="HEADER"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="source_identity"><h2>NEET (UG) · 2020 · English Set E1 · Q160</h2><p class="g9-prov">Official past paper, faithful restatement of the original</p></div><div data-g9