ry="core2-wrong-route"><summary>Common wrong route · open if you want a warning</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="common_wrong_route"><h4>Common wrong route</h4><p>Assuming halving the time only doubles acceleration, or omitting the normal-force factor in kinetic friction.</p></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-attempt" data-g9-component="ATTEMPT" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="ATTEMPT"><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your answer<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div></div></section></div><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-representation" data-blueprint-slot="representation" data-required="false"><div class="g9-component g9-c-visual-card" data-g9-component="REPRESENTATION" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="VISUAL_CARD" data-g9-component-items="1"><div class="g9-card-head"><strong>Representation</strong></div><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="PYQ-PHY-JEEMAIN-2026-06APR-S2-Q46-REP-PYQ-JEEMAIN-2026-Q46-FRICTION" data-g9-stage="PRE_ATTEMPT" data-g9-representation="REP-PYQ-JEEMAIN-2026-Q46-FRICTION" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="1" data-g9-stages-total="1" data-g9-stages=""><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 440 255" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEMAIN-2026-06APR-S2-Q46-REP-PYQ-JEEMAIN-2026-Q46-FRICTION-1--jm26q46-title g9fig-CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEMAIN-2026-06APR-S2-Q46-REP-PYQ-JEEMAIN-2026-Q46-FRICTION-1--jm26q46-desc">\n<title id="g9fig-CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEMAIN-2026-06APR-S2-Q46-REP-PYQ-JEEMAIN-2026-Q46-FRICTION-1--jm26q46-title">Same 45 degree incline in rough and smooth trials</title><desc id="g9fig-CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEMAIN-2026-06APR-S2-Q46-REP-PYQ-JEEMAIN-2026-Q46-FRICTION-1--jm26q46-desc">A block starts from rest and traverses the same distance down the same 45 degree incline. The rough trial takes t and the frictionless trial takes t over 2. No coefficient is evaluated.</desc>\n<defs><marker id="g9fig-CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEMAIN-2026-06APR-S2-Q46-REP-PYQ-JEEMAIN-2026-Q46-FRICTION-1--jm26q46-a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker></defs>\n<g font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="14" fill="currentColor" stroke="currentColor"><text x="16" y="24" stroke="none" font-weight="700">same 45° incline · same distance</text><text x="16" y="44" stroke="none">starts from rest</text><text x="40" y="54" stroke="none" >rough trial: time t</text><line x1="28" y1="84" x2="202" y2="184" stroke-width="2" /><rect x="96" y="112" width="54" height="40" fill="none" stroke-width="2" transform="rotate(30 123 132)"/><text x="32" y="205" stroke="none" >rough contact</text><text x="254" y="54" stroke="none" >smooth trial: time t/2</text><line x1="238" y1="84" x2="414" y2="184" stroke-width="2" /><rect x="306" y="112" width="54" height="40" fill="none" stroke-width="2" transform="rotate(30 333 132)"/><text x="258" y="205" stroke="none" >frictionless</text><text x="16" y="238" stroke="none" >incline angle = 45°</text></g></svg>\n<figcaption data-g9-block="stage_caption" data-g9-caption="stages"></figcaption></figure></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-support" data-blueprint-slot="support" data-required="false"><div class="g9-component g9-c-ladder" data-g9-component="HINT_LADDER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="LADDER" data-g9-component-items="5"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core2-hints"><summary>Need a hint? · guided support</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-eyebrow">Hint ladder · reveal only what you need</div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="authored_core2_support"><h4>Guided support</h4><div class="g9-ladder" data-g9-ladder-ref="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEMAIN-2026-06APR-S2-Q46-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-group="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL"><ol data-g9-ladder></ol><ol class="g9-rung-map" aria-label="Rungs still to come"><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H0</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Representation</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H1</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Key concept</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H2</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Crux</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H3</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Equation / formal model</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H4</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Assembly checkpoint</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li></ol><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEMAIN-2026-06APR-S2-Q46-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-1"><li data-g9-rung="1" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[0]" data-g9-support-reveals="CONCEPT" data-g9-support-stage="REPRESENTATION" data-g9-support-kind="REPRESENT" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEMAIN-2026-06APR-S2-Q46-MOVE-1"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H0</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Representation</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><p data-g9-support-prompt>What are N and the friction direction in the rough sliding trial?</p><details data-g9-support-reveal><summary>Reveal support</summary><p>Draw one incline twice—rough and smooth—and take downhill as positive.</p></details></li></template><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEMAIN-2026-06APR-S2-Q46-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-2"><li data-g9-rung="2" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[1]" data-g9-support-reveals="CONCEPT" data-g9-support-stage="KEY_CONCEPT" data-g9-support-kind="CONNECT" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEMAIN-2026-06APR-S2-Q46-MOVE-2"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H1</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Key concept</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><p data-g9-support-prompt>Which kinematic relation compares their accelerations without needing the distance?</p><details data-g9-support-reveal><summary>Reveal support</summary><p>The two trials cover the same distance from rest.</p></details></li></template><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEMAIN-2026-06APR-S2-Q46-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-3"><li data-g9-rung="3" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[2]" data-g9-support-reveals="METHOD" data-g9-support-stage="CRUX" data-g9-support-kind="CONNECT" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEMAIN-2026-06APR-S2-Q46-MOVE-3"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H2</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Crux</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><p data-g9-support-prompt>If t becomes t/2 in s=(1/2)at², by what factor must a change?</p><details data-g9-support-reveal><summary>Reveal support</summary><p>Do not treat time as linear in acceleration for fixed distance.</p></details></li></template><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEMAIN-2026-06APR-S2-Q46-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-4"><li data-g9-rung="4" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[3]" data-g9-support-reveals="METHOD" data-g9-support-stage="FORMAL_MODEL" data-g9-support-kind="EXECUTE" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEMAIN-2026-06APR-S2-Q46-MOVE-4"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H3</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Equation / formal model</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><p data-g9-support-prompt>What equation in μ follows from a_s=4a_r?</p><details data-g9-support-reveal><summary>Reveal support</summary><p>At 45°, sinθ=cosθ, so the rough acceleration factors cleanly.</p></details></li></template><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEMAIN-2026-06APR-S2-Q46-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-5"><li data-g9-rung="5" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[4]" data-g9-support-reveals="METHOD" data-g9-support-stage="CHECKPOINT" data-g9-support-kind="EXECUTE" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEMAIN-2026-06APR-S2-Q46-MOVE-4"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H4</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Assembly checkpoint</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><p data-g9-support-prompt>Does a_r=a_s/4 imply t_r=2t_s for the same distance?</p><details data-g9-support-reveal><summary>Reveal support</summary><p>Check the time ratio from your μ rather than only the algebra.</p></details></li></template><button type="button" data-g9-next-rung>Show guided support</button></div></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-link-list" data-g9-component="CONCEPT_NAV" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="LINK_LIST"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="concept_navigation"><h4>Need the concept again?</h4><ul><li><a data-g9-concept-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEMAIN-2026-06APR-S2-Q46" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT" href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION-QUANT">Quantitative static and kinetic friction conditions</a></li><li><a data-g9-concept-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEMAIN-2026-06APR-S2-Q46" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW" href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW">Net force, not any one force, sets acceleration</a></li></ul></div></div></section></div></div><section class="blueprint-slot slot-solution" data-blueprint-slot="solution" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure" data-g9-component="SOLUTION" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE"><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEMAIN-2026-06APR-S2-Q46-solution"><summary>Answer and working</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEMAIN-2026-06APR-S2-Q46-solution"><div class="g9-component g9-c-step-list" data-g9-component="SOLUTION_STEPS" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEP_LIST" data-g9-component-items="4"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="structured_working"><h4>Reasoning route</h4><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="1" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEMAIN-2026-06APR-S2-Q46-MOVE-1" data-g9-solution-kind="REPRESENT" data-g9-solution-stage="REPRESENT" data-g9-solution-crux="false"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">1</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Represent</h4><p data-g9-solution-action>Write the downhill accelerations for the same 45° plane: a_r=g(sin45°-μcos45°) and a_s=g sin45°.</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>45° incline</li><li>rough and smooth trials</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> On the rough run the block slides, so kinetic friction μN acts upslope with N=mgcos45°; the smooth run has no friction.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> a_r=g(1-μ)/√2; a_s=g/√2.</p></div></section><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="2" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEMAIN-2026-06APR-S2-Q46-MOVE-2" data-g9-solution-kind="CONNECT" data-g9-solution-stage="CONNECT" data-g9-solution-crux="false"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">2</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Connect</h4><p data-g9-solution-action>For equal distance from rest, use s=(1/2)at² to compare the two runs.</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>same distance</li><li>start from rest</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> Both runs start from rest and cover the same path length, so the product at² is the same.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> a_rt²=a_s(t/2)².</p></div></section><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="3" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEMAIN-2026-06APR-S2-Q46-MOVE-3" data-g9-solution-kind="CONNECT" data-g9-solution-stage="CONNECT" data-g9-solution-crux="true"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">3</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Connect</h4><p class="g9-prov" data-g9-crux-label>Key move</p><p data-g9-solution-action>Because the smooth time is t/2, its acceleration is four times the rough acceleration, not twice.</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>time ratio 2:1</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> Time enters the displacement relation as t², so halving time multiplies the required constant acceleration by four.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> a_s=4a_r.</p></div></section><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="4" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEMAIN-2026-06APR-S2-Q46-MOVE-4" data-g9-solution-kind="VERIFY" data-g9-solution-stage="INTERPRET" data-g9-solution-crux="false"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">4</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Interpret</h4><p data-g9-solution-action>Substitute the acceleration expressions: 1=4(1-μ), giving μ=3/4 and α=75.</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>a_s=4a_r</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> The common factor g/√2 cancels. With μ=0.75 the rough acceleration is exactly one quarter of the smooth value, which restores the stated time ratio.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> α=75.</p></div></section></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-answer-box" data-g9-component="ANSWER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="ANSWER_BOX"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="answer"><p>75.</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-check-box" data-g9-component="CHECK" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CHECK_BOX"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="independent_check"><h4>Independent check</h4><p>With mu=0.75, the rough acceleration is one quarter of the smooth acceleration, so its travel time is twice as large, exactly as stated.</p></div></div></template></div></section></article></section><section id="g9-role-CORE2A" data-g9-role-section="CORE2A"><h1>Force and laws of motion: Supported practice</h1><article id="g9-CORE2A--Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-05" data-g9-unit="Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-05" data-g9-kind="QUESTION" data-g9-role="CORE2A" data-g9-search-text="Physics Laws of Motion Net force, not any one force, sets acceleration Medium concept Connected reasoning Newton-law and observer-frame practice Constructed response Authored practice A 2.5 kg trolley on a level track is pulled forward with 9 N while a 4 N drag acts backward. Take forward as positive. Find its acceleration. Then the pull is removed while the trolley is still moving forward: find its acceleration now. The drag stays 4 N backward while the trolley moves forward. Forward is the positive direction." class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="provenance"><p class="g9-prov">AUTHORED practice</p></div><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE2A" data-g9-meta-record="Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-05" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-NLM-FIRST-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Laws of Motion"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Laws of Motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Net force, not any one force, sets acceleration"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Net force, not any one force, sets acceleration</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-SECOND-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="question-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-05" data-g9-meta-value="D2"><strong>Question difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Connected reasoning</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="family" data-g9-meta-ref="FAM-PHY-NLM-PRACTICE" data-g9-meta-value="Newton-law and observer-frame practice"><strong>Family:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Newton-law and observer-frame practice</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="question-type" data-g9-meta-ref="Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-05" data-g9-meta-value="constructed_response"><strong>Question type:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Constructed response</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="provenance" data-g9-meta-ref="Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-05" data-g9-meta-value="AUTHORED"><strong>Provenance:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Authored practice</span></span></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="family_identity"><p>Newton-law and observer-frame practice</p></div></section><div class="g9-split"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-attempt" data-blueprint-slot="attempt" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="stem"><h2>A 2.5 kg trolley on a level track is pulled forward with 9 N while a 4 N drag acts backward. Take forward as positive. Find its acceleration. Then the pull is removed while the trolley is still moving forward: find its acceleration now.</h2></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="conditions"><h4>Conditions</h4><ul><li>The drag stays 4 N backward while the trolley moves forward.</li><li>Forward is the positive direction.</li></ul></div><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-05-REP-NLM-TROLLEY-9N-4N" data-g9-stage="PRE_ATTEMPT" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-TROLLEY-9N-4N" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="1" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-TROLLEY-V0" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg role="img" aria-label="The trolley" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 820 260">\n  \n  \n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-05-REP-NLM-TROLLEY-9N-4N-1--rep-nlm-trolley-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-05-REP-NLM-TROLLEY-9N-4N-1--rep-nlm-trolley-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-TROLLEY-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">A 2.5 kg trolley on a level track</text>\n    <line x1="20" y1="200" x2="420" y2="200" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="30" y1="200" x2="22" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="48" y1="200" x2="40" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="66" y1="200" x2="58" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="84" y1="200" x2="76" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="102" y1="200" x2="94" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="120" y1="200" x2="112" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="138" y1="200" x2="130" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="156" y1="200" x2="148" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="174" y1="200" x2="166" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="192" y1="200" x2="184" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="210" y1="200" x2="202" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="228" y1="200" x2="220" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="246" y1="200" x2="238" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="264" y1="200" x2="256" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="282" y1="200" x2="274" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="200" x2="292" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="318" y1="200" x2="310" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="336" y1="200" x2="328" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="354" y1="200" x2="346" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="372" y1="200" x2="364" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="390" y1="200" x2="382" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="408" y1="200" x2="400" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <rect x="150" y="132" width="110" height="50" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="205" y="162" text-anchor="middle">2.5 kg</text>\n    <circle cx="172" cy="191" r="9" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="238" cy="191" r="9" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="262" y1="157" x2="332" y2="157" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-05-REP-NLM-TROLLEY-9N-4N-1--rep-nlm-trolley-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="268" y="147">pull 9 N</text>\n    <line x1="148" y1="157" x2="88" y2="157" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-05-REP-NLM-TROLLEY-9N-4N-1--rep-nlm-trolley-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="142" y="147" text-anchor="end">drag 4 N</text>\n    <text x="20" y="240">forward (right) positive; drag stays 4 N back while moving forward</text>\n  </g>\n  \n  \n</svg>\n<figcaption data-g9-block="stage_caption" data-g9-caption="stages">The trolley</figcaption></figure><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your attempt<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-reasoning" data-blueprint-slot="reasoning" data-required="true"><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-05-reasoning"><summary>Reasoning route and full solution</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-05-reasoning"><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-05-bound-REP-NLM-TROLLEY-9N-4N" data-g9-stage="POST_ATTEMPT" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-TROLLEY-9N-4N" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="3" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-TROLLEY-V0 VIS-NLM-TROLLEY-V1 VIS-NLM-TROLLEY-V2" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 820 260" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-05-bound-REP-NLM-TROLLEY-9N-4N-1--rep-nlm-trolley-title g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-05-bound-REP-NLM-TROLLEY-9N-4N-1--rep-nlm-trolley-desc">\n  <title id="g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-05-bound-REP-NLM-TROLLEY-9N-4N-1--rep-nlm-trolley-title">Trolley with a 9 N pull and 4 N drag, then the pull removed</title>\n  <desc id="g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-05-bound-REP-NLM-TROLLEY-9N-4N-1--rep-nlm-trolley-desc">Stage 1: A 2.5 kg trolley on a level track is pulled forward with 9 N while a 4 N drag acts backward; forward is positive. Stage 2: With the pull, F_net = 9 − 4 = +5 N and a = +2 m/s². Stage 3: With the pull removed and the trolley still moving forward, F_net = −4 N and a = −1.6 m/s², backward while it moves forward.</desc>\n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-05-bound-REP-NLM-TROLLEY-9N-4N-1--rep-nlm-trolley-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-05-bound-REP-NLM-TROLLEY-9N-4N-1--rep-nlm-trolley-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-TROLLEY-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">A 2.5 kg trolley on a level track</text>\n    <line x1="20" y1="200" x2="420" y2="200" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="30" y1="200" x2="22" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="48" y1="200" x2="40" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="66" y1="200" x2="58" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="84" y1="200" x2="76" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="102" y1="200" x2="94" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="120" y1="200" x2="112" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="138" y1="200" x2="130" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="156" y1="200" x2="148" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="174" y1="200" x2="166" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="192" y1="200" x2="184" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="210" y1="200" x2="202" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="228" y1="200" x2="220" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="246" y1="200" x2="238" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="264" y1="200" x2="256" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="282" y1="200" x2="274" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="300" y1="200" x2="292" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="318" y1="200" x2="310" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="336" y1="200" x2="328" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="354" y1="200" x2="346" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="372" y1="200" x2="364" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="390" y1="200" x2="382" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <line x1="408" y1="200" x2="400" y2="208" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>\n    <rect x="150" y="132" width="110" height="50" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="205" y="162" text-anchor="middle">2.5 kg</text>\n    <circle cx="172" cy="191" r="9" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <circle cx="238" cy="191" r="9" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="262" y1="157" x2="332" y2="157" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-05-bound-REP-NLM-TROLLEY-9N-4N-1--rep-nlm-trolley-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="268" y="147">pull 9 N</text>\n    <line x1="148" y1="157" x2="88" y2="157" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-05-bound-REP-NLM-TROLLEY-9N-4N-1--rep-nlm-trolley-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="142" y="147" text-anchor="end">drag 4 N</text>\n    <text x="20" y="240">forward (right) positive; drag stays 4 N back while moving forward</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-TROLLEY-V1" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="460" y="60" font-weight="600">with the pull:</text>\n    <text x="460" y="82">F_net = 9 − 4 = +5 N</text>\n    <text x="460" y="104" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">a = 5/2.5 = +2 m/s²</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-TROLLEY-V2" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="460" y="150" font-weight="600">pull removed, still moving forward:</text>\n    <text x="460" y="172">F_net = −4 N</text>\n    <text x="460" y="194" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">a = −4/2.5 = −1.6 m/s²</text>\n    <text x="460" y="216">backward a while moving forward</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n<div class="g9-stage-controls"><div class="g9-stage-chips" role="group" aria-label="Stages"><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="0" data-g9-stage-desc="Show the trolley with its pull and drag.">1: The trolley</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="1" data-g9-stage-desc="Sum the signed forces and divide by the mass.">2: With the pull</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="2" data-g9-stage-desc="Recompute with only the drag while the trolley still moves forward.">3: Pull removed</button></div><p class="g9-stage-desc" data-g9-stage-desc-text></p><div class="g9-stage-stepper"><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="prev">Previous stage</button><span data-g9-stage-label></span><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="next">Next stage</button></div></div><figcaption data-g9-block="representation_bridge" data-g9-caption="purpose">Show the trolley with its 9 N pull and 4 N drag so the signed net force, not the motion, sets the acceleration.</figcaption></figure><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution"><ol><li>With the pull: F_net = +9 − 4 = +5 N, so a = 5/2.5 = +2 m/s².</li><li>Without the pull: F_net = −4 N, so a = −4/2.5 = −1.6 m/s².</li><li>The trolley is still moving forward, but its acceleration now points backward, so it slows down.</li></ol></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="answer"><h4>Answer</h4><p>With the pull: a = +2 m/s². After the pull is removed: a = −1.6 m/s², backward while the trolley still moves forward.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="independent_check"><h4>Independent check</h4><p>Multiply back: 2.5 × 2 = 5 N = 9 − 4, and 2.5 × (−1.6) = −4 N, the drag alone.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="failure_signal"><h4>If you went wrong</h4><p>You got 3.6 m/s² (9 N alone) or 5.2 m/s² (9 + 4): the drag opposes the pull, so F_net = 9 − 4 = 5 N. Or you said the second acceleration is zero or forward because the trolley still moves forward: it points along the net force, which is now backward.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exposure_closure"><h4>What this establishes</h4><p>Established: acceleration is the signed net force divided by the mass, and it can point against the velocity. The transfer task sets up the same law on an incline with two different choices of axes.</p></div></template></section></div><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-support" data-blueprint-slot="support" data-required="false"><div class="g9-ladder" data-g9-ladder-ref="CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-05-authored"><ol data-g9-ladder><li data-g9-rung="1">Which forces act on the trolley along the track, and which way does each one point?</li></ol><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-05-authored-2"><li data-g9-rung="2">Draw the trolley as a dot on a forward +x axis, with a 9 N arrow forward and a 4 N arrow backward; cross out the 9 N arrow for the second part.</li></template><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-05-authored-3"><li data-g9-rung="3">Add the signed forces first, then divide the sum by the mass; after the pull is removed, only the drag is left in the sum.</li></template><button type="button" data-g9-next-rung>Next hint</button></div></section></div></div></article><article id="g9-CORE2A--Q-PHY-NLM-2A-01" data-g9-unit="Q-PHY-NLM-2A-01" data-g9-kind="QUESTION" data-g9-role="CORE2A" data-g9-search-text="Physics Laws of Motion Zero net force allows steady motion Medium concept Connected reasoning Newton-law and observer-frame practice Constructed response Authored practice An 80 kg skydiver falls at a steady 50 m/s. Take g = 10 m/s². Find the air resistance on the skydiver and the net force. Just after the parachute opens, the skydiver is still moving downward but slowing: is the net force then zero, upward or downward? The steady fall is in a straight vertical line. g = 10 m/s²." class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="provenance"><p class="g9-prov">AUTHORED practice</p></div><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE2A" data-g9-meta-record="Q-PHY-NLM-2A-01" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-NLM-FIRST-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Laws of Motion"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Laws of Motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION" data-g9-meta-value="Zero net force allows steady motion"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Zero net force allows steady motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-NET-ZERO-MOTION" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="question-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="Q-PHY-NLM-2A-01" data-g9-meta-value="D2"><strong>Question difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Connected reasoning</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="family" data-g9-meta-ref="FAM-PHY-NLM-PRACTICE" data-g9-meta-value="Newton-law and observer-frame practice"><strong>Family:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Newton-law and observer-frame practice</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="question-type" data-g9-meta-ref="Q-PHY-NLM-2A-01" data-g9-meta-value="constructed_response"><strong>Question type:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Constructed response</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="provenance" data-g9-meta-ref="Q-PHY-NLM-2A-01" data-g9-meta-value="AUTHORED"><strong>Provenance:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Authored practice</span></span></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="family_identity"><p>Newton-law and observer-frame practice</p></div></section><div class="g9-split"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-attempt" data-blueprint-slot="attempt" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="stem"><h2>An 80 kg skydiver falls at a steady 50 m/s. Take g = 10 m/s². Find the air resistance on the skydiver and the net force. Just after the parachute opens, the skydiver is still moving downward but slowing: is the net force then zero, upward or downward?</h2></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="conditions"><h4>Conditions</h4><ul><li>The steady fall is in a straight vertical line.</li><li>g = 10 m/s².</li></ul></div><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="Q-PHY-NLM-2A-01-REP-NLM-SKYDIVER-80KG" data-g9-stage="PRE_ATTEMPT" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-SKYDIVER-80KG" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="1" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-SKYDIVER-V0" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg role="img" aria-label="The fall" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 820 300">\n  \n  \n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-01-REP-NLM-SKYDIVER-80KG-1--rep-nlm-skydiver-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-01-REP-NLM-SKYDIVER-80KG-1--rep-nlm-skydiver-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-SKYDIVER-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">An 80 kg skydiver falling at a steady 50 m/s</text>\n    <circle cx="160" cy="110.8" r="10.8" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="160" y1="121.6" x2="160" y2="154" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="160" y1="154" x2="146.5" y2="190" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="160" y1="154" x2="173.5" y2="190" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="142" y1="134.2" x2="178" y2="134.2" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="220" y1="110" x2="220" y2="170" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-01-REP-NLM-SKYDIVER-80KG-1--rep-nlm-skydiver-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="228" y="150">v = 50 m/s (steady)</text>\n    <text x="20" y="290">straight vertical fall; g = 10 m/s²</text>\n  </g>\n  \n  \n</svg>\n<figcaption data-g9-block="stage_caption" data-g9-caption="stages">The fall</figcaption></figure><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your attempt<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-reasoning" data-blueprint-slot="reasoning" data-required="true"><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-01-reasoning"><summary>Reasoning route and full solution</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-01-reasoning"><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="Q-PHY-NLM-2A-01-bound-REP-NLM-SKYDIVER-80KG" data-g9-stage="POST_ATTEMPT" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-SKYDIVER-80KG" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="3" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-SKYDIVER-V0 VIS-NLM-SKYDIVER-V1 VIS-NLM-SKYDIVER-V2" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 820 300" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-01-bound-REP-NLM-SKYDIVER-80KG-1--rep-nlm-skydiver-title g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-01-bound-REP-NLM-SKYDIVER-80KG-1--rep-nlm-skydiver-desc">\n  <title id="g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-01-bound-REP-NLM-SKYDIVER-80KG-1--rep-nlm-skydiver-title">Skydiver at a steady 50 m/s, then just after the parachute opens</title>\n  <desc id="g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-01-bound-REP-NLM-SKYDIVER-80KG-1--rep-nlm-skydiver-desc">Stage 1: An 80 kg skydiver falls in a straight vertical line at a steady 50 m/s; g = 10 m/s². Stage 2: At steady speed the net force is zero, so the air resistance is 800 N upward, equal to the 800 N weight. Stage 3: Just after the parachute opens the skydiver still moves down but slows, so the net force points upward.</desc>\n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-01-bound-REP-NLM-SKYDIVER-80KG-1--rep-nlm-skydiver-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-01-bound-REP-NLM-SKYDIVER-80KG-1--rep-nlm-skydiver-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-SKYDIVER-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">An 80 kg skydiver falling at a steady 50 m/s</text>\n    <circle cx="160" cy="110.8" r="10.8" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="160" y1="121.6" x2="160" y2="154" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="160" y1="154" x2="146.5" y2="190" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="160" y1="154" x2="173.5" y2="190" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="142" y1="134.2" x2="178" y2="134.2" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="220" y1="110" x2="220" y2="170" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-01-bound-REP-NLM-SKYDIVER-80KG-1--rep-nlm-skydiver-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="228" y="150">v = 50 m/s (steady)</text>\n    <text x="20" y="290">straight vertical fall; g = 10 m/s²</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-SKYDIVER-V1" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <line x1="150" y1="196" x2="150" y2="236" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-01-bound-REP-NLM-SKYDIVER-80KG-1--rep-nlm-skydiver-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="158" y="240">Earth on skydiver: 800 N</text>\n    <line x1="170" y1="96" x2="170" y2="56" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-01-bound-REP-NLM-SKYDIVER-80KG-1--rep-nlm-skydiver-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="178" y="70">air on skydiver: 800 N</text>\n    <text x="470" y="60" font-weight="600">steady fall: velocity constant</text>\n    <text x="470" y="82">so F_net = 0</text>\n    <text x="470" y="104" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">air resistance = 800 N up</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-SKYDIVER-V2" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="470" y="150" font-weight="600">parachute opens:</text>\n    <text x="470" y="172">still moving down, but slowing</text>\n    <text x="470" y="194">velocity change is upward</text>\n    <text x="470" y="216" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">net force: upward</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n<div class="g9-stage-controls"><div class="g9-stage-chips" role="group" aria-label="Stages"><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="0" data-g9-stage-desc="Show the skydiver falling at steady speed.">1: The fall</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="1" data-g9-stage-desc="Balance the forces for constant velocity.">2: Steady fall</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="2" data-g9-stage-desc="Read the net force from the change of velocity.">3: Parachute open</button></div><p class="g9-stage-desc" data-g9-stage-desc-text></p><div class="g9-stage-stepper"><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="prev">Previous stage</button><span data-g9-stage-label></span><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="next">Next stage</button></div></div><figcaption data-g9-block="representation_bridge" data-g9-caption="purpose">Show the skydiver in steady fall and just after the parachute opens, so the net force follows the change of velocity.</figcaption></figure><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution"><ol><li>A steady 50 m/s means the velocity is not changing, so the net force is zero.</li><li>Weight = 80 × 10 = 800 N down, so the air resistance must be 800 N up.</li><li>After the parachute opens the downward velocity is shrinking, so the acceleration, and with it the net force, points upward.</li></ol></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="answer"><h4>Answer</h4><p>In the steady fall the air resistance is 800 N upward and the net force is zero. Just after the parachute opens the net force points upward, even though the skydiver is still moving down.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="independent_check"><h4>Independent check</h4><p>The size of the velocity plays no part: the same zero net force would hold at a steady 5 m/s or at rest. Only a change of velocity needs a net force.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="failure_signal"><h4>If you went wrong</h4><p>You said the steady fall needs a net downward force because the skydiver is moving down, or that the net force stays downward after the parachute opens: the net force follows the change of velocity, not the velocity.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exposure_closure"><h4>What this establishes</h4><p>Established: zero net force means an unchanging velocity, and a slowing motion has a net force opposite the velocity. Later tasks read steady straight motion as a balanced-force state.</p></div></template></section></div><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-support" data-blueprint-slot="support" data-required="false"><div class="g9-ladder" data-g9-ladder-ref="CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-01-authored"><ol data-g9-ladder><li data-g9-rung="1">During the steady fall, is the skydiver&#x27;s velocity changing?</li></ol><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-01-authored-2"><li data-g9-rung="2">Draw the skydiver twice, in the steady fall and just after the parachute opens, each with a velocity arrow and a separate net-force arrow. Leave the net-force arrow empty where the velocity is not changing.</li></template><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-01-authored-3"><li data-g9-rung="3">Zero net force goes with an unchanging velocity, however large; a slowing fall is a change of velocity, so ask which way that change points.</li></template><button type="button" data-g9-next-rung>Next hint</button></div></section></div></div></article><article id="g9-CORE2A--Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-02" data-g9-unit="Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-02" data-g9-kind="QUESTION" data-g9-role="CORE2A" data-g9-search-text="Physics Laws of Motion Nonzero forces can cancel Medium concept Connected reasoning Newton-law and observer-frame practice Constructed response Authored practice A 2 kg book lies at rest on a table while a hand presses straight down on it with 5 N. Take g = 10 m/s². List every force on the book with its agent and size, find the table&#x27;s push, and state the net force and what it predicts about the book&#x27;s velocity. The book stays at rest. g = 10 m/s²; take up as positive." class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="provenance"><p class="g9-prov">AUTHORED practice</p></div><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE2A" data-g9-meta-record="Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-02" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-NLM-FIRST-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Laws of Motion"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Laws of Motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO" data-g9-meta-value="Nonzero forces can cancel"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Nonzero forces can cancel</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FORCES-SUM-ZERO" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="question-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-02" data-g9-meta-value="D2"><strong>Question difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Connected reasoning</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="family" data-g9-meta-ref="FAM-PHY-NLM-PRACTICE" data-g9-meta-value="Newton-law and observer-frame practice"><strong>Family:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Newton-law and observer-frame practice</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="question-type" data-g9-meta-ref="Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-02" data-g9-meta-value="constructed_response"><strong>Question type:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Constructed response</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="provenance" data-g9-meta-ref="Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-02" data-g9-meta-value="AUTHORED"><strong>Provenance:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Authored practice</span></span></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="family_identity"><p>Newton-law and observer-frame practice</p></div></section><div class="g9-split"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-attempt" data-blueprint-slot="attempt" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="stem"><h2>A 2 kg book lies at rest on a table while a hand presses straight down on it with 5 N. Take g = 10 m/s². List every force on the book with its agent and size, find the table&#x27;s push, and state the net force and what it predicts about the book&#x27;s velocity.</h2></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="conditions"><h4>Conditions</h4><ul><li>The book stays at rest.</li><li>g = 10 m/s²; take up as positive.</li></ul></div><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-02-REP-NLM-BOOK-HAND-PRESS" data-g9-stage="PRE_ATTEMPT" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-BOOK-HAND-PRESS" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="1" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-BOOKHAND-V0" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg role="img" aria-label="The book" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 820 300">\n  \n  \n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-02-REP-NLM-BOOK-HAND-PRESS-1--rep-nlm-bookhand-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-02-REP-NLM-BOOK-HAND-PRESS-1--rep-nlm-bookhand-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-BOOKHAND-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">A 2 kg book at rest; a hand presses down with 5 N</text>\n    <line x1="60" y1="190" x2="360" y2="190" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="80" y1="190" x2="80" y2="260" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="3"/>\n    <line x1="340" y1="190" x2="340" y2="260" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="3"/>\n    <rect x="160" y="160" width="100" height="30" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="210" y="180" text-anchor="middle">book 2 kg</text>\n    <path d="M235 100 q10 -20 30 0 v30 h-30 z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="275" y="110">hand, 5 N down</text>\n    <text x="20" y="290">g = 10 m/s²; up positive</text>\n  </g>\n  \n  \n</svg>\n<figcaption data-g9-block="stage_caption" data-g9-caption="stages">The book</figcaption></figure><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your attempt<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-reasoning" data-blueprint-slot="reasoning" data-required="true"><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-02-reasoning"><summary>Reasoning route and full solution</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-02-reasoning"><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-02-bound-REP-NLM-BOOK-HAND-PRESS" data-g9-stage="POST_ATTEMPT" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-BOOK-HAND-PRESS" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="3" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-BOOKHAND-V0 VIS-NLM-BOOKHAND-V1 VIS-NLM-BOOKHAND-V2" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 820 300" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-02-bound-REP-NLM-BOOK-HAND-PRESS-1--rep-nlm-bookhand-title g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-02-bound-REP-NLM-BOOK-HAND-PRESS-1--rep-nlm-bookhand-desc">\n  <title id="g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-02-bound-REP-NLM-BOOK-HAND-PRESS-1--rep-nlm-bookhand-title">Book at rest with a hand pressing down: three forces, zero net</title>\n  <desc id="g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-02-bound-REP-NLM-BOOK-HAND-PRESS-1--rep-nlm-bookhand-desc">Stage 1: A 2 kg book lies at rest on a table while a hand presses straight down on it with 5 N; g = 10 m/s², up positive. Stage 2: Three forces act on the book: the Earth pulls it down with 20 N, the hand pushes down with 5 N, and the table pushes up with N; at rest N − 20 − 5 = 0. Stage 3: The table\'s push is 25 N and the net force is zero, so the book\'s zero velocity does not change although three nonzero forces act.</desc>\n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-02-bound-REP-NLM-BOOK-HAND-PRESS-1--rep-nlm-bookhand-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-02-bound-REP-NLM-BOOK-HAND-PRESS-1--rep-nlm-bookhand-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-BOOKHAND-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">A 2 kg book at rest; a hand presses down with 5 N</text>\n    <line x1="60" y1="190" x2="360" y2="190" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="80" y1="190" x2="80" y2="260" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="3"/>\n    <line x1="340" y1="190" x2="340" y2="260" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="3"/>\n    <rect x="160" y="160" width="100" height="30" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="210" y="180" text-anchor="middle">book 2 kg</text>\n    <path d="M235 100 q10 -20 30 0 v30 h-30 z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="275" y="110">hand, 5 N down</text>\n    <text x="20" y="290">g = 10 m/s²; up positive</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-BOOKHAND-V1" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <line x1="250" y1="132" x2="250" y2="158" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-02-bound-REP-NLM-BOOK-HAND-PRESS-1--rep-nlm-bookhand-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="258" y="152">hand on book: 5 N</text>\n    <line x1="210" y1="180" x2="210" y2="240" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-02-bound-REP-NLM-BOOK-HAND-PRESS-1--rep-nlm-bookhand-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="218" y="244">Earth on book: 20 N</text>\n    <line x1="175" y1="175" x2="175" y2="115" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-02-bound-REP-NLM-BOOK-HAND-PRESS-1--rep-nlm-bookhand-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="167" y="118" text-anchor="end">table on book: N</text>\n    <text x="470" y="60" font-weight="600">forces on the book:</text>\n    <text x="470" y="82">−20 N (Earth), −5 N (hand), +N (table)</text>\n    <text x="470" y="104">at rest: N − 20 − 5 = 0</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-BOOKHAND-V2" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="470" y="150" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">table\'s push N = 25 N</text>\n    <text x="470" y="172" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">net force = 0</text>\n    <text x="470" y="194">velocity (zero) does not change,</text>\n    <text x="470" y="216">though three nonzero forces act</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n<div class="g9-stage-controls"><div class="g9-stage-chips" role="group" aria-label="Stages"><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="0" data-g9-stage-desc="Show the book, the table and the pressing hand.">1: The book</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="1" data-g9-stage-desc="List every force on the book with its agent.">2: Forces with agents</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="2" data-g9-stage-desc="Solve for N and state what zero net force predicts.">3: Table push and prediction</button></div><p class="g9-stage-desc" data-g9-stage-desc-text></p><div class="g9-stage-stepper"><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="prev">Previous stage</button><span data-g9-stage-label></span><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="next">Next stage</button></div></div><figcaption data-g9-block="representation_bridge" data-g9-caption="purpose">Show the pressed book so each force is listed with its agent and the table&#x27;s push comes from the balance, not from the weight alone.</figcaption></figure><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution"><ol><li>List the agents: Earth (20 N down), hand (5 N down), table (up, size unknown).</li><li>The book stays at rest, so the signed sum is zero: N − 20 − 5 = 0, giving N = 25 N.</li><li>A zero net force predicts no change of velocity; it does not mean no forces act.</li></ol></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="answer"><h4>Answer</h4><p>Earth pulls the book down with 20 N, the hand pushes down with 5 N and the table pushes up with 25 N. The net force is zero, so the book&#x27;s velocity (zero) does not change, though three nonzero forces act.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="independent_check"><h4>Independent check</h4><p>Remove the hand: the same sum gives N = 20 N, so the table&#x27;s push adjusts to what the other forces need.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="failure_signal"><h4>If you went wrong</h4><p>You gave the table&#x27;s push as 20 N, the book&#x27;s weight: the hand&#x27;s 5 N press must also be balanced, so N = 25 N. Or you wrote that no forces act because the book is at rest.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exposure_closure"><h4>What this establishes</h4><p>Established: forces can be real and nonzero while their sum is zero, and it is the sum that sets the change of velocity. Later tasks keep every force listed on a free-body diagram before any are combined.</p></div></template></section></div><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-support" data-blueprint-slot="support" data-required="false"><div class="g9-ladder" data-g9-ladder-ref="CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-02-authored"><ol data-g9-ladder><li data-g9-rung="1">Before adding anything, how many bodies touch or attract the book?</li></ol><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-02-authored-2"><li data-g9-rung="2">Draw the book with three arrows at full length (Earth, hand, table) and write their signed sum beside it without erasing any arrow.</li></template><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-02-authored-3"><li data-g9-rung="3">List each force with its agent and signed size, then set the sum to match the book&#x27;s acceleration, which is zero here.</li></template><button type="button" data-g9-next-rung>Next hint</button></div></section></div></div></article><article id="g9-CORE2A--Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-03" data-g9-unit="Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-03" data-g9-kind="QUESTION" data-g9-role="CORE2A" data-g9-search-text="Physics Laws of Motion A free-body diagram belongs to one chosen body Hard concept Connected reasoning Newton-law and observer-frame practice Constructed response Authored practice A 0.1 kg magnet is stuck at rest on a vertical steel fridge door; the door&#x27;s magnetic pull on it is 3 N. Take g = 10 m/s². List every force on the magnet with its agent, direction and size. For each one, name its third-law partner and the body that partner acts on. The magnet does not slide. g = 10 m/s²." class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="provenance"><p class="g9-prov">AUTHORED practice</p></div><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE2A" data-g9-meta-record="Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-03" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-NLM-FIRST-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Laws of Motion"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Laws of Motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP" data-g9-meta-value="A free-body diagram belongs to one chosen body"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>A free-body diagram belongs to one chosen body</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP" data-g9-meta-value="HARD"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Hard concept</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="question-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-03" data-g9-meta-value="D2"><strong>Question difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Connected reasoning</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="family" data-g9-meta-ref="FAM-PHY-NLM-PRACTICE" data-g9-meta-value="Newton-law and observer-frame practice"><strong>Family:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Newton-law and observer-frame practice</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="question-type" data-g9-meta-ref="Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-03" data-g9-meta-value="constructed_response"><strong>Question type:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Constructed response</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="provenance" data-g9-meta-ref="Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-03" data-g9-meta-value="AUTHORED"><strong>Provenance:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Authored practice</span></span></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="family_identity"><p>Newton-law and observer-frame practice</p></div></section><div class="g9-split"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-attempt" data-blueprint-slot="attempt" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="stem"><h2>A 0.1 kg magnet is stuck at rest on a vertical steel fridge door; the door&#x27;s magnetic pull on it is 3 N. Take g = 10 m/s². List every force on the magnet with its agent, direction and size. For each one, name its third-law partner and the body that partner acts on.</h2></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="conditions"><h4>Conditions</h4><ul><li>The magnet does not slide.</li><li>g = 10 m/s².</li></ul></div><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-03-REP-NLM-FRIDGE-MAGNET" data-g9-stage="PRE_ATTEMPT" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-FRIDGE-MAGNET" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="1" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-MAGNET-V0" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg role="img" aria-label="The magnet" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 900 340">\n  \n  \n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-03-REP-NLM-FRIDGE-MAGNET-1--rep-nlm-magnet-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-03-REP-NLM-FRIDGE-MAGNET-1--rep-nlm-magnet-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-MAGNET-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">A 0.1 kg magnet stuck at rest on a vertical fridge door</text>\n    <rect x="60" y="50" width="40" height="230" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="80" y="300" text-anchor="middle">door</text>\n    <rect x="100" y="130" width="50" height="60" fill="currentColor"/>\n    <text x="158" y="124">magnet</text>\n    <text x="20" y="330">door\'s magnetic pull on it: 3 N; g = 10 m/s²</text>\n  </g>\n  \n  \n</svg>\n<figcaption data-g9-block="stage_caption" data-g9-caption="stages">The magnet</figcaption></figure><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your attempt<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-reasoning" data-blueprint-slot="reasoning" data-required="true"><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-03-reasoning"><summary>Reasoning route and full solution</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-03-reasoning"><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-03-bound-REP-NLM-FRIDGE-MAGNET" data-g9-stage="POST_ATTEMPT" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-FRIDGE-MAGNET" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="3" data-g9-stages-total="3" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-MAGNET-V0 VIS-NLM-MAGNET-V1 VIS-NLM-MAGNET-V2" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 900 340" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-03-bound-REP-NLM-FRIDGE-MAGNET-1--rep-nlm-magnet-title g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-03-bound-REP-NLM-FRIDGE-MAGNET-1--rep-nlm-magnet-desc">\n  <title id="g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-03-bound-REP-NLM-FRIDGE-MAGNET-1--rep-nlm-magnet-title">Magnet on a fridge door: four forces on it, four partners elsewhere</title>\n  <desc id="g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-03-bound-REP-NLM-FRIDGE-MAGNET-1--rep-nlm-magnet-desc">Stage 1: A 0.1 kg magnet is stuck at rest on a vertical steel fridge door; the door\'s magnetic pull on it is 3 N; g = 10 m/s². Stage 2: On the magnet: weight 1 N down from the Earth, magnetic pull 3 N toward the door from the fridge, normal push 3 N away from the door and static friction 1 N up from the door surface. Stage 3: The partners act on other bodies: the magnet pulls the Earth up 1 N, pulls the fridge toward itself 3 N, presses into the door 3 N and drags the door\'s surface down 1 N; none is on the magnet\'s diagram.</desc>\n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-03-bound-REP-NLM-FRIDGE-MAGNET-1--rep-nlm-magnet-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-03-bound-REP-NLM-FRIDGE-MAGNET-1--rep-nlm-magnet-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-MAGNET-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">A 0.1 kg magnet stuck at rest on a vertical fridge door</text>\n    <rect x="60" y="50" width="40" height="230" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="80" y="300" text-anchor="middle">door</text>\n    <rect x="100" y="130" width="50" height="60" fill="currentColor"/>\n    <text x="158" y="124">magnet</text>\n    <text x="20" y="330">door\'s magnetic pull on it: 3 N; g = 10 m/s²</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-MAGNET-V1" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <line x1="125" y1="192" x2="125" y2="238" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-03-bound-REP-NLM-FRIDGE-MAGNET-1--rep-nlm-magnet-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="133" y="236">Earth: 1 N down</text>\n    <line x1="220" y1="178" x2="154" y2="178" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-03-bound-REP-NLM-FRIDGE-MAGNET-1--rep-nlm-magnet-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="226" y="183">fridge (magnetic): 3 N toward door</text>\n    <line x1="152" y1="148" x2="212" y2="148" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-03-bound-REP-NLM-FRIDGE-MAGNET-1--rep-nlm-magnet-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="218" y="153">door (normal): 3 N away</text>\n    <line x1="125" y1="128" x2="125" y2="88" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-03-bound-REP-NLM-FRIDGE-MAGNET-1--rep-nlm-magnet-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="133" y="84">door surface (friction): 1 N up</text>\n    <text x="510" y="60" font-weight="600">forces on the magnet:</text>\n    <text x="510" y="82">weight 1 N down (Earth)</text>\n    <text x="510" y="104">magnetic pull 3 N toward door (fridge)</text>\n    <text x="510" y="126">normal push 3 N away from door</text>\n    <text x="510" y="148">static friction 1 N up (door surface)</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-MAGNET-V2" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="510" y="180" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">third-law partners, on other bodies:</text>\n    <text x="510" y="202" fill="#1f6fb2">magnet pulls Earth up 1 N</text>\n    <text x="510" y="224" fill="#1f6fb2">magnet pulls fridge 3 N toward itself</text>\n    <text x="510" y="246" fill="#1f6fb2">magnet presses into the door 3 N</text>\n    <text x="510" y="268" fill="#1f6fb2">magnet drags door surface down 1 N</text>\n    <text x="510" y="290">none of them is on the magnet\'s diagram</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n<div class="g9-stage-controls"><div class="g9-stage-chips" role="group" aria-label="Stages"><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="0" data-g9-stage-desc="Show the magnet at rest on the vertical door.">1: The magnet</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="1" data-g9-stage-desc="List each force on the magnet with its agent, direction and size.">2: Forces on the magnet</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="2" data-g9-stage-desc="Name each force&#x27;s third-law partner and the body it acts on.">3: Partners</button></div><p class="g9-stage-desc" data-g9-stage-desc-text></p><div class="g9-stage-stepper"><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="prev">Previous stage</button><span data-g9-stage-label></span><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="next">Next stage</button></div></div><figcaption data-g9-block="representation_bridge" data-g9-caption="purpose">Show the magnet on the vertical door with its four forces and, separately, where each third-law partner acts.</figcaption></figure><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution"><ol><li>Choose the magnet and ask who acts on it: Earth, the fridge&#x27;s magnetism, and the door surface (a normal push and friction).</li><li>At rest: the normal push balances the 3 N pull, and friction balances the 1 N weight.</li><li>Swap agent and target for each force to get its partner; each partner acts on the Earth or on the fridge.</li></ol></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="answer"><h4>Answer</h4><p>On the magnet: weight 1 N down (Earth), magnetic pull 3 N toward the door (fridge), normal push 3 N away from the door (door surface), static friction 1 N up (door surface). Partners: the magnet pulls the Earth up 1 N, pulls the fridge toward itself 3 N, presses into the door 3 N and drags the door&#x27;s surface down 1 N. None of the partners is on the magnet&#x27;s diagram.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="independent_check"><h4>Independent check</h4><p>Switch the chosen body to the fridge door: three partner arrows now appear on its diagram (3 N toward the magnet, 3 N into the door, 1 N down along its surface) and the magnet&#x27;s four forces leave it. The fourth partner, the magnet&#x27;s 1 N upward pull on the Earth, goes on the Earth&#x27;s diagram.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="failure_signal"><h4>If you went wrong</h4><p>You named the normal push as the partner of the magnetic pull, or the friction as the partner of the weight: those pairs both act on the magnet, so they balance each other and are not third-law partners.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exposure_closure"><h4>What this establishes</h4><p>Established: a diagram holds only the forces on its own body, so a partner force never cancels on it. The transfer task finds the partners of a block&#x27;s contact forces on the wedge it slides on.</p></div></template></section></div><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-support" data-blueprint-slot="support" data-required="false"><div class="g9-ladder" data-g9-ladder-ref="CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-03-authored"><ol data-g9-ladder><li data-g9-rung="1">Which single body are you describing first, and which bodies touch or attract it?<figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-03-hint-1-REP-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP" data-g9-stage="POST_ATTEMPT" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="1" data-g9-stages-total="5" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-FBD-V1" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg role="img" aria-label="Orientation" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 640 480">\n  \n  \n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-03-hint-1-REP-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-1--rep-nlm-fbd-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-03-hint-1-REP-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-1--rep-nlm-fbd-arrow-accent" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  \n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-FBD-V1" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <rect x="70" y="190" width="140" height="55" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="5"/>\n    <circle cx="140" cy="257" r="23" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="4"/>\n    <text x="20" y="28" font-weight="600">chosen body: cart</text>\n    <line x1="30" y1="110" x2="85" y2="110" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-03-hint-1-REP-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-1--rep-nlm-fbd-arrow)"/>\n    <line x1="30" y1="110" x2="30" y2="55" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-03-hint-1-REP-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-1--rep-nlm-fbd-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="70" y="130">+x</text>\n    <text x="38" y="64">+y</text>\n  </g>\n  \n  \n  \n</svg>\n<figcaption data-g9-block="representation_bridge" data-g9-caption="purpose">Make force ownership visible for one chosen body: identify the body and external agents first, then represent only forces acting on that body; resolved components remain a coordinate representation of one physical force.</figcaption></figure></li></ol><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-03-authored-2"><li data-g9-rung="2">Draw the magnet and the door as two separate outlines. Put each &#x27;on magnet&#x27; arrow on the magnet and each partner on the door (or the Earth).</li></template><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-03-authored-3"><li data-g9-rung="3">For each force on the magnet, write &#x27;X on magnet&#x27;, then swap the names to get its partner &#x27;magnet on X&#x27;. That partner goes on X&#x27;s diagram.<figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-03-hint-3-REP-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP" data-g9-stage="POST_ATTEMPT" data-g9-representation="REP-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP" data-g9-kind="FREE_BODY_DIAGRAM" data-reveal-stages="1" data-g9-stages-total="5" data-g9-stages="VIS-NLM-FBD-V2" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><svg role="img" aria-label="Interaction structure" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 640 480">\n  \n  \n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-03-hint-3-REP-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-1--rep-nlm-fbd-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE2A-Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-03-hint-3-REP-NLM-FBD-BODY-OWNERSHIP-1--rep-nlm-fbd-arrow-accent" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  \n  \n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-NLM-FBD-V2" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <rect x="54" y="172" width="172" height="118" rx="14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="7 5"/>\n    <text x="236" y="306">system boundary: cart only</text>\n    <text x="20" y="366" font-weight="600">External agents acting on the cart: horse, Earth, ground</text>\n  </g>\n  \n  \n</svg>\n<figcaption data-g9-block="representation_bridge" data-g9-caption="purpose">Make force ownership visible for one chosen body: identify the body and external agents first, then represent only forces acting on that body; resolved components remain a coordinate representation of one physical force.</figcaption></figure></li></template><button type="button" data-g9-next-rung>Next hint</button></div></section></div></div></article><article id="g9-CORE2A--Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-04" data-g9-unit="Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-04" data-g9-kind="QUESTION" data-g9-role="CORE2A" data-g9-search-text="Physics Laws of Motion Friction opposes relative sliding at a contact Medium concept Connected reasoning Newton-law and observer-frame practice Constructed response Authored practice A 20 kg crate rides on the flat bed of a truck. The truck brakes, slowing at 3 m/s², and the crate does not slip on the bed. Which way does friction from the bed act on the crate, and how large is it? The crate moves with the truck throughout (no slipping). The truck moves forward while slowing at 3 m/s²." class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="provenance"><p class="g9-prov">AUTHORED practice</p></div><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE2A" data-g9-meta-record="Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-04" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-NLM-FIRST-LAW" data-g9-meta-value="Laws of Motion"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Laws of Motion</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION" data-g9-meta-value="Friction opposes relative sliding at a contact"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Friction opposes relative sliding at a contact</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-NLM-FRICTION" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="question-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-04" data-g9-meta-value="D2"><strong>Question difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Connected reasoning</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="family" data-g9-meta-ref="FAM-PHY-NLM-PRACTICE" data-g9-meta-value="Newton-law and observer-frame practice"><strong>Family:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Newton-law and observer-frame practice</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="question-type" data-g9-meta-ref="Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-04" data-g9-meta-value="constructed_response"><strong>Question type:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Constructed response</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="provenance" data-g9-meta-ref="Q-PHY-NLM-2A-COV-04" data-g9-meta-value="AUTHORED"><strong>Provenance:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Authored practice</span></span></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="family_identity"><p>Newton-law and observer-frame practice</p></div></section><div class="g9-split"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-attempt" data-blueprint-slot="attempt" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="stem"><h2>A 20 kg crate rides on the flat bed of a truck. The truck brakes, slowing at 3 m/s², and the crate does not slip on the bed. Which way does friction from the bed act on the crate, and how large is it?</h2></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="conditions"><h4>Conditions</h4><ul><li>The crate moves with the truck throughout (no slipping).</li><li>The truck moves forward while slowing at 3 m/s².</li></ul></div><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-f