="../../../physics/index.html">Physics</a><a href="../../../chemistry/index.html">Chemistry</a><a href="../../../mathematics/index.html">Mathematics</a><a href="../../index.html">Question Bank</a></nav><div class="g9-header-actions"><button type="button" class="g9-header-btn g9-search-btn" data-g9-action="search" title="Search Grade9V3 (Ctrl/⌘ K)" aria-label="Search"><span class="g9-btn-icon">🔍</span><span class="g9-btn-text">Search</span></button><button type="button" class="g9-header-btn g9-display-btn" data-g9-action="display" title="Display & Theme Settings" aria-label="Display & Theme"><span class="g9-btn-icon">🌙 / ☀️</span><span class="g9-btn-text">Display</span></button><a class="g9-header-btn" data-g9-action="pdf" href="core1.pdf" title="Open the PDF of this page to print it">PDF</a></div></div><div data-g9-search-panel hidden><input data-g9-search-input type="search" aria-label="Search this page"></div><div data-g9-display-panel hidden><button type="button" data-g9-font="dec">A-</button><button type="button" data-g9-font="reset">A</button><button type="button" data-g9-font="inc">A+</button><button type="button" data-g9-theme="light">Light</button><button type="button" data-g9-theme="dark">Dark</button><button type="button" data-g9-zoom="dec">Zoom -</button><button type="button" data-g9-zoom="reset">100%</button><button type="button" data-g9-zoom="inc">Zoom +</button></div></header><div class="g9-concept-triad-bar"><div class="g9-triad-inner"><div class="g9-triad-context" aria-label="breadcrumb"><a href="../../index.html">Home</a> / <a href="../../../physics/index.html">Physics</a> / <a href="../../../topics/nlm/index.html">Two-dimensional kinematics and projectile model</a> / <span aria-current="page">CORE1</span></div><span class="g9-triad-concept-title">Concept: Two-dimensional kinematics and projectile model</span><div class="g9-triad-actions"><a class="g9-triad-btn g9-btn-learn" href="core1a.html">📖 Learn</a><a class="g9-triad-btn g9-btn-practice" href="core2.html">✍️ Practice</a><a class="g9-triad-btn g9-btn-qb" href="../../../question-bank/index.html?capability=CAP-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL">All questions in QB &rarr;</a></div></div></div><noscript>Answers open after you attempt; this page needs JavaScript.</noscript><main><h1>Two-dimensional kinematics and projectile model: Orientation map</h1><article id="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1" data-g9-search-text="Physics Motion in 2 D Split one plane-motion event into independent x/y components with one clock Medium concept Split one plane-motion event into independent x/y components with one clock"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="scope"><h2>Split one plane-motion event into independent x/y components with one clock</h2></div><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-KIN-2D-MOTION" data-g9-meta-value="Motion in 2 D"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Motion in 2 D</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" data-g9-meta-value="Split one plane-motion event into independent x/y components with one clock"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Split one plane-motion event into independent x/y components with one clock</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-orientation" data-blueprint-slot="orientation" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="hard_transition"><h4>Hard transition</h4><p>Perpendicular components may be solved separately because each axis equation contains only that axis&#x27;s quantities, but they still describe one object at one physical time.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_prompt"><h4>Before you go on</h4><p>A particle has x-velocity 6 m/s and y-velocity -8 m/s at t=3 s. Explain what &#x27;independent components&#x27; means and what must remain shared between the x and y descriptions.</p></div></section></article><article id="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1" data-g9-search-text="Physics Motion in 2 D Apply constant-acceleration kinematics component by component Medium concept Apply constant-acceleration kinematics component by component"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="scope"><h2>Apply constant-acceleration kinematics component by component</h2></div><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-KIN-2D-MOTION" data-g9-meta-value="Motion in 2 D"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Motion in 2 D</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" data-g9-meta-value="Apply constant-acceleration kinematics component by component"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Apply constant-acceleration kinematics component by component</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-orientation" data-blueprint-slot="orientation" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="hard_transition"><h4>Hard transition</h4><p>The one-dimensional constant-acceleration equations can be applied to each perpendicular component independently when a_x and a_y are each constant over the same interval.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="governing_relation"><h4>Governing relation</h4><div class="g9-relation"><p class="g9-expr">v_x = u_x + a_x t; v_y = u_y + a_y t</p><p>Each perpendicular velocity component follows the 1D constant-acceleration relation over one common interval.</p><ul><li>a_x and a_y are constant over the interval.</li><li>Both component equations use the same elapsed time and frame.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-relation"><p class="g9-expr">Delta x = u_x t + 0.5 a_x t^2; Delta y = u_y t + 0.5 a_y t^2</p><p>Each perpendicular displacement component follows the 1D constant-acceleration displacement relation over one common interval.</p><ul><li>a_x and a_y are constant over the interval.</li><li>Both component equations use the same elapsed time and frame.</li></ul></div></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_prompt"><h4>Before you go on</h4><p>A particle starts with u=(0,5) m/s and constant a=(10,4) m/s². Set up the x and y position equations at common time t without solving a particular numerical t.</p></div></section></article><article id="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1" data-g9-search-text="Physics Motion in 2 D Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences Medium concept Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="scope"><h2>Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences</h2></div><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-KIN-2D-MOTION" data-g9-meta-value="Motion in 2 D"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Motion in 2 D</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-meta-value="Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-orientation" data-blueprint-slot="orientation" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="hard_transition"><h4>Hard transition</h4><p>After release in the ideal near-Earth model, projectile motion is not a new set of laws: it is the same component kinematics with a_x=0 and a_y=-g (for +y upward).</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="governing_relation"><h4>Governing relation</h4><div class="g9-relation"><p class="g9-expr">a_x = 0; a_y = -g; v_x = u_x; v_y = u_y - g t; Delta x = u_x t; Delta y = u_y t - 0.5 g t^2</p><p>With +y upward, air resistance neglected and near-Earth constant g, projectile motion is 2D constant acceleration specialized to zero horizontal and downward vertical acceleration.</p><ul><li>Near-Earth region with approximately constant g.</li><li>Air resistance neglected.</li><li>+y is declared upward for the displayed signs.</li><li>The object is in free flight after release.</li></ul></div></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_prompt"><h4>Before you go on</h4><p>A stone is launched horizontally from a cliff with speed u. With +y upward and air resistance neglected, write its component acceleration, velocity and displacement relations after release and explain why one common t is used.</p></div></section></article></main><footer data-g9-footer>Physics · Two-dimensional kinematics and projectile model</footer><script>\n(()=>{const q=(s,r=document)=>[...r.querySelectorAll(s)];\nconst store={get:k=>{try{return localStorage.getItem(\'g9-\'+k)}catch(e){return null}},set:(k,v)=>{try{localStorage.setItem(\'g9-\'+k,v);return true}catch(e){return false}},remove:k=>{try{localStorage.removeItem(\'g9-\'+k);return true}catch(e){return false}}};\nconst root=document.documentElement;const scope=root.dataset.g9Product&&root.dataset.g9RenderDigest?root.dataset.g9Product+\':\'+root.dataset.g9RenderDigest:\'\';\nconst apply=()=>{root.dataset.theme=store.get(\'theme\')||root.dataset.theme||\'light\';root.style.setProperty(\'--g9-zoom\',store.get(\'zoom\')||\'1\');};apply();\nq(\'[data-g9-theme]\').forEach(b=>b.onclick=()=>{store.set(\'theme\',b.dataset.g9Theme);apply()});\nq(\'[data-g9-zoom]\').forEach(b=>b.onclick=()=>{let z=parseFloat(store.get(\'zoom\')||\'1\');z=b.dataset.g9Zoom===\'inc\'?Math.min(1.6,z+0.1):b.dataset.g9Zoom===\'dec\'?Math.max(0.8,z-0.1):1;store.set(\'zoom\',z.toFixed(1));apply()});\nq(\'[data-g9-font]\').forEach(b=>b.onclick=()=>q(\'[data-g9-zoom="\'+b.dataset.g9Font+\'"]\')[0]?.click());\nfunction initFigure(f){if