 href="../../../physics/index.html">Physics</a><a href="../../../chemistry/index.html">Chemistry</a><a href="../../../mathematics/index.html">Mathematics</a><a href="../../index.html">Question Bank</a></nav><div class="g9-header-actions"><button type="button" class="g9-header-btn g9-search-btn" data-g9-action="search" title="Search Grade9V3 (Ctrl/⌘ K)" aria-label="Search"><span class="g9-btn-icon">🔍</span><span class="g9-btn-text">Search</span></button><button type="button" class="g9-header-btn g9-display-btn" data-g9-action="display" title="Display & Theme Settings" aria-label="Display & Theme"><span class="g9-btn-icon">🌙 / ☀️</span><span class="g9-btn-text">Display</span></button><a class="g9-header-btn" data-g9-action="pdf" href="core1b.pdf" title="Open the PDF of this page to print it">PDF</a></div></div><div data-g9-search-panel hidden><input data-g9-search-input type="search" aria-label="Search this page"></div><div data-g9-display-panel hidden><button type="button" data-g9-font="dec">A-</button><button type="button" data-g9-font="reset">A</button><button type="button" data-g9-font="inc">A+</button><button type="button" data-g9-theme="light">Light</button><button type="button" data-g9-theme="dark">Dark</button><button type="button" data-g9-zoom="dec">Zoom -</button><button type="button" data-g9-zoom="reset">100%</button><button type="button" data-g9-zoom="inc">Zoom +</button></div></header><div class="g9-concept-triad-bar"><div class="g9-triad-inner"><div class="g9-triad-context" aria-label="breadcrumb"><a href="../../index.html">Home</a> / <a href="../../../physics/index.html">Physics</a> / <a href="../../../topics/nlm/index.html">Two-dimensional kinematics and projectile model</a> / <span aria-current="page">CORE1B</span></div><span class="g9-triad-concept-title">Concept: Two-dimensional kinematics and projectile model</span><div class="g9-triad-actions"><a class="g9-triad-btn g9-btn-learn" href="core1a.html">📖 Learn</a><a class="g9-triad-btn g9-btn-practice" href="core2.html">✍️ Practice</a><a class="g9-triad-btn g9-btn-qb" href="../../../question-bank/index.html?capability=CAP-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL">All questions in QB &rarr;</a></div></div></div><noscript>Answers open after you attempt; this page needs JavaScript.</noscript><main><h1>Two-dimensional kinematics and projectile model: Reconstruction</h1><article id="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1B" data-g9-search-text="Physics Motion in 2 D Split one plane-motion event into independent x/y components with one clock Medium concept Split one plane-motion event into independent x/y components with one clock" class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><h2>Split one plane-motion event into independent x/y components with one clock</h2><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1B" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-KIN-2D-MOTION" data-g9-meta-value="Motion in 2 D"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Motion in 2 D</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" data-g9-meta-value="Split one plane-motion event into independent x/y components with one clock"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Split one plane-motion event into independent x/y components with one clock</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span></div></section><div class="g9-split g9-split-primary-only"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-attempt" data-blueprint-slot="attempt" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="predict"><h4>Predict</h4><p>If x and y components are independent, does that mean the object has two different clocks?</p></div><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your attempt<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-reconstruction" data-blueprint-slot="reconstruction" data-required="true"><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-reconstruct"><summary>Reconstruct</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-reconstruct"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="reconstruct"><ol><li>What must be declared before any component can have a sign?</li><li>What separates between axes, and what must stay common because both descriptions belong to one event?</li><li>Which x and y values may be recombined into one state?</li></ol></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="diagnose"><h4>Diagnose</h4><ul><li>At one instant 2 s after launch, may the x equation use t=2 s while the y equation uses another time?</li><li>May x(2 s) be combined with y(3 s) to describe the particle&#x27;s position?</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="repair"><h4>Repair</h4><ul><li>No. Independence separates the axis equations; it does not give the same object two clocks.</li><li>No. A plane state is a simultaneous pair. Recombine x(t) and y(t) only at the same t in the same frame.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="success_criteria"><h4>What your answer should contain</h4><p>A two-column x/y setup with one shared t.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="model_response"><h4>What a complete answer does</h4><p>Axes are kept separate while time/frame remain shared.</p><ul><li>Separate x and y equations; same t for both.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="rejoin_jump"><h4>The step you rebuilt</h4><p>Perpendicular components may be solved separately because each axis equation contains only that axis&#x27;s quantities, but they still describe one object at one physical time.</p></div></template><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="boundary_test"><h4>Boundary test</h4><p>If a_x=0 but a_y is nonzero, may v_x stay constant while v_y changes?</p></div><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-boundary"><summary>Boundary answer</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-boundary"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="boundary_answer"><p>Yes. That is exactly what independent component evolution permits.</p><p>One component can change while the other does not.</p></div></template></section></div></div></article><article id="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1B" data-g9-search-text="Physics Motion in 2 D Apply constant-acceleration kinematics component by component Medium concept Apply constant-acceleration kinematics component by component" class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><h2>Apply constant-acceleration kinematics component by component</h2><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1B" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-KIN-2D-MOTION" data-g9-meta-value="Motion in 2 D"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Motion in 2 D</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" data-g9-meta-value="Apply constant-acceleration kinematics component by component"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Apply constant-acceleration kinematics component by component</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span></div></section><div class="g9-split g9-split-primary-only"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-attempt" data-blueprint-slot="attempt" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="predict"><h4>Predict</h4><p>If a_x is nonzero and a_y is zero, which velocity component changes with time?</p></div><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your attempt<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-reconstruction" data-blueprint-slot="reconstruction" data-required="true"><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-reconstruct"><summary>Reconstruct</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-reconstruct"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="reconstruct"><ol><li>Before using a constant-acceleration formula, what must be true about that axis&#x27;s acceleration component?</li><li>What does the 1D relation become when written only along x, and what changes for y?</li><li>If one component equation finds the event time, what time must the other component use?</li></ol></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="diagnose"><h4>Diagnose</h4><ul><li>If a=(10 i + 4 j) m/s², should both component equations use a=√116?</li><li>If a_x is constant but a_y changes with time, may the same constant-acceleration displacement formula be used exactly on y over the whole interval?</li><li>If x-motion says an event occurs at t=3 s, what time belongs in the y equation for that same event?</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="repair"><h4>Repair</h4><ul><li>No. Use a_x=10 only in the x equation and a_y=4 only in the y equation; the magnitude is not either component.</li><li>No. Model validity is checked per axis. A constant a_x does not make a_y constant.</li><li>Use t=3 s. Both components describe the same event and therefore share one clock.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="success_criteria"><h4>What your answer should contain</h4><p>Two correct component constant-acceleration equations using one t.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="model_response"><h4>What a complete answer does</h4><p>Each axis uses only its own signed initial values and acceleration while sharing the same time.</p><ul><li>v_x=u_x+a_x t and v_y=u_y+a_y t with one t.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="rejoin_jump"><h4>The step you rebuilt</h4><p>The one-dimensional constant-acceleration equations can be applied to each perpendicular component independently when a_x and a_y are each constant over the same interval.</p></div></template><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="boundary_test"><h4>Boundary test</h4><p>If a_y changes with time while a_x stays constant, may the constant-acceleration equations still be exact on both axes for the whole interval?</p></div><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-boundary"><summary>Boundary answer</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-boundary"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="boundary_answer"><p>No. The x relation may remain exact, but the y constant-acceleration relation is not exact over that interval.</p><p>Model validity is checked per component.</p></div></template></section></div></div></article><article id="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1B" data-g9-search-text="Physics Motion in 2 D Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences Medium concept Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences" class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><h2>Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences</h2><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1B" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-KIN-2D-MOTION" data-g9-meta-value="Motion in 2 D"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Motion in 2 D</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-meta-value="Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span></div></section><div class="g9-split g9-split-primary-only"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-attempt" data-blueprint-slot="attempt" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="predict"><h4>Predict</h4><p>Immediately after a horizontal launch, which acceleration component is zero in the ideal model?</p></div><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your attempt<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-reconstruction" data-blueprint-slot="reconstruction" data-required="true"><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-reconstruct"><summary>Reconstruct</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-reconstruct"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="reconstruct"><ol><li>What forces remain after release in the idealized problem?</li><li>What are the horizontal and vertical component equations, and what clock do they share?</li><li>What exact component condition identifies the event the question asks about?</li><li>If the event is the apex, what becomes zero and what definitely does not?</li><li>Does launch height equal landing height? If not, which shortcut becomes invalid?</li></ol></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="diagnose"><h4>Diagnose</h4><ul><li>If a_x=0 after release, can v_x still be nonzero?</li><li>One second after a horizontal launch, is v_y still zero under gravity?</li><li>At the apex of an oblique projectile, which of v_x, v_y and a_y is zero?</li><li>A ball launched from a roof lands on the ground below. May the same-height time-of-flight shortcut be used without checking the vertical displacement?</li><li>On ascent and descent at the same height, how do the horizontal and vertical velocity components compare?</li><li>If the horizontal launch speed doubles from the same height, does the ideal fall time change?</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="repair"><h4>Repair</h4><ul><li>Yes. Zero horizontal acceleration means horizontal velocity stays constant, not that it becomes zero.</li><li>No. u_y=0 only at release; a_y=-g makes v_y=-gt afterward.</li><li>Only v_y is zero at that instant. v_x remains u_x and a_y remains -g.</li><li>No. Same-height shortcuts require Delta y=0 between launch and landing. For unequal heights, solve the vertical position condition.</li><li>v_x is the same, while v_y has equal magnitude and opposite sign; the speed matches but the velocity vector does not.</li><li>No. The vertical position equation determines the fall time; horizontal speed changes the range, not the fall time.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="success_criteria"><h4>What your answer should contain</h4><p>A model declaration and component-equation setup for a horizontal or oblique projectile.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="model_response"><h4>What a complete answer does</h4><p>The learner selects gravity-only free flight, uses a_x=0/a_y=-g and one common time before calculating any named projectile quantity.</p><ul><li>State assumptions, set component accelerations, then solve x/y with one t.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="rejoin_jump"><h4>The step you rebuilt</h4><p>After release in the ideal near-Earth model, projectile motion is not a new set of laws: it is the same component kinematics with a_x=0 and a_y=-g (for +y upward).</p></div></template><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="boundary_test"><h4>Boundary test</h4><p>A launched object continues under significant horizontal thrust after release. May the standard a_x=0 projectile specialization be used?</p></div><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-boundary"><summary>Boundary answer</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-boundary"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="boundary_answer"><p>No. The object is not in gravity-only free flight; its horizontal acceleration is not zero under that model.</p><p>Projectile equations are conditional consequences of a declared force/model state.</p></div></template></section></div></div></article></main><footer data-g9-footer>Physics · Two-dimensional kinematics and projectile model</footer><script>\n(()=>{const q=(s,r=document)=>[...r.querySelectorAll(s)];\nconst store={get:k=>{try{return localStorage.getItem(\'g9-\'+k)}catch(e){return null}},set:(k,v)=>{try{localStorage.setItem(\'g9-\'+k,v);return true}catch(e){return false}},remove:k=>{try{localStorage.removeItem(\'g9-\'+k);return true}catch(e){return false}}};\nconst root=document.documentElement;const scope=root.dataset.g9Product&&root.dataset.g9RenderDigest?root.dataset.g9Product+\':\'+root.dataset.g9RenderDigest:\'\';\nconst apply=()=>{root.dataset.theme=store.get(\'theme\')||root.dataset.theme||\'light\';root.style.setProperty(\'--g9-zoom\',store.get(\'zoom\')||\'1\');};apply();\nq(\'[data-g9-theme]\').forEach(b=>b.onclick=()=>{store.set(\'theme\',b.dataset.g9Theme);apply()});\nq(\'[data-g9-zoom]\').forEach(b=>b.onclick=()=>{let z=parseFloat(store.get(\'zoom\')||\'1\');z=b.dataset.g9Zoom===\'inc\'?Math.min(1.6,z+0.1):b.dataset.g9Zoom===\'dec\'?Math.max(0.8,z-0.1):1;store.set(\'zoom\',z.toFixed(1));apply()});\nq(\'[data-g9-font]\').forEach(b=>b.onclick=()=>q(\'[data-g9-zoom="\'+b.dataset.g9Font+\'"]\')[0]?.click());\nfunction initFigure(f){if(f.dataset.g9Init)return;f.dataset.g9In