eme Settings" aria-label="Display & Theme"><span class="g9-btn-icon">🌙 / ☀️</span><span class="g9-btn-text">Display</span></button></div></div><div data-g9-search-panel hidden><input data-g9-search-input type="search" aria-label="Search this page"></div><div data-g9-display-panel hidden><button type="button" data-g9-font="dec">A-</button><button type="button" data-g9-font="reset">A</button><button type="button" data-g9-font="inc">A+</button><button type="button" data-g9-theme="light">Light</button><button type="button" data-g9-theme="dark">Dark</button><button type="button" data-g9-zoom="dec">Zoom -</button><button type="button" data-g9-zoom="reset">100%</button><button type="button" data-g9-zoom="inc">Zoom +</button></div></header><div class="g9-concept-triad-bar"><div class="g9-triad-inner"><div class="g9-triad-context" aria-label="breadcrumb"><a href="../../index.html">Home</a> / <a href="../../../physics/index.html">Physics</a> / <a href="../../../topics/nlm/index.html">Two-dimensional kinematics and projectile model</a> / <span aria-current="page">CORE1</span></div><span class="g9-triad-concept-title">Concept: Two-dimensional kinematics and projectile model</span><div class="g9-triad-actions"><a class="g9-triad-btn g9-btn-learn" href="core1a.html">📖 Learn</a><a class="g9-triad-btn g9-btn-practice" href="core2.html">✍️ Practice</a><a class="g9-triad-btn g9-btn-qb" href="../../../question-bank/index.html?capability=CAP-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL">All questions in QB &rarr;</a></div></div></div><noscript>Answers open after you attempt; this page needs JavaScript.</noscript><main><section id="g9-role-CORE1" data-g9-role-section="CORE1"><h1>Two-dimensional kinematics and projectile model: Orientation map</h1><article id="g9-CORE1--MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1" data-g9-search-text="Physics Motion in 2 D Split one plane-motion event into independent x/y components with one clock Medium concept Split one plane-motion event into independent x/y components with one clock"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="scope"><h2>Split one plane-motion event into independent x/y components with one clock</h2></div><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-KIN-2D-MOTION" data-g9-meta-value="Motion in 2 D"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Motion in 2 D</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" data-g9-meta-value="Split one plane-motion event into independent x/y components with one clock"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Split one plane-motion event into independent x/y components with one clock</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-orientation" data-blueprint-slot="orientation" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="hard_transition"><h4>Hard transition</h4><p>Perpendicular components may be solved separately because each axis equation contains only that axis&#x27;s quantities, but they still describe one object at one physical time.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_prompt"><h4>Before you go on</h4><p>A particle has x-velocity 6 m/s and y-velocity -8 m/s at t=3 s. Explain what &#x27;independent components&#x27; means and what must remain shared between the x and y descriptions.</p></div></section></article><article id="g9-CORE1--MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1" data-g9-search-text="Physics Motion in 2 D Apply constant-acceleration kinematics component by component Medium concept Apply constant-acceleration kinematics component by component"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="scope"><h2>Apply constant-acceleration kinematics component by component</h2></div><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-KIN-2D-MOTION" data-g9-meta-value="Motion in 2 D"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Motion in 2 D</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" data-g9-meta-value="Apply constant-acceleration kinematics component by component"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Apply constant-acceleration kinematics component by component</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-orientation" data-blueprint-slot="orientation" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="hard_transition"><h4>Hard transition</h4><p>The one-dimensional constant-acceleration equations can be applied to each perpendicular component independently when a_x and a_y are each constant over the same interval.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="governing_relation"><h4>Governing relation</h4><div class="g9-relation"><p class="g9-expr">v_x = u_x + a_x t; v_y = u_y + a_y t</p><p>Each perpendicular velocity component follows the 1D constant-acceleration relation over one common interval.</p><ul><li>a_x and a_y are constant over the interval.</li><li>Both component equations use the same elapsed time and frame.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-relation"><p class="g9-expr">Delta x = u_x t + 0.5 a_x t^2; Delta y = u_y t + 0.5 a_y t^2</p><p>Each perpendicular displacement component follows the 1D constant-acceleration displacement relation over one common interval.</p><ul><li>a_x and a_y are constant over the interval.</li><li>Both component equations use the same elapsed time and frame.</li></ul></div></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_prompt"><h4>Before you go on</h4><p>A particle starts with u=(0,5) m/s and constant a=(10,4) m/s². Set up the x and y position equations at common time t without solving a particular numerical t.</p></div></section></article><article id="g9-CORE1--MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1" data-g9-search-text="Physics Motion in 2 D Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences Medium concept Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="scope"><h2>Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences</h2></div><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-KIN-2D-MOTION" data-g9-meta-value="Motion in 2 D"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Motion in 2 D</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-meta-value="Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-orientation" data-blueprint-slot="orientation" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="hard_transition"><h4>Hard transition</h4><p>After release in the ideal near-Earth model, projectile motion is not a new set of laws: it is the same component kinematics with a_x=0 and a_y=-g (for +y upward).</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="governing_relation"><h4>Governing relation</h4><div class="g9-relation"><p class="g9-expr">a_x = 0; a_y = -g; v_x = u_x; v_y = u_y - g t; Delta x = u_x t; Delta y = u_y t - 0.5 g t^2</p><p>With +y upward, air resistance neglected and near-Earth constant g, projectile motion is 2D constant acceleration specialized to zero horizontal and downward vertical acceleration.</p><ul><li>Near-Earth region with approximately constant g.</li><li>Air resistance neglected.</li><li>+y is declared upward for the displayed signs.</li><li>The object is in free flight after release.</li></ul></div></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_prompt"><h4>Before you go on</h4><p>A stone is launched horizontally from a cliff with speed u. With +y upward and air resistance neglected, write its component acceleration, velocity and displacement relations after release and explain why one common t is used.</p></div></section></article></section><section id="g9-role-CORE1A" data-g9-role-section="CORE1A"><h1>Two-dimensional kinematics and projectile model: Construction</h1><section class="g9-core1a-book" data-g9-bucket-orientation data-g9-bucket-ref="BUCKET-PHY-KIN-2D-MOTION"><p class="g9-prov">Concept Book</p><h2>Two-dimensional kinematics and projectile model</h2><div class="g9-bucket-orientation-grid"><div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="learning_promise"><h4>Learning promise</h4><p>Independent perpendicular component motion in one frame, constant-acceleration component equations with common time, and the ideal near-Earth projectile specialization for horizontal and oblique launch questions.</p></div><nav class="g9-block" data-g9-block="concept_route" data-g9-concept-route aria-label="Concept Book route"><h4>Concept route</h4><ol><li data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS"><a href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS">Split one plane-motion event into independent x/y components with one clock</a><ol><li><a href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-1">Construction 1</a></li></ol></li><li data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION"><a href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION">Apply constant-acceleration kinematics component by component</a><ol><li><a href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-1">Construction 1</a></li></ol></li><li data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL"><a href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL">Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences</a></li></ol></nav></div><aside><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="foundation_route"><h4>Foundation route</h4><ul><li data-g9-foundation-ref="BUCKET-PHY-VEC-ADD-SUB" data-g9-availability="unlinked">Vector addition, subtraction and orientation <span class="g9-availability-note">— direct route unavailable in this product</span></li><li data-g9-foundation-ref="BUCKET-PHY-KIN-1D-MOTION" data-g9-availability="unlinked">One-dimensional motion <span class="g9-availability-note">— direct route unavailable in this product</span></li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="model_contract"><h4>Model contract</h4><ul><li>Use one declared non-rotating x-y frame for the whole event; both component equations use the same elapsed time.</li><li>When +y is chosen upward, the ideal projectile model uses a_x=0 and a_y=-g after release.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="scope_boundary"><h4>Outside this book</h4><ul><li>Calculus-based differentiation of a time-dependent position vector.</li><li>Derivation or memorization of a large catalogue of range/height/time-of-flight formulas as separate capabilities.</li><li>Trajectory-equation derivation as a first-slice requirement.</li><li>Air drag, Coriolis effects, variable gravity, target-on-incline projectile geometry and other advanced variants.</li></ul></div></aside></div></section><article id="g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1A" data-g9-search-text="Physics Motion in 2 D Split one plane-motion event into independent x/y components with one clock Medium concept Split one plane-motion event into independent x/y components with one clock" class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-concept-header" data-g9-component="CONCEPT_HEADER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="CONCEPT_HEADER"><h2>Split one plane-motion event into independent x/y components with one clock</h2><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1A" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-KIN-2D-MOTION" data-g9-meta-value="Motion in 2 D"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Motion in 2 D</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" data-g9-meta-value="Split one plane-motion event into independent x/y components with one clock"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Split one plane-motion event into independent x/y components with one clock</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span></div></div></section><div class="g9-split"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-construction" data-blueprint-slot="construction" data-required="true"><section id="g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-1" class="g9-cu" data-g9-cu="CU-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-1"><div class="g9-component g9-c-unit-header" data-g9-component="UNIT_HEADER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="UNIT_HEADER" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-1"><div class="g9-unit-head"><span class="g9-unit-no" aria-hidden="true">1</span><h3>Construction</h3><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-concept">Medium concept</span></div><nav class="g9-cu-nav" data-g9-unit-navigation aria-label="Concept Book section navigation"><span data-g9-route-position>Section 1 of 3</span><span class="g9-cu-nav-links"><a data-g9-next-section href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-1" aria-label="Next section: Construction 1">Next →</a></span></nav></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-banner" data-g9-component="KEY_STEP" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="BANNER"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="inferential_jump"><h4>The key step</h4><p>Perpendicular components may be solved separately because each axis equation contains only that axis&#x27;s quantities, but they still describe one object at one physical time.</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-tile" data-g9-component="MODEL_CONTRACT" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_TILE"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-prerequisites"><summary>What you need before this</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="entry_assumptions"><h4>Prerequisites</h4><ul><li>Can read signed vector components in a declared x-y frame.</li></ul><ul><li data-g9-prereq="CAP-VECTOR-SIGNED-COMPONENT" data-bridged="true">Choosing axes and keeping components signed (Physics)</li></ul></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-step-cards" data-g9-component="CONSTRUCTION_STEPS" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEP_CARDS" data-g9-component-items="4" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-1"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="construction"><ol><li data-g9-step="K2D1-1"><strong>Choose one fixed x-y frame, one origin and one time origin for the whole plane-motion event.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> A component value is meaningful only after its frame and sign convention are fixed.<br><em>Result:</em> one declared frame, origin, axis orientation and t=0</li><li data-g9-step="K2D1-2"><strong>Represent position, velocity and acceleration as signed x and y components in that same frame.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> A plane vector is completely represented by its perpendicular Cartesian components once the frame is declared.<br><em>Result:</em> signed component state (x,y), (v_x,v_y), (a_x,a_y)</li><li data-g9-step="K2D1-3"><strong>Keep x-quantities in the x description and y-quantities in the y description while using one shared elapsed time.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> The perpendicular component equations are independent in Cartesian kinematics, but both describe the same object during the same physical interval.<br><em>Result:</em> two axis histories coupled by one clock</li><li data-g9-step="K2D1-4"><strong>Recombine only component values that refer to the same frame and the same instant.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> Mixing x at one time with y at another does not describe any single physical state of the object.<br><em>Result:</em> one physically valid plane state reconstructed from simultaneous components</li></ol></div></div></section></section></div><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-representation" data-blueprint-slot="representation" data-required="true"></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-repair_closure" data-blueprint-slot="repair_closure" data-required="true"><section class="g9-cu-support" data-g9-support-for="CU-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-1"><h3>Construction 1</h3><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-trap-card" data-g9-component="TRAP_REPAIR" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_TRAP_CARD" data-g9-component-items="2" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-1"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-trap-repair"><summary>Mistake clinic · diagnose and repair</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="wrong_path"><h4>A tempting wrong path</h4><ul><li>Independent x and y motions may use different elapsed times.</li><li>The x-motion and y-motion are two different objects that can be paired arbitrarily later.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="diagnose"><h4>Diagnose</h4><ul><li>At one instant 2 s after launch, may the x equation use t=2 s while the y equation uses another time?</li><li>May x(2 s) be combined with y(3 s) to describe the particle&#x27;s position?</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="repair"><h4>Repair</h4><ul><li>No. Independence separates the axis equations; it does not give the same object two clocks.</li><li>No. A plane state is a simultaneous pair. Recombine x(t) and y(t) only at the same t in the same frame.</li></ul></div></div></details></div></section></section></div></div><div class="g9-split g9-split-support-only"><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-repair_closure" data-blueprint-slot="repair_closure" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-recall-card" data-g9-component="EXIT_RECALL" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="RECALL_CARD"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_task"><h4>Now you do one</h4><p>A particle has x-velocity 6 m/s and y-velocity -8 m/s at t=3 s. Explain what &#x27;independent components&#x27; means and what must remain shared between the x and y descriptions.</p></div><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your answer<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1A-MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-exit"><summary>Model answer</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1A-MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-exit"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_answer"><p>The x and y component equations are solved separately in the same frame, but both describe the same particle at the same t=3 s.</p><ol><li>Keep x quantities in the x equation and y quantities in the y equation.</li><li>Use one common elapsed time for the same physical instant.</li><li>Recombine components only after each axis is handled consistently.</li></ol></div></template></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-chips" data-g9-component="PRACTICE_LINKS" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="CHIPS"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="practice_navigation"><h4>Practice this concept in Core2</h4><ul><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q33" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q33">IIT-JEE · 2011 · Paper II · Q33</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2018-P2-Q08" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2018-P2-Q08">JEE (Advanced) · 2018 · Paper 2 · Q8</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2019-P2-Q09" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2019-P2-Q09">JEE (Advanced) · 2019 · Paper 2 · Q9</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q05" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q05">JEE (Advanced) · 2021 · Paper 1 · Q5</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q06" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q06">JEE (Advanced) · 2021 · Paper 1 · Q6</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2024-P2-Q09" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2024-P2-Q09">JEE (Advanced) · 2024 · Paper 2 · Q9</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2012-P1-Q05" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2012-P1-Q05">IIT-JEE · 2012 · Paper I · Q5</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2022-P1-Q08" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2022-P1-Q08">JEE (Advanced) · 2022 · Paper 1 · Q8</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2023-P1-Q01" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2023-P1-Q01">JEE (Advanced) · 2023 · Paper 1 · Q1</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2025-P2-Q15" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2025-P2-Q15">JEE (Advanced) · 2025 · Paper 2 · Q15</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q26" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q26">IIT-JEE · 2011 · Paper II · Q26</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P1-Q11" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P1-Q11">JEE (Advanced) · 2014 · Paper 1 · Q11</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2026-P1-Q06" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2026-P1-Q06">JEE (Advanced) · 2026 · Paper 1 · Q6</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2013-P1-Q07" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2013-P1-Q07">JEE (Advanced) · 2013 · Paper 1 · Q7</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEMAIN-2026-04APR-S2-Q29" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-JEEMAIN-2026-04APR-S2-Q29">JEE Main · 2026 · Session 2 · 04 Apr 2026 · Shift 2 · Q29</a></li></ul></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-visual-card" data-g9-component="INTERACTIVE_BRIDGE" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="VISUAL_CARD"><aside class="g9-interactive-bridge" data-g9-interactive-bridge><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-interactive">Try Visually</span><h3 class="g9-interactive-title">Motion in a Plane Research Suite</h3><p class="g9-interactive-desc">Explore this concept with interactive controls and real-time visual response.</p><a class="g9-bridge-link g9-action-interactive" data-g9-interactive-link href="../../../public/physics/motion-2d/explorers/motion-in-a-plane/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Try it visually &rarr;</a></aside><aside class="g9-interactive-bridge" data-g9-interactive-bridge><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-interactive">Try Visually</span><h3 class="g9-interactive-title">2D Motion Master Suite</h3><p class="g9-interactive-desc">Explore this concept with interactive controls and real-time visual response.</p><a class="g9-bridge-link g9-action-interactive" data-g9-interactive-link href="../../../public/physics/motion-in-2d/explorers/motions_in_2d/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Try it visually &rarr;</a></aside></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-chips" data-g9-component="LEARNING_TRANSITIONS" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="CHIPS"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="learning_transitions"><h4>Next steps</h4><div class="g9-transitions-row" data-g9-transitions><a class="g9-transition-chip g9-chip-practice" data-g9-transition="PRACTICE" href="core2.html">Practice this concept &rarr;</a><a class="g9-transition-chip g9-chip-qb" data-g9-transition="QUESTION_BANK" href="../../../public/question-bank/index.html?capability=MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS">All questions in Question Bank &rarr;</a></div></div></div></section></div></div></article><article id="g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1A" data-g9-search-text="Physics Motion in 2 D Apply constant-acceleration kinematics component by component Medium concept Apply constant-acceleration kinematics component by component" class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-concept-header" data-g9-component="CONCEPT_HEADER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="CONCEPT_HEADER"><h2>Apply constant-acceleration kinematics component by component</h2><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1A" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-KIN-2D-MOTION" data-g9-meta-value="Motion in 2 D"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Motion in 2 D</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" data-g9-meta-value="Apply constant-acceleration kinematics component by component"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Apply constant-acceleration kinematics component by component</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span></div></div></section><div class="g9-split"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-construction" data-blueprint-slot="construction" data-required="true"><section id="g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-1" class="g9-cu" data-g9-cu="CU-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-1"><div class="g9-component g9-c-unit-header" data-g9-component="UNIT_HEADER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="UNIT_HEADER" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-1"><div class="g9-unit-head"><span class="g9-unit-no" aria-hidden="true">1</span><h3>Construction</h3><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-concept">Medium concept</span></div><nav class="g9-cu-nav" data-g9-unit-navigation aria-label="Concept Book section navigation"><span data-g9-route-position>Section 2 of 3</span><span class="g9-cu-nav-links"><a data-g9-prev-section href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-1" aria-label="Previous section: Construction 1">← Previous</a><a data-g9-next-section href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" aria-label="Next section: Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences">Next →</a></span></nav></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-banner" data-g9-component="KEY_STEP" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="BANNER"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="inferential_jump"><h4>The key step</h4><p>The one-dimensional constant-acceleration equations can be applied to each perpendicular component independently when a_x and a_y are each constant over the same interval.</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-tile" data-g9-component="MODEL_CONTRACT" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_TILE"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-prerequisites"><summary>What you need before this</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="entry_assumptions"><h4>Prerequisites</h4><ul><li>Can use the existing one-dimensional constant-acceleration model.</li><li>Can keep x/y components separate with one common time.</li></ul><ul><li data-g9-prereq="CAP-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" data-bridged="true"><a href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS">Represent one plane-motion event as independent signed x/y component histories that share one elapsed time.</a></li><li data-g9-prereq="CAP-KIN-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" data-bridged="true">Derive the constant-acceleration equations from one velocity-time graph (Physics)</li></ul></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-step-cards" data-g9-component="CONSTRUCTION_STEPS" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEP_CARDS" data-g9-component-items="3" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-1"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="construction"><ol><li data-g9-step="K2D2-1"><strong>Check separately whether a_x and a_y are constant over the interval before choosing constant-acceleration equations on either axis.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> The familiar one-dimensional kinematic relations are exact only on an axis whose acceleration component is constant over the interval.<br><em>Result:</em> per-axis model-validity decision</li><li data-g9-step="K2D2-2"><strong>Apply the one-dimensional constant-acceleration relations independently to x and y using only that axis&#x27;s signed values and the same t.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> Each Cartesian component obeys the same one-dimensional relation when its acceleration component is constant.<br><em>Result:</em> v_x=u_x+a_x t; v_y=u_y+a_y t; Delta x=u_x t+0.5a_x t^2; Delta y=u_y t+0.5a_y t^2</li><li data-g9-step="K2D2-3"><strong>If one axis determines an event time, reuse that same time on the other axis before recombining and checking the plane state.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> The event occurs once for the object; a time obtained from one component is the clock value for every component at that same event.<br><em>Result:</em> event time transferred across axes, then one checked plane state</li></ol></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-equation-card" data-g9-component="EQUATIONS" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_EQUATION_CARD" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-1"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-equations"><summary>Formula reference · meaning and conditions</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="equation_matrix"><h4>Equations and validity</h4><div class="g9-table-scroll" data-g9-equation-matrix><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Equation</th><th scope="col">What it tells you</th><th scope="col">When you can use it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr data-g9-relation-ref="REL-KIN-2D-VELOCITY"><td><p class="g9-expr">v_x = u_x + a_x t; v_y = u_y + a_y t</p></td><td><p>Each perpendicular velocity component follows the 1D constant-acceleration relation over one common interval.</p></td><td><ul><li>a_x and a_y are constant over the interval.</li><li>Both component equations use the same elapsed time and frame.</li></ul></td></tr><tr data-g9-relation-ref="REL-KIN-2D-DISPLACEMENT"><td><p class="g9-expr">Delta x = u_x t + 0.5 a_x t^2; Delta y = u_y t + 0.5 a_y t^2</p></td><td><p>Each perpendicular displacement component follows the 1D constant-acceleration displacement relation over one common interval.</p></td><td><ul><li>a_x and a_y are constant over the interval.</li><li>Both component equations use the same elapsed time and frame.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></div></details></div></section></section></div><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-representation" data-blueprint-slot="representation" data-required="true"></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-repair_closure" data-blueprint-slot="repair_closure" data-required="true"><section class="g9-cu-support" data-g9-support-for="CU-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-1"><h3>Construction 1</h3><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-trap-card" data-g9-component="TRAP_REPAIR" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_TRAP_CARD" data-g9-component-items="3" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-1"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-trap-repair"><summary>Mistake clinic · diagnose and repair</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="wrong_path"><h4>A tempting wrong path</h4><ul><li>The magnitude of acceleration should be inserted unchanged into both x and y equations.</li><li>If one acceleration component is constant, the constant-acceleration equations are automatically valid on both axes.</li><li>A time found from the x equation may be replaced by a different convenient time in the y equation.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="diagnose"><h4>Diagnose</h4><ul><li>If a=(10 i + 4 j) m/s², should both component equations use a=√116?</li><li>If a_x is constant but a_y changes with time, may the same constant-acceleration displacement formula be used exactly on y over the whole interval?</li><li>If x-motion says an event occurs at t=3 s, what time belongs in the y equation for that same event?</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="repair"><h4>Repair</h4><ul><li>No. Use a_x=10 only in the x equation and a_y=4 only in the y equation; the magnitude is not either component.</li><li>No. Model validity is checked per axis. A constant a_x does not make a_y constant.</li><li>Use t=3 s. Both components describe the same event and therefore share one clock.</li></ul></div></div></details></div></section></section></div></div><div class="g9-split g9-split-support-only"><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-repair_closure" data-blueprint-slot="repair_closure" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-recall-card" data-g9-component="EXIT_RECALL" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="RECALL_CARD"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_task"><h4>Now you do one</h4><p>A particle starts with u=(0,5) m/s and constant a=(10,4) m/s². Set up the x and y position equations at common time t without solving a particular numerical t.</p></div><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your answer<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1A-MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-exit"><summary>Model answer</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1A-MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-exit"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_answer"><p>Use Delta x=0*t+0.5(10)t² and Delta y=5t+0.5(4)t² with the same t.</p><ol><li>Read signed component initial velocities and accelerations.</li><li>Apply the 1D constant-acceleration displacement relation separately.</li><li>Keep one common elapsed time.</li></ol></div></template></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-chips" data-g9-component="PRACTICE_LINKS" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="CHIPS"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="practice_navigation"><h4>Practice this concept in Core2</h4><ul><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q33" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q33">IIT-JEE · 2011 · Paper II · Q33</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2025-P2-Q15" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2025-P2-Q15">JEE (Advanced) · 2025 · Paper 2 · Q15</a></li></ul></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-chips" data-g9-component="LEARNING_TRANSITIONS" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="CHIPS"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="learning_transitions"><h4>Next steps</h4><div class="g9-transitions-row" data-g9-transitions><a class="g9-transition-chip g9-chip-practice" data-g9-transition="PRACTICE" href="core2.html">Practice this concept &rarr;</a><a class="g9-transition-chip g9-chip-qb" data-g9-transition="QUESTION_BANK" href="../../../public/question-bank/index.html?capability=MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION">All questions in Question Bank &rarr;</a></div></div></div></section></div></div></article><article id="g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1A" data-g9-search-text="Physics Motion in 2 D Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences Medium concept Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences" class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-concept-header" data-g9-component="CONCEPT_HEADER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="CONCEPT_HEADER"><h2>Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences</h2><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1A" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-KIN-2D-MOTION" data-g9-meta-value="Motion in 2 D"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Motion in 2 D</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-meta-value="Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span></div></div></section><div class="g9-split"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-construction" data-blueprint-slot="construction" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-banner" data-g9-component="KEY_STEP" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="BANNER"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="inferential_jump"><h4>The key step</h4><p>After release in the ideal near-Earth model, projectile motion is not a new set of laws: it is the same component kinematics with a_x=0 and a_y=-g (for +y upward).</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-tile" data-g9-component="MODEL_CONTRACT" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_TILE"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-prerequisites"><summary>What you need before this</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="entry_assumptions"><h4>Prerequisites</h4><ul><li>Can solve 2D constant-acceleration component equations.</li><li>If an oblique launch angle is given, angle-based vector decomposition is available as a separate prerequisite for that question.</li></ul><ul><li data-g9-prereq="CAP-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" data-bridged="true"><a href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION">Apply constant-acceleration kinematics independently to x and y components of one plane-motion event.</a></li></ul></div></div></details></div><section class="g9-cu g9-path-bridge" data-g9-derivation-bridge="true"><nav class="g9-cu-nav" data-g9-unit-navigation aria-label="Concept Book section navigation"><span data-g9-route-position>Section 3 of 3</span><span class="g9-cu-nav-links"><a data-g9-prev-section href="#g9-CORE1A--CU-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-1" aria-label="Previous section: Construction 1">← Previous</a></span></nav><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="construction"><ol><li data-g9-step="K2D3-1"><strong>Decide whether the object is in ideal near-Earth free flight after release; neglect air resistance, declare +y, then set a_x=0 and a_y=-g.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> The standard projectile equations are a conditional specialization of 2D constant acceleration, not an automatic label for every launched object.<br><em>Result:</em> gravity-only projectile acceleration state</li><li data-g9-step="K2D3-2"><strong>Use supplied or separately resolved u_x and u_y with one common t in the horizontal and vertical component equations.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> Horizontal, oblique and downward/upward initial components change the initial state, not the gravity-only model.<br><em>Result:</em> v_x=u_x; v_y=u_y-g t; Delta x=u_x t; Delta y=u_y t-0.5g t^2</li><li data-g9-step="K2D3-3"><strong>Translate the requested event into the component condition that defines it before solving for time.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> Apex, same-height return, ground impact and a specified later instant are different events; each is identified by a different component condition.<br><em>Result:</em> event condition chosen before calculation</li><li data-g9-step="K2D3-4"><strong>For an apex event, set v_y=0 while keeping v_x=u_x and a_y=-g.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> At the highest point only the vertical velocity component is momentarily zero; gravity still accelerates downward and horizontal motion continues.<br><em>Result:</em> apex condition v_y=0 with nonzero horizontal velocity and nonzero downward acceleration</li><li data-g9-step="K2D3-5"><strong>For a return to the launch height, set Delta y=0 and treat any same-height flight-time/range shortcut as conditional on that equality.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> The shortcut follows from the vertical displacement returning to its initial value; it is not valid when the landing height differs.<br><em>Result:</em> same-height event condition and its validity boundary</li><li data-g9-step="K2D3-6"><strong>For any unequal-height landing, use the actual Delta y=y_f-y_i in the vertical position equation and retain the actual u_y; for a horizontal launch specifically, set u_y=0, then obtain the event time vertically and share it with x.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> Initial vertical velocity and landing displacement describe different facts. The landing event is fixed by the actual vertical geometry, while u_y=0 is only the horizontal-launch initial condition.<br><em>Result:</em> Delta y=y_f-y_i=u_y t_event-0.5g t_event^2; for horizontal launch u_y=0; reuse the solved t_event on x</li><li data-g9-step="K2D3-7"><strong>At the selected event time, reconstruct the requested velocity, speed, direction, displacement or range from simultaneous components and check qualitative limits.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> The reported projectile result is a consequence of one component model and one event, so its components must refer to the same instant and satisfy a_x=0, a_y=-g.<br><em>Result:</em> checked projectile consequence at one physical event</li></ol></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="equation_matrix"><h4>Equations and validity</h4><div class="g9-table-scroll" data-g9-equation-matrix><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Equation</th><th scope="col">What it tells you</th><th scope="col">When you can use it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr data-g9-relation-ref="REL-PROJECTILE-COMPONENT-MODEL"><td><p class="g9-expr">a_x = 0; a_y = -g; v_x = u_x; v_y = u_y - g t; Delta x = u_x t; Delta y = u_y t - 0.5 g t^2</p></td><td><p>With +y upward, air resistance neglected and near-Earth constant g, projectile motion is 2D constant acceleration specialized to zero horizontal and downward vertical acceleration.</p></td><td><ul><li>Near-Earth region with approximately constant g.</li><li>Air resistance neglected.</li><li>+y is declared upward for the displayed signs.</li><li>The object is in free flight after release.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></section></section></div><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-repair_closure" data-blueprint-slot="repair_closure" data-required="true"><section class="g9-cu-support" data-g9-support-for="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL"><h3>Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences</h3><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="wrong_path"><h4>A tempting wrong path</h4><ul><li>A projectile needs a horizontal force to keep moving horizontally.</li><li>A horizontal launch has no vertical velocity throughout the flight.</li><li>At the highest point the whole velocity and the acceleration are both zero.</li><li>Same-height flight formulas may be used whenever the question is about a projectile range or flight time.</li><li>At two points of the same height on an ideal trajectory, the velocity vectors are identical.</li><li>Horizontal speed determines how long a horizontally launched object takes to fall.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="diagnose"><h4>Diagnose</h4><ul><li>If a_x=0 after release, can v_x still be nonzero?</li><li>One second after a horizontal launch, is v_y still zero under gravity?</li><li>At the apex of an oblique projectile, which of v_x, v_y and a_y is zero?</li><li>A ball launched from a roof lands on the ground below. May the same-height time-of-flight shortcut be used without checking the vertical displacement?</li><li>On ascent and descent at the same height, how do the horizontal and vertical velocity components compare?</li><li>If the horizontal launch speed doubles from the same height, does the ideal fall time change?</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="repair"><h4>Repair</h4><ul><li>Yes. Zero horizontal acceleration means horizontal velocity stays constant, not that it becomes zero.</li><li>No. u_y=0 only at release; a_y=-g makes v_y=-gt afterward.</li><li>Only v_y is zero at that instant. v_x remains u_x and a_y remains -g.</li><li>No. Same-height shortcuts require Delta y=0 between launch and landing. For unequal heights, solve the vertical position condition.</li><li>v_x is the same, while v_y has equal magnitude and opposite sign; the speed matches but the velocity vector does not.</li><li>No. The vertical position equation determines the fall time; horizontal speed changes the range, not the fall time.</li></ul></div></section><div class="g9-component g9-c-recall-card" data-g9-component="EXIT_RECALL" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="RECALL_CARD"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_task"><h4>Now you do one</h4><p>A stone is launched horizontally from a cliff with speed u. With +y upward and air resistance neglected, write its component acceleration, velocity and displacement relations after release and explain why one common t is used.</p></div><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your answer<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1A-MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-exit"><summary>Model answer</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1A-MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-exit"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_answer"><p>a_x=0, a_y=-g; v_x=u, v_y=-gt; Delta x=ut, Delta y=-0.5gt², all at the same elapsed time t.</p><ol><li>After release only gravity acts in the ideal model.</li><li>Gravity has no horizontal component in the chosen frame.</li><li>Both component coordinates describe the same stone at the same instant.</li></ol></div></template></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-chips" data-g9-component="PRACTICE_LINKS" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="CHIPS"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="practice_navigation"><h4>Practice this concept in Core2</h4><ul><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q33" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q33">IIT-JEE · 2011 · Paper II · Q33</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2018-P2-Q08" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2018-P2-Q08">JEE (Advanced) · 2018 · Paper 2 · Q8</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2019-P2-Q09" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2019-P2-Q09">JEE (Advanced) · 2019 · Paper 2 · Q9</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q05" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q05">JEE (Advanced) · 2021 · Paper 1 · Q5</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q06" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q06">JEE (Advanced) · 2021 · Paper 1 · Q6</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2024-P2-Q09" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2024-P2-Q09">JEE (Advanced) · 2024 · Paper 2 · Q9</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2012-P1-Q05" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2012-P1-Q05">IIT-JEE · 2012 · Paper I · Q5</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2022-P1-Q08" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2022-P1-Q08">JEE (Advanced) · 2022 · Paper 1 · Q8</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2023-P1-Q01" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2023-P1-Q01">JEE (Advanced) · 2023 · Paper 1 · Q1</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q26" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q26">IIT-JEE · 2011 · Paper II · Q26</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2026-P1-Q06" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2026-P1-Q06">JEE (Advanced) · 2026 · Paper 1 · Q6</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2013-P1-Q07" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2013-P1-Q07">JEE (Advanced) · 2013 · Paper 1 · Q7</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEMAIN-2026-04APR-S2-Q29" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" href="#g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-JEEMAIN-2026-04APR-S2-Q29">JEE Main · 2026 · Session 2 · 04 Apr 2026 · Shift 2 · Q29</a></li></ul></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-chips" data-g9-component="LEARNING_TRANSITIONS" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="CHIPS"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="learning_transitions"><h4>Next steps</h4><div class="g9-transitions-row" data-g9-transitions><a class="g9-transition-chip g9-chip-practice" data-g9-transition="PRACTICE" href="core2.html">Practice this concept &rarr;</a><a class="g9-transition-chip g9-chip-qb" data-g9-transition="QUESTION_BANK" href="../../../public/question-bank/index.html?capability=MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL">All questions in Question Bank &rarr;</a></div></div></div></section></div></div></article></section><section id="g9-role-CORE1B" data-g9-role-section="CORE1B"><h1>Two-dimensional kinematics and projectile model: Reconstruction</h1><article id="g9-CORE1B--MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1B" data-g9-search-text="Physics Motion in 2 D Split one plane-motion event into independent x/y components with one clock Medium concept Split one plane-motion event into independent x/y components with one clock" class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><h2>Split one plane-motion event into independent x/y components with one clock</h2><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1B" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-KIN-2D-MOTION" data-g9-meta-value="Motion in 2 D"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Motion in 2 D</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" data-g9-meta-value="Split one plane-motion event into independent x/y components with one clock"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Split one plane-motion event into independent x/y components with one clock</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span></div></section><div class="g9-split g9-split-primary-only"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-attempt" data-blueprint-slot="attempt" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="predict"><h4>Predict</h4><p>If x and y components are independent, does that mean the object has two different clocks?</p></div><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your attempt<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-reconstruction" data-blueprint-slot="reconstruction" data-required="true"><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-reconstruct"><summary>Reconstruct</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-reconstruct"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="reconstruct"><ol><li>What must be declared before any component can have a sign?</li><li>What separates between axes, and what must stay common because both descriptions belong to one event?</li><li>Which x and y values may be recombined into one state?</li></ol></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="diagnose"><h4>Diagnose</h4><ul><li>At one instant 2 s after launch, may the x equation use t=2 s while the y equation uses another time?</li><li>May x(2 s) be combined with y(3 s) to describe the particle&#x27;s position?</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="repair"><h4>Repair</h4><ul><li>No. Independence separates the axis equations; it does not give the same object two clocks.</li><li>No. A plane state is a simultaneous pair. Recombine x(t) and y(t) only at the same t in the same frame.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="success_criteria"><h4>What your answer should contain</h4><p>A two-column x/y setup with one shared t.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="model_response"><h4>What a complete answer does</h4><p>Axes are kept separate while time/frame remain shared.</p><ul><li>Separate x and y equations; same t for both.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="rejoin_jump"><h4>The step you rebuilt</h4><p>Perpendicular components may be solved separately because each axis equation contains only that axis&#x27;s quantities, but they still describe one object at one physical time.</p></div></template><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="boundary_test"><h4>Boundary test</h4><p>If a_x=0 but a_y is nonzero, may v_x stay constant while v_y changes?</p></div><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-boundary"><summary>Boundary answer</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-boundary"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="boundary_answer"><p>Yes. That is exactly what independent component evolution permits.</p><p>One component can change while the other does not.</p></div></template></section></div></div></article><article id="g9-CORE1B--MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1B" data-g9-search-text="Physics Motion in 2 D Apply constant-acceleration kinematics component by component Medium concept Apply constant-acceleration kinematics component by component" class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><h2>Apply constant-acceleration kinematics component by component</h2><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1B" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-KIN-2D-MOTION" data-g9-meta-value="Motion in 2 D"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Motion in 2 D</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" data-g9-meta-value="Apply constant-acceleration kinematics component by component"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Apply constant-acceleration kinematics component by component</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span></div></section><div class="g9-split g9-split-primary-only"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-attempt" data-blueprint-slot="attempt" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="predict"><h4>Predict</h4><p>If a_x is nonzero and a_y is zero, which velocity component changes with time?</p></div><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your attempt<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-reconstruction" data-blueprint-slot="reconstruction" data-required="true"><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-reconstruct"><summary>Reconstruct</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-reconstruct"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="reconstruct"><ol><li>Before using a constant-acceleration formula, what must be true about that axis&#x27;s acceleration component?</li><li>What does the 1D relation become when written only along x, and what changes for y?</li><li>If one component equation finds the event time, what time must the other component use?</li></ol></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="diagnose"><h4>Diagnose</h4><ul><li>If a=(10 i + 4 j) m/s², should both component equations use a=√116?</li><li>If a_x is constant but a_y changes with time, may the same constant-acceleration displacement formula be used exactly on y over the whole interval?</li><li>If x-motion says an event occurs at t=3 s, what time belongs in the y equation for that same event?</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="repair"><h4>Repair</h4><ul><li>No. Use a_x=10 only in the x equation and a_y=4 only in the y equation; the magnitude is not either component.</li><li>No. Model validity is checked per axis. A constant a_x does not make a_y constant.</li><li>Use t=3 s. Both components describe the same event and therefore share one clock.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="success_criteria"><h4>What your answer should contain</h4><p>Two correct component constant-acceleration equations using one t.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="model_response"><h4>What a complete answer does</h4><p>Each axis uses only its own signed initial values and acceleration while sharing the same time.</p><ul><li>v_x=u_x+a_x t and v_y=u_y+a_y t with one t.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="rejoin_jump"><h4>The step you rebuilt</h4><p>The one-dimensional constant-acceleration equations can be applied to each perpendicular component independently when a_x and a_y are each constant over the same interval.</p></div></template><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="boundary_test"><h4>Boundary test</h4><p>If a_y changes with time while a_x stays constant, may the constant-acceleration equations still be exact on both axes for the whole interval?</p></div><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-boundary"><summary>Boundary answer</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-boundary"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="boundary_answer"><p>No. The x relation may remain exact, but the y constant-acceleration relation is not exact over that interval.</p><p>Model validity is checked per component.</p></div></template></section></div></div></article><article id="g9-CORE1B--MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1B" data-g9-search-text="Physics Motion in 2 D Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences Medium concept Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences" class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><h2>Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences</h2><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1B" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-KIN-2D-MOTION" data-g9-meta-value="Motion in 2 D"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Motion in 2 D</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-meta-value="Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span></div></section><div class="g9-split g9-split-primary-only"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-attempt" data-blueprint-slot="attempt" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="predict"><h4>Predict</h4><p>Immediately after a horizontal launch, which acceleration component is zero in the ideal model?</p></div><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your attempt<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-reconstruction" data-blueprint-slot="reconstruction" data-required="true"><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-reconstruct"><summary>Reconstruct</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-reconstruct"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="reconstruct"><ol><li>What forces remain after release in the idealized problem?</li><li>What are the horizontal and vertical component equations, and what clock do they share?</li><li>What exact component condition identifies the event the question asks about?</li><li>If the event is the apex, what becomes zero and what definitely does not?</li><li>Does launch height equal landing height? If not, which shortcut becomes invalid?</li></ol></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="diagnose"><h4>Diagnose</h4><ul><li>If a_x=0 after release, can v_x still be nonzero?</li><li>One second after a horizontal launch, is v_y still zero under gravity?</li><li>At the apex of an oblique projectile, which of v_x, v_y and a_y is zero?</li><li>A ball launched from a roof lands on the ground below. May the same-height time-of-flight shortcut be used without checking the vertical displacement?</li><li>On ascent and descent at the same height, how do the horizontal and vertical velocity components compare?</li><li>If the horizontal launch speed doubles from the same height, does the ideal fall time change?</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="repair"><h4>Repair</h4><ul><li>Yes. Zero horizontal acceleration means horizontal velocity stays constant, not that it becomes zero.</li><li>No. u_y=0 only at release; a_y=-g makes v_y=-gt afterward.</li><li>Only v_y is zero at that instant. v_x remains u_x and a_y remains -g.</li><li>No. Same-height shortcuts require Delta y=0 between launch and landing. For unequal heights, solve the vertical position condition.</li><li>v_x is the same, while v_y has equal magnitude and opposite sign; the speed matches but the velocity vector does not.</li><li>No. The vertical position equation determines the fall time; horizontal speed changes the range, not the fall time.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="success_criteria"><h4>What your answer should contain</h4><p>A model declaration and component-equation setup for a horizontal or oblique projectile.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="model_response"><h4>What a complete answer does</h4><p>The learner selects gravity-only free flight, uses a_x=0/a_y=-g and one common time before calculating any named projectile quantity.</p><ul><li>State assumptions, set component accelerations, then solve x/y with one t.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="rejoin_jump"><h4>The step you rebuilt</h4><p>After release in the ideal near-Earth model, projectile motion is not a new set of laws: it is the same component kinematics with a_x=0 and a_y=-g (for +y upward).</p></div></template><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="boundary_test"><h4>Boundary test</h4><p>A launched object continues under significant horizontal thrust after release. May the standard a_x=0 projectile specialization be used?</p></div><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-boundary"><summary>Boundary answer</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1B-MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-boundary"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="boundary_answer"><p>No. The object is not in gravity-only free flight; its horizontal acceleration is not zero under that model.</p><p>Projectile equations are conditional consequences of a declared force/model state.</p></div></template></section></div></div></article></section><section id="g9-role-CORE2" data-g9-role-section="CORE2"><h1>Two-dimensional kinematics and projectile model: Source questions</h1><article id="g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q33" data-g9-unit="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q33" data-g9-kind="QUESTION" data-g9-role="CORE2" data-g9-search-text="Physics Motion in 2 D Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences Medium concept Multi-step reasoning Motion in 2D component and projectile practice Integer answer IIT-JEE 2011 · Paper II · Q33 Verified past-exam adaptation Inside a train accelerating uniformly along a straight track, a boy throws a ball forward at 10 m/s relative to the train and 60 degrees above the horizontal. To catch it again at the launch height he moves 1.15 m forward inside the train. Using g=10 m/s^2, find the train acceleration in m/s^2. Throw speed is relative to the train. Train acceleration is constant. Catch occurs at the original vertical level." class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-header" data-g9-component="IDENTITY" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="HEADER"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="source_identity"><h2>IIT-JEE · 2011 · Paper II · Q33</h2><p class="g9-prov">Official past paper, faithful restatement of the original</p></div><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE2" data-g9-meta-record="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q33" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-KIN-2D-MOTION" data-g9-meta-value="Motion in 2 D"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Motion in 2 D</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-meta-value="Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="question-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q33" data-g9-meta-value="D3"><strong>Question difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Multi-step reasoning</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="family" data-g9-meta-ref="FAM-PHY-KIN-2D-PRACTICE" data-g9-meta-value="Motion in 2D component and projectile practice"><strong>Family:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Motion in 2D component and projectile practice</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="question-type" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q33" data-g9-meta-value="integer_answer"><strong>Question type:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Integer answer</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="source" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q33" data-g9-meta-value="IIT-JEE 2011 · Paper II · Q33"><strong>Source:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>IIT-JEE 2011 · Paper II · Q33</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="provenance" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q33" data-g9-meta-value="PYQ_ADAPTED"><strong>Provenance:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Verified past-exam adaptation</span></span></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-link-list" data-g9-component="SOURCE_PDF" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="LINK_LIST"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="source_pdf"><a class="g9-pdf-link g9-source-pdf" data-g9-source-pdf href="https://jeeadv.ac.in/past_qps/2011_2.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" type="application/pdf" aria-label="Open the original past paper as a PDF to print it"><svg class="g9-pdf-icon" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="24" height="24"><path d="M6 2.5h8l4.5 4.5v14.5H6z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linejoin="round"/><path d="M14 2.5V7h4.5M9 12h6.5M9 15.5h6.5M9 19h4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg><span>Source paper (PDF)</span></a></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-grid" data-g9-component="DIFFICULTY_WHY" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_GRID"><button type="button" class="g9-why-toggle" data-g9-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="g9-why-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q33"><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-band" data-g9-band="D3">D3 · 7/10 · about 3 min</span><span>Why this difficulty?</span></button><div id="g9-why-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q33" class="g9-why" hidden><p>Combines a projectile event clock with accelerated relative horizontal displacement.</p><div class=g9-dgrid><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Choosing the model</span><b>2/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Representation</span><b>2/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Reasoning chain</span><b>1/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Algebra and numbers</span><b>1/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Traps and exceptions</span><b>1/2</b></div></div></div></div></section><div class="g9-split"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-attempt" data-blueprint-slot="attempt" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-stem" data-g9-component="STEM" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEM"><div class="g9-eyebrow">Attempt first</div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="stem"><p>Inside a train accelerating uniformly along a straight track, a boy throws a ball forward at 10 m/s relative to the train and 60 degrees above the horizontal. To catch it again at the launch height he moves 1.15 m forward inside the train. Using g=10 m/<span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, find the train acceleration in m/<span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-callout-info" data-g9-component="CONDITIONS" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CALLOUT_INFO"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="conditions"><h4>Conditions</h4><ul><li>Throw speed is relative to the train.</li><li>Train acceleration is constant.</li><li>Catch occurs at the original vertical level.</li></ul></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-callout-warn" data-g9-component="TRAP" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CALLOUT_WARN"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core2-wrong-route"><summary>Common wrong route · open if you want a warning</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="common_wrong_route"><h4>Common wrong route</h4><p>Treating the train as constant-velocity and using only the ball&#x27;s horizontal component.</p></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-attempt" data-g9-component="ATTEMPT" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="ATTEMPT"><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your answer<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div></div></section></div><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-representation" data-blueprint-slot="representation" data-required="false"></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-support" data-blueprint-slot="support" data-required="false"><div class="g9-component g9-c-ladder" data-g9-component="HINT_LADDER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="LADDER" data-g9-component-items="3"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core2-hints"><summary>Need a hint? · guided support</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-eyebrow">Hint ladder · reveal only what you need</div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="authored_core2_support"><h4>Guided support</h4><div class="g9-ladder" data-g9-ladder-ref="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q33-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-group="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL"><ol data-g9-ladder></ol><ol class="g9-rung-map" aria-label="Rungs still to come"><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H0</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Represent</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H1</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Connect</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H2</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Carry out</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li></ol><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q33-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-1"><li data-g9-rung="1" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[0]" data-g9-support-reveals="CONCEPT" data-g9-support-kind="REPRESENT" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q33-MOVE-1"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H0</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Represent</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><div data-g9-support-reveal><p>Use the vertical return-to-launch-height condition to find the one common catch time.</p></div></li></template><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q33-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-2"><li data-g9-rung="2" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[1]" data-g9-support-reveals="METHOD" data-g9-support-kind="CONNECT" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q33-MOVE-2"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H1</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Connect</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><div data-g9-support-reveal><p>Write ball-minus-train horizontal displacement over that same interval.</p></div></li></template><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q33-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-3"><li data-g9-rung="3" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[2]" data-g9-support-reveals="METHOD" data-g9-support-kind="EXECUTE" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q33-MOVE-3"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H2</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Carry out</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><div data-g9-support-reveal><p>Set the relative displacement equal to the boy&#x27;s 1.15 m motion.</p></div></li></template><button type="button" data-g9-next-rung>Show guided support</button></div></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-link-list" data-g9-component="CONCEPT_NAV" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="LINK_LIST"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="concept_navigation"><h4>Need the concept again?</h4><ul><li><a data-g9-concept-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q33" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL">Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences</a></li><li><a data-g9-concept-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q33" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS">Split one plane-motion event into independent x/y components with one clock</a></li><li><a data-g9-concept-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q33" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION">Apply constant-acceleration kinematics component by component</a></li></ul></div></div></section></div></div><section class="blueprint-slot slot-solution" data-blueprint-slot="solution" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure" data-g9-component="SOLUTION" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE"><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q33-solution"><summary>Answer and working</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q33-solution"><div class="g9-component g9-c-step-list" data-g9-component="SOLUTION_STEPS" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEP_LIST" data-g9-component-items="3"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="structured_working"><h4>Reasoning route</h4><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="1" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q33-MOVE-1" data-g9-solution-kind="REPRESENT" data-g9-solution-stage="REPRESENT" data-g9-solution-crux="false"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">1</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Represent</h4><p data-g9-solution-action>Vertical motion fixes the common flight time: T=2(10 sin60)/10=sqrt(3) s.</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>stated source quantities</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> This follows from the stated physical or chemical model and the quantities preserved in the source-matched task.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> intermediate result 1</p></div></section><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="2" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q33-MOVE-2" data-g9-solution-kind="CONNECT" data-g9-solution-stage="CONNECT" data-g9-solution-crux="true"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">2</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Connect</h4><p class="g9-prov" data-g9-crux-label>Key move</p><p data-g9-solution-action>Relative horizontal displacement in the accelerating train is (10 cos60)T - (1/2)aT^2.</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>stated source quantities</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> This follows from the stated physical or chemical model and the quantities preserved in the source-matched task.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> intermediate result 2</p></div></section><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="3" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q33-MOVE-3" data-g9-solution-kind="TRANSFORM" data-g9-solution-stage="CALCULATE" data-g9-solution-crux="false"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">3</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Calculate</h4><p data-g9-solution-action>Set this equal to 1.15 m: 5sqrt(3) - 1.5a = 1.15, giving a≈5 m/s^2.</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>stated source quantities</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> This follows from the stated physical or chemical model and the quantities preserved in the source-matched task.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> 5 m/s^2.</p></div></section></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-answer-box" data-g9-component="ANSWER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="ANSWER_BOX"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="answer"><p>5 m/<span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-check-box" data-g9-component="CHECK" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CHECK_BOX"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="independent_check"><h4>Independent check</h4><p>For a=5, the relative displacement is about 1.16 m, consistent with the stated 1.15 m after rounding.</p></div></div></template></div></section></article><article id="g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2018-P2-Q08" data-g9-unit="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2018-P2-Q08" data-g9-kind="QUESTION" data-g9-role="CORE2" data-g9-search-text="Physics Motion in 2 D Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences Medium concept Connected reasoning Motion in 2D component and projectile practice Numerical value JEE (Advanced) 2018 · Paper 2 · Q8 Verified past-exam adaptation A ball is launched from level ground at 45 degrees and reaches a maximum height of 120 m. At its first impact with the ground it loses half its kinetic energy. Immediately after the bounce its velocity is at 30 degrees above the horizontal. Find the new maximum height. First flight starts and ends at ground level. Half the kinetic energy is lost at the first bounce. Post-bounce launch angle is 30 degrees." class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-header" data-g9-component="IDENTITY" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="HEADER"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="source_identity"><h2>JEE (Advanced) · 2018 · Paper 2 · Q8</h2><p class="g9-prov">Official past paper, faithful restatement of the original</p></div><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE2" data-g9-meta-record="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2018-P2-Q08" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-KIN-2D-MOTION" data-g9-meta-value="Motion in 2 D"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Motion in 2 D</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-meta-value="Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="question-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2018-P2-Q08" data-g9-meta-value="D2"><strong>Question difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Connected reasoning</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="family" data-g9-meta-ref="FAM-PHY-KIN-2D-PRACTICE" data-g9-meta-value="Motion in 2D component and projectile practice"><strong>Family:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Motion in 2D component and projectile practice</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="question-type" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2018-P2-Q08" data-g9-meta-value="numerical_value"><strong>Question type:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Numerical value</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="source" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2018-P2-Q08" data-g9-meta-value="JEE (Advanced) 2018 · Paper 2 · Q8"><strong>Source:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>JEE (Advanced) 2018 · Paper 2 · Q8</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="provenance" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2018-P2-Q08" data-g9-meta-value="PYQ_ADAPTED"><strong>Provenance:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Verified past-exam adaptation</span></span></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-link-list" data-g9-component="SOURCE_PDF" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="LINK_LIST"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="source_pdf"><a class="g9-pdf-link g9-source-pdf" data-g9-source-pdf href="https://jeeadv.ac.in/past_qps/2018_2.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" type="application/pdf" aria-label="Open the original past paper as a PDF to print it"><svg class="g9-pdf-icon" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="24" height="24"><path d="M6 2.5h8l4.5 4.5v14.5H6z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linejoin="round"/><path d="M14 2.5V7h4.5M9 12h6.5M9 15.5h6.5M9 19h4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg><span>Source paper (PDF)</span></a></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-grid" data-g9-component="DIFFICULTY_WHY" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_GRID"><button type="button" class="g9-why-toggle" data-g9-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="g9-why-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2018-P2-Q08"><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-band" data-g9-band="D2">D2 · 5/10 · about 2 min</span><span>Why this difficulty?</span></button><div id="g9-why-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2018-P2-Q08" class="g9-why" hidden><p>Requires translating energy loss into speed and then extracting the new vertical component.</p><div class=g9-dgrid><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Choosing the model</span><b>1/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Representation</span><b>2/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Reasoning chain</span><b>1/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Algebra and numbers</span><b>1/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Traps and exceptions</span><b>0/2</b></div></div></div></div></section><div class="g9-split"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-attempt" data-blueprint-slot="attempt" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-stem" data-g9-component="STEM" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEM"><div class="g9-eyebrow">Attempt first</div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="stem"><p>A ball is launched from level ground at 45 degrees and reaches a maximum height of 120 m. At its first impact with the ground it loses half its kinetic energy. Immediately after the bounce its velocity is at 30 degrees above the horizontal. Find the new maximum height.</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-callout-info" data-g9-component="CONDITIONS" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CALLOUT_INFO"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="conditions"><h4>Conditions</h4><ul><li>First flight starts and ends at ground level.</li><li>Half the kinetic energy is lost at the first bounce.</li><li>Post-bounce launch angle is 30 degrees.</li></ul></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-callout-warn" data-g9-component="TRAP" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CALLOUT_WARN"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core2-wrong-route"><summary>Common wrong route · open if you want a warning</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="common_wrong_route"><h4>Common wrong route</h4><p>Halving the speed instead of halving speed squared when kinetic energy halves.</p></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-attempt" data-g9-component="ATTEMPT" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="ATTEMPT"><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your answer<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div></div></section></div><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-representation" data-blueprint-slot="representation" data-required="false"></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-support" data-blueprint-slot="support" data-required="false"><div class="g9-component g9-c-ladder" data-g9-component="HINT_LADDER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="LADDER" data-g9-component-items="3"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core2-hints"><summary>Need a hint? · guided support</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-eyebrow">Hint ladder · reveal only what you need</div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="authored_core2_support"><h4>Guided support</h4><div class="g9-ladder" data-g9-ladder-ref="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2018-P2-Q08-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-group="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL"><ol data-g9-ladder></ol><ol class="g9-rung-map" aria-label="Rungs still to come"><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H0</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Represent</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H1</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Connect</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H2</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Carry out</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li></ol><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2018-P2-Q08-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-1"><li data-g9-rung="1" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[0]" data-g9-support-reveals="CONCEPT" data-g9-support-kind="REPRESENT" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2018-P2-Q08-MOVE-1"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H0</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Represent</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><div data-g9-support-reveal><p>Use H=<span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{y}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></span>/(2g) to infer the original vertical component.</p></div></li></template><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2018-P2-Q08-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-2"><li data-g9-rung="2" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[1]" data-g9-support-reveals="METHOD" data-g9-support-kind="CONNECT" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2018-P2-Q08-MOVE-2"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H1</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Connect</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><div data-g9-support-reveal><p>A 50% kinetic-energy loss means what happens to speed squared?</p></div></li></template><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2018-P2-Q08-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-3"><li data-g9-rung="3" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[2]" data-g9-support-reveals="METHOD" data-g9-support-kind="EXECUTE" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2018-P2-Q08-MOVE-3"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H2</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Carry out</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><div data-g9-support-reveal><p>Use only the post-bounce vertical component to get the new maximum height.</p></div></li></template><button type="button" data-g9-next-rung>Show guided support</button></div></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-link-list" data-g9-component="CONCEPT_NAV" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="LINK_LIST"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="concept_navigation"><h4>Need the concept again?</h4><ul><li><a data-g9-concept-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2018-P2-Q08" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL">Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences</a></li><li><a data-g9-concept-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2018-P2-Q08" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS">Split one plane-motion event into independent x/y components with one clock</a></li></ul></div></div></section></div></div><section class="blueprint-slot slot-solution" data-blueprint-slot="solution" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure" data-g9-component="SOLUTION" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE"><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2018-P2-Q08-solution"><summary>Answer and working</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2018-P2-Q08-solution"><div class="g9-component g9-c-step-list" data-g9-component="SOLUTION_STEPS" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEP_LIST" data-g9-component-items="3"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="structured_working"><h4>Reasoning route</h4><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="1" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2018-P2-Q08-MOVE-1" data-g9-solution-kind="REPRESENT" data-g9-solution-stage="REPRESENT" data-g9-solution-crux="false"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">1</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Represent</h4><p data-g9-solution-action>Initial maximum height gives u_y^2=2g(120). At 45 degrees, u^2=2u_y^2=480g.</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>stated source quantities</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> This follows from the stated physical or chemical model and the quantities preserved in the source-matched task.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> intermediate result 1</p></div></section><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="2" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2018-P2-Q08-MOVE-2" data-g9-solution-kind="CONNECT" data-g9-solution-stage="CONNECT" data-g9-solution-crux="true"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">2</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Connect</h4><p class="g9-prov" data-g9-crux-label>Key move</p><p data-g9-solution-action>After losing half the kinetic energy, the speed squared becomes v^2=u^2/2=240g.</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>stated source quantities</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> This follows from the stated physical or chemical model and the quantities preserved in the source-matched task.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> intermediate result 2</p></div></section><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="3" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2018-P2-Q08-MOVE-3" data-g9-solution-kind="TRANSFORM" data-g9-solution-stage="CALCULATE" data-g9-solution-crux="false"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">3</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Calculate</h4><p data-g9-solution-action>The new vertical speed squared is v^2 sin^2 30 = 60g, so H&#x27;=60g/(2g)=30 m.</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>stated source quantities</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> This follows from the stated physical or chemical model and the quantities preserved in the source-matched task.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> 30 m.</p></div></section></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-answer-box" data-g9-component="ANSWER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="ANSWER_BOX"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="answer"><p>30 m.</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-check-box" data-g9-component="CHECK" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CHECK_BOX"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="independent_check"><h4>Independent check</h4><p>The post-bounce height is one quarter of 120 m, consistent with half energy and a smaller vertical fraction.</p></div></div></template></div></section></article><article id="g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2019-P2-Q09" data-g9-unit="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2019-P2-Q09" data-g9-kind="QUESTION" data-g9-role="CORE2" data-g9-search-text="Physics Motion in 2 D Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences Medium concept Multi-step reasoning Motion in 2D component and projectile practice Numerical value JEE (Advanced) 2019 · Paper 2 · Q9 Verified past-exam adaptation A projectile is launched from the ground at speed u0 and angle theta. After each landing it rebounds at the same angle but with speed reduced by a constant factor alpha: u0/alpha, u0/alpha^2, and so on. If the magnitude of average velocity over the entire sequence is 0.8 times the magnitude V1 of the average velocity over the first flight, find alpha. Every rebound uses the same angle theta. Successive launch speeds form u0/alpha^n. The sequence continues indefinitely." class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-header" data-g9-component="IDENTITY" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="HEADER"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="source_identity"><h2>JEE (Advanced) · 2019 · Paper 2 · Q9</h2><p class="g9-prov">Official past paper, faithful restatement of the original</p></div><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE2" data-g9-meta-record="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2019-P2-Q09" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-KIN-2D-MOTION" data-g9-meta-value="Motion in 2 D"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Motion in 2 D</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-meta-value="Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="question-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2019-P2-Q09" data-g9-meta-value="D3"><strong>Question difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Multi-step reasoning</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="family" data-g9-meta-ref="FAM-PHY-KIN-2D-PRACTICE" data-g9-meta-value="Motion in 2D component and projectile practice"><strong>Family:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Motion in 2D component and projectile practice</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="question-type" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2019-P2-Q09" data-g9-meta-value="numerical_value"><strong>Question type:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Numerical value</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="source" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2019-P2-Q09" data-g9-meta-value="JEE (Advanced) 2019 · Paper 2 · Q9"><strong>Source:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>JEE (Advanced) 2019 · Paper 2 · Q9</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="provenance" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2019-P2-Q09" data-g9-meta-value="PYQ_ADAPTED"><strong>Provenance:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Verified past-exam adaptation</span></span></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-link-list" data-g9-component="SOURCE_PDF" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="LINK_LIST"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="source_pdf"><a class="g9-pdf-link g9-source-pdf" data-g9-source-pdf href="https://jeeadv.ac.in/past_qps/2019_2_English.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" type="application/pdf" aria-label="Open the original past paper as a PDF to print it"><svg class="g9-pdf-icon" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="24" height="24"><path d="M6 2.5h8l4.5 4.5v14.5H6z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linejoin="round"/><path d="M14 2.5V7h4.5M9 12h6.5M9 15.5h6.5M9 19h4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg><span>Source paper (PDF)</span></a></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-grid" data-g9-component="DIFFICULTY_WHY" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_GRID"><button type="button" class="g9-why-toggle" data-g9-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="g9-why-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2019-P2-Q09"><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-band" data-g9-band="D3">D3 · 7/10 · about 3 min</span><span>Why this difficulty?</span></button><div id="g9-why-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2019-P2-Q09" class="g9-why" hidden><p>Requires recognizing two different geometric series, for range and time, before taking their ratio.</p><div class=g9-dgrid><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Choosing the model</span><b>2/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Representation</span><b>2/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Reasoning chain</span><b>1/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Algebra and numbers</span><b>1/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Traps and exceptions</span><b>1/2</b></div></div></div></div></section><div class="g9-split"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-attempt" data-blueprint-slot="attempt" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-stem" data-g9-component="STEM" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEM"><div class="g9-eyebrow">Attempt first</div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="stem"><p>A projectile is launched from the ground at speed u0 and angle theta. After each landing it rebounds at the same angle but with speed reduced by a constant factor alpha: u0/alpha, u0/<span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">alpha^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, and so on. If the magnitude of average velocity over the entire sequence is 0.8 times the magnitude V1 of the average velocity over the first flight, find alpha.</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-callout-info" data-g9-component="CONDITIONS" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CALLOUT_INFO"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="conditions"><h4>Conditions</h4><ul><li>Every rebound uses the same angle theta.</li><li>Successive launch speeds form u0/<span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">alpha^{n}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</li><li>The sequence continues indefinitely.</li></ul></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-callout-warn" data-g9-component="TRAP" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CALLOUT_WARN"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core2-wrong-route"><summary>Common wrong route · open if you want a warning</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="common_wrong_route"><h4>Common wrong route</h4><p>Averaging the speeds of the flights instead of total displacement divided by total time.</p></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-attempt" data-g9-component="ATTEMPT" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="ATTEMPT"><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your answer<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div></div></section></div><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-representation" data-blueprint-slot="representation" data-required="false"></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-support" data-blueprint-slot="support" data-required="false"><div class="g9-component g9-c-ladder" data-g9-component="HINT_LADDER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="LADDER" data-g9-component-items="3"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core2-hints"><summary>Need a hint? · guided support</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-eyebrow">Hint ladder · reveal only what you need</div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="authored_core2_support"><h4>Guided support</h4><div class="g9-ladder" data-g9-ladder-ref="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2019-P2-Q09-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-group="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL"><ol data-g9-ladder></ol><ol class="g9-rung-map" aria-label="Rungs still to come"><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H0</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Represent</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H1</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Connect</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H2</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Carry out</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li></ol><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2019-P2-Q09-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-1"><li data-g9-rung="1" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[0]" data-g9-support-reveals="CONCEPT" data-g9-support-kind="REPRESENT" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2019-P2-Q09-MOVE-1"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H0</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Represent</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><div data-g9-support-reveal><p>For fixed theta, determine how range and flight time scale with launch speed.</p></div></li></template><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2019-P2-Q09-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-2"><li data-g9-rung="2" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[1]" data-g9-support-reveals="METHOD" data-g9-support-kind="CONNECT" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2019-P2-Q09-MOVE-2"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H1</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Connect</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><div data-g9-support-reveal><p>Write one geometric series for all ranges and a different one for all flight times.</p></div></li></template><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2019-P2-Q09-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-3"><li data-g9-rung="3" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[2]" data-g9-support-reveals="METHOD" data-g9-support-kind="EXECUTE" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2019-P2-Q09-MOVE-3"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H2</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Carry out</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><div data-g9-support-reveal><p>Divide the two sums and compare with the first-flight average velocity.</p></div></li></template><button type="button" data-g9-next-rung>Show guided support</button></div></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-link-list" data-g9-component="CONCEPT_NAV" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="LINK_LIST"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="concept_navigation"><h4>Need the concept again?</h4><ul><li><a data-g9-concept-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2019-P2-Q09" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL">Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences</a></li><li><a data-g9-concept-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2019-P2-Q09" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS">Split one plane-motion event into independent x/y components with one clock</a></li></ul></div></div></section></div></div><section class="blueprint-slot slot-solution" data-blueprint-slot="solution" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure" data-g9-component="SOLUTION" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE"><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2019-P2-Q09-solution"><summary>Answer and working</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2019-P2-Q09-solution"><div class="g9-component g9-c-step-list" data-g9-component="SOLUTION_STEPS" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEP_LIST" data-g9-component-items="3"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="structured_working"><h4>Reasoning route</h4><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="1" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2019-P2-Q09-MOVE-1" data-g9-solution-kind="REPRESENT" data-g9-solution-stage="REPRESENT" data-g9-solution-crux="false"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">1</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Represent</h4><p data-g9-solution-action>For a fixed launch angle, each flight time is proportional to speed and each range is proportional to speed squared.</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>stated source quantities</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> This follows from the stated physical or chemical model and the quantities preserved in the source-matched task.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> intermediate result 1</p></div></section><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="2" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2019-P2-Q09-MOVE-2" data-g9-solution-kind="CONNECT" data-g9-solution-stage="CONNECT" data-g9-solution-crux="true"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">2</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Connect</h4><p class="g9-prov" data-g9-crux-label>Key move</p><p data-g9-solution-action>Total displacement is R1[1+alpha^-2+alpha^-4+...] while total time is T1[1+alpha^-1+alpha^-2+...].</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>stated source quantities</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> This follows from the stated physical or chemical model and the quantities preserved in the source-matched task.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> intermediate result 2</p></div></section><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="3" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2019-P2-Q09-MOVE-3" data-g9-solution-kind="TRANSFORM" data-g9-solution-stage="CALCULATE" data-g9-solution-crux="false"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">3</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Calculate</h4><p data-g9-solution-action>Thus V_avg/V1 = [1/(1-alpha^-2)]/[1/(1-alpha^-1)] = alpha/(alpha+1)=0.8, giving alpha=4.</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>stated source quantities</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> This follows from the stated physical or chemical model and the quantities preserved in the source-matched task.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> 4.</p></div></section></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-answer-box" data-g9-component="ANSWER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="ANSWER_BOX"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="answer"><p>4.</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-check-box" data-g9-component="CHECK" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CHECK_BOX"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="independent_check"><h4>Independent check</h4><p>alpha=4 gives 4/5=0.8 exactly.</p></div></div></template></div></section></article><article id="g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q05" data-g9-unit="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q05" data-g9-kind="QUESTION" data-g9-role="CORE2" data-g9-search-text="Physics Motion in 2 D Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences Medium concept Connected reasoning Motion in 2D component and projectile practice Numerical value JEE (Advanced) 2021 · Paper 1 · Q5 Verified past-exam adaptation A projectile is launched from ground at 5sqrt(2) m/s, at 45 degrees from the vertical. At the highest point it splits into two equal masses. One fragment falls vertically to the ground 0.5 s after splitting. The other fragment reaches the ground t seconds after splitting. Find t. g=10 m/s^2. The split occurs at the apex. Fragments have equal mass." class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-header" data-g9-component="IDENTITY" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="HEADER"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="source_identity"><h2>JEE (Advanced) · 2021 · Paper 1 · Q5</h2><p class="g9-prov">Official past paper, faithful restatement of the original</p></div><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE2" data-g9-meta-record="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q05" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-KIN-2D-MOTION" data-g9-meta-value="Motion in 2 D"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Motion in 2 D</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-meta-value="Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="question-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q05" data-g9-meta-value="D2"><strong>Question difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Connected reasoning</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="family" data-g9-meta-ref="FAM-PHY-KIN-2D-PRACTICE" data-g9-meta-value="Motion in 2D component and projectile practice"><strong>Family:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Motion in 2D component and projectile practice</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="question-type" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q05" data-g9-meta-value="numerical_value"><strong>Question type:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Numerical value</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="source" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q05" data-g9-meta-value="JEE (Advanced) 2021 · Paper 1 · Q5"><strong>Source:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>JEE (Advanced) 2021 · Paper 1 · Q5</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="provenance" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q05" data-g9-meta-value="PYQ_ADAPTED"><strong>Provenance:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Verified past-exam adaptation</span></span></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-link-list" data-g9-component="SOURCE_PDF" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="LINK_LIST"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="source_pdf"><a class="g9-pdf-link g9-source-pdf" data-g9-source-pdf href="https://jeeadv.ac.in/past_qps/2021_1_English.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" type="application/pdf" aria-label="Open the original past paper as a PDF to print it"><svg class="g9-pdf-icon" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="24" height="24"><path d="M6 2.5h8l4.5 4.5v14.5H6z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linejoin="round"/><path d="M14 2.5V7h4.5M9 12h6.5M9 15.5h6.5M9 19h4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg><span>Source paper (PDF)</span></a></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-grid" data-g9-component="DIFFICULTY_WHY" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_GRID"><button type="button" class="g9-why-toggle" data-g9-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="g9-why-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q05"><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-band" data-g9-band="D2">D2 · 4/10 · about 2 min</span><span>Why this difficulty?</span></button><div id="g9-why-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q05" class="g9-why" hidden><p>The split event must be separated from the vertical free-fall event shared by the fragments.</p><div class=g9-dgrid><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Choosing the model</span><b>1/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Representation</span><b>1/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Reasoning chain</span><b>1/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Algebra and numbers</span><b>1/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Traps and exceptions</span><b>0/2</b></div></div></div></div></section><div class="g9-split"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-attempt" data-blueprint-slot="attempt" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-stem" data-g9-component="STEM" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEM"><div class="g9-eyebrow">Attempt first</div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="stem"><p>A projectile is launched from ground at 5<span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\\sqrt{2}</annotation></semantics></math></span> m/s, at 45 degrees from the vertical. At the highest point it splits into two equal masses. One fragment falls vertically to the ground 0.5 s after splitting. The other fragment reaches the ground t seconds after splitting. Find t.</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-callout-info" data-g9-component="CONDITIONS" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CALLOUT_INFO"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="conditions"><h4>Conditions</h4><ul><li>g=10 m/<span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</li><li>The split occurs at the apex.</li><li>Fragments have equal mass.</li></ul></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-callout-warn" data-g9-component="TRAP" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CALLOUT_WARN"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core2-wrong-route"><summary>Common wrong route · open if you want a warning</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="common_wrong_route"><h4>Common wrong route</h4><p>Recomputing a full projectile flight from launch instead of starting the post-split clock at the apex.</p></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-attempt" data-g9-component="ATTEMPT" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="ATTEMPT"><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your answer<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div></div></section></div><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-representation" data-blueprint-slot="representation" data-required="false"></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-support" data-blueprint-slot="support" data-required="false"><div class="g9-component g9-c-ladder" data-g9-component="HINT_LADDER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="LADDER" data-g9-component-items="2"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core2-hints"><summary>Need a hint? · guided support</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-eyebrow">Hint ladder · reveal only what you need</div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="authored_core2_support"><h4>Guided support</h4><div class="g9-ladder" data-g9-ladder-ref="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q05-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-group="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL"><ol data-g9-ladder></ol><ol class="g9-rung-map" aria-label="Rungs still to come"><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H0</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Represent</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H1</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Connect</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li></ol><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q05-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-1"><li data-g9-rung="1" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[0]" data-g9-support-reveals="CONCEPT" data-g9-support-kind="REPRESENT" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q05-MOVE-1"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H0</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Represent</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><div data-g9-support-reveal><p>Treat the split as a new initial condition at the apex, where the original projectile&#x27;s vertical velocity is zero.</p></div></li></template><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q05-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-2"><li data-g9-rung="2" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[1]" data-g9-support-reveals="METHOD" data-g9-support-kind="CONNECT" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q05-MOVE-2"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H1</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Connect</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><div data-g9-support-reveal><p>Find the apex height from u_y, then ask what initial vertical velocity lets a fragment fall that height in exactly 0.5 s.</p></div></li></template><button type="button" data-g9-next-rung>Show guided support</button></div></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-link-list" data-g9-component="CONCEPT_NAV" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="LINK_LIST"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="concept_navigation"><h4>Need the concept again?</h4><ul><li><a data-g9-concept-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q05" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL">Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences</a></li><li><a data-g9-concept-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q05" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS">Split one plane-motion event into independent x/y components with one clock</a></li></ul></div></div></section></div></div><section class="blueprint-slot slot-solution" data-blueprint-slot="solution" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure" data-g9-component="SOLUTION" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE"><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q05-solution"><summary>Answer and working</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q05-solution"><div class="g9-component g9-c-step-list" data-g9-component="SOLUTION_STEPS" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEP_LIST" data-g9-component-items="3"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="structured_working"><h4>Reasoning route</h4><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="1" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q05-MOVE-1" data-g9-solution-kind="REPRESENT" data-g9-solution-stage="REPRESENT" data-g9-solution-crux="false"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">1</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Represent</h4><p data-g9-solution-action>Resolve the launch: 5√2 m/s at 45° from the vertical gives u_x = u_y = 5 m/s. The apex is at t = u_y/g = 0.5 s, at height H = u_y²/2g = 25/20 = 1.25 m, where the velocity is (5, 0) m/s.</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>stated source quantities</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> Follows from the gravity-only projectile model and momentum conservation at the split, as shown in this step.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> Apex at 0.5 s, H = 1.25 m, velocity (5, 0) m/s.</p></div></section><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="2" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q05-MOVE-2" data-g9-solution-kind="CONNECT" data-g9-solution-stage="CONNECT" data-g9-solution-crux="true"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">2</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Connect</h4><p class="g9-prov" data-g9-crux-label>Key move</p><p data-g9-solution-action>The fragment that falls vertically covers 1.25 m in 0.5 s. From rest, a 1.25 m fall takes √(2H/g) = √(2.5/10) = 0.5 s, so that fragment starts from rest: zero vertical and zero horizontal velocity just after the split.</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>stated source quantities</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> Follows from the gravity-only projectile model and momentum conservation at the split, as shown in this step.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> The vertical fragment starts from rest at the split.</p></div></section><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="3" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q05-MOVE-3" data-g9-solution-kind="TRANSFORM" data-g9-solution-stage="CALCULATE" data-g9-solution-crux="false"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">3</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Calculate</h4><p data-g9-solution-action>Momentum at the split: before, M(5, 0) m/s. After, (M/2)(0, 0) + (M/2)v₂, so v₂ = (10, 0) m/s. The other fragment also starts with zero vertical velocity from 1.25 m, so it lands after t = √(2H/g) = 0.5 s (at x = 2.5 + 10 × 0.5 = 7.5 m from the launch point).</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>stated source quantities</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> Follows from the gravity-only projectile model and momentum conservation at the split, as shown in this step.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> 0.5 s.</p></div></section></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-answer-box" data-g9-component="ANSWER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="ANSWER_BOX"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="answer"><p>0.5 s.</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-check-box" data-g9-component="CHECK" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CHECK_BOX"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="independent_check"><h4>Independent check</h4><p>Vertical momentum is zero before the split and both fragments have zero vertical velocity after it, so vertical momentum is conserved (0 = 0); both then fall 1.25 m from rest in the same 0.5 s, and horizontal momentum M × 5 = (M/2) × 10 checks the second fragment&#x27;s speed.</p></div></div></template></div></section></article><article id="g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q06" data-g9-unit="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q06" data-g9-kind="QUESTION" data-g9-role="CORE2" data-g9-search-text="Physics Motion in 2 D Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences Medium concept Multi-step reasoning Motion in 2D component and projectile practice Numerical value JEE (Advanced) 2021 · Paper 1 · Q6 Verified past-exam adaptation Use the same projectile-splitting setup as Q5: launch speed 5sqrt(2) m/s at 45 degrees from vertical; at the apex it splits into equal masses and one fragment drops vertically, reaching ground 0.5 s later. Find the horizontal distance x from the launch point at which the other fragment lands. g=10 m/s^2. Equal fragments. One fragment has zero horizontal velocity immediately after splitting." class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-header" data-g9-component="IDENTITY" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="HEADER"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="source_identity"><h2>JEE (Advanced) · 2021 · Paper 1 · Q6</h2><p class="g9-prov">Official past paper, faithful restatement of the original</p></div><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE2" data-g9-meta-record="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q06" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-KIN-2D-MOTION" data-g9-meta-value="Motion in 2 D"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Motion in 2 D</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-meta-value="Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="question-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q06" data-g9-meta-value="D3"><strong>Question difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Multi-step reasoning</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="family" data-g9-meta-ref="FAM-PHY-KIN-2D-PRACTICE" data-g9-meta-value="Motion in 2D component and projectile practice"><strong>Family:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Motion in 2D component and projectile practice</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="question-type" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q06" data-g9-meta-value="numerical_value"><strong>Question type:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Numerical value</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="source" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q06" data-g9-meta-value="JEE (Advanced) 2021 · Paper 1 · Q6"><strong>Source:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>JEE (Advanced) 2021 · Paper 1 · Q6</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="provenance" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q06" data-g9-meta-value="PYQ_ADAPTED"><strong>Provenance:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Verified past-exam adaptation</span></span></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-link-list" data-g9-component="SOURCE_PDF" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="LINK_LIST"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="source_pdf"><a class="g9-pdf-link g9-source-pdf" data-g9-source-pdf href="https://jeeadv.ac.in/past_qps/2021_1_English.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" type="application/pdf" aria-label="Open the original past paper as a PDF to print it"><svg class="g9-pdf-icon" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="24" height="24"><path d="M6 2.5h8l4.5 4.5v14.5H6z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linejoin="round"/><path d="M14 2.5V7h4.5M9 12h6.5M9 15.5h6.5M9 19h4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg><span>Source paper (PDF)</span></a></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-grid" data-g9-component="DIFFICULTY_WHY" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_GRID"><button type="button" class="g9-why-toggle" data-g9-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="g9-why-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q06"><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-band" data-g9-band="D3">D3 · 6/10 · about 2 min</span><span>Why this difficulty?</span></button><div id="g9-why-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q06" class="g9-why" hidden><p>Combines projectile timing with momentum conservation at an internal fragmentation event.</p><div class=g9-dgrid><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Choosing the model</span><b>2/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Representation</span><b>1/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Reasoning chain</span><b>1/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Algebra and numbers</span><b>1/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Traps and exceptions</span><b>1/2</b></div></div></div></div></section><div class="g9-split"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-attempt" data-blueprint-slot="attempt" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-stem" data-g9-component="STEM" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEM"><div class="g9-eyebrow">Attempt first</div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="stem"><p>Use the same projectile-splitting setup as Q5: launch speed 5<span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\\sqrt{2}</annotation></semantics></math></span> m/s at 45 degrees from vertical; at the apex it splits into equal masses and one fragment drops vertically, reaching ground 0.5 s later. Find the horizontal distance x from the launch point at which the other fragment lands.</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-callout-info" data-g9-component="CONDITIONS" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CALLOUT_INFO"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="conditions"><h4>Conditions</h4><ul><li>g=10 m/<span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</li><li>Equal fragments.</li><li>One fragment has zero horizontal velocity immediately after splitting.</li></ul></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-callout-warn" data-g9-component="TRAP" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CALLOUT_WARN"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core2-wrong-route"><summary>Common wrong route · open if you want a warning</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="common_wrong_route"><h4>Common wrong route</h4><p>Assigning the second fragment the original 5 m/s horizontal speed instead of conserving momentum.</p></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-attempt" data-g9-component="ATTEMPT" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="ATTEMPT"><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your answer<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div></div></section></div><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-representation" data-blueprint-slot="representation" data-required="false"></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-support" data-blueprint-slot="support" data-required="false"><div class="g9-component g9-c-ladder" data-g9-component="HINT_LADDER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="LADDER" data-g9-component-items="3"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core2-hints"><summary>Need a hint? · guided support</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-eyebrow">Hint ladder · reveal only what you need</div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="authored_core2_support"><h4>Guided support</h4><div class="g9-ladder" data-g9-ladder-ref="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q06-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-group="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL"><ol data-g9-ladder></ol><ol class="g9-rung-map" aria-label="Rungs still to come"><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H0</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Represent</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H1</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Connect</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H2</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Carry out</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li></ol><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q06-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-1"><li data-g9-rung="1" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[0]" data-g9-support-reveals="CONCEPT" data-g9-support-kind="REPRESENT" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q06-MOVE-1"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H0</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Represent</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><div data-g9-support-reveal><p>Find where the original projectile reaches its apex using its 5 m/s horizontal component.</p></div></li></template><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q06-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-2"><li data-g9-rung="2" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[1]" data-g9-support-reveals="METHOD" data-g9-support-kind="CONNECT" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q06-MOVE-2"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H1</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Connect</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><div data-g9-support-reveal><p>At the split, conserve horizontal momentum between the two equal fragments.</p></div></li></template><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q06-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-3"><li data-g9-rung="3" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[2]" data-g9-support-reveals="METHOD" data-g9-support-kind="EXECUTE" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q06-MOVE-3"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H2</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Carry out</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><div data-g9-support-reveal><p>Use the Q5 post-split fall time for the surviving fragment&#x27;s horizontal travel.</p></div></li></template><button type="button" data-g9-next-rung>Show guided support</button></div></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-link-list" data-g9-component="CONCEPT_NAV" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="LINK_LIST"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="concept_navigation"><h4>Need the concept again?</h4><ul><li><a data-g9-concept-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q06" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL">Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences</a></li><li><a data-g9-concept-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q06" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS">Split one plane-motion event into independent x/y components with one clock</a></li></ul></div></div></section></div></div><section class="blueprint-slot slot-solution" data-blueprint-slot="solution" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure" data-g9-component="SOLUTION" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE"><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q06-solution"><summary>Answer and working</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q06-solution"><div class="g9-component g9-c-step-list" data-g9-component="SOLUTION_STEPS" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEP_LIST" data-g9-component-items="3"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="structured_working"><h4>Reasoning route</h4><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="1" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q06-MOVE-1" data-g9-solution-kind="REPRESENT" data-g9-solution-stage="REPRESENT" data-g9-solution-crux="false"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">1</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Represent</h4><p data-g9-solution-action>The original horizontal speed is 5 m/s and the apex time is 0.5 s, so the split occurs at x=2.5 m.</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>stated source quantities</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> This follows from the stated physical or chemical model and the quantities preserved in the source-matched task.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> intermediate result 1</p></div></section><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="2" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q06-MOVE-2" data-g9-solution-kind="CONNECT" data-g9-solution-stage="CONNECT" data-g9-solution-crux="true"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">2</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Connect</h4><p class="g9-prov" data-g9-crux-label>Key move</p><p data-g9-solution-action>Horizontal momentum just before splitting is M·5. If one equal fragment has zero horizontal speed, the other equal fragment must have horizontal speed 10 m/s.</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>stated source quantities</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> This follows from the stated physical or chemical model and the quantities preserved in the source-matched task.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> intermediate result 2</p></div></section><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="3" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q06-MOVE-3" data-g9-solution-kind="TRANSFORM" data-g9-solution-stage="CALCULATE" data-g9-solution-crux="false"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">3</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Calculate</h4><p data-g9-solution-action>During the 0.5 s post-split fall it travels another 5 m horizontally, so x=2.5+5=7.5 m.</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>stated source quantities</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> This follows from the stated physical or chemical model and the quantities preserved in the source-matched task.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> 7.5 m.</p></div></section></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-answer-box" data-g9-component="ANSWER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="ANSWER_BOX"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="answer"><p>7.5 m.</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-check-box" data-g9-component="CHECK" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CHECK_BOX"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="independent_check"><h4>Independent check</h4><p>Horizontal momentum at the split is conserved: (M/2)·0 + (M/2)·10 = M·5.</p></div></div></template></div></section></article><article id="g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2024-P2-Q09" data-g9-unit="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2024-P2-Q09" data-g9-kind="QUESTION" data-g9-role="CORE2" data-g9-search-text="Physics Motion in 2 D Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences Medium concept Multi-step reasoning Motion in 2D component and projectile practice Non-negative integer JEE (Advanced) 2024 · Paper 2 · Q9 Verified past-exam adaptation A ball is thrown from (0,0) with fixed speed v0 at angle theta0. Simultaneously, a stone is thrown from (L,0) toward the ball at angle 180°-theta1 with a suitable speed. For (theta0,theta1)=(45°,45°) the hit occurs after T1; for (60°,30°) it occurs after T2. Find (T1/T2)^2. Both projectiles are launched simultaneously from the same vertical level. Gravity is common to both particles. The stone&#x27;s speed is chosen so that collision occurs." class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-header" data-g9-component="IDENTITY" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="HEADER"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="source_identity"><h2>JEE (Advanced) · 2024 · Paper 2 · Q9</h2><p class="g9-prov">Official past paper, faithful restatement of the original</p></div><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE2" data-g9-meta-record="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2024-P2-Q09" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-KIN-2D-MOTION" data-g9-meta-value="Motion in 2 D"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Motion in 2 D</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-meta-value="Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="question-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2024-P2-Q09" data-g9-meta-value="D3"><strong>Question difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Multi-step reasoning</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="family" data-g9-meta-ref="FAM-PHY-KIN-2D-PRACTICE" data-g9-meta-value="Motion in 2D component and projectile practice"><strong>Family:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Motion in 2D component and projectile practice</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="question-type" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2024-P2-Q09" data-g9-meta-value="non_negative_integer"><strong>Question type:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Non-negative integer</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="source" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2024-P2-Q09" data-g9-meta-value="JEE (Advanced) 2024 · Paper 2 · Q9"><strong>Source:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>JEE (Advanced) 2024 · Paper 2 · Q9</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="provenance" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2024-P2-Q09" data-g9-meta-value="PYQ_ADAPTED"><strong>Provenance:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Verified past-exam adaptation</span></span></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-link-list" data-g9-component="SOURCE_PDF" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="LINK_LIST"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="source_pdf"><a class="g9-pdf-link g9-source-pdf" data-g9-source-pdf href="https://jeeadv.ac.in/past_qps/2024_2_English.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" type="application/pdf" aria-label="Open the original past paper as a PDF to print it"><svg class="g9-pdf-icon" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="24" height="24"><path d="M6 2.5h8l4.5 4.5v14.5H6z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linejoin="round"/><path d="M14 2.5V7h4.5M9 12h6.5M9 15.5h6.5M9 19h4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg><span>Source paper (PDF)</span></a></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-grid" data-g9-component="DIFFICULTY_WHY" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_GRID"><button type="button" class="g9-why-toggle" data-g9-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="g9-why-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2024-P2-Q09"><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-band" data-g9-band="D3">D3 · 7/10 · about 3 min</span><span>Why this difficulty?</span></button><div id="g9-why-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2024-P2-Q09" class="g9-why" hidden><p>The clean route uses relative projectile motion to cancel gravity and eliminate the unknown stone speed.</p><div class=g9-dgrid><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Choosing the model</span><b>2/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Representation</span><b>2/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Reasoning chain</span><b>1/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Algebra and numbers</span><b>1/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Traps and exceptions</span><b>1/2</b></div></div></div></div></section><div class="g9-split"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-attempt" data-blueprint-slot="attempt" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-stem" data-g9-component="STEM" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEM"><div class="g9-eyebrow">Attempt first</div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="stem"><p>A ball is thrown from (0,0) with fixed speed v0 at angle theta0. Simultaneously, a stone is thrown from (L,0) toward the ball at angle 180°-theta1 with a suitable speed. For (theta0,theta1)=(45°,45°) the hit occurs after T1; for (60°,30°) it occurs after T2. Find (T1/T2)^2.</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-callout-info" data-g9-component="CONDITIONS" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CALLOUT_INFO"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="conditions"><h4>Conditions</h4><ul><li>Both projectiles are launched simultaneously from the same vertical level.</li><li>Gravity is common to both particles.</li><li>The stone&#x27;s speed is chosen so that collision occurs.</li></ul></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-callout-warn" data-g9-component="TRAP" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CALLOUT_WARN"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core2-wrong-route"><summary>Common wrong route · open if you want a warning</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="common_wrong_route"><h4>Common wrong route</h4><p>Solving two full projectile trajectories independently without exploiting common gravitational acceleration.</p></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-attempt" data-g9-component="ATTEMPT" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="ATTEMPT"><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your answer<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div></div></section></div><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-representation" data-blueprint-slot="representation" data-required="false"></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-support" data-blueprint-slot="support" data-required="false"><div class="g9-component g9-c-ladder" data-g9-component="HINT_LADDER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="LADDER" data-g9-component-items="3"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core2-hints"><summary>Need a hint? · guided support</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-eyebrow">Hint ladder · reveal only what you need</div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="authored_core2_support"><h4>Guided support</h4><div class="g9-ladder" data-g9-ladder-ref="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2024-P2-Q09-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-group="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL"><ol data-g9-ladder></ol><ol class="g9-rung-map" aria-label="Rungs still to come"><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H0</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Represent</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H1</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Connect</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H2</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Carry out</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li></ol><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2024-P2-Q09-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-1"><li data-g9-rung="1" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[0]" data-g9-support-reveals="CONCEPT" data-g9-support-kind="REPRESENT" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2024-P2-Q09-MOVE-1"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H0</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Represent</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><div data-g9-support-reveal><p>Write the ball-minus-stone relative position; what happens to the identical gravitational acceleration terms?</p></div></li></template><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2024-P2-Q09-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-2"><li data-g9-rung="2" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[1]" data-g9-support-reveals="METHOD" data-g9-support-kind="CONNECT" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2024-P2-Q09-MOVE-2"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H1</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Connect</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><div data-g9-support-reveal><p>Use the vertical collision condition to eliminate the stone&#x27;s unknown launch speed.</p></div></li></template><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2024-P2-Q09-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-3"><li data-g9-rung="3" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[2]" data-g9-support-reveals="METHOD" data-g9-support-kind="EXECUTE" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2024-P2-Q09-MOVE-3"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H2</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Carry out</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><div data-g9-support-reveal><p>Evaluate the resulting collision-time expression for the two angle pairs.</p></div></li></template><button type="button" data-g9-next-rung>Show guided support</button></div></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-link-list" data-g9-component="CONCEPT_NAV" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="LINK_LIST"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="concept_navigation"><h4>Need the concept again?</h4><ul><li><a data-g9-concept-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2024-P2-Q09" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL">Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences</a></li><li><a data-g9-concept-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2024-P2-Q09" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS">Split one plane-motion event into independent x/y components with one clock</a></li></ul></div></div></section></div></div><section class="blueprint-slot slot-solution" data-blueprint-slot="solution" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure" data-g9-component="SOLUTION" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE"><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2024-P2-Q09-solution"><summary>Answer and working</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2024-P2-Q09-solution"><div class="g9-component g9-c-step-list" data-g9-component="SOLUTION_STEPS" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEP_LIST" data-g9-component-items="3"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="structured_working"><h4>Reasoning route</h4><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="1" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2024-P2-Q09-MOVE-1" data-g9-solution-kind="REPRESENT" data-g9-solution-stage="REPRESENT" data-g9-solution-crux="false"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">1</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Represent</h4><p data-g9-solution-action>Because both bodies have the same gravitational acceleration, subtracting their position equations removes the -gt^2/2 term.</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>stated source quantities</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> This follows from the stated physical or chemical model and the quantities preserved in the source-matched task.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> intermediate result 1</p></div></section><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="2" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2024-P2-Q09-MOVE-2" data-g9-solution-kind="CONNECT" data-g9-solution-stage="CONNECT" data-g9-solution-crux="true"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">2</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Connect</h4><p class="g9-prov" data-g9-crux-label>Key move</p><p data-g9-solution-action>For collision, the vertical relative displacement condition gives the stone speed in terms of v0 and the two angles; eliminating it yields T = L sin(theta1)/[v0 sin(theta0+theta1)].</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>stated source quantities</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> This follows from the stated physical or chemical model and the quantities preserved in the source-matched task.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> intermediate result 2</p></div></section><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="3" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2024-P2-Q09-MOVE-3" data-g9-solution-kind="TRANSFORM" data-g9-solution-stage="CALCULATE" data-g9-solution-crux="false"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">3</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Calculate</h4><p data-g9-solution-action>Thus T1=L/(sqrt(2)v0) and T2=L/(2v0), so (T1/T2)^2=2.</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>stated source quantities</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> This follows from the stated physical or chemical model and the quantities preserved in the source-matched task.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> 2.</p></div></section></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-answer-box" data-g9-component="ANSWER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="ANSWER_BOX"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="answer"><p>2.</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-check-box" data-g9-component="CHECK" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CHECK_BOX"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="independent_check"><h4>Independent check</h4><p>Both angle pairs have theta0+theta1=90°, so only <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>\u2061</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\\sin (theta1)</annotation></semantics></math></span> changes: <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>\u2061</mo><mn>45</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\\sin 45</annotation></semantics></math></span>/<span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>\u2061</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\\sin 30</annotation></semantics></math></span>=sqrt2.</p></div></div></template></div></section></article><article id="g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2012-P1-Q05" data-g9-unit="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2012-P1-Q05" data-g9-kind="QUESTION" data-g9-role="CORE2" data-g9-search-text="Physics Motion in 2 D Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences Medium concept Multi-step reasoning Motion in 2D component and projectile practice Single-correct multiple choice IIT-JEE 2012 · Paper I · Q5 Verified past-exam adaptation A block on a frictionless horizontal surface is attached to a spring of natural length 4.9 m whose other end is fixed to a wall. It is stretched by 0.2 m and released, executing SHM with angular frequency pi/3 rad/s. At the same instant a pebble is launched at 45 degrees, toward the block, from a point on the surface 10 m from the wall (on the block&#x27;s side). If it meets the block after 1 s, determine the launch speed. Take g=10 m/s^2. The spring-block motion and projectile launch begin simultaneously. The block starts from maximum extension. Distances along the surface are measured from the wall to which the spring is fixed; the block starts 5.1 m from it and the pebble&#x27;s launch point is 10 m from it. (A) sqrt(50) m/s (B) sqrt(51) m/s (C) sqrt(52) m/s (D) sqrt(53) m/s" class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-header" data-g9-component="IDENTITY" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="HEADER"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="source_identity"><h2>IIT-JEE · 2012 · Paper I · Q5</h2><p class="g9-prov">Official past paper, faithful restatement of the original</p></div><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE2" data-g9-meta-record="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2012-P1-Q05" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-KIN-2D-MOTION" data-g9-meta-value="Motion in 2 D"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Motion in 2 D</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-meta-value="Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="question-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2012-P1-Q05" data-g9-meta-value="D3"><strong>Question difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Multi-step reasoning</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="family" data-g9-meta-ref="FAM-PHY-KIN-2D-PRACTICE" data-g9-meta-value="Motion in 2D component and projectile practice"><strong>Family:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Motion in 2D component and projectile practice</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="question-type" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2012-P1-Q05" data-g9-meta-value="single_correct_mcq"><strong>Question type:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Single-correct multiple choice</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="source" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2012-P1-Q05" data-g9-meta-value="IIT-JEE 2012 · Paper I · Q5"><strong>Source:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>IIT-JEE 2012 · Paper I · Q5</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="provenance" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2012-P1-Q05" data-g9-meta-value="PYQ_ADAPTED"><strong>Provenance:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Verified past-exam adaptation</span></span></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-link-list" data-g9-component="SOURCE_PDF" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="LINK_LIST"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="source_pdf"><a class="g9-pdf-link g9-source-pdf" data-g9-source-pdf href="https://jeeadv.ac.in/past_qps/2012_1.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" type="application/pdf" aria-label="Open the original past paper as a PDF to print it"><svg class="g9-pdf-icon" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="24" height="24"><path d="M6 2.5h8l4.5 4.5v14.5H6z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linejoin="round"/><path d="M14 2.5V7h4.5M9 12h6.5M9 15.5h6.5M9 19h4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg><span>Source paper (PDF)</span></a></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-grid" data-g9-component="DIFFICULTY_WHY" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_GRID"><button type="button" class="g9-why-toggle" data-g9-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="g9-why-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2012-P1-Q05"><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-band" data-g9-band="D3">D3 · 7/10 · about 3 min</span><span>Why this difficulty?</span></button><div id="g9-why-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2012-P1-Q05" class="g9-why" hidden><p>Synchronizes an SHM event with a projectile event using one shared time.</p><div class=g9-dgrid><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Choosing the model</span><b>2/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Representation</span><b>2/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Reasoning chain</span><b>1/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Algebra and numbers</span><b>1/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Traps and exceptions</span><b>1/2</b></div></div></div></div></section><div class="g9-split"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-attempt" data-blueprint-slot="attempt" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-stem" data-g9-component="STEM" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEM"><div class="g9-eyebrow">Attempt first</div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="stem"><p>A block on a frictionless horizontal surface is attached to a spring of natural length 4.9 m whose other end is fixed to a wall. It is stretched by 0.2 m and released, executing SHM with angular frequency pi/3 rad/s. At the same instant a pebble is launched at 45 degrees, toward the block, from a point on the surface 10 m from the wall (on the block&#x27;s side). If it meets the block after 1 s, determine the launch speed.</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-callout-info" data-g9-component="CONDITIONS" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CALLOUT_INFO"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="conditions"><h4>Conditions</h4><ul><li>Take g=10 m/<span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</li><li>The spring-block motion and projectile launch begin simultaneously.</li><li>The block starts from maximum extension.</li><li>Distances along the surface are measured from the wall to which the spring is fixed; the block starts 5.1 m from it and the pebble&#x27;s launch point is 10 m from it.</li></ul></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-callout-warn" data-g9-component="TRAP" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CALLOUT_WARN"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core2-wrong-route"><summary>Common wrong route · open if you want a warning</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="common_wrong_route"><h4>Common wrong route</h4><p>Solving the projectile without first locating the moving block at t=1 s.</p></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-attempt" data-g9-component="ATTEMPT" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="ATTEMPT"><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your answer<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div></div></section></div><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-representation" data-blueprint-slot="representation" data-required="false"></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-support" data-blueprint-slot="support" data-required="false"><div class="g9-component g9-c-ladder" data-g9-component="HINT_LADDER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="LADDER" data-g9-component-items="3"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core2-hints"><summary>Need a hint? · guided support</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-eyebrow">Hint ladder · reveal only what you need</div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="authored_core2_support"><h4>Guided support</h4><div class="g9-ladder" data-g9-ladder-ref="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2012-P1-Q05-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-group="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL"><ol data-g9-ladder></ol><ol class="g9-rung-map" aria-label="Rungs still to come"><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H0</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Represent</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H1</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Connect</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H2</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Carry out</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li></ol><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2012-P1-Q05-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-1"><li data-g9-rung="1" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[0]" data-g9-support-reveals="CONCEPT" data-g9-support-kind="REPRESENT" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2012-P1-Q05-MOVE-1"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H0</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Represent</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><div data-g9-support-reveal><p>Evaluate the spring extension at t=1 s from its cosine law.</p></div></li></template><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2012-P1-Q05-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-2"><li data-g9-rung="2" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[1]" data-g9-support-reveals="METHOD" data-g9-support-kind="CONNECT" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2012-P1-Q05-MOVE-2"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H1</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Connect</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><div data-g9-support-reveal><p>Translate the resulting block position into the pebble&#x27;s required horizontal displacement.</p></div></li></template><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2012-P1-Q05-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-3"><li data-g9-rung="3" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[2]" data-g9-support-reveals="METHOD" data-g9-support-kind="EXECUTE" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2012-P1-Q05-MOVE-3"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H2</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Carry out</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><div data-g9-support-reveal><p>Check the vertical projectile displacement at the same time.</p></div></li></template><button type="button" data-g9-next-rung>Show guided support</button></div></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-link-list" data-g9-component="CONCEPT_NAV" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="LINK_LIST"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="concept_navigation"><h4>Need the concept again?</h4><ul><li><a data-g9-concept-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2012-P1-Q05" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL">Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences</a></li><li><a data-g9-concept-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2012-P1-Q05" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS">Split one plane-motion event into independent x/y components with one clock</a></li></ul></div></div></section></div></div><section class="blueprint-slot slot-solution" data-blueprint-slot="solution" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure" data-g9-component="SOLUTION" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE"><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2012-P1-Q05-solution"><summary>Answer and working</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2012-P1-Q05-solution"><div class="g9-component g9-c-step-list" data-g9-component="SOLUTION_STEPS" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEP_LIST" data-g9-component-items="3"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="structured_working"><h4>Reasoning route</h4><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="1" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2012-P1-Q05-MOVE-1" data-g9-solution-kind="REPRESENT" data-g9-solution-stage="REPRESENT" data-g9-solution-crux="false"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">1</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Represent</h4><p data-g9-solution-action>After 1 s the spring extension is 0.2 cos(pi/3) = 0.1 m, so the block is 4.9 + 0.1 = 5.0 m from the wall.</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>stated source quantities</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> This follows from the stated model, conservation law, or stoichiometric relation preserved in the source-matched task.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> intermediate result 1</p></div></section><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="2" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2012-P1-Q05-MOVE-2" data-g9-solution-kind="CONNECT" data-g9-solution-stage="CONNECT" data-g9-solution-crux="true"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">2</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Connect</h4><p class="g9-prov" data-g9-crux-label>Key move</p><p data-g9-solution-action>The pebble starts 10 m from the wall and must reach the block, 5.0 m from the wall, in 1 s: it covers 5 m horizontally, so v cos45 = 5.</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>stated source quantities</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> This follows from the stated model, conservation law, or stoichiometric relation preserved in the source-matched task.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> intermediate result 2</p></div></section><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="3" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2012-P1-Q05-MOVE-3" data-g9-solution-kind="TRANSFORM" data-g9-solution-stage="CALCULATE" data-g9-solution-crux="false"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">3</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Calculate</h4><p data-g9-solution-action>Hence v=5sqrt(2)=sqrt(50) m/s; its vertical displacement after 1 s is also zero, so the meeting height is consistent.</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>stated source quantities</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> This follows from the stated model, conservation law, or stoichiometric relation preserved in the source-matched task.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> Option (A): sqrt(50) m/s.</p></div></section></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-answer-box" data-g9-component="ANSWER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="ANSWER_BOX"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="answer"><p>Option (A): <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>50</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\\sqrt{50}</annotation></semantics></math></span> m/s.</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-check-box" data-g9-component="CHECK" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CHECK_BOX"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="independent_check"><h4>Independent check</h4><p>With v/<span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\\sqrt{2}</annotation></semantics></math></span>=5 m/s, y(1)=5-5=0 and the horizontal coordinate is exactly the block coordinate.</p></div></div></template></div></section></article><article id="g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2022-P1-Q08" data-g9-unit="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2022-P1-Q08" data-g9-kind="QUESTION" data-g9-role="CORE2" data-g9-search-text="Physics Motion in 2 D Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences Medium concept Multi-step reasoning Motion in 2D component and projectile practice Numerical value JEE (Advanced) 2022 · Paper 1 · Q8 Verified past-exam adaptation A projectile has ordinary range d when launched at speed v and angle theta under gravity g. At the highest point it enters a region where the effective downward acceleration is g/0.81. If its new total horizontal range is d&#x27;=n d, find n. The acceleration changes only after the projectile reaches its apex. Horizontal velocity is unchanged at the boundary." class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-header" data-g9-component="IDENTITY" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="HEADER"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="source_identity"><h2>JEE (Advanced) · 2022 · Paper 1 · Q8</h2><p class="g9-prov">Official past paper, faithful restatement of the original</p></div><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE2" data-g9-meta-record="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2022-P1-Q08" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-KIN-2D-MOTION" data-g9-meta-value="Motion in 2 D"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Motion in 2 D</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-meta-value="Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="question-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2022-P1-Q08" data-g9-meta-value="D3"><strong>Question difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Multi-step reasoning</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="family" data-g9-meta-ref="FAM-PHY-KIN-2D-PRACTICE" data-g9-meta-value="Motion in 2D component and projectile practice"><strong>Family:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Motion in 2D component and projectile practice</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="question-type" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2022-P1-Q08" data-g9-meta-value="numerical_value"><strong>Question type:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Numerical value</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="source" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2022-P1-Q08" data-g9-meta-value="JEE (Advanced) 2022 · Paper 1 · Q8"><strong>Source:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>JEE (Advanced) 2022 · Paper 1 · Q8</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="provenance" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2022-P1-Q08" data-g9-meta-value="PYQ_ADAPTED"><strong>Provenance:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Verified past-exam adaptation</span></span></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-link-list" data-g9-component="SOURCE_PDF" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="LINK_LIST"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="source_pdf"><a class="g9-pdf-link g9-source-pdf" data-g9-source-pdf href="https://jeeadv.ac.in/past_qps/2022_1_English.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" type="application/pdf" aria-label="Open the original past paper as a PDF to print it"><svg class="g9-pdf-icon" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="24" height="24"><path d="M6 2.5h8l4.5 4.5v14.5H6z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linejoin="round"/><path d="M14 2.5V7h4.5M9 12h6.5M9 15.5h6.5M9 19h4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg><span>Source paper (PDF)</span></a></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-grid" data-g9-component="DIFFICULTY_WHY" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_GRID"><button type="button" class="g9-why-toggle" data-g9-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="g9-why-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2022-P1-Q08"><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-band" data-g9-band="D3">D3 · 6/10 · about 2 min</span><span>Why this difficulty?</span></button><div id="g9-why-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2022-P1-Q08" class="g9-why" hidden><p>Splits one projectile into two acceleration regimes while preserving the apex state.</p><div class=g9-dgrid><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Choosing the model</span><b>2/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Representation</span><b>1/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Reasoning chain</span><b>1/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Algebra and numbers</span><b>1/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Traps and exceptions</span><b>1/2</b></div></div></div></div></section><div class="g9-split"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-attempt" data-blueprint-slot="attempt" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-stem" data-g9-component="STEM" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEM"><div class="g9-eyebrow">Attempt first</div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="stem"><p>A projectile has ordinary range d when launched at speed v and angle theta under gravity g. At the highest point it enters a region where the effective downward acceleration is g/0.81. If its new total horizontal range is d&#x27;=n d, find n.</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-callout-info" data-g9-component="CONDITIONS" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CALLOUT_INFO"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="conditions"><h4>Conditions</h4><ul><li>The acceleration changes only after the projectile reaches its apex.</li><li>Horizontal velocity is unchanged at the boundary.</li></ul></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-callout-warn" data-g9-component="TRAP" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CALLOUT_WARN"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core2-wrong-route"><summary>Common wrong route · open if you want a warning</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="common_wrong_route"><h4>Common wrong route</h4><p>Recomputing the whole flight with the modified gravity instead of changing only the descent.</p></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-attempt" data-g9-component="ATTEMPT" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="ATTEMPT"><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your answer<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div></div></section></div><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-representation" data-blueprint-slot="representation" data-required="false"></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-support" data-blueprint-slot="support" data-required="false"><div class="g9-component g9-c-ladder" data-g9-component="HINT_LADDER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="LADDER" data-g9-component-items="3"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core2-hints"><summary>Need a hint? · guided support</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-eyebrow">Hint ladder · reveal only what you need</div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="authored_core2_support"><h4>Guided support</h4><div class="g9-ladder" data-g9-ladder-ref="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2022-P1-Q08-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-group="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL"><ol data-g9-ladder></ol><ol class="g9-rung-map" aria-label="Rungs still to come"><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H0</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Represent</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H1</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Connect</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H2</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Carry out</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li></ol><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2022-P1-Q08-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-1"><li data-g9-rung="1" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[0]" data-g9-support-reveals="CONCEPT" data-g9-support-kind="REPRESENT" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2022-P1-Q08-MOVE-1"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H0</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Represent</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><div data-g9-support-reveal><p>Use symmetry only for the ordinary projectile to identify the apex position.</p></div></li></template><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2022-P1-Q08-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-2"><li data-g9-rung="2" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[1]" data-g9-support-reveals="METHOD" data-g9-support-kind="CONNECT" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2022-P1-Q08-MOVE-2"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H1</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Connect</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><div data-g9-support-reveal><p>Compare the fall time from a fixed height under g and g/0.81.</p></div></li></template><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2022-P1-Q08-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-3"><li data-g9-rung="3" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[2]" data-g9-support-reveals="METHOD" data-g9-support-kind="EXECUTE" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2022-P1-Q08-MOVE-3"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H2</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Carry out</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><div data-g9-support-reveal><p>Convert that time ratio into the second-stage horizontal distance.</p></div></li></template><button type="button" data-g9-next-rung>Show guided support</button></div></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-link-list" data-g9-component="CONCEPT_NAV" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="LINK_LIST"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="concept_navigation"><h4>Need the concept again?</h4><ul><li><a data-g9-concept-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2022-P1-Q08" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL">Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences</a></li><li><a data-g9-concept-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2022-P1-Q08" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS">Split one plane-motion event into independent x/y components with one clock</a></li></ul></div></div></section></div></div><section class="blueprint-slot slot-solution" data-blueprint-slot="solution" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure" data-g9-component="SOLUTION" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE"><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2022-P1-Q08-solution"><summary>Answer and working</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2022-P1-Q08-solution"><div class="g9-component g9-c-step-list" data-g9-component="SOLUTION_STEPS" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEP_LIST" data-g9-component-items="3"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="structured_working"><h4>Reasoning route</h4><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="1" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2022-P1-Q08-MOVE-1" data-g9-solution-kind="REPRESENT" data-g9-solution-stage="REPRESENT" data-g9-solution-crux="false"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">1</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Represent</h4><p data-g9-solution-action>The projectile reaches the apex after covering half of its ordinary range, d/2.</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>stated source quantities</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> This follows from the stated model, conservation law, or stoichiometric relation preserved in the source-matched task.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> intermediate result 1</p></div></section><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="2" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2022-P1-Q08-MOVE-2" data-g9-solution-kind="CONNECT" data-g9-solution-stage="CONNECT" data-g9-solution-crux="true"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">2</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Connect</h4><p class="g9-prov" data-g9-crux-label>Key move</p><p data-g9-solution-action>The apex height is unchanged, but the downward acceleration becomes g/0.81, so the fall time from the apex is 0.9 times the original descent time.</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>stated source quantities</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> This follows from the stated model, conservation law, or stoichiometric relation preserved in the source-matched task.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> intermediate result 2</p></div></section><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="3" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2022-P1-Q08-MOVE-3" data-g9-solution-kind="TRANSFORM" data-g9-solution-stage="CALCULATE" data-g9-solution-crux="false"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">3</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Calculate</h4><p data-g9-solution-action>The second horizontal segment is therefore 0.9(d/2); hence d&#x27;=d/2+0.9d/2=0.95d.</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>stated source quantities</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> This follows from the stated model, conservation law, or stoichiometric relation preserved in the source-matched task.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> 0.95.</p></div></section></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-answer-box" data-g9-component="ANSWER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="ANSWER_BOX"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="answer"><p>0.95.</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-check-box" data-g9-component="CHECK" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CHECK_BOX"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="independent_check"><h4>Independent check</h4><p>Stronger effective gravity must shorten only the post-apex horizontal segment, so n must lie between 0.5 and 1.</p></div></div></template></div></section></article><article id="g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2023-P1-Q01" data-g9-unit="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2023-P1-Q01" data-g9-kind="QUESTION" data-g9-role="CORE2" data-g9-search-text="Physics Motion in 2 D Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences Medium concept High-transfer / exception-sensitive Motion in 2D component and projectile practice Multiple-correct multiple choice JEE (Advanced) 2023 · Paper 1 · Q1 Verified past-exam adaptation A ball slides without friction from a height h above a terrace whose top is 3h above the ground, leaves horizontally, strikes the ground and rebounds with coefficient of restitution 1/sqrt(3). Determine which of the source statements about launch speed, impact angle, rebound velocity and rebound geometry are correct. Slide is frictionless. Ground is horizontal. Coefficient of restitution is 1/sqrt(3). x-hat points horizontally in the direction the ball leaves the terrace, and z-hat points vertically upward. (A) u0=sqrt(2gh) horizontally (B) rebound velocity equals sqrt(2gh)(x-hat - z-hat) (C) impact angle is 60 degrees (D) horizontal range divided by rebound maximum height is 2sqrt(3)" class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-header" data-g9-component="IDENTITY" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="HEADER"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="source_identity"><h2>JEE (Advanced) · 2023 · Paper 1 · Q1</h2><p class="g9-prov">Official past paper, faithful restatement of the original</p></div><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE2" data-g9-meta-record="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2023-P1-Q01" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-KIN-2D-MOTION" data-g9-meta-value="Motion in 2 D"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Motion in 2 D</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-meta-value="Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="question-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2023-P1-Q01" data-g9-meta-value="D4"><strong>Question difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>High-transfer / exception-sensitive</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="family" data-g9-meta-ref="FAM-PHY-KIN-2D-PRACTICE" data-g9-meta-value="Motion in 2D component and projectile practice"><strong>Family:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Motion in 2D component and projectile practice</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="question-type" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2023-P1-Q01" data-g9-meta-value="multi_correct_mcq"><strong>Question type:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Multiple-correct multiple choice</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="source" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2023-P1-Q01" data-g9-meta-value="JEE (Advanced) 2023 · Paper 1 · Q1"><strong>Source:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>JEE (Advanced) 2023 · Paper 1 · Q1</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="provenance" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2023-P1-Q01" data-g9-meta-value="PYQ_ADAPTED"><strong>Provenance:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Verified past-exam adaptation</span></span></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-link-list" data-g9-component="SOURCE_PDF" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="LINK_LIST"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="source_pdf"><a class="g9-pdf-link g9-source-pdf" data-g9-source-pdf href="https://jeeadv.ac.in/past_qps/2023_1_English.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" type="application/pdf" aria-label="Open the original past paper as a PDF to print it"><svg class="g9-pdf-icon" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="24" height="24"><path d="M6 2.5h8l4.5 4.5v14.5H6z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linejoin="round"/><path d="M14 2.5V7h4.5M9 12h6.5M9 15.5h6.5M9 19h4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg><span>Source paper (PDF)</span></a></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-grid" data-g9-component="DIFFICULTY_WHY" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_GRID"><button type="button" class="g9-why-toggle" data-g9-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="g9-why-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2023-P1-Q01"><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-band" data-g9-band="D4">D4 · 8/10 · about 4 min</span><span>Why this difficulty?</span></button><div id="g9-why-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2023-P1-Q01" class="g9-why" hidden><p>Chains energy, projectile motion and restitution across two events.</p><div class=g9-dgrid><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Choosing the model</span><b>2/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Representation</span><b>2/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Reasoning chain</span><b>2/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Algebra and numbers</span><b>1/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Traps and exceptions</span><b>1/2</b></div></div></div></div></section><div class="g9-split"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-attempt" data-blueprint-slot="attempt" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-stem" data-g9-component="STEM" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEM"><div class="g9-eyebrow">Attempt first</div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="stem"><p>A ball slides without friction from a height h above a terrace whose top is 3h above the ground, leaves horizontally, strikes the ground and rebounds with coefficient of restitution 1/<span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\\sqrt{3}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Determine which of the source statements about launch speed, impact angle, rebound velocity and rebound geometry are correct.</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-callout-info" data-g9-component="CONDITIONS" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CALLOUT_INFO"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="conditions"><h4>Conditions</h4><ul><li>Slide is frictionless.</li><li>Ground is horizontal.</li><li>Coefficient of restitution is 1/<span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\\sqrt{3}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</li><li>x-hat points horizontally in the direction the ball leaves the terrace, and z-hat points vertically upward.</li></ul></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-callout-warn" data-g9-component="TRAP" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CALLOUT_WARN"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core2-wrong-route"><summary>Common wrong route · open if you want a warning</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="common_wrong_route"><h4>Common wrong route</h4><p>Losing the sign of the rebound vertical velocity or mixing launch/drop heights.</p></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-attempt" data-g9-component="ATTEMPT" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="ATTEMPT"><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your answer<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div></div></section></div><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-representation" data-blueprint-slot="representation" data-required="false"></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-support" data-blueprint-slot="support" data-required="false"><div class="g9-component g9-c-ladder" data-g9-component="HINT_LADDER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="LADDER" data-g9-component-items="3"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core2-hints"><summary>Need a hint? · guided support</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-eyebrow">Hint ladder · reveal only what you need</div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="authored_core2_support"><h4>Guided support</h4><div class="g9-ladder" data-g9-ladder-ref="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2023-P1-Q01-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-group="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL"><ol data-g9-ladder></ol><ol class="g9-rung-map" aria-label="Rungs still to come"><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H0</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Represent</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H1</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Connect</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H2</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Carry out</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li></ol><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2023-P1-Q01-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-1"><li data-g9-rung="1" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[0]" data-g9-support-reveals="CONCEPT" data-g9-support-kind="REPRESENT" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2023-P1-Q01-MOVE-1"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H0</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Represent</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><div data-g9-support-reveal><p>Use energy conservation on the slide to get the horizontal launch speed.</p></div></li></template><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2023-P1-Q01-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-2"><li data-g9-rung="2" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[1]" data-g9-support-reveals="METHOD" data-g9-support-kind="CONNECT" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2023-P1-Q01-MOVE-2"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H1</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Connect</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><div data-g9-support-reveal><p>Find the vertical impact component from the 3h fall.</p></div></li></template><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2023-P1-Q01-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-3"><li data-g9-rung="3" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[2]" data-g9-support-reveals="METHOD" data-g9-support-kind="EXECUTE" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2023-P1-Q01-MOVE-3"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H2</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Carry out</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><div data-g9-support-reveal><p>Apply restitution to the vertical component, then compute rebound height and compare with the first-flight range.</p></div></li></template><button type="button" data-g9-next-rung>Show guided support</button></div></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-link-list" data-g9-component="CONCEPT_NAV" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="LINK_LIST"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="concept_navigation"><h4>Need the concept again?</h4><ul><li><a data-g9-concept-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2023-P1-Q01" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL">Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences</a></li><li><a data-g9-concept-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2023-P1-Q01" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS">Split one plane-motion event into independent x/y components with one clock</a></li></ul></div></div></section></div></div><section class="blueprint-slot slot-solution" data-blueprint-slot="solution" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure" data-g9-component="SOLUTION" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE"><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2023-P1-Q01-solution"><summary>Answer and working</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2023-P1-Q01-solution"><div class="g9-component g9-c-step-list" data-g9-component="SOLUTION_STEPS" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEP_LIST" data-g9-component-items="3"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="structured_working"><h4>Reasoning route</h4><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="1" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2023-P1-Q01-MOVE-1" data-g9-solution-kind="REPRESENT" data-g9-solution-stage="REPRESENT" data-g9-solution-crux="false"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">1</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Represent</h4><p data-g9-solution-action>Energy on the slide gives horizontal launch speed u0=sqrt(2gh).</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>stated source quantities</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> This follows from the stated model, conservation law, or stoichiometric relation preserved in the source-matched task.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> intermediate result 1</p></div></section><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="2" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2023-P1-Q01-MOVE-2" data-g9-solution-kind="CONNECT" data-g9-solution-stage="CONNECT" data-g9-solution-crux="true"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">2</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Connect</h4><p class="g9-prov" data-g9-crux-label>Key move</p><p data-g9-solution-action>Falling through 3h gives vertical impact speed sqrt(6gh), so tan(theta)=sqrt(6gh)/sqrt(2gh)=sqrt(3), hence theta=60 degrees.</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>stated source quantities</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> This follows from the stated model, conservation law, or stoichiometric relation preserved in the source-matched task.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> intermediate result 2</p></div></section><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="3" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2023-P1-Q01-MOVE-3" data-g9-solution-kind="TRANSFORM" data-g9-solution-stage="CALCULATE" data-g9-solution-crux="false"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">3</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Calculate</h4><p data-g9-solution-action>After impact the upward vertical component is sqrt(2gh); rebound height is h, while pre-impact flight gives horizontal distance 2sqrt(3)h, so d/h1=2sqrt(3).</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>stated source quantities</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> This follows from the stated model, conservation law, or stoichiometric relation preserved in the source-matched task.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> Options (A), (C) and (D).</p></div></section></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-answer-box" data-g9-component="ANSWER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="ANSWER_BOX"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="answer"><p>Options (A), (C) and (D).</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-check-box" data-g9-component="CHECK" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CHECK_BOX"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="independent_check"><h4>Independent check</h4><p>The restitution factor maps the downward <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>6</mn><mi>g</mi><mi>h</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\\sqrt{6gh}</annotation></semantics></math></span> to an upward <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>g</mi><mi>h</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\\sqrt{2gh}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so the rebound velocity is <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>g</mi><mi>h</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\\sqrt{2gh}</annotation></semantics></math></span>(x-hat + z-hat) with z-hat upward; option (B)&#x27;s (x-hat - z-hat) points downward and is not correct.</p></div></div></template></div></section></article><article id="g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2025-P2-Q15" data-g9-unit="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2025-P2-Q15" data-g9-kind="QUESTION" data-g9-role="CORE2" data-g9-search-text="Physics Motion in 2 D Apply constant-acceleration kinematics component by component Medium concept High-transfer / exception-sensitive Motion in 2D component and projectile practice Numerical value JEE (Advanced) 2025 · Paper 2 · Q15 Verified past-exam adaptation A 0.2 kg projectile is launched at 270 m/s at 60 degrees in a medium with linear drag F=-c v, where c=0.1 kg/s. It hits a vertical wall after 2 s. Taking e=2.7 for the exponential evaluation specified by the source, determine the horizontal distance to the wall. Linear viscous drag acts opposite the instantaneous velocity. Use the source&#x27;s e=2.7 approximation." class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-header" data-g9-component="IDENTITY" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="HEADER"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="source_identity"><h2>JEE (Advanced) · 2025 · Paper 2 · Q15</h2><p class="g9-prov">Official past paper, faithful restatement of the original</p></div><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE2" data-g9-meta-record="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2025-P2-Q15" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-KIN-2D-MOTION" data-g9-meta-value="Motion in 2 D"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Motion in 2 D</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" data-g9-meta-value="Apply constant-acceleration kinematics component by component"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Apply constant-acceleration kinematics component by component</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="question-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2025-P2-Q15" data-g9-meta-value="D4"><strong>Question difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>High-transfer / exception-sensitive</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="family" data-g9-meta-ref="FAM-PHY-KIN-2D-PRACTICE" data-g9-meta-value="Motion in 2D component and projectile practice"><strong>Family:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Motion in 2D component and projectile practice</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="question-type" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2025-P2-Q15" data-g9-meta-value="numerical_value"><strong>Question type:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Numerical value</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="source" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2025-P2-Q15" data-g9-meta-value="JEE (Advanced) 2025 · Paper 2 · Q15"><strong>Source:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>JEE (Advanced) 2025 · Paper 2 · Q15</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="provenance" data-g9-meta-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2025-P2-Q15" data-g9-meta-value="PYQ_ADAPTED"><strong>Provenance:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Verified past-exam adaptation</span></span></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-link-list" data-g9-component="SOURCE_PDF" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="LINK_LIST"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="source_pdf"><a class="g9-pdf-link g9-source-pdf" data-g9-source-pdf href="https://jeeadv.ac.in/past_qps/2025_2_English.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" type="application/pdf" aria-label="Open the original past paper as a PDF to print it"><svg class="g9-pdf-icon" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="24" height="24"><path d="M6 2.5h8l4.5 4.5v14.5H6z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linejoin="round"/><path d="M14 2.5V7h4.5M9 12h6.5M9 15.5h6.5M9 19h4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg><span>Source paper (PDF)</span></a></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-grid" data-g9-component="DIFFICULTY_WHY" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_GRID"><button type="button" class="g9-why-toggle" data-g9-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="g9-why-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2025-P2-Q15"><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-band" data-g9-band="D4">D4 · 8/10 · about 4 min</span><span>Why this difficulty?</span></button><div id="g9-why-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2025-P2-Q15" class="g9-why" hidden><p>Requires solving a velocity-dependent Newton-II equation and integrating the horizontal component.</p><div class=g9-dgrid><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Choosing the model</span><b>2/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Representation</span><b>2/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Reasoning chain</span><b>2/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Algebra and numbers</span><b>1/2</b></div><div class="g9-dcell"><span>Traps and exceptions</span><b>1/2</b></div></div></div></div></section><div class="g9-split"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-attempt" data-blueprint-slot="attempt" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-stem" data-g9-component="STEM" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEM"><div class="g9-eyebrow">Attempt first</div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="stem"><p>A 0.2 kg projectile is launched at 270 m/s at 60 degrees in a medium with linear drag F=-c v, where c=0.1 kg/s. It hits a vertical wall after 2 s. Taking e=2.7 for the exponential evaluation specified by the source, determine the horizontal distance to the wall.</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-callout-info" data-g9-component="CONDITIONS" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CALLOUT_INFO"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="conditions"><h4>Conditions</h4><ul><li>Linear viscous drag acts opposite the instantaneous velocity.</li><li>Use the source&#x27;s e=2.7 approximation.</li></ul></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-callout-warn" data-g9-component="TRAP" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CALLOUT_WARN"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core2-wrong-route"><summary>Common wrong route · open if you want a warning</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="common_wrong_route"><h4>Common wrong route</h4><p>Using constant-acceleration projectile formulas despite velocity-dependent drag.</p></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-attempt" data-g9-component="ATTEMPT" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="ATTEMPT"><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your answer<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div></div></section></div><div class="g9-col g9-col-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-representation" data-blueprint-slot="representation" data-required="false"></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-support" data-blueprint-slot="support" data-required="false"><div class="g9-component g9-c-ladder" data-g9-component="HINT_LADDER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="LADDER" data-g9-component-items="3"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core2-hints"><summary>Need a hint? · guided support</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-eyebrow">Hint ladder · reveal only what you need</div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="authored_core2_support"><h4>Guided support</h4><div class="g9-ladder" data-g9-ladder-ref="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2025-P2-Q15-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-group="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL"><ol data-g9-ladder></ol><ol class="g9-rung-map" aria-label="Rungs still to come"><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H0</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Represent</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H1</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Connect</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li><li data-g9-rung-ghost><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H2</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Carry out</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div></li></ol><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2025-P2-Q15-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-1"><li data-g9-rung="1" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[0]" data-g9-support-reveals="CONCEPT" data-g9-support-kind="REPRESENT" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2025-P2-Q15-MOVE-1"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H0</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Represent</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><div data-g9-support-reveal><p>Write Newton II for the horizontal component only.</p></div></li></template><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2025-P2-Q15-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-2"><li data-g9-rung="2" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[1]" data-g9-support-reveals="METHOD" data-g9-support-kind="CONNECT" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2025-P2-Q15-MOVE-2"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H1</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Connect</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><div data-g9-support-reveal><p>Identify the decay time constant m/c.</p></div></li></template><template data-g9-rung-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2025-P2-Q15-AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL-3"><li data-g9-rung="3" data-g9-support-provenance="AUTHORED_CORE2_PROMPT_REVEAL" data-g9-support-source="scaffolds[2]" data-g9-support-reveals="METHOD" data-g9-support-kind="EXECUTE" data-g9-supports-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2025-P2-Q15-MOVE-3"><div class="g9-rung-head"><span class="g9-rung-no">H2</span><span class="g9-rung-label" data-g9-support-stage-label>Carry out</span><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-guided">Guided</span></div><div data-g9-support-reveal><p>Integrate vx(t) from 0 to 2 s rather than multiplying the final speed by time.</p></div></li></template><button type="button" data-g9-next-rung>Show guided support</button></div></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-link-list" data-g9-component="CONCEPT_NAV" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="LINK_LIST"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="concept_navigation"><h4>Need the concept again?</h4><ul><li><a data-g9-concept-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2025-P2-Q15" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION">Apply constant-acceleration kinematics component by component</a></li><li><a data-g9-concept-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2025-P2-Q15" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="#g9-CORE1A--MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS">Split one plane-motion event into independent x/y components with one clock</a></li></ul></div></div></section></div></div><section class="blueprint-slot slot-solution" data-blueprint-slot="solution" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure" data-g9-component="SOLUTION" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE"><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2025-P2-Q15-solution"><summary>Answer and working</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE2-PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2025-P2-Q15-solution"><div class="g9-component g9-c-step-list" data-g9-component="SOLUTION_STEPS" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEP_LIST" data-g9-component-items="3"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="structured_working"><h4>Reasoning route</h4><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="1" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2025-P2-Q15-MOVE-1" data-g9-solution-kind="REPRESENT" data-g9-solution-stage="REPRESENT" data-g9-solution-crux="false"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">1</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Represent</h4><p data-g9-solution-action>The horizontal component obeys m dvx/dt=-c vx, so vx(t)=vx0 exp[-(c/m)t].</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>stated source quantities</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> This follows from the stated model, conservation law, or stoichiometric relation preserved in the source-matched task.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> intermediate result 1</p></div></section><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="2" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2025-P2-Q15-MOVE-2" data-g9-solution-kind="CONNECT" data-g9-solution-stage="CONNECT" data-g9-solution-crux="true"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">2</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Connect</h4><p class="g9-prov" data-g9-crux-label>Key move</p><p data-g9-solution-action>Here vx0=270 cos60=135 m/s and m/c=2 s, so x(t)=vx0(m/c)[1-exp(-ct/m)].</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>stated source quantities</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> This follows from the stated model, conservation law, or stoichiometric relation preserved in the source-matched task.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> intermediate result 2</p></div></section><section class="g9-solution-move" data-g9-solution-order="3" data-g9-solution-move="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2025-P2-Q15-MOVE-3" data-g9-solution-kind="TRANSFORM" data-g9-solution-stage="CALCULATE" data-g9-solution-crux="false"><div class="g9-step-no" aria-hidden="true">3</div><div class="g9-step-body"><h4 data-g9-solution-stage-label>Calculate</h4><p data-g9-solution-action>At t=2 s, x=270(1-1/e)=270(1-1/2.7)=170 m.</p><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="solution_inputs"><h4>Uses</h4><ul><li>stated source quantities</li></ul></div><p data-g9-solution-why><em>Why valid:</em> This follows from the stated model, conservation law, or stoichiometric relation preserved in the source-matched task.</p><p data-g9-solution-output><strong>Result:</strong> 170 m (within the official accepted interval 167–171 m).</p></div></section></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-answer-box" data-g9-component="ANSWER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="ANSWER_BOX"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="answer"><p>170 m (within the official accepted interval 167–171 m).</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-check-box" data-g9-component="CHECK" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="CHECK_BOX"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="independent_check"><h4>Independent check</h4><p>The distance is smaller than the no-drag value 135×2=270 m, as it must be.</p></div></div></template></div></section></article><article id="g9-CORE2--PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q26" data-g9-unit="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q26" data-g9-kind="QUESTION" data-g9-role="CORE2" data-g9-search-text="Physics Motion in 2 D Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences Medium concept High-transfer / exception-sensitive Motion in 2D component and projectile practice Single-correct multiple choice IIT-JEE 2011 · Paper II · Q26 Verified past-exam adaptation A 0.2 kg ball rests on top of a 5 m vertical post. A 0.01 kg bullet moving horizontally with speed V strikes the ball centrally. After the collision the ball and bullet move independently and land 20 m and 100 m, respectively, from the foot of the post. Find the bullet&#x27;s initial speed. Both bodies begin their post-collision projectile motion from the same 5 m height. Use g=10 m/s^2 for the numerical evaluation consistent with the source options. (A) 250 m/s (B) 250sqrt(2) m/s (C) 400 m/s (D) 500 m/s" class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-header" data-g9-component="IDENTITY" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="HEADER"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="source_identity"><h2>IIT-JEE · 2011 · Paper II · Q26</h2><p class="g9-prov">Official past paper, faithful restatement of the original</p></div><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE2" data-g9-meta-record="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q26" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta